VEL.register("ascs",{"id":"ascs","track":{"code":"ASCS","name":"Air Systems Cleaning Specialist","kind":"Exam prep","org":"NADCA","domains":[["Benefits",16],["Mechanical",15],["Assessments",12],["Cleaning & Restoration",42],["Health & Safety",7],["Standards",8]],"exam":{"questions":150,"minutes":210,"cut":72,"cutNote":"NADCA sets the cut per exam form and does not publish it. 72% is the last figure NADCA published, so treat it as a floor."},"facts":["150 multiple-choice questions, 3.5 hours, at a Kryterion testing center or a NADCA event.","Six domains from the 2026 Candidate Guide. Cleaning & Restoration is 42% of the exam.","Keyed to ACR 2025, The NADCA Standard for Assessment, Cleaning & Restoration of HVAC Systems.","Register: buy the exam voucher at nadca.com (member price for member companies), then book at Kryterion."],"sims":[[30,"Quick check"],[50,"Short form"],[150,"Full length"]],"sources":[["ACR 2025, The NADCA Standard","https://acrstandard.nadca.com/"],["NADCA ASCS 2026 Candidate Guide","https://nadca.com/sites/nadca/files/docs/2026/NADCA_ASCS%20Candidate%20Guide.pdf"],["NADCA Open Air Plenums white paper","https://nadca.com/resources"],["EPA: Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","https://www.epa.gov/indoor-air-quality-iaq/should-you-have-air-ducts-your-home-cleaned"],["OSHA 29 CFR 1910 (general industry)","https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1910"]]},"units":[{"id":"acr:G-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR 2025 is the standard of care for the HVAC cleaning and restoration industry.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What is ACR 2025 to the HVAC cleaning and restoration industry?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0760"]},{"id":"acr:G-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR 2025 supersedes all previous editions of the Standard.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What happens to earlier editions of the Standard now that ACR 2025 is out?","trap":"Guides built on ACR 2021 cost points. 2021 wording (mold, AHJ, old method numbers) is superseded.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0785","ascs-0798"]},{"id":"acr:G-3","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR is a performance standard. It also gives minimum procedural requirements.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What type of standard is ACR, and what else does it give beyond performance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0724"]},{"id":"acr:G-4","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard defines the minimum performance requirements and procedural requirements.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which two kinds of minimum requirements does the Standard define?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-9a","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard gives minimum standards and guidance to industry professionals and service providers.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does the Standard give to industry professionals and service providers?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-9b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"It also gives guidance to building owners and others who manage HVAC systems and projects.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides industry professionals, who else does the Standard give guidance to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-5","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard applies to the airside surfaces of HVAC components.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which surfaces of HVAC components does the Standard apply to?","trap":"Airside surface excludes open air plenums and interstitial cavities (Definitions).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-6","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes non-ducted open air plenums.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which type of plenum does the ACR 2025 scope exclude?","trap":"A return plenum inside ductwork is in scope (1.7.3). The scope clause excludes non-ducted open air plenums, not ducted return plenums.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0494","ascs-0721"]},{"id":"acr:G-7","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes mechanical repair of electrical components and pneumatic components.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Mechanical repair of which two kinds of components is outside the ACR scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-8","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes repair of high-pressure vessels, gas controls, and oil controls.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Repair of which vessels and which controls is outside the ACR scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-9","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The scope excludes preventative maintenance tasks that the OEM prescribes or recommends.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which maintenance tasks does the ACR scope exclude?","trap":"OEM preventative maintenance is outside the scope, yet cleaning as part of OEM recommended maintenance is still a need-to-clean trigger.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-13","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"DEF","text":"The Standard does not address all hazards and risks.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Does the Standard claim to address every hazard and risk of the work?","trap":"The Standard also names no PPE anywhere. It is an engineering controls document.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-16","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"NOTE","text":"NADCA gives no warranty of energy savings.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What warranty does NADCA give about energy savings from work to the Standard?","trap":"Any answer that promises a fixed or typical percentage of energy savings is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0015"]},{"id":"acr:G-17","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-what-acr-covers","tag":"NOTE","text":"NADCA gives no warranty that the work corrects indoor air quality concerns.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Does NADCA warrant that work to the Standard corrects indoor air quality concerns?","trap":"An IAQ program is a reason to clean, but NADCA warrants no IAQ correction.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0015"]},{"id":"acr:G-19","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"SHALL denotes a mandatory requirement. The conformance criterion permits no deviation.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does SHALL denote in ACR 2025, and how much deviation does it permit?","trap":"SHALL is the only one of the four words that is a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0729","ascs-0778","ascs-0786"]},{"id":"acr:G-20","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"RECOMMEND means that the practice is advised or suggested. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does RECOMMEND mean in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"Recommended clauses often read mandatory: hazmat consultation, ASCS oversight, all 16 triggers to clean. None of them is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0779"]},{"id":"acr:G-21","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"MAY signifies permission that the document expresses. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does MAY signify in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"MAY is permission, not ability. Swapping MAY and CAN is the most missed definition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0730","ascs-0780"]},{"id":"acr:G-22","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"CAN signifies an ability or a possibility open to the user. It is not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What does CAN signify in the Standard, and is it a requirement?","trap":"CAN is ability or possibility, not permission. Neither word creates a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0781"]},{"id":"acr:G-23","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"DEF","text":"A Requirement is a mandatory practice for compliance with the Standard.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How does the Standard define a Requirement?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-24","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"NOTE","text":"MAY gives permission. CAN gives ability. This is the most missed pair in the Standard.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Which pair of obligation words is missed most, and what separates them?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0730"]},{"id":"acr:G-25","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-obligation-words","tag":"NOTE","text":"Component lists in the Standard read \"but not limited to\". They are open lists, not closed lists.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How do you read a component list in the Standard that says 'but not limited to'?","trap":"An item missing from a listed set is not excluded by that omission. The list gives examples.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-10","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"A person who does work to this Standard has training and qualifications first.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides relevant knowledge and experience, what must a person have before doing work to this Standard?","trap":"Training and qualification are required. ASCS is only recommended: for project oversight here, and as an example qualified inspector in 1.2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-11","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"That person has relevant knowledge and experience before work of any type.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Besides training and qualifications, what must a person have before work of any type?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-12","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"REC","text":"An ASCS, or a person with equivalent certification, has responsibility for project oversight.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Who is recommended to have responsibility for project oversight?","trap":"Recommended, not required. An equivalent certification also meets it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0722"]},{"id":"acr:X-9","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASCS oversight is a recommendation. Training and qualification are mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"Of ASCS oversight and training and qualification, which is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0722"]},{"id":"acr:G-14","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"The user does the work in strict accordance with applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"How must the user do the work with respect to codes, laws, and regulations?","trap":"2025 dropped the Authority Having Jurisdiction wording. An answer that cites the AHJ reflects the 2021 edition.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-14a","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition directed the user to the Authority Having Jurisdiction here.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"In the clause on following codes, laws, and regulations, what did the 2021 edition direct the user to that 2025 no longer names?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:G-15","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"SHALL","text":"A code, law, or regulation on microbial assessment or remediation supersedes contradictory text.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"When a code, law, or regulation on microbial assessment or remediation contradicts the Standard, which governs?","trap":"The law wins over the Standard's text, not the other way around.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0723"]},{"id":"acr:G-18","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-people-and-codes","tag":"MAY","text":"Common sense, experience, and professional judgment may justify a deviation in certain circumstances.","src":"ACR 2025, General","prompt":"What may justify a deviation from the Standard in certain circumstances?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-0","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"A cleanliness inspection or a building history that shows one condition below starts a cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Under Determining the Need to Clean, what is recommended to start a cleaning, and how many listed conditions does it take?","trap":"All 16 triggers are recommendations. None of them makes cleaning mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-1","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The components show the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"What is the lowest particulate trigger for cleaning in ACR 2025?","trap":"2021 needed components contaminated with an accumulation. 2025 needs only the presence of particulate.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0021"]},{"id":"acr:N-18","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"NOTE","text":"N-1 lowered the trigger from \"contaminated with an accumulation\" to the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"How did ACR 2025 change the first need-to-clean trigger from the 2021 wording?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-2","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Accumulation of particulate compromises the performance of the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger ties particulate to how well the components work?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0023"]},{"id":"acr:N-3","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The components are a source of unacceptable odors.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is based on smell?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0024"]},{"id":"acr:N-4","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The components discharge visible dirt or debris into the conditioned space.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is about what the system blows into the conditioned space?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0021","ascs-0037"]},{"id":"acr:N-5","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Fire, smoke, or water damage contaminates the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which three kinds of damage that contaminate components are a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0020"]},{"id":"acr:N-6","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Birds, rodents, insects, or their byproducts infest the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which infestations of components are a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0019"]},{"id":"acr:N-7","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The components are at risk for fire hazard.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is about a risk rather than a contaminant already present?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-8","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Construction particulate or debris contaminates the components.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger covers construction contamination of existing components?","trap":"A separate trigger covers a newly installed component or duct contaminated by construction dust.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0039"]},{"id":"acr:N-16","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Construction dust or other debris contaminates a newly installed component or duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger applies to a newly installed component or duct?","trap":"Newly installed does not mean exempt. Construction dust on new work is its own trigger.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0018","ascs-0053"]},{"id":"acr:N-9","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Microbial contamination reaches Condition 2 or Condition 3.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"At which microbial Conditions is cleaning a listed trigger?","trap":"Condition 1 is a normal fungal ecology and is not a trigger.","delta":true,"num":{"q":"Microbial contamination at Condition __ is a need-to-clean trigger.","a":"2 or 3","d":["1 or 2","3 only","1, 2, or 3"]},"qs":["ascs-0021","ascs-0039","ascs-0725"]},{"id":"acr:N-10","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"Fibrous glass duct liner, duct board, or other porous components deteriorate.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger covers deterioration, and of which materials?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0030","ascs-0727"]},{"id":"acr:N-11","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The work is part of an HVAC maintenance program that ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 defines.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which standard's HVAC maintenance program is a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers inspection and maintenance. NFPA 90A covers installation.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0070","ascs-0728"]},{"id":"acr:N-12","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The work is part of the equipment manufacturer recommended maintenance practices.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger comes from the equipment maker's guidance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:N-13","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The work is part of a proactive energy management program.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is tied to energy?","trap":"An energy program justifies cleaning, but NADCA warrants no energy savings.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0025"]},{"id":"acr:N-14","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The work is part of a proactive indoor air quality management program.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which need-to-clean trigger is tied to managing indoor air quality?","trap":"NADCA still gives no warranty that cleaning corrects IAQ concerns.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0027"]},{"id":"acr:N-15","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"REC","text":"The work is a component of LEED Certification.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"Which green building certification is listed as a need-to-clean trigger?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0022"]},{"id":"acr:N-17","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"acr-need-to-clean","tag":"MAY","text":"When the need to clean is in question, Method 2 or Method 3 determines that need.","src":"ACR 2025, Need to clean","prompt":"When the need to clean is in question, which methods may determine it?","trap":"Method 3 is the NADCA Vacuum Test. 2021 had two Method 2s, so older guides number the methods differently.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0305","ascs-0726"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the need for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Per the Section 1 overview, what does an inspection determine about cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0049"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Besides the need for cleaning, what does an inspection determine about the extent of the job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0280","ascs-0307"]},{"id":"acr:1.0-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection determines the engineering controls, the safety measures, and the equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.0","prompt":"Which three job setup items does an inspection determine under 1.0?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0307"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection occurs before the cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"When must the first inspection of a cleaning and restoration project occur?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0241","ascs-0290","ascs-0309"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspection occurs after the cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"Besides before the project, when else must an inspection occur?","trap":"Do not merge it with verification, which occurs right after each component is cleaned, before the system operates (5.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0241","ascs-0290","ascs-0309"]},{"id":"acr:1.1-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"REC","text":"Routine inspections are part of a maintenance, energy, and indoor air quality plan.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.1","prompt":"Where do routine inspections fit, according to 1.1?","trap":"Routine inspections are recommended. The before and after project inspections are required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.10-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The information from the inspection receives documentation and evaluation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What happens to the information gathered in the inspection, per 1.10?","trap":"An inspection gathers information. An assessment reviews and evaluates it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.10-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The assessment gives a recommendation on the need for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What does the system component assessment give about whether to clean?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0260","ascs-0313"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The assessment gives a clearly defined scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What kind of scope of work must the system component assessment give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0049","ascs-0260","ascs-0280","ascs-0300","ascs-0313"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The assessment gives the recommended cleaning techniques.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"Which assessment output covers how the components will be cleaned?","trap":"The assessment shall give recommended techniques, but means and methods in the work plan (2.3) are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0260","ascs-0579"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-e","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The assessment gives a determination of the required environmental engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"Which assessment output covers protection of the work environment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0300","ascs-0579"]},{"id":"acr:1.10-f","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspection-purpose","tag":"SHALL","text":"The assessment gives any unique requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.10","prompt":"What catch-all item must the system component assessment give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"REC","text":"A qualified inspector, such as an ASCS or a CVI, determines the preliminary state of cleanliness.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Who is recommended to determine the preliminary state of cleanliness?","trap":"ASCS or CVI is a recommendation. The four areas of verifiable knowledge are the requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0291"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-h","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"NOTE","text":"Certification is a recommendation. The four knowledge areas are the mandatory minimum.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"In 1.2, which part is a recommendation and which is the mandatory minimum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0242"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"At minimum, the inspector has verifiable working knowledge of basic HVAC system design.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns how HVAC systems are designed, and at what level?","trap":"The word is verifiable working knowledge. It is required whether or not the inspector holds a certification.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0242","ascs-0306"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of fundamental HVAC engineering practices.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns HVAC engineering?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of current cleaning and restoration techniques.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns cleaning methods?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.2-e","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector has verifiable working knowledge of applicable industry standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"Which required inspector knowledge area concerns standards?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0306"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-f","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"An inspector for microbial contamination has qualification through training and experience.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"How must an inspector for microbial contamination be qualified?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0243"]},{"id":"acr:1.2-g","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"That inspector holds a license, where the law requires one, to determine Conditions 1, 2, and 3.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2","prompt":"When must a microbial inspector hold a license to determine Conditions 1, 2, and 3?","trap":"A license is required only where the law requires one, not everywhere.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0243"]},{"id":"acr:1.2.1-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Before the inspection, the inspector understands the effect of the inspection on the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1","prompt":"Before the inspection, what must the inspector understand about the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0273"]},{"id":"acr:1.2.1-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"The inspector understands the effect of the inspection on the occupants.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.2.1","prompt":"In the 1.2.1 risk assessment, whose welfare, besides the building, must the inspector consider?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0273","ascs-0301"]},{"id":"acr:1.3-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"HVAC mechanical plans and building plans give information for the scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"What do HVAC mechanical plans and building plans give for the job?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.3-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Building floor plans and mechanical plans are in use during the inspection, if they exist.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"When must building floor plans and mechanical plans be in use during an inspection?","trap":"Required only if the plans exist.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0244","ascs-0299"]},{"id":"acr:1.3-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"These plans are also in use during the cleaning work and the restoration work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"Besides the inspection, when else must the floor plans and mechanical plans be in use?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0244","ascs-0299"]},{"id":"acr:1.4-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"Inspection activities can influence the indoor environment of a building.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What can inspection activities influence in a building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.4-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"The primary concern is disturbance of settled particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What is the primary concern when inspection activities affect the indoor environment?","trap":"Disturbing settled particulate is first. Release into occupied areas is the second concern.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0302"]},{"id":"acr:1.4-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"DEF","text":"The second concern is release of disturbed particles into occupied areas.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What is the second concern for the indoor environment during an inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.4-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-inspector-prep","tag":"SHALL","text":"Appropriate engineering controls manage the general workspace environment during an inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.4","prompt":"What must manage the general workspace environment during an inspection?","trap":"Engineering controls are required during the inspection itself, not only during cleaning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0245","ascs-0302","ascs-0315"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"Component inspections are part of the building maintenance, energy, and indoor air quality plan.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"Per 1.5, component inspections are recommended as part of which building plan?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Only the visual means of routine inspection (1.5.1) is a SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.5-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"The inspections follow ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 and NFPA 90A.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"Which two standards are routine inspections recommended to follow?","trap":"Recommended, not required. ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 is the maintenance standard, NFPA 90A the installation standard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0728"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers inspection and maintenance of commercial building HVAC systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"What does ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0777"]},{"id":"acr:1.5-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"NFPA 90A covers installation of air-conditioning systems and ventilating systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5","prompt":"What does NFPA 90A cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Routine inspection of components for cleanliness occurs by visual means.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"By what means must routine inspection of components for cleanliness occur?","trap":"The visual means is a SHALL, even though the Table 1 schedule itself is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0270"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Table 1 gives a recommended inspection schedule. The intervals are minimum recommendations.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"What kind of schedule does Table 1 give, and what are its intervals?","trap":"Table 1 is recommended, not required, and its intervals are minimums. More frequent inspection fits it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0270"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"When geographical, human, or mechanical conditions make it necessary, inspections occur more often.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1","prompt":"When are inspections recommended more often than the Table 1 intervals?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0274"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"The air-handling unit interval is 1 year for all five building use classifications.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"What is the Table 1 air-handling unit inspection interval, and for which building uses?","trap":"Residential ducts are 2 years, but the Residential AHU is still 1 year.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 AHU interval, Residential vs Healthcare: __ .","a":"1 year vs 1 year","d":["2 years vs 1 year","1 year vs 2 years","2 years vs 2 years"]},"qs":["ascs-0247","ascs-0308"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-e","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Residential and Marine share the same intervals. Both are 1 year, 2 years, 2 years.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"Which two Table 1 building uses have 2-year duct intervals, and what are their three intervals?","trap":"Marine groups with Residential, not Commercial. Ship ducts are 2 years.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 intervals for Residential and Marine (AHU, supply, return and exhaust) are __ .","a":"1 year, 2 years, 2 years","d":["1 year, 1 year, 1 year","2 years, 2 years, 2 years","1 year, 1 year, 2 years"]},"qs":["ascs-0246","ascs-0248","ascs-0287","ascs-0310"]},{"id":"acr:1.5.1-f","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Commercial, Industrial, and Healthcare share the same intervals. All three are 1 year.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.5.1, Table 1","prompt":"Which three Table 1 building uses have 1-year intervals for everything?","trap":"Healthcare is not stricter than Commercial or Industrial. The third column covers return and exhaust ducts together.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 1 intervals for Commercial, Industrial, and Healthcare (AHU, supply, return and exhaust) are __ .","a":"1 year, 1 year, 1 year","d":["1 year, 2 years, 2 years","2 years, 2 years, 2 years","1 year, 2 years, 1 year"]},"qs":["ascs-0248","ascs-0286","ascs-0308","ascs-0311","ascs-0320"]},{"id":"acr:1.6-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Components collect large amounts of debris and particulate during construction activities.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"Why do construction activities matter for HVAC components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0036"]},{"id":"acr:1.6-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"REC","text":"An inspection for cleanliness occurs before the system operates.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"For construction, retrofit, or remodel work, what is recommended before the system operates?","trap":"2025 recommends inspection only. 2021 required inspection and verification as clean.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0249","ascs-0314"]},{"id":"acr:1.6-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-table-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition required inspection and verification as clean. The 2025 edition asks only for inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.6","prompt":"How did the 1.6 construction clause change from 2021 to 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cleanliness inspection includes a minimum of 10% of the components under inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"What minimum share of the components under inspection must a cleanliness inspection include?","trap":"10% is the inspection minimum only. Verification covers all specified components, and S520 projects inspect all components.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Outside an S520 remediation project, the minimum share of components a cleanliness inspection must include is __ .","a":"10%","d":["5%","100%","99.97%"]},"qs":["ascs-0250","ascs-0288"]},{"id":"acr:1.7-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"Components include supply air ducts, return air ducts, and VAV boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Besides fan powered terminal boxes and mixing boxes, which components does 1.7 name?","trap":"The list is open ('but not limited to'). Unlisted components still count.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"Components also include fan powered terminal boxes and mixing boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Which two other box-type components does 1.7 name besides VAV boxes?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"For a microbial remediation project to ANSI/IICRC S520, the inspection includes all components.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"How much of the system must the inspection include for a microbial remediation project to ANSI/IICRC S520?","trap":"S500 is water damage and S590 is HVAC assessment. Only S520 remediation triggers the all-components inspection.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The all-components inspection rule applies to a microbial remediation project done to __ .","a":"ANSI/IICRC S520","d":["ANSI/IICRC S500","ANSI/IICRC S590","ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001"]},"qs":["ascs-0251","ascs-0289"]},{"id":"acr:1.7-e","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"NOTE","text":"The S520 exception replaces the 10% minimum. It does not add to it.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7","prompt":"Does the S520 all-components rule add to the 10% minimum or replace it?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0289"]},{"id":"acr:X-7","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 10% figure is for inspection. Verification covers all specified components.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7, §5.0","prompt":"Is the 10% figure for inspection or for verification, and what does verification cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes filters and air bypass.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which filtration items must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"1.7.1 is the only 1.7 component list written as 'shall include'. Supply and return lists say 'includes'.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0275","ascs-0297"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes heating coils and cooling coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which coils must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"Reheat coils are on the supply list (1.7.2), not the AHU list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0297"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes condensate pans and condensate drain lines.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which water-handling items under the coil must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0312"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes humidification systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which moisture-adding system must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0252"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-e","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes acoustic insulation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which insulation must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-f","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes fans and fan compartments.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which air-moving items must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0312"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-g","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes dampers and door gaskets.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which two items that control or seal airflow must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"Dampers appear on both the AHU list and the return list (1.7.3).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0252","ascs-0297","ascs-0312"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-h","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection includes general unit integrity.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which overall condition item must the AHU inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.1-i","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The AHU category includes fan coil units and evaporative coolers.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"Which two unit types does the Standard place in the AHU inspection category?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes air ducts and mixing boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Besides VAV boxes, fan powered terminal boxes, and reheat coils, what does the 1.7.2 supply inspection include?","trap":"Mixing boxes are named on the supply list, not the return list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0123"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes VAV boxes and fan powered terminal boxes.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Which terminal units does the supply air duct inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.2-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The supply inspection includes reheat coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.2","prompt":"Which coils does the supply air duct inspection include?","trap":"Heating and cooling coils are on the AHU list. Reheat coils are on the supply list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0253"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The return inspection includes return air ducts and dampers.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Which ducts and which airflow devices does the 1.7.3 return inspection include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"The return inspection includes return plenums.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Which plenums does the return air duct inspection include?","trap":"A return plenum in the duct system is in scope. A non-ducted open air plenum falls under 1.7.4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0105","ascs-0271"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"A return plenum inside ductwork is different from a non-ducted open air plenum.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"Is a return plenum inside ductwork the same as a non-ducted open air plenum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0271","ascs-0721"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.3-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"1.7.1 says \"shall include\". 1.7.2 and 1.7.3 say only \"includes\". The contrast is deliberate.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.3","prompt":"How does the wording of 1.7.1 differ from 1.7.2 and 1.7.3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"MAY","text":"An open air plenum inspection is part of the contractual scope of work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"How may a non-ducted open air plenum inspection become part of a project?","trap":"It is a MAY: included only by contract. Open air plenums are outside the Standard's scope.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0254"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"Open air plenums present unique cleaning challenges and environmental control challenges.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"Why are non-ducted open air plenums harder to work in?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"REC","text":"The NADCA White Paper on open air plenums gives the guidance for this work.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"Which document is recommended as guidance for open air plenum work?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0494"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.4-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"This subsection is new in 2025. It moved Exhaust to 1.7.5 and Damaged Components to 1.7.6.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.4","prompt":"What did the new 2025 subsection 1.7.4 do to the subsections after it?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.5-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"General exhaust, bathroom exhaust, and heat recovery exhaust are part of the ventilation system.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","prompt":"Which three kinds of exhaust does the Standard name as part of the ventilation system?","trap":"The exhaust clause is not new in 2025. Only its number moved from 1.7.4 to 1.7.5.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0255","ascs-0304"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.5-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"DEF","text":"These exhaust components are part of the overall inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","prompt":"How do general, bathroom, and heat recovery exhaust components relate to the inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0255","ascs-0304"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.5-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"This subsection is not new. Only its number changed from 1.7.4 to 1.7.5.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5","prompt":"Is the 2025 exhaust air duct subsection new, or only renumbered?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.7.6-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"SHALL","text":"Damaged components or unsafe components under observation receive documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6","prompt":"What must happen to damaged or unsafe components observed during an inspection?","trap":"Documentation is required. This clause does not require repair.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0256","ascs-0316"]},{"id":"acr:1.7.6-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-other-components","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard requires documentation only. It does not require repair at this clause.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.6","prompt":"Does 1.7.6 require repair of damaged components, or only documentation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0256","ascs-0316"]},{"id":"acr:1.8-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"When exposure to hazardous materials is possible, consultation with a qualified IEP occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"When exposure to hazardous materials is possible, which professional is named first for the recommended consultation?","trap":"Recommended, not required, though most candidates read it as mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0261","ascs-0679"]},{"id":"acr:1.8-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"A health and safety professional is an alternative to the IEP.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"Who is the alternative to the IEP for a hazardous materials consultation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.8-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"This clause reads as mandatory to most candidates. The Standard makes it a recommendation.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"How does the hazardous materials clause read to most candidates, and what is it really?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-8","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"Hazardous material consultation (1.8) is a recommendation, not a requirement.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8","prompt":"Is consultation on possible hazardous material exposure (1.8) required or recommended?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0261"]},{"id":"acr:1.9-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"When suspect contamination appears, a preliminary determination of Condition 1, 2, or 3 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"When suspect microbial contamination appears, what preliminary determination is recommended?","trap":"The determination is recommended. A license to determine Conditions is needed only where the law requires one.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0259","ascs-0292"]},{"id":"acr:1.9-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"An IEP or other appropriate professional assesses the cause and the extent.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"Who is recommended to assess the cause and extent of suspect microbial contamination?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0292"]},{"id":"acr:1.9-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading was \"Inspecting for Mold Contamination\". The words \"other biological activity\" are gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9","prompt":"How did the 1.9 heading change from the 2021 edition?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"Suspect microbial contamination in the AHU air-handling components starts a duct inspection.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"What must suspect microbial contamination in the AHU air-handling components start?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0318","ascs-0319"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"The supply ducts and the return ducts get an inspection during that same inspection time.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"When suspect microbial contamination is in the AHU, which ducts must be inspected, and when?","trap":"Not at the next Table 1 date and not after an IEP confirms growth. Same inspection time.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0257","ascs-0298","ascs-0318","ascs-0319"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-c","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"SHALL","text":"This inspection replaces the Table 1 interval for that system.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"How does the same-visit duct inspection for suspect microbial growth in the AHU relate to Table 1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0257","ascs-0298"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.1-d","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"NOTE","text":"This requirement also existed in 2021. Only the word \"mold\" changed to \"microbial\".","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","prompt":"Is the same-visit duct inspection rule for microbial growth in the AHU new in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:1.9.2-a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"DEF","text":"Water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings is outside this scope.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","prompt":"Is water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings within the 1.9 scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0258"]},{"id":"acr:1.9.2-b","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-hazmat-microbial","tag":"REC","text":"ANSI/IICRC S520 and applicable codes govern that condition.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.9.2","prompt":"What governs water damage or suspect growth on building products or furnishings?","trap":"S520 is named here, not S500, even though water damage is part of the condition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0258"]},{"id":"acr:2.0-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contractor gives a written work plan before any cleaning work starts.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"What must the contractor give before any cleaning work starts?","trap":"The plan itself is mandatory, but most of its subsections are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0262","ascs-0542","ascs-0610"]},{"id":"acr:2.0-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The work plan communicates responsibilities and specific tasks for the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"What does the work plan communicate for the project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.0-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The work plan uses the information from the inspection and the system assessment.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"Which earlier steps supply the information the work plan uses?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0610"]},{"id":"acr:2.0-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"NOTE","text":"The plan itself is mandatory. Most of its subsections are recommendations.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.0","prompt":"In Section 2, which is mandatory: the plan itself or most of its subsections?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"The plan gives the client, the contractor, and the field personnel a clear understanding.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.1","prompt":"Which three parties does the work plan give a clear understanding to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0601"]},{"id":"acr:2.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"DEF","text":"That understanding covers the work tasks and the procedures for the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.1","prompt":"What does the clear understanding from the work plan cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0601"]},{"id":"acr:2.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work identifies the components for cleaning or restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Besides engineering controls and unique requirements, what must the scope of work identify?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0263","ascs-0541","ascs-0611"]},{"id":"acr:2.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work includes the environmental engineering controls for the workspace.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Which workspace item must the scope of work include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0541","ascs-0611"]},{"id":"acr:2.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scope of work includes any unique requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.2","prompt":"Besides components and engineering controls, what must the scope of work include?","trap":"The scope of work (2.2) is a SHALL. Means and methods right after it (2.3) is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0611"]},{"id":"acr:2.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan identifies the specific means and methods of cleaning and restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.3","prompt":"What is the work plan recommended to identify about how cleaning and restoration will be done?","trap":"Recommended here, yet the 1.10 assessment shall give recommended cleaning techniques.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0263","ascs-0264"]},{"id":"acr:2.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan names all firms, contractors, and representatives, with contact information.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.4","prompt":"For other trades, who is the work plan recommended to name, and with what details?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan identifies the tasks of the other parties.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.4","prompt":"Besides naming the other parties, what is the work plan recommended to identify about them?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan gives the dates and times of the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.5","prompt":"What detailed timing is the work plan recommended to give?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.5-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The work plan gives an overall timeframe for completion.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.5","prompt":"What overall timing is the work plan recommended to give?","trap":"The project schedule is recommended, not required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"When more than one company works on the project, a communication plan applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.6","prompt":"When does a work site communication plan apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.6-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-work-plan","tag":"REC","text":"The plan names the individuals, their companies, contact information, and responsibilities.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.6","prompt":"What does the work site communication plan name?","trap":"2.4 names firms and their tasks. 2.6 names individuals and responsibilities. Both are recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan lists all general use products and specific chemical type products.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"Which products must the work plan list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0276","ascs-0602"]},{"id":"acr:2.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan lists all coatings specific to the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"Besides products, what else specific to the project must the work plan list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0276"]},{"id":"acr:2.7-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The manufacturer instructions for use and application are available to workers at all times.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7","prompt":"What product information must be available to workers at all times?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0266","ascs-0602","ascs-0612"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The work plan includes Safety Data Sheets for all chemical products on the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Which safety documents must the work plan include for chemical products?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0265","ascs-0543","ascs-0612"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Safety Data Sheets stay on site for review for the duration of the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Where, and for how long, must the Safety Data Sheets be kept?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0265"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"CAN","text":"The plan includes documentation of product submittal to the client.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.1","prompt":"Besides Safety Data Sheets, what client-facing product record can the plan include under 2.7.1?","trap":"This is a CAN, a possibility. SDS in the plan and on site are SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0277"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"The plan describes the engineering controls for chemical vapors and odors, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.2","prompt":"What must the plan describe for chemical vapors and odors?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0269"]},{"id":"acr:2.7.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"These controls limit occupant exposure and worker exposure.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.7.2","prompt":"Whose exposure do the controls for chemical vapors and odors limit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.8-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"SHALL","text":"When detection equipment goes off-line, the work plan addresses life safety concerns.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What must the work plan address when detection equipment goes off-line?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0267","ascs-0586"]},{"id":"acr:2.8-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"Life safety detection equipment includes air duct sensors and smoke detectors.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What does life safety detection equipment include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:2.8-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"DEF","text":"This task will need the input of other parties.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"Can the contractor handle the off-line detection safety task alone?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0586"]},{"id":"acr:2.8-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-submittals-safety","tag":"REC","text":"The plan defines the responsibilities of each designated representative for the project duration.","src":"ACR 2025 §2.8","prompt":"What is the safety plan recommended to define for the project duration?","trap":"Recommended, while addressing life safety for off-line detectors (2.8) is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0268"]},{"id":"acr:3.0-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Engineering controls prevent cross-contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 §3.0 say engineering controls must do?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0682"]},{"id":"acr:3.0-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include source control and isolation barriers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two engineering controls in §3.0 deal with stopping contamination at its origin and physically separating the work area?","trap":"The 3.0 list is permissive (may include). Barriers become mandatory only at Level 2 (3.10.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include pressure differentials and dust suppression methods.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two engineering controls in §3.0 deal with air pressure and with keeping dust from getting airborne?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include HEPA vacuuming and HEPA filtration.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two HEPA-based methods does §3.0 list as engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include detailed cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which cleaning practice does §3.0 list on its own as an engineering control?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include temperature control and humidity control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which two indoor conditions does §3.0 say can be controlled as engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0464"]},{"id":"acr:3.0-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"MAY","text":"Engineering controls include a sanitary approach.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Which general work approach closes the §3.0 list of engineering controls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.0-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard names no personal protective equipment. PPE is not an engineering control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 name any personal protective equipment, and is PPE an engineering control?","trap":"Engineering controls change the work environment. A respirator or suit protects one worker and is not on the 3.0 list.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0464"]},{"id":"acr:X-12","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard never specifies personal protective equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.0","prompt":"In which ACR 2025 clause is personal protective equipment specified?","trap":"Any option citing an ACR respirator or PPE rule is wrong. PPE requirements come from OSHA, not ACR.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Appropriate engineering controls are mandatory on every cleaning and restoration project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"On which cleaning and restoration projects are appropriate engineering controls mandatory?","trap":"Not only microbial or healthcare jobs. Every project, including small residential ones.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0604"]},{"id":"acr:3.13-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Protection of workers and occupants is a priority on every project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Per the §3.13 summary, whose protection is a priority on every project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Prevention of cross-contamination is a priority on every project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Besides protecting people, what other priority does §3.13 set for every project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0682"]},{"id":"acr:3.13-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"REC","text":"The listed engineering controls are minimum requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"How does the §3.13 summary characterize the engineering controls listed in Section 3?","trap":"This summary line carries a recommendation, not a shall. Read the obligation word, not the word 'minimum'.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.13-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-engineering-controls","tag":"REC","text":"An IEP gives advice on project-specific engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.13","prompt":"Who does §3.13 recommend for advice on project-specific engineering controls?","trap":"Consulting an IEP is recommended, not required, just like the hazardous material consultation at 1.8.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"All contractor equipment stays in good working order, consistent with jurisdictional requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"In what condition must all contractor equipment be kept under §3.1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0463"]},{"id":"acr:3.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes vacuum collection equipment, power tools, and pressurized air sources.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which vacuum, power tool, and compressed-air items head the §3.1 list of contractor equipment?","trap":"Equipment lists read 'but not limited to' (G-25). A tool missing from the list is not exempt.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes electrical power cords, plugs, and ground fault protection devices.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which electrical items does §3.1 list as contractor equipment to keep in good working order?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes vacuum collection hoses, fluid lines, and pneumatic lines.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which hoses and lines does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes manual and mechanical rotary brush systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which brush systems does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes robotic equipment and pneumatic cleaning systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which automated and air-driven cleaning systems does §3.1 list as contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This equipment includes air duct zoning devices, ladders, staging equipment, and hand tools.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1","prompt":"Which zoning, access, and small-tool items close the §3.1 list of contractor equipment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0463"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment receives cleaning and inspection before it enters the work site.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1","prompt":"What must happen to equipment before it enters the work site?","trap":"This is a shall. The on-site vacuum check at 3.1.4 is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0334"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This step prevents contaminants entering the indoor environment or the HVAC system components.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1","prompt":"Why must equipment be cleaned and inspected before it enters the work site?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment receives service during the project as the conditions need it.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2","prompt":"When must equipment be serviced during a project?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This service limits cross-contamination from poor hygiene and unsafe operating conditions.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2","prompt":"Servicing equipment during the project limits cross-contamination from which two problems?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Any on-site opening of contaminated vacuum equipment occurs in a containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Inside the building, where is the only place contaminated vacuum equipment may be opened on site?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0336","ascs-0561"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"The alternative location is outside of the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Besides a containment area, where may contaminated vacuum equipment be opened on site?","trap":"A mechanical room or hallway does not qualify unless it is set up as a containment area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0336","ascs-0561"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.2.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"DEF","text":"This rule covers servicing and filter maintenance.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.2.1","prompt":"Which vacuum tasks does the rule on opening contaminated vacuum equipment cover?","trap":"Changing a vacuum filter counts as opening it. It follows the same containment-or-outside rule.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"Collection devices, vacuums, and tools receive cleaning or sealing before relocation in the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.3","prompt":"What must happen to collection devices, vacuums, and tools before they move to another area inside the building?","trap":"Cleaning OR sealing satisfies it. Full cleaning between areas is not the only option.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0337","ascs-0574"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"SHALL","text":"The same equipment receives cleaning or sealing before it leaves the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.3","prompt":"What must happen to collection devices, vacuums, and tools before they leave the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.1.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"REC","text":"An on-site maintenance verification of vacuum equipment occurs before work starts.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.4","prompt":"What check of vacuum equipment does §3.1.4 recommend before work starts?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Its neighbor 3.1.1, clean and inspect before entering the site, is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0335"]},{"id":"acr:3.1.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-hygiene","tag":"NOTE","text":"3.1.1 is mandatory. 3.1.4 directly below it is only a recommendation.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.4","prompt":"Of the two equipment checks at 3.1.1 and 3.1.4, which is mandatory and which is only recommended?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Generators, vacuum trucks, and air compressors stand in a position that stops emissions entry.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2","prompt":"How must generators, vacuum trucks, and air compressors be positioned on a job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0342","ascs-0556","ascs-0686"]},{"id":"acr:3.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The emissions of concern are combustion emissions and air exhaust emissions.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2","prompt":"Which two kinds of emissions from fuel-powered equipment does §3.2 aim to keep out of the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0342","ascs-0556"]},{"id":"acr:3.2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"The location receives monitoring and management during the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2.1","prompt":"After fuel-powered equipment is placed, what must happen to its location for the rest of the project?","trap":"Placing it once is not enough. The location is monitored and managed for the whole project.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0465"]},{"id":"acr:3.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum equipment that exhausts inside the building envelope uses HEPA filtration.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3","prompt":"What filtration must vacuum equipment use when it exhausts inside the building envelope?","trap":"Non-HEPA equipment is not banned. It must exhaust outdoors (4.1.3).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0487"]},{"id":"acr:3.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"That filtration has 99.97% collection efficiency at 0.3 micron particle size.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3","prompt":"What collection efficiency, at what particle size, must that HEPA filtration achieve?","trap":"The 0.8 and 0.45 micron figures in ACR are Method 3 filter pore sizes, not the HEPA test size.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"HEPA filtration is rated at 99.97% collection efficiency at a particle size of __ .","a":"0.3 micron","d":["0.45 micron","0.8 micron","0.12 micron"]},"qs":["ascs-0338"]},{"id":"acr:3.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Continuous negative pressure exists in the portion of the components under cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"What pressure condition must exist in the portion of components under cleaning, and for how long?","trap":"ACR gives no numeric negative pressure. Any answer in inches of water column is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0339","ascs-0486","ascs-0618","ascs-0662"]},{"id":"acr:3.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The reference for that pressure is the surrounding indoor spaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"Relative to what is the section under cleaning kept negative in §3.4?","trap":"The reference is the surrounding indoor spaces, not outdoors.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.4-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"The negative pressure is verifiable at representative locations during the cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"Where and when must the §3.4 negative pressure be verifiable?","trap":"2025 says verifiable, not verified. Clause 4.1.1 still requires verification of the differential during the project.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0339","ascs-0557","ascs-0618"]},{"id":"acr:3.4-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition said \"verified\". The 2025 edition says \"verifiable\".","src":"ACR 2025 §3.4","prompt":"What single word changed in the negative pressure clause 3.4 between 2021 and 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"All contaminated materials receive containment before they leave the building.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5","prompt":"What must happen to contaminated materials before they leave the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0358","ascs-0558","ascs-0665"]},{"id":"acr:3.5.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Materials that government agencies deem hazardous follow applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.5.1","prompt":"What governs the handling of materials that government agencies deem hazardous?","trap":"ACR sets no hazardous waste procedure of its own. It defers to applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0665","ascs-0681"]},{"id":"acr:3.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"REC","text":"Ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers is a supplemental engineering control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"Under §3.6, what role does ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers play?","trap":"Recommended at 3.6. It becomes a shall, at 4 ACH, only inside Level 2 and higher containment (3.10.7).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0340","ascs-0560"]},{"id":"acr:3.6-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The purpose is particle reduction during the work and immediately after the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"What is the purpose of ambient air cleaning, and when does it apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.6-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition recommended 4 air changes per hour here. The 2025 edition gives no number.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.6","prompt":"What air change rate does 2025 §3.6 give for ambient air cleaning, and what did 2021 say there?","trap":"'4 ACH on every job' is 2021 thinking. In 2025, 4 ACH lives only at Level 2.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and chemicals follow the manufacturer recommended procedures.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.7","prompt":"Whose procedures govern the use of cleaning agents and chemicals under §3.7?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0359"]},{"id":"acr:3.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"SHALL","text":"They also follow the product application instructions, with exhaust ventilation where required.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.7","prompt":"Besides manufacturer procedures, what else must chemical use follow under §3.7, including ventilation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0359"]},{"id":"acr:3.8-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-site-controls","tag":"REC","text":"Redundant equipment stays on site for a negative pressure failure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.8","prompt":"What does §3.8 recommend keeping on site in case negative pressure fails?","trap":"Recommended only. Candidates often mark backup equipment as required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0341","ascs-0559"]},{"id":"acr:3-L1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 1 = negative pressure, protective coverings, cross-contamination control.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"What three things make up Level 1 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0344","ascs-0614"]},{"id":"acr:3-L2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 2 = Level 1 + barriers + two-layer 6-mil floor + 4 ACH + manometer + dismantle hygiene.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"What does Level 2 containment add on top of Level 1?","trap":"The barriers are required, but their specs (floor to ceiling, 6-mil, airtight seals) are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3-L3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 3 = Level 2 + single chamber decon + continuous monitoring + particulate baseline.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11","prompt":"What does Level 3 containment add on top of Level 2?","trap":"Level 3 is one decon chamber. Two chambers is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0354","ascs-0616"]},{"id":"acr:3-L4","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 4 = Level 3 + second decon chamber + constant recording monitor with alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12","prompt":"What does Level 4 containment add on top of Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0357","ascs-0524","ascs-0641"]},{"id":"acr:3-Lx","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"DEF","text":"The instrument ladder is validate, then monitor continuously, then record with an alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6, 3.11.3, 3.12.3","prompt":"What is the pressure-instrument ladder from Level 2 to Level 4?","trap":"Level 2 validates with a manometer. Continuous monitoring starts at Level 3. Recording with an alarm is Level 4 only.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Level 1 is the minimum containment on all component cleaning projects. No project is exempt.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Which component cleaning projects need at least Level 1 containment?","trap":"Even a small residential job. There is no exemption for project size or type.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0343","ascs-0518","ascs-0613"]},{"id":"acr:3.9.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The component or area under cleaning stays under negative pressure during all cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1","prompt":"What pressure requirement is part one of Level 1 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0486"]},{"id":"acr:3.9.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"That pressure stops migration of particulate out of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.1","prompt":"Why does Level 1 keep the component under negative pressure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Clean protective coverings stay in place within the work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.2","prompt":"What does part two of Level 1 require within the work area?","trap":"Level 1 coverings are not the Level 2 poly floor. No mil thickness or layer count applies at Level 1.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coverings extend past the work area to protect flooring, equipment, and furniture, whenever necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.2","prompt":"When must Level 1 protective coverings extend past the work area, and what do they protect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0344","ascs-0459","ascs-0605"]},{"id":"acr:3.9.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Engineering controls limit contaminant discharge from the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.3","prompt":"Under part three of Level 1, what must engineering controls limit coming out of the components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Engineering controls limit cross-contamination into occupied space during cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9.3","prompt":"Under part three of Level 1, what must engineering controls limit reaching occupied space during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"Level 1 has three parts. Barriers, scrubbers, and floors belong to Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"How many parts does Level 1 containment have, and which common items are not among them?","trap":"Four parts is the 2021 edition. 2025 has three.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Level 1 containment in ACR 2025 has __ parts.","a":"three","d":["two","four","five"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition had four parts. 3.9.3 Cleaning Equipment and Tools is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Which Level 1 subsection from 2021 was removed in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.9-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 clause 3.9.4 Cross-Contamination Control became 3.9.3 in 2025.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"What is the 2025 clause number for Level 1 cross-contamination control, and what was it in 2021?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","tag":"NOTE","text":"Level 1 applies to every project. It needs no barriers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9","prompt":"Does Level 1 containment apply to every project, and does it require barriers?","trap":"Options that add poly barriers or air scrubbers to Level 1 are describing Level 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0518","ascs-0615"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1 requirements apply to Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.1","prompt":"Which Level 1 requirements carry into Level 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Temporary barriers isolate the work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2","prompt":"What isolates the work area in Level 2 containment?","trap":"The barriers are required. Only their specs in 3.10.2.1 to 3.10.2.4 are recommendations.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0345","ascs-0615"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barriers extend from floor to ceiling, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.1","prompt":"How far should Level 2 barriers extend vertically?","trap":"Recommended, and only where applicable.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"Above-ceiling work areas stay isolated from occupied spaces and non-work areas, where applicable.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.2","prompt":"What does §3.10.2.2 recommend for work areas above the ceiling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barrier material is 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene sheeting or equivalent.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.3","prompt":"What barrier material does §3.10.2.3 recommend for Level 2?","trap":"Recommended. The same 6-mil material is mandatory only for the two-layer floor at 3.10.3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0346","ascs-0519"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.2.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"REC","text":"The barriers have an airtight seal at the ceiling, the floor, and the walls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.4","prompt":"Where should Level 2 barriers be sealed airtight?","trap":"Barrier seals are recommended. The floor-to-wall seal at 3.10.3 is required.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment has a two-layer floor of 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene or equivalent.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"What floor must Level 2 containment have?","trap":"Two layers, and mandatory. Do not confuse it with the recommended barrier spec.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Level 2 containment floor is two layers of __ fire retardant polyethylene or equivalent.","a":"6-mil","d":["4-mil","10-mil","15-mil"]},"qs":["ascs-0347","ascs-0617"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The floor material extends at least 6 inches (15.2 centimeters) up the side walls.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"How far up the side walls must the Level 2 floor poly run?","trap":"6 inches is the floor turn-up. 4 inches is panel fastener spacing and 1 inch is panel overlap.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum distance Level 2 floor poly must run up the side walls is __ .","a":"6 inches","d":["1 inch","4 inches","12 inches"]},"qs":["ascs-0348","ascs-0520","ascs-0617"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.3-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"The floor material has a seal to the side walls that stays airtight during depressurization.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.3","prompt":"How must the Level 2 floor poly be sealed to the side walls?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"DEF","text":"A zipper-type access with a single flap over the zipper is the most common practice.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4","prompt":"What is the most common way to enter Level 2 containment?","trap":"Described as common practice, not as a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0460","ascs-0578"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"CAN","text":"When a zipper is not practical, a vertical cut in the side wall gives access.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4.1","prompt":"What entry option exists when a zipper is not practical in Level 2 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.4.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two flaps cover that vertical cut, one flap on each side of the polyethylene.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.4.1","prompt":"How must a vertical-cut entry in Level 2 poly be covered?","trap":"Zipper entry: one flap over it. Vertical cut: two flaps, one on each side.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The number of flaps required over a vertical-cut entry in Level 2 poly is __ .","a":"two","d":["one","three","four"]},"qs":["ascs-0349"]},{"id":"acr:3.10-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The barrier material at 3.10.2.3 is a recommendation. The floor at 3.10.3 is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"Which Level 2 clause is mandatory: the barrier material at 3.10.2.3 or the floor at 3.10.3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 6-mil barrier material is a recommendation. The 6-mil two-layer floor is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.2.3, 3.10.3","prompt":"Same 6-mil material, opposite obligations: which Level 2 use is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0346"]},{"id":"acr:3.10-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-barriers","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading read \"Level 2 Containment (Temporary Barriers)\".","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10","prompt":"What did the 2021 heading for Level 2 containment include that 2025 dropped?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment area stays under negative pressure at all times.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5","prompt":"When must the Level 2 containment area be under negative pressure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.5-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"That pressure stops airborne migration of particulate out of the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.5","prompt":"What does negative pressure in Level 2 containment prevent?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"A manometer or an airflow measuring device validates the negative pressurization.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6","prompt":"What instrument validates negative pressurization at Level 2?","trap":"Level 2 validates. Continuous monitoring is Level 3, and a recording monitor with alarm is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0350","ascs-0521"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers occurs at Level 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What ambient air cleaning is required inside Level 2 containment?","trap":"At 3.6 air scrubbing is only recommended. At Level 2 it is a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"That air cleaning maintains a minimum of four (4) air changes per hour.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What minimum air change rate must Level 2 air scrubbing maintain?","trap":"A minimum, not a target or a maximum.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum rate Level 2 HEPA air scrubbing must maintain is __ .","a":"4 air changes per hour","d":["2 air changes per hour","6 air changes per hour","12 air changes per hour"]},"qs":["ascs-0351","ascs-0522"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.7-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"NOTE","text":"This is the only air changes per hour figure in the 2025 edition.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"Besides Level 2 air scrubbing, where does ACR 2025 give an air changes per hour figure?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"NOTE","text":"The only ACH figure in the Standard is 4 ACH at Level 2 (3.10.7).","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.7","prompt":"What is the only ACH figure in ACR 2025, and in which clause does it appear?","trap":"It left 3.6 in 2025. 'Scrub at 4 ACH on every job' is wrong.","delta":true,"num":{"q":"ACR 2025 states its only air-change figure, 4 ACH, in the clause for Level __ containment.","a":"2","d":["1","3","4"]},"qs":["ascs-0522"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.8-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Interior surfaces get a wet wipe or a HEPA vacuum before removal of the enclosure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8","prompt":"What must happen to the interior surfaces of Level 2 containment before the enclosure is removed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0352","ascs-0575"]},{"id":"acr:3.10.8-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-2-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"In healthcare, a post-remediation verification occurs before removal of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.8","prompt":"What extra step comes before containment removal on healthcare projects?","trap":"Only healthcare is named. It is not a requirement on every Level 2 job.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0353"]},{"id":"acr:3.11-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 3 is a containment with a single chamber decontamination unit.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11","prompt":"What defines Level 3 containment?","trap":"Single chamber is Level 3. Two chambers is Level 4.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Level 3 containment uses a __ decontamination unit.","a":"single-chamber","d":["two-chamber","three-chamber","four-chamber"]},"qs":["ascs-0354","ascs-0523","ascs-0616"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1 and Level 2 requirements apply to Level 3.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.1","prompt":"Which lower-level requirements apply to Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"A single chamber decontamination facility works together with the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What facility must work together with the containment area at Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The chamber has an attachment and a seal directly to the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"How is the Level 3 decon chamber joined to the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0461","ascs-0603"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"A zipper access with a single flap separates the chamber from the containment area.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What normally separates the Level 3 decon chamber from the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.2-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two flaps, as Level 2 describes them, are the alternative separation.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.2","prompt":"What is the alternative separation between the Level 3 chamber and the containment area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment gets continuous monitoring for negative pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"How must negative pressure be monitored at Level 3?","trap":"Level 2 only validates. Level 3 monitors continuously but need not record. Recording starts at Level 4.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0525"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The instrument is sensitive enough to detect a loss of negative pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"How sensitive must the Level 3 pressure monitoring instrument be?","trap":"No numeric sensitivity. ACR gives no inches-of-water figure anywhere.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Background monitoring for total particulate occurs before the set-up of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"When must background total particulate monitoring happen at Level 3?","trap":"Before set-up is the shall. Monitoring during set-up is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0355","ascs-0462","ascs-0576"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"This background monitoring establishes the baseline airborne total particulate concentrations.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What does the Level 3 background particulate monitoring establish?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0576"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"REC","text":"Monitoring also occurs during the set-up of the containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What additional particulate monitoring does §3.11.3 recommend at Level 3?","trap":"Recommended, sitting between two shall items: before set-up and during the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0462"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Real time monitoring for total particulate occurs on a regular basis during the work.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What particulate monitoring must happen during the work at Level 3?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"When airborne particulate levels go above the background levels, work stops.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"At Level 3, what triggers a work stoppage?","trap":"The trigger is any rise above background, not a fixed number or a percentage.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0356","ascs-0577"]},{"id":"acr:3.11.3-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Work restarts only after the levels return to background AND the cause has a correction.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3","prompt":"What two conditions must both be met before Level 3 work restarts?","trap":"Levels falling back alone is not enough. The cause must also be corrected.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0356","ascs-0577"]},{"id":"acr:3.12-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"DEF","text":"Level 4 is a containment with a two-chamber decontamination unit.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12","prompt":"What defines Level 4 containment?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0524","ascs-0641"]},{"id":"acr:3.12.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"All Level 1, Level 2, and Level 3 requirements apply to Level 4.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.1","prompt":"Which lower-level requirements apply to Level 4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The decontamination facility has two chambers.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.2","prompt":"How many chambers must the Level 4 decontamination facility have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A Level 4 decontamination facility has __ chambers.","a":"two","d":["one","three","four"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"Each chamber follows the construction requirements for a Level 3 chamber.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.2","prompt":"What construction rules does each Level 4 decon chamber follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The Level 3 monitoring requirements apply.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.3","prompt":"Which monitoring requirements carry into Level 4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:3.12.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-level-3-4","tag":"SHALL","text":"The containment also has a constant recording pressurization monitor with an appropriate alarm.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.12.3","prompt":"What pressure monitor does Level 4 add?","trap":"Recording plus alarm is Level 4 only. Continuous monitoring without recording satisfies Level 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0357"]},{"id":"acr:4.0-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"All procedures achieve the minimum level of visibly clean.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.0","prompt":"What minimum cleanliness level must all Section 4 procedures achieve?","trap":"Visibly clean is the floor, not the ceiling. A contract can specify a level of cleanliness verification instead.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0360","ascs-0505","ascs-0606"]},{"id":"acr:4.0-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The specified level of cleanliness verification in the contractual documents applies instead.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.0","prompt":"When the contract documents specify a level of cleanliness verification, which level applies?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0360","ascs-0606"]},{"id":"acr:4.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The components and ducts stay at an appropriate negative pressure differential.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"What pressure condition must components and ducts be held at under §4.1?","trap":"'Appropriate' is the only size given. ACR sets no inches-of-water value.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0508"]},{"id":"acr:4.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"The reference is the indoor non-work area.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"Relative to what area is the duct kept negative under §4.1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"This condition holds before the cleaning process and through the whole cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"When must negative duct pressure be in place, and for how long?","trap":"Before cleaning starts, not once agitation begins.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"The differential holds between the portion under cleaning and surrounding indoor occupant spaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1","prompt":"Between which two areas must the §4.1 pressure differential hold?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0508"]},{"id":"acr:4.1.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Under all circumstances, verification of the pressurization differential occurs during the project.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.1","prompt":"Under what circumstances must the pressurization differential be verified during the project?","trap":"Clause 3.4 says verifiable. Clause 4.1.1 still requires actual verification under all circumstances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0361","ascs-0509"]},{"id":"acr:4.1.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors is HEPA-filtered.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.2","prompt":"What filtration must vacuum equipment have if it exhausts indoors, under §4.1.2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0510"]},{"id":"acr:4.1.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"That equipment retains the dislodged debris.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.2","prompt":"Besides HEPA filtration, what must indoor-exhausting vacuum equipment do with dislodged debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.1.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","tag":"SHALL","text":"Equipment for negative duct pressurization without HEPA filtration exhausts outdoors.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.3","prompt":"Where must negative duct pressurization equipment exhaust if it lacks HEPA filtration?","trap":"Non-HEPA equipment is allowed. It just may not exhaust inside the building.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0362","ascs-0487","ascs-0511"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not degrade the structural integrity of the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.1","prompt":"What must a service opening not degrade about the strength of the components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0484"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not degrade the thermal or functional integrity.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.1","prompt":"Besides structural integrity, which other kinds of integrity must a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0484","ascs-0538"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening allows proper closure.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.2","prompt":"What must every service opening allow once the work is done?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening does not hinder, restrict, or alter the airflow in the duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.3","prompt":"What must a service opening not do to the airflow in the duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0363"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening complies with industry standards and local codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.4","prompt":"What must a service opening comply with under §4.2.4?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The materials are acceptable under those standards and codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.4","prompt":"What must be true of the materials used for a service opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The opening and its closure leave no exposed fibrous glass edges in the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.1","prompt":"What must a service opening in fibrous glass duct not leave in the airstream?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0587"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"Removed fibrous glass gets a repair or a replacement with like material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What must happen to fibrous glass removed to make a service opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0371","ascs-0588"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The replacement material has the same thickness as the original material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"How thick must replacement fibrous glass at a service opening be?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0371"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The repair leaves no break that degrades the R value or the service rating.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"Which two ratings must a fibrous glass repair at a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0588"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"The repair leaves no break that degrades the vapor barrier or the air barrier.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"Which two barriers must a fibrous glass repair at a service opening not degrade?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.8.2-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"This rule includes any disruption to the external duct wrap.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What external insulation did 2025 bring under the fibrous glass repair rule at service openings?","trap":"New in 2025. Cutting through exterior duct wrap now triggers the same repair rules.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.9-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"No service opening goes into flexible ductwork.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","prompt":"Into which type of duct may no service opening be cut?","trap":"Flex is accessed by disconnecting it, never by cutting an opening.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0372","ascs-0535","ascs-0637"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.9.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"REC","text":"The manufacturer instructions govern disconnection and reattachment of flexible ductwork.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9.1","prompt":"What does §4.2.9.1 recommend should govern disconnecting and reattaching flex duct?","trap":"The flex opening ban is a shall. Following the manufacturer for reattachment is a recommendation.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0373","ascs-0637"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"All service openings comply with applicable UL, IMC, SMACNA, and NFPA standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"Which four standard families must all service openings comply with under §4.2.10?","trap":"IMC is new to this list in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0470","ascs-0589"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"SHALL","text":"They also comply with applicable local, regional, provincial, and national codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"Which levels of code must service openings comply with under 2025 §4.2.10?","trap":"2025 says provincial and national. 'State codes' is the 2021 wording.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0470","ascs-0589"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.10-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-openings","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition read \"local, regional, and state codes\".","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.10","prompt":"How did the 2021 edition word the code list in §4.2.10?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has an equivalent gauge to the duct, or a heavier gauge.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.1","prompt":"What gauge must a service panel be?","trap":"Duct gauge sizes a field-made panel. Pressure class sizes a prefabricated access door.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0366","ascs-0537","ascs-0659"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"DEF","text":"This rule protects the structural integrity of the duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.1","prompt":"Why must a service panel be of equal or heavier gauge than the duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has mechanical fastening at minimum every 4 inches on center.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"How often must a service panel be mechanically fastened?","trap":"4 inches is fastener spacing. 1 inch is the overlap. Swapping them is the classic miss.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum on-center spacing of service panel screws or rivets is __ .","a":"4 inches","d":["1 inch","6 inches","12 inches"]},"qs":["ascs-0364","ascs-0621"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The fastening method is screws or rivets.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"What fasteners does §4.2.5.2 require for service panels?","trap":"Tape is a seal, not a mechanical fastener.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The panel overlaps the ductwork surfaces by a minimum of 1 inch on all sides.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.2","prompt":"By how much must a service panel overlap the duct, and on which sides?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A service panel overlaps the ductwork surfaces by a minimum of __ on all sides.","a":"1 inch","d":["2 inches","4 inches","6 inches"]},"qs":["ascs-0365","ascs-0536","ascs-0622"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service panel has a seal of gaskets, duct sealants, mastic, or tape.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.3","prompt":"With what must a service panel be sealed?","trap":"Mandatory in 2025. Older study guides call panel sealing a recommendation.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0466","ascs-0659"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition made this seal a recommendation. The 2025 edition makes it mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.3","prompt":"How did the obligation to seal service panels change from 2021 to 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.5.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"All tapes and mastics for closure meet the requirements of UL 181A/B.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4","prompt":"What UL listing must tapes and mastics used to close service openings meet?","trap":"UL 181A/B for tapes and mastics. Plain UL 181 is for surface treatments (4.15.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0367","ascs-0623"]},{"id":"acr:X-13","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"UL 181 is for surface treatments. UL 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4, 4.15.1","prompt":"A tape that closes a service opening meets which UL listing, and a surface treatment inside the duct meets which?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0367"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The gauge of the door follows the pressure class of the duct system.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What sets the gauge of a prefabricated duct access door?","trap":"Not the duct gauge. That rule is for service panels (4.2.5.1).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0368"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"Installation follows the manufacturer specifications.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What governs installation of a prefabricated duct access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.6-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"Pressure class governs the prefabricated door. Duct gauge governs the field-made panel.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"What governs gauge for a prefabricated access door, and what governs it for a field-made panel?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-15","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"Pressure class sizes a prefabricated door. Duct gauge sizes a field-made panel.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.6","prompt":"An option says a prefab access door must match the duct gauge. What are the correct gauge rules for doors and panels?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0366","ascs-0368"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"When the ducted airstream contacts door materials or panel materials, those materials have a classification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"When must access door or panel materials carry a fire classification?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The flame-spread rating is not over 25, with no continued progressive combustion.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"What flame-spread rating limit applies to door and panel materials in the airstream?","trap":"Flame 25, smoke 50. The exam flips the pair.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum flame-spread rating for door and panel materials in the airstream is __ .","a":"25","d":["50","75","200"]},"qs":["ascs-0369"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"The smoke-developed rating is not over 50.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"What smoke-developed rating limit applies to door and panel materials in the airstream?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The maximum smoke-developed rating for door and panel materials in the airstream is __ .","a":"50","d":["25","75","450"]},"qs":["ascs-0370"]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"UL 723 determines both ratings.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Which test standard determines the flame-spread and smoke-developed ratings?","trap":"UL 723 is the fire test. UL 181 and UL 181A/B are product listings for coatings and closures.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.2.7-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"SHALL","text":"These materials comply with SMACNA and other applicable standards.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Besides the UL 723 ratings, which standards must door and panel materials comply with?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:X-14","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-service-panels","tag":"NOTE","text":"Flame-spread is 25. Smoke-developed is 50. The pair inverts often.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Which limit is 25 and which is 50: flame-spread or smoke-developed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0369","ascs-0370"]},{"id":"acr:4.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A suitable agitation device dislodges non-adhered substances from the airside surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"In the core cleaning method, what dislodges non-adhered substances from airside surfaces?","trap":"Only non-adhered substances. Paint, mastic, sealant, and stains are adhered and are not the target.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0374","ascs-0507","ascs-0624"]},{"id":"acr:4.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum collection device then captures the dislodged debris.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"After agitation, what captures the dislodged debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0046","ascs-0374","ascs-0507","ascs-0582","ascs-0624","ascs-0640","ascs-0740"]},{"id":"acr:4.3-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"NOTE","text":"Agitation and capture together are the method. Negative pressure alone is not cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.3","prompt":"Does running negative pressure alone count as cleaning under ACR?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0046","ascs-0624","ascs-0640"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Wet process cleaning does not damage the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What must wet process cleaning not damage within the HVAC system itself?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0548"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Wet process cleaning does not damage the facility equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What did 2025 add to the list of things wet process cleaning must not damage?","trap":"Added in 2025: the facility's own equipment, not only the HVAC components.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0548"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and water never contact electrical components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"Under §4.4, which components other than porous ones must water and cleaning agents never contact?","trap":"The only exception is electric resistance coils, cleaned wet only after de-energizing and lockout/tagout (4.11.8).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0375","ascs-0534"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning agents and water never contact fibrous glass or other porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"Under §4.4, which materials must water and cleaning agents never contact besides electrical components?","trap":"Wet cleaning is for non-porous surfaces. Duct liner and duct board stay dry.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0375"]},{"id":"acr:4.4-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"NOTE","text":"The only carve-out is 4.11.8 for electric resistance coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4","prompt":"What is the only carve-out from the rule keeping water off electrical components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Air ducts lose all non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7","prompt":"What must be removed from air ducts under §4.7?","trap":"All non-adhered substances, not just the visible ones. Adhered substances like paint are not the target.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0478"]},{"id":"acr:4.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Air ducts are capable of a pass on the NADCA cleanliness verification tests.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7","prompt":"What test performance must cleaned air ducts be capable of?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0478","ascs-0539"]},{"id":"acr:4.7.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Service openings are large enough for mechanical cleaning and for verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.1","prompt":"How large must service openings be for air duct cleaning?","trap":"Sized for verification too, not just for the brush or hose.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0383"]},{"id":"acr:4.7.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Mechanical agitation methods remove particulate, debris, and surface contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.2","prompt":"What method removes particulate, debris, and surface contamination from air ducts?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum collection device captures the dislodged substances.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.3","prompt":"In air duct cleaning, what captures the substances that agitation dislodges?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.7.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-agitate-capture","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning activities do not damage any components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.7.4","prompt":"What must duct cleaning activities not do to any components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"Vacuum collection equipment operates continuously during cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5","prompt":"When must vacuum collection equipment run?","trap":"Continuously during cleaning, not only while the brush is turning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0376","ascs-0642"]},{"id":"acr:4.5-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The equipment works with agitation tools to convey debris and prevent cross-contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5","prompt":"What two jobs does vacuum collection equipment do while working with agitation tools?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The device maintains sufficient velocity in the portion under cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"What airflow condition must the vacuum collection device maintain in the portion under cleaning?","trap":"'Sufficient' velocity is the shall. Table 2 values are recommendations, not required numbers.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"SHALL","text":"The device also maintains a sufficient negative pressure differential.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"Besides velocity, what must the vacuum collection device maintain?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.5.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"Table 2 gives recommended velocities for different types of contaminant.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.5.1","prompt":"What does Table 2 give, and how is it organized?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0377"]},{"id":"acr:T2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual is the source of Table 2.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What document is the source of ACR Table 2?","trap":"ACGIH, not ASHRAE or SMACNA.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0380"]},{"id":"acr:T2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"Vapors, gases, and smoke are the only class with no fixed minimum velocity.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"Which Table 2 contaminant class has no fixed minimum velocity?","trap":"Metal smokes from welding are not in this class. Table 2 lists them at 2,000 to 2,500 fpm.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, vapors, gases, and smoke take any desired velocity. The economic optimum is usually 1,000 to 2,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What velocity does Table 2 give for vapors, gases, and smoke?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"For vapors, gases, and smoke, Table 2 allows any velocity, with an economic optimum usually of __ .","a":"1,000 to 2,000 fpm","d":["2,000 to 2,500 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,000 to 3,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, fumes and metal smokes, such as welding fumes, have a minimum duct design velocity of 2,000 to 2,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for fumes and metal smokes such as welding fumes?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives fumes and metal smokes, such as welding, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"2,000 to 2,500 fpm","d":["1,000 to 2,000 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, fine light dust, such as cotton lint, wood flour, and litho powder, has a minimum duct design velocity of 2,500 to 3,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for fine light dust such as cotton lint?","trap":"Cotton lint is fine light dust (2,500 to 3,000). Jute lint is a dry dust (3,000 to 3,500).","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives fine light dust, such as cotton lint and wood flour, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"2,500 to 3,000 fpm","d":["2,000 to 2,500 fpm","3,000 to 3,500 fpm","4,000 to 4,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r4","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, dry dusts and powders, such as fine rubber dust, jute lint, and soap dust, have a minimum duct design velocity of 3,000 to 3,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for dry dusts and powders such as soap dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives dry dusts and powders, such as soap dust and jute lint, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"3,000 to 3,500 fpm","d":["2,500 to 3,000 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm","4,500 fpm and up"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r5","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, average industrial dust, such as grinding dust, granite dust, and silica flour, has a minimum duct design velocity of 3,500 to 4,000 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for average industrial dust such as grinding dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives average industrial dust, such as grinding dust and silica flour, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"3,500 to 4,000 fpm","d":["3,000 to 3,500 fpm","4,000 to 4,500 fpm","2,000 to 2,500 fpm"]},"qs":["ascs-0378"]},{"id":"acr:T2-r6","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust, metal turnings, and sand blast dust, have a minimum duct design velocity of 4,000 to 4,500 fpm.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for heavy dusts such as wet sawdust?","trap":"Wet sawdust is a heavy dust (4,000 to 4,500). Moist cement dust is heavy or moist (4,500 and up).","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust and metal turnings, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"4,000 to 4,500 fpm","d":["3,500 to 4,000 fpm","4,500 fpm and up","3,000 to 3,500 fpm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:T2-r7","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-vacuum-velocities","tag":"DEF","text":"In Table 2, heavy or moist dusts, such as lead dust with small chips, moist cement dust, and quick-lime, have a minimum duct design velocity of 4,500 fpm and up.","src":"ACR 2025 Table 2","prompt":"What minimum duct design velocity does Table 2 give for heavy or moist dusts such as moist cement dust?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Table 2 gives heavy or moist dusts, such as moist cement dust and quick-lime, a minimum duct design velocity of __ .","a":"4,500 fpm and up","d":["4,000 to 4,500 fpm","3,500 to 4,000 fpm","2,500 to 3,000 fpm"]},"qs":["ascs-0379"]},{"id":"acr:4.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"REC","text":"Coils, fans, condensate pans, drains, and similar non-porous surfaces get wet cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6","prompt":"Which air-handling unit surfaces does §4.6 recommend for wet cleaning?","trap":"Recommended, and only for non-porous surfaces. Porous AHU liner never gets wet (4.4).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0381","ascs-0544","ascs-0625"]},{"id":"acr:4.6.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water extraction control collects the debris and prevents water damage.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.1","prompt":"When wet cleaning an AHU, what two things must water extraction control do?","trap":"Wet cleaning is only recommended, but once you do it, water extraction control is a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0469","ascs-0625"]},{"id":"acr:4.6.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Waste water capture, containment, testing, and disposal follow applicable regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.2","prompt":"What must waste water capture, containment, testing, and disposal follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0382","ascs-0581"]},{"id":"acr:4.6.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"DEF","text":"Those regulations are local, regional, state, and federal.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.6.2","prompt":"Which levels of regulation govern AHU waste water under §4.6.2?","trap":"This clause says state and federal. The service opening clause 4.2.10 says provincial and national.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0382","ascs-0581"]},{"id":"acr:4.8-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Dampers and air-directional devices get a position mark before cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.8","prompt":"What must be done to dampers and air-directional devices before cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0384","ascs-0533","ascs-0626"]},{"id":"acr:4.8-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"They return to that marked position after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.8","prompt":"Where must dampers and air-directional devices be set after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0384","ascs-0533","ascs-0626"]},{"id":"acr:4.9-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"REC","text":"Air distribution devices come out for proper cleaning, where removal is possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"What does §4.9 recommend for registers, grilles, and diffusers during cleaning?","trap":"Removal is recommended, where possible. Returning them to position is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0552"]},{"id":"acr:4.9-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"These devices return to their previous position.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"Where must registers, grilles, and diffusers go after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0385","ascs-0552"]},{"id":"acr:4.9-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"NOTE","text":"Removal is a recommendation. Restoration of position is mandatory.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.9","prompt":"For registers, grilles, and diffusers, which step is recommended and which is mandatory?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0385"]},{"id":"acr:4.10-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"SHALL","text":"Cleaning activities do not impair, alter, or damage detection equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.10","prompt":"What must cleaning activities not do to smoke and fire detection equipment?","trap":"When detection equipment goes off-line, the work plan must address life safety (2.8).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0386","ascs-0553"]},{"id":"acr:4.10-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","tag":"DEF","text":"This rule covers equipment in the facility and equipment that serves the components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.10","prompt":"Which smoke and fire detection equipment does §4.10 protect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Both the upstream side and the downstream side of each coil section get access.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"Which sides of each coil section must be accessed for cleaning?","trap":"Both faces, upstream and downstream. Cleaning from one side only does not comply.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0388","ascs-0527"]},{"id":"acr:4.11-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"CAN","text":"When both sides of a coil section have no access, removal or replacement becomes necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"What may become necessary when neither side of a coil section can be accessed?","trap":"CAN, a possibility. Not an automatic requirement to replace.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0389","ascs-0583"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"A visual inspection of the coil and the drain pan occurs before cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1","prompt":"What must be visually inspected before coil cleaning begins?","trap":"Coil AND drain pan. Suspect microbial growth on either one sends you to Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0390"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"DEF","text":"That inspection determines the need for Type 1 or Type 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1","prompt":"What decision does the pre-cleaning coil inspection make?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0390"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When Type 1 cannot clean the coil properly, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.1","prompt":"What happens when Type 1 cannot properly clean the coil?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0607"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When suspect microbial contamination appears on the coil or the drain pan, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.2","prompt":"What finding on the coil or drain pan requires Type 2?","trap":"Suspected contamination is enough. It sends the coil straight to Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0391","ascs-0529"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"CAN","text":"Replacement becomes necessary for damaged, deteriorating, or corroded metal fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.3","prompt":"Which fin conditions can make coil replacement necessary?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0392","ascs-0590"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.1.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"REC","text":"When cleaning will cause more damage to the coil, replacement happens.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1.3","prompt":"What does §4.11.1.3 recommend when cleaning would cause more damage to the coil?","trap":"Recommended, not required. The fin-damage line beside it is CAN, a possibility.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0392"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Type 1 dry cleaning methods remove loose dust, dirt, or debris from coil surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"What does Type 1 dry cleaning remove from coil surfaces?","trap":"Type 1 is dry. Water at any pressure is Type 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0528","ascs-0584"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"Negative air machines operate continuously during the Type 1 process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"What equipment must run continuously during Type 1 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil stays isolated from the duct system during the process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"During Type 1 cleaning, what must the coil be isolated from?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0393","ascs-0530"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"DEF","text":"Isolation stops migration and redeposit of disrupted particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Why is the coil isolated from the duct system during Type 1?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0393","ascs-0530"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which vacuuming method is a Type 1 coil cleaning option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include brushes for penetration between the coil fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which hand tool that reaches between the fins is a Type 1 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include compressed air.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which air-driven method is a Type 1 coil cleaning option?","trap":"Compressed air is Type 1 (dry). Pressure washing is Type 2 (wet).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0394"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.2-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 1 methods include fin straightening tools.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.2","prompt":"Which tool for bent fins is listed among Type 1 methods?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"A post-cleaning inspection follows Type 1 cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.3","prompt":"What must follow Type 1 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains or the coil is impacted, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.3","prompt":"After Type 1, which findings send the coil to Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0395","ascs-0585"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"DEF","text":"Type 2 is wet cleaning. It removes debris on coil, drain pan, and drain line surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"What is Type 2 coil cleaning, and which surfaces does it address?","trap":"Type 2 covers the drain pan and drain line surfaces too, not only the coil.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0528","ascs-0584"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When assessment determines that Type 1 will not be sufficient, Type 2 occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Under 2025 §4.11.4, what assessment result triggers Type 2?","trap":"New in 2025. You no longer have to try Type 1 and fail first.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains after Type 1 methods, Type 2 also occurs.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Under §4.11.4, besides an assessment that Type 1 will not be sufficient, what result also triggers Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0395"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition required Type 1 first, always. The 2025 edition does not.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"How did the rule on doing Type 1 before Type 2 change between 2021 and 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include application of coil cleaning products.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which chemical method is a Type 2 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include water washing at normal water line pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which low-pressure water method is a Type 2 option?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include pressure washing equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which high-pressure water equipment is a Type 2 option?","trap":"Permitted, but 4.11.4.2 still forbids damaging or eroding the coil or fins.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0396","ascs-0467"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"MAY","text":"Type 2 methods include hot water equipment or steam cleaning equipment.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which heated methods are Type 2 options?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"Coil cleaning products follow the product labeling of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"What governs the use of coil cleaning products?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4-g3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 Type 2 list opened with \"All methods under Type 1\". That bullet is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4","prompt":"Which bullet was removed from the start of the Type 2 methods list in 2025?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The condensate drain pan and the drain line get cleaning and flushing.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1","prompt":"What must be done to the condensate drain pan and drain line in Type 2?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0554","ascs-0627"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The drain pan gets inspection and a drainage test before cleaning and after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1","prompt":"When must the drain pan be inspected and drainage tested?","trap":"Before AND after cleaning. A test only at the end does not comply.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0397","ascs-0554","ascs-0608","ascs-0627","ascs-0658"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"Methods and products do not damage or erode the coil surface or the fins.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"What must coil cleaning methods and products not do to the coil surface or fins?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"They conform to the coil manufacturer recommendations, where those exist.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"Whose recommendations must coil cleaning methods and products conform to, where they exist?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0609"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.4.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"REC","text":"The coil cleaning solutions are as close to pH neutral as possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.2","prompt":"What pH does §4.11.4.2 recommend for coil cleaning solutions?","trap":"Recommended, not required. ACR does not ban acid or alkaline coil cleaners.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0398","ascs-0403","ascs-0531"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A post-cleaning inspection follows Type 2 cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What must follow Type 2 coil cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"SHALL","text":"When debris remains on the coil, the process repeats.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What happens when debris remains on the coil after Type 2?","trap":"Repeat first. Replacement comes up only when Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0399"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.5-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-type2","tag":"CAN","text":"When Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris, replacement becomes necessary.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","prompt":"What may become necessary when Type 2 methods cannot remove the debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0399","ascs-0555"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"DEF","text":"Visual observation of coil surfaces can mislead the inspector.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Why is visual observation alone a weak test of coil cleanliness?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0400","ascs-0532"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.6-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"REC","text":"A static pressure drop measurement occurs before and after the cleaning process.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"What measurement does §4.11.6 recommend before and after coil cleaning?","trap":"Recommended, not required, and ACR gives no target pressure drop value.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0400","ascs-0532"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"DEF","text":"Coil cleaning normally needs pressurized water and chemical agents.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"What does coil cleaning normally require, per §4.11.7?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0468"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Precautions capture the rinse water for duct mounted coils without drain pans.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"What must be done about rinse water when cleaning duct-mounted coils without drain pans?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0401","ascs-0591"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.7-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Type 1 methods, Type 2 methods, or both apply to inline coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.7","prompt":"Which cleaning types apply to inline coils?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The power source to electric resistance coils gets de-energization and lockout/tagout.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What must happen to the power source before cleaning electric resistance coils?","trap":"Switching it off is not enough. De-energize AND lockout/tagout.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0402","ascs-0526","ascs-0674"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"Only non-corrosive detergents apply during wet process cleaning of these coils.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What type of detergent may be used when wet cleaning electric resistance coils?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0403"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil gets a rinse free of chemicals.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"After detergent, what must be done to an electric resistance coil?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0628","ascs-0685"]},{"id":"acr:4.11.8-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-coil-special","tag":"SHALL","text":"The coil is thoroughly dry before it returns to an energized state.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8","prompt":"What condition must an electric resistance coil be in before it is re-energized?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0628","ascs-0685"]},{"id":"acr:4.12-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"All products comply with local, regional, state, and federal regulations.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.12","prompt":"What regulations must all products used for odor and emission control comply with?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0387"]},{"id":"acr:4.13-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Remediation follows ANSI/IICRC S520 for professional mold remediation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"Which standard governs remediation of microbial contamination under §4.13?","trap":"S520 is mold remediation. S500 is water damage. S590 is HVAC assessment after water, fire, or mold.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0404","ascs-0546"]},{"id":"acr:4.13-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Remediation also follows the component provisions of this Standard.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"Besides S520, what else must microbial remediation follow?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0404"]},{"id":"acr:4.13-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 heading read \"Remediation of Mold Contamination\".","src":"ACR 2025 §4.13","prompt":"What was the 2021 heading of clause 4.13?","trap":"2025 says microbial throughout, where 2021 said mold.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Restoration procedures occur only after mechanical cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14","prompt":"When may restoration procedures begin?","trap":"Restoration needs mechanical cleaning first. Surface treatments need more: a PASS on verification (4.15).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0405","ascs-0540"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Fire, smoke, flood, or water damage starts the procedures in 4.23 and 4.24.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.1","prompt":"Which types of damage start the procedures in 4.23 and 4.24?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.14.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Compromised components get attention as part of the restoration, to the extent possible.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.2","prompt":"How does restoration treat compromised components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0474"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"REC","text":"When cleaning and restoration cannot achieve the levels, components get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.3","prompt":"What does §4.14.3 recommend when cleaning and restoration cannot reach the required levels?","trap":"Replacement here is recommended. For fire-exposed parts that cannot withstand cleaning, 4.23 makes replacement a shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0406"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning receive documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"What record must be made of components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0407","ascs-0663"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"The customer receives notice of those components.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"Who must be told about components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0407"]},{"id":"acr:4.14.4-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Those components get a recommendation for repair or replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14.4","prompt":"What must be recommended for components that cannot withstand proper cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0407","ascs-0663"]},{"id":"acr:4.15-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"MAY","text":"Surface treatments restore the integrity of material surfaces as an alternative to replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What may surface treatments be used for instead of replacement?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.15-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface treatments apply only after confirmation that the system is clean.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What must be confirmed before surface treatments are applied?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0408","ascs-0547"]},{"id":"acr:4.15-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"They apply only after the system passes the specified level of cleanliness verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15","prompt":"What verification result must come before surface treatments are applied?","trap":"Not just after cleaning. A PASS on the specified verification comes first.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0408","ascs-0547"]},{"id":"acr:4.15.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface treatments meet the requirements of UL 181.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15.1","prompt":"What UL listing must surface treatments meet?","trap":"UL 181, not UL 181A/B. 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0409"]},{"id":"acr:4.15.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"UL 181 covers surface treatments. UL 181A/B covers closure tapes and mastics.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.15.1","prompt":"In ACR, what does UL 181 cover and what does UL 181A/B cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0409"]},{"id":"acr:X-10","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-restoration-order","tag":"NOTE","text":"Restoration follows cleaning. Surface treatments follow a PASS on verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.14, 4.15","prompt":"What must come before restoration, and what must come before surface treatments?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0540"]},{"id":"acr:4.17-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"Fibrous glass duct liner and duct board follow Section 4.7 for cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.17","prompt":"Which section's cleaning rules apply to fibrous glass duct liner and duct board?","trap":"Same agitate-and-capture method as any duct, but water and cleaning agents stay off it (4.4).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0550"]},{"id":"acr:4.17.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"The methods create no abrasions, breaks, or tears on the fibrous glass surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.17.1","prompt":"What damage must fibrous glass cleaning methods not create?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0412","ascs-0549","ascs-0629"]},{"id":"acr:4.18-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"MAY","text":"Resurfacing applies to friable components or components with abrasion or degradation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18","prompt":"Which fibrous glass conditions may call for resurfacing?","trap":"MAY, a permission. Resurfacing is optional here. Only fire-damaged friable or odor-retaining areas must be resurfaced or replaced (4.23.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0413"]},{"id":"acr:4.18-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"MAY","text":"When the work plan asks for smoother fibrous glass surfaces, resurfacing also applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18","prompt":"Besides damage, what work plan goal may justify resurfacing fibrous glass?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"An assessment determines that the surface gives a strong bondable surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.1","prompt":"What must an assessment confirm before fibrous glass is resurfaced?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0414"]},{"id":"acr:4.18.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"That assessment happens after proper mechanical cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.1","prompt":"When does the bondability assessment for resurfacing happen?","trap":"After proper mechanical cleaning, not before.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"SHALL","text":"No resurfacing occurs on material that is beyond restoration and fails one further test.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"When is resurfacing of fibrous glass forbidden?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0415","ascs-0597"]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"That further test is unsuitability to support proper application of a surfacing product.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"Which condition about applying a surfacing product rules out resurfacing material beyond restoration?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.18.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"The alternative further test is inability to give a long-term bondable surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.18.2","prompt":"Which long-term bonding condition is the alternative test that rules out resurfacing?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0597"]},{"id":"acr:4.19-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"REC","text":"Replacement follows damage, deterioration, delamination, or friable material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.19","prompt":"Which fibrous glass conditions does §4.19 recommend replacement for?","trap":"Recommended, and only after cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0050","ascs-0416","ascs-0598"]},{"id":"acr:4.19-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"DEF","text":"This rule applies only after cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.19","prompt":"When does the §4.19 replacement recommendation for damaged fibrous glass apply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0598"]},{"id":"acr:4.21-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-fibrous-glass","tag":"REC","text":"When a cleaned non-porous surface will still contribute particulate or odors, restoration follows.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.21","prompt":"When does §4.21 recommend restoring a non-porous surface after cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0420","ascs-0592"]},{"id":"acr:4.16-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"REC","text":"Porous materials with growth at Condition 3 get proper removal and replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.16","prompt":"What does §4.16 recommend for porous materials with Condition 3 growth?","trap":"Removal is recommended, not required. The mechanical surface cleaning that follows is the shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0410","ascs-0545","ascs-0636"]},{"id":"acr:4.16-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Surface cleaning by mechanical methods follows that task.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.16","prompt":"What must follow removal of microbial contaminated porous material?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0411","ascs-0636"]},{"id":"acr:4.20-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Metal surfaces get a scrape clean after removal of degraded thermal-acoustic material.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20","prompt":"What must be done to metal surfaces after degraded thermal-acoustic material is removed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0417","ascs-0599"]},{"id":"acr:4.20-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"The base surface is free of loose visible debris before the new insulation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20","prompt":"What condition must the base surface be in before new insulation is installed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0417","ascs-0599"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"A microbial cause of removal makes the base surface reach a Condition 1 status first.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.1","prompt":"When insulation was removed for a microbial cause, what status must the base surface reach before new insulation?","trap":"Condition 1 is normal fungal ecology and can include traces of growth. It does not mean sterile.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0418","ascs-0551"]},{"id":"acr:X-11","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"NOTE","text":"Condition 1 is a cleaning target at one clause only. That clause is 4.20.1.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.1","prompt":"At which single clause does ACR use Condition 1 as a cleaning target?","trap":"Other clauses target visibly clean or a verification PASS, not a Condition.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Replacement materials meet or exceed the original specifications or current codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.2","prompt":"What must replacement insulation materials meet or exceed?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0473"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Installation follows the written instructions of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.2","prompt":"Whose instructions govern installation of replacement insulation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.20.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"Installation complies with current SMACNA, NAIMA, and other applicable codes.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.3","prompt":"Which industry standards govern installation of replacement thermal-acoustic insulation?","trap":"SMACNA and NAIMA for insulation installation. UL 723 is the fire test and UL 181 the coating listing.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0419"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"New fibrous glass surfaces are capable of meeting the NADCA verification requirements.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.4","prompt":"What verification capability must new fibrous glass surfaces have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0600"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"No cleaning process damages a properly designed, installed, and sound system.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.5","prompt":"What must no cleaning process do to a properly designed, installed, and sound system?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0471"]},{"id":"acr:4.20.5-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-insulation-replacement","tag":"SHALL","text":"No cleaning process affects the performance, operation, or normal life expectancy.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.20.5","prompt":"Which three things about a sound system must no cleaning process affect?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.22-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water-damaged surfaces get evaluation and categorization by recognized methods.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"What must happen to water-damaged surfaces under §4.22?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0421","ascs-0593"]},{"id":"acr:4.22-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 gives those methods for professional water damage restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"Which standard gives the recognized methods for evaluating and categorizing water damage?","trap":"S500 is water. S520 is mold. S590 is assessing HVAC after water, fire, or mold.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0421","ascs-0593"]},{"id":"acr:4.22-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"The category of the water largely dictates the cleaning methods and the engineering controls.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"What largely dictates the cleaning methods and engineering controls on a water-damage job?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.22-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"All components with salvage value get thorough cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.22","prompt":"Which water-damaged components must be thoroughly cleaned?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components under heat and smoke get an evaluation for restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23","prompt":"What must happen to components exposed to heat and smoke?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components that cannot withstand proper cleaning and restoration get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23","prompt":"What must happen to fire-exposed components that cannot withstand proper cleaning and restoration?","trap":"A shall here. The general replacement line at 4.14.3 is only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:4.23.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Porous surfaces get an evaluation for friability and odor retention after cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.1","prompt":"After cleaning fire-damaged porous surfaces, which two properties must be evaluated?","trap":"Evaluate after cleaning, not before.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0422","ascs-0595"]},{"id":"acr:4.23.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Friable areas or odor-retaining areas get resurfacing or replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.2","prompt":"What must happen to fire-damaged areas that are friable or retain odor?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0423"]},{"id":"acr:4.23.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Heat-damaged surfaces reach an acceptable condition, or they get a replacement.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.3","prompt":"What are the two acceptable outcomes for heat-damaged surfaces?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0472"]},{"id":"acr:4.23.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"REC","text":"Sampling follows ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 for a question about soot or smoke.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.23.4","prompt":"Which standard does §4.23.4 recommend for sampling when soot or smoke is in question?","trap":"Recommended. IESO/RIA 6001 is for fire residue. NIOSH 0500 is the Method 3 dust analysis.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0424","ascs-0596"]},{"id":"acr:4.24-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"Pre-existing damage found during cleaning receives documentation.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24","prompt":"What record must be made of pre-existing damage found during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0425"]},{"id":"acr:4.24-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"SHALL","text":"The client receives notice of that damage.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24","prompt":"Who must be notified of pre-existing damage found during cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0425"]},{"id":"acr:4.24.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"Repair or replacement of malfunctioning mechanical devices is outside this scope.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24.1","prompt":"What mechanical work falls outside the §4.24 component repair scope?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0041"]},{"id":"acr:4.24.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-damage-repair","tag":"DEF","text":"Restoration does not include the sealing of air leaks in duct systems.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.24.1","prompt":"Which duct system task does ACR restoration not include?","trap":"Sealing duct leaks is not restoration. Sealing a service panel (4.2.5.3) is a separate shall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0426","ascs-0594"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"The purpose of verification is a determination of the presence of particulate.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Under ACR 2025, what is the purpose of cleanliness verification?","trap":"Verification checks for particulate only. It is not a mold test and not a hazardous material test.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0427","ascs-0643"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Verification does not determine microbial contamination.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 cleanliness verification determine microbial contamination?","trap":"A verification PASS says nothing about mold. Condition 1, 2 or 3 status is an IEP determination.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0427","ascs-0643"]},{"id":"acr:5.0-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Verification does not determine the presence of hazardous materials.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 cleanliness verification determine whether hazardous materials are present?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.0-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs on all specified components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"Which components must receive cleanliness verification?","trap":"The 10% minimum belongs to inspection (1.7), not verification. Verification covers all specified components.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.0-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Components reach visibly clean, at minimum, or the specified method of verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0","prompt":"What cleanliness result must cleaned components meet, at minimum, under Section 5?","trap":"Visibly clean is the floor. When the contract documents specify a verification method, that method applies.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.0-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 10% figure belongs to component inspection at 1.7. It is not a verification figure.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.0, §1.7","prompt":"Does the 10% minimum in ACR 2025 belong to verification or to component inspection?","trap":"Verification covers all specified components. 10% is the inspection minimum, and S520 microbial projects inspect all components.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs immediately after the cleaning of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.1","prompt":"How soon after cleaning a component must verification occur?","trap":"Two timing rules go together: immediately after cleaning, and before the system operates.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0428","ascs-0662"]},{"id":"acr:5.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"Verification occurs before the system operates.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.1","prompt":"Relative to putting the system back into operation, when must verification occur?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0428"]},{"id":"acr:5-M1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 1 is Visual Inspection. It applies to porous and non-porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"What is Method 1, and which component surfaces does it apply to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0279","ascs-0429","ascs-0630"]},{"id":"acr:5-M2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 2 is the Surface Comparison Test. It applies to porous and non-porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"What is Method 2, and which component surfaces does it apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"2021 limited Method 2 to porous surfaces, and the 2025 Definitions entry still says porous. Section 5.3 governs.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0432","ascs-0514"]},{"id":"acr:5-M3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 3 is the NADCA Vacuum Test. It applies to non-porous components only.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4, §5.3.3","prompt":"What is Method 3, and which component surfaces does it apply to?","trap":"The 2021 numbering had two \"Method 2\"s. In 2025 the Vacuum Test is Method 3, and never on porous surfaces.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0278","ascs-0513","ascs-0633"]},{"id":"acr:5.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"SHALL","text":"A visual inspection of porous and non-porous components determines visibly clean status.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"How is visibly clean status determined for porous and non-porous components?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0429","ascs-0630"]},{"id":"acr:5.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"A surface is visibly clean only if it is free from non-adhered substances and debris.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"Under Method 1 (5.2), when is a surface visibly clean?","trap":"Adhered substances such as paint or stains are not the test. Visibly clean means free of non-adhered substances and debris.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0505","ascs-0517"]},{"id":"acr:5.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"A visibly clean component needs no further verification method.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2","prompt":"When a component is found visibly clean, is another verification method required?","trap":"Method 2 or 3 follows only when Method 1 is inconclusive or disputed, or the contract specifies it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0430","ascs-0631"]},{"id":"acr:5.2.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"REC","text":"When Method 1 is inconclusive or in dispute, Method 2 or Method 3 verifies cleanliness.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 recommend when a Method 1 visual result is inconclusive or in dispute?","trap":"This escalation is recommended, not required. Method 3 still applies only to non-porous surfaces.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0279","ascs-0303","ascs-0431","ascs-0437","ascs-0631"]},{"id":"acr:5.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"MAY","text":"Method 2 determines the cleanliness of porous and non-porous component surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"Which component surfaces may Method 2, the Surface Comparison Test, evaluate in ACR 2025?","trap":"The 2021 edition said porous only. The 2025 edition widened Method 2 to porous and non-porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0432","ascs-0514"]},{"id":"acr:5.3-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition limited Method 2 to porous surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"To which surfaces did the 2021 edition limit the Surface Comparison Test?","trap":"A study guide built on 2021 says porous only. That answer is wrong for the 2025 exam.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2025 Definitions entry still reads \"porous\". The Section 5.3 body text governs.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3, Definitions","prompt":"When the 2025 Definitions entry and Section 5.3 disagree on Method 2 scope, which text governs?","trap":"An option quoting the Definitions entry (porous only) looks authoritative. The 5.3 body text says porous and non-porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"MAY","text":"When Method 2 is inconclusive or in dispute, Method 3 makes the final determination.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"What makes the final determination when a Method 2 result is inconclusive or in dispute?","trap":"Only on non-porous surfaces. On porous material Method 2 ends the escalation. Clause 5.3.3 is new in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0437","ascs-0573"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"DEF","text":"The NADCA Vacuum Test does not apply to porous components.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"Does the NADCA Vacuum Test apply to porous components?","trap":"Polymetric flex duct film is non-porous, so Method 3 can apply to it. Duct liner and duct board are porous.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0438","ascs-0513"]},{"id":"acr:5-M4","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"The escalation is Method 1, then Method 2 or Method 3, then Method 3 as the final word.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.2.1, §5.3.3","prompt":"What is the verification escalation order across the three methods?","trap":"Method 1 disputed goes to Method 2 or 3. Method 2 disputed goes to Method 3. Nothing escalates past Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0573"]},{"id":"acr:5-M5","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-verification-overview","tag":"NOTE","text":"On porous material, Method 2 is the end of the escalation. Method 3 cannot apply.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.3","prompt":"On porous material, where does the verification escalation end?","trap":"Wood and concrete are porous too, so a disputed Method 2 on them cannot go to Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"DEF","text":"The test compares the visible characteristics of the surface before and after contact vacuuming.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3","prompt":"What does the Method 2 Surface Comparison Test compare?","trap":"Method 2 is still a visual judgment. Nothing is weighed; weighing is Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A vacuum brush attaches to a running contact vacuum.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"In Method 2, what machine is the vacuum brush attached to, and what state must that machine be in?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The brush passes over the surface test area four (4) times.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"How many times does the brush pass over the Method 2 test area?","trap":"Method 2 is 4 brush passes. Method 3 is 2 passes per slot, once in each direction.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"In Method 2, the brush passes over the surface test area __.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","3 times","6 times"]},"qs":["ascs-0433","ascs-0515"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The pressure is light to moderate, as in routine cleaning.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What brush pressure does Method 2 use?","trap":"Method 2 uses light to moderate pressure. The Method 3 cassette rides the template with no downward pressure.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contact vacuum is HEPA-filtered.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What filtration must the Method 2 contact vacuum have?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0632"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contact vacuum achieves a minimum of 80 inches of static lift (WC).","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What minimum static lift must the Method 2 contact vacuum achieve?","trap":"80 in. WC is vacuum lift for Method 2. The Standard gives no numeric value for containment negative pressure.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum static lift the Method 2 contact vacuum must achieve is __ (WC).","a":"80 inches","d":["15 inches","37 inches","100 inches"]},"qs":["ascs-0434","ascs-0516","ascs-0632","ascs-0638"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The contact vacuum has a 2.5 inch round nylon brush.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What size and type of brush does the Method 2 contact vacuum use?","trap":"Brush 2.5 in., hose 1.5 in. The two sizes swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 2 contact vacuum has a __ round nylon brush.","a":"2.5 inch","d":["1.5 inch","4 inch","6 inch"]},"qs":["ascs-0435"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.1-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"The brush attaches to a 1.5 inch diameter vacuum hose.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1","prompt":"What diameter vacuum hose does the Method 2 brush attach to?","trap":"Hose 1.5 in., brush 2.5 in. The two sizes swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 2 brush attaches to a __ diameter vacuum hose.","a":"1.5 inch","d":["1 inch","2.5 inch","4 inch"]},"qs":["ascs-0435"]},{"id":"acr:5.3.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"SHALL","text":"A comparison determines any significant change in the visible characteristics.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.2","prompt":"After the Method 2 brush passes, what does the comparison look for?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.3.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-2","tag":"DEF","text":"The surface is clean only if no significant visible difference exists.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.2","prompt":"What is the Method 2 pass condition?","trap":"A visible difference after brushing means dirt came off, so the surface fails. No significant difference is a pass.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0436"]},{"id":"acr:5.4-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"Method 3 evaluates particulate levels on non-porous component surfaces by scientific means.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What does Method 3 evaluate, on which surfaces, and by what means?","trap":"Non-porous only. It is the only method of the three that weighs what it collects.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"A template applies to the ducted airside surface of the component.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, where is the template applied?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"A closed-face vacuum cassette attaches to a calibrated air sampling pump.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what does the closed-face vacuum cassette attach to?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The cassette passes over two (2) openings of 2 cm x 25 cm in the template.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In the Method 3 overview, how many template openings does the cassette pass over, and what size is each?","trap":"Two slots of 2 cm x 25 cm are 50 cm2 each, 100 cm2 together. Use that math to check the numbers.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 cassette passes over two template openings of __ x 25 cm.","a":"2 cm","d":["2.5 cm","3.7 cm","5 cm"]},"qs":["ascs-0562"]},{"id":"acr:5.4-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"CAN","text":"No portion of the cassette can contact the component surface at any time.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"Can any portion of the Method 3 cassette contact the component surface at any time?","trap":"The cassette edges rest on the template, never on the duct surface.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0439"]},{"id":"acr:5.4-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The template holds the cassette above the surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what holds the cassette above the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"Airflow accelerates through the narrow opening between the template and the surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"During the NADCA Vacuum Test, where does the airflow accelerate?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The increased velocity dislodges latent particulate onto the filter media.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"In Method 3, what does the increased air velocity do?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-i","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"After the procedure, the cassette gets preparation and a weight measurement.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What happens to the Method 3 cassette after the sampling procedure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4-j","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"That measurement gives the total debris on the filter media.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4","prompt":"What does the Method 3 cassette weight measurement give?","trap":"The pass limit is a net weight per 100 cm2, not a particle count.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The air sampling pump draws a minimum of 15 liters per minute.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What minimum flow must the Method 3 air sampling pump draw?","trap":"Three flow numbers: pump capable of 15, sample at 17 plus or minus 2, calibrator accurate at 15.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum flow the Method 3 air sampling pump must draw is __.","a":"15 L/min","d":["5 L/min","17 L/min","19 L/min"]},"qs":["ascs-0440","ascs-0563"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette is closed-face and holds 37 mm matched weight filters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What type of cassette does Method 3 use, and what filters does it hold?","trap":"37 mm is the filter. 3.7 cm is the maximum alternate slot width. Same digits, different parts.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 cassette is closed-face and holds __ matched weight filters.","a":"37 mm","d":["3.7 mm","15 mm","25 mm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The filter media is 37 mm mixed cellulose ester matched weight filters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What material are the Method 3 filters made of?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0443","ascs-0566"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The pore size is 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What pore sizes are allowed for the Method 3 filter media?","trap":"Filter pore size is 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer. HEPA's 0.3 micron is a different number for a different job.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 filter pore size is 0.8 or __.","a":"0.45 micrometer","d":["0.3 micrometer","0.7 micrometer","0.75 micrometer"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"Two filters go in series, preloaded in a three-piece cassette.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How many Method 3 filters are used, how are they arranged, and in what kind of cassette?","trap":"Two filters, three-piece cassette. The two counts swap easily.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"Two filters go in series, preloaded in a __ cassette.","a":"three-piece","d":["one-piece","two-piece","four-piece"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The calibration device is accurate to ±5% at 15 liters per minute.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How accurate must the Method 3 calibration device be, and at what flow?","trap":"The calibrator is checked at 15 L/min, but the sampling flow is set to 17 plus or minus 2 L/min.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The required accuracy of the Method 3 calibration device at 15 liters per minute is __.","a":"±5%","d":["±2%","±10%","±15%"]},"qs":["ascs-0442","ascs-0567"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template is 15 mil thick (0.381 mm).","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How thick is the Method 3 template?","trap":"15 mil is template thickness. 15 L/min is pump capability. Same number, different parts.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 template is __ thick.","a":"15 mil","d":["6 mil","10 mil","37 mil"]},"qs":["ascs-0446","ascs-0569"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template gives a 100 cm2 sampling area.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"What sampling area does the standard Method 3 template give?","trap":"Each slot is 50 cm2. Two slots make 100 cm2, the same area the pass limit is expressed in.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 template gives a __ sampling area.","a":"100 cm2","d":["25 cm2","50 cm2","75 cm2"]},"qs":["ascs-0444","ascs-0634"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1-i","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template has two (2) slots of 2 cm x 25 cm, at least 2.5 cm apart.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1","prompt":"How many slots does the Method 3 template have, what size are they, and how far apart?","trap":"2.5 cm is the minimum slot spacing. 2.5 in. is the Method 2 brush. Watch the units.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The minimum spacing between the two 2 cm x 25 cm template slots is __.","a":"2.5 cm","d":["2.0 cm","3.7 cm","5 cm"]},"qs":["ascs-0445","ascs-0562","ascs-0634"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"The standard template openings are 2 centimeters wide by 25 centimeters long.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.1","prompt":"What are the width and length of the standard Method 3 template openings?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The standard template openings are 2 centimeters wide by __ long.","a":"25 centimeters","d":["2.5 centimeters","3.7 centimeters","15 centimeters"]},"qs":["ascs-0445"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.1-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"MAY","text":"When the standard slot does not fit the space, slots of other sizes apply.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.1","prompt":"What may be used when the standard 2 cm x 25 cm template slot does not fit the space?","trap":"Other slot sizes are allowed, but the total must still be 100 cm2 and each width 2.0 to 3.7 cm.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The total sampled area equals 100 square centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"With an alternate-size template, what must the total sampled area equal?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The maximum width of the opening does not exceed 3.7 centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"What is the maximum opening width for an alternate Method 3 template slot?","trap":"3.7 cm maximum slot width, not the 37 mm filter. The 2.0 cm minimum and 3.7 cm maximum bracket the width.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The largest width allowed for an alternate template opening is __.","a":"3.7 centimeters","d":["2.0 centimeters","2.5 centimeters","5 centimeters"]},"qs":["ascs-0447"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"DEF","text":"This maximum stops contact between the cassette and the sampled surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"Why does an alternate template opening have a maximum width?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.1.2-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-kit","tag":"SHALL","text":"The minimum opening width is 2.0 centimeters or more.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1.2","prompt":"What is the minimum opening width for an alternate Method 3 template slot?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The smallest width allowed for an alternate template opening is __.","a":"2.0 centimeters","d":["1.5 centimeters","2.5 centimeters","3.7 centimeters"]},"qs":["ascs-0447"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template has a secure attachment, for example tape at four corners.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How is the Method 3 template secured to the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0476"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The template lies flat against the sampled surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How must the Method 3 template sit against the sampled surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-c","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The sampled surface is dry.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What condition must the surface be in for Method 3 sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0633"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-d","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The air handler does not run during the sampling.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"May the air handler run during Method 3 sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0448"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-e","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The supplier seals the cassettes with shrink tape.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who seals new Method 3 cassettes, and with what?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0475"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-f","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The protective plugs come out of the new cassette.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What is removed from a new Method 3 cassette before sampling?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-g","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The outlet end of the cassette attaches to the vacuum pump tubing.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Which end of the Method 3 cassette attaches to the vacuum pump tubing?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-h","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"A calibration device adjusts the air flow to 17 liters per minute, plus or minus 2.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"To what airflow is the Method 3 sampling flow adjusted, and with what?","trap":"Sampling flow is 17 plus or minus 2 L/min. 15 L/min is the pump minimum and the calibrator accuracy point.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"A calibration device adjusts the Method 3 sampling air flow to __, plus or minus 2.","a":"17 L/min","d":["15 L/min","19 L/min","25 L/min"]},"qs":["ascs-0441","ascs-0564","ascs-0644"]},{"id":"acr:X-4","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"Three flow numbers exist for Method 3. They are 15 L/min, 17±2 L/min, and ±5% at 15 L/min.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.1, §5.4.2","prompt":"What are the three flow numbers in Method 3?","trap":"Pump capable of 15, sample at 17 plus or minus 2, calibrator accurate to plus or minus 5% at 15.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0440","ascs-0441","ascs-0442","ascs-0564","ascs-0567","ascs-0644"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-i","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette slides from one end of each template opening to the other.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How does the Method 3 cassette travel along each template opening?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-j","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The rate is not greater than 5 cm per second, about 5 seconds per slot each direction.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How fast may the cassette travel along a Method 3 template slot?","trap":"5 cm per second over a 25 cm slot is about 5 seconds each way. Not the plus or minus 5% calibrator.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The fastest the cassette may travel along each Method 3 template slot is __.","a":"5 cm per second","d":["2 cm per second","2.5 cm per second","25 cm per second"]},"qs":["ascs-0449","ascs-0565","ascs-0635","ascs-0639"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-k","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The edges of the cassette always rest on the template.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"During Method 3 sampling, what do the cassette edges rest on?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-l","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette does not touch the duct surface.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"During the sampling passes, may the Method 3 cassette touch the duct surface?","trap":"The Method 2 brush contacts the surface. The Method 3 cassette never does.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0439"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-m","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"Each template opening gets a vacuum twice, once in each direction.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How many times is each Method 3 template opening vacuumed, and in which directions?","trap":"Two passes per slot in Method 3. Four brush passes is Method 2.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"In total, each Method 3 template opening is vacuumed __.","a":"twice","d":["once","three times","four times"]},"qs":["ascs-0450","ascs-0570"]},{"id":"acr:X-5","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"Method 2 uses four brush passes. Method 3 uses two passes per slot.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.3.1, §5.4.2","prompt":"How many passes does Method 2 use compared with Method 3?","trap":"Four is the Method 2 brush count. Two per slot, one in each direction, is Method 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0433","ascs-0450"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-n","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette touches the template surface with no downward pressure.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"How much downward pressure does the Method 3 cassette put on the template?","trap":"Method 2 uses light to moderate brush pressure. Method 3 uses no downward pressure at all.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-o","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"MAY","text":"The vacuum pump goes off after the two passes. Then the plugs go back.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What happens to the pump and the cassette plugs after the two passes?","trap":"The 2021 clear plastic cover step before pump shutdown is gone in 2025.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-y","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-field","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition had a \"clear plastic cover\" step before pump shutdown. It is gone.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What 2021 Method 3 step before pump shutdown was removed in 2025?","trap":"A 2021 study guide still lists the clear plastic cover. It is not a 2025 step.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-p","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The cassette gets a label and a record of the sampled surface area.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"After Method 3 sampling, what must be put on or recorded with each cassette?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-q","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Analysis follows NIOSH Method 0500 for total nuisance dust.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What analytical method is used for Method 3 cassettes?","trap":"NIOSH 0500 measures total nuisance dust by weight. It is not a microbial analysis.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0451","ascs-0568"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-r","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scale sensitivity is equal to or greater than 0.7 milligram.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"What scale sensitivity does ACR 2025 require for weighing Method 3 filters?","trap":"It reads 0.7 mg or more, backwards from lab logic, but it is verbatim. 0.75 is the pass limit, not the scale.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"ACR 2025 words the Method 3 scale sensitivity as equal to or greater than __.","a":"0.7 mg","d":["0.45 mg","0.75 mg","0.8 mg"]},"qs":["ascs-0452","ascs-0571"]},{"id":"acr:X-6","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"NOTE","text":"Scale sensitivity is 0.7 mg or MORE. This reads backwards but it is verbatim.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Is the Method 3 scale sensitivity worded as 0.7 mg or less, or 0.7 mg or more?","trap":"Lab logic says a finer scale is better, so \"or less\" feels right. The Standard says equal to or greater than.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0571"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-s","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The scale has calibration to the written recommendations of the manufacturer.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"To what is the Method 3 weighing scale calibrated?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-t","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"Results appear in milligrams per 100 square centimeters.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"In what units are Method 3 results reported?","trap":"The reporting area, 100 cm2, matches the template sampling area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0477"]},{"id":"acr:5.5-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"The net weight of the collected debris does not exceed 0.75 mg/100 cm2.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.5","prompt":"What is the Method 3 passing criterion?","trap":"0.75 mg/100 cm2 is the pass limit. 0.7 mg is the scale sensitivity. The limit is on net weight.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"The Method 3 pass limit, the maximum net weight of collected debris, is __.","a":"0.75 mg/100 cm2","d":["0.45 mg/100 cm2","0.7 mg/100 cm2","0.8 mg/100 cm2"]},"qs":["ascs-0452","ascs-0453","ascs-0512"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-u","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"A qualified individual of the client or the client agent procures the samples.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who does ACR 2025 recommend should procure Method 3 samples?","trap":"Recommended, not required. Answers that make client sampling mandatory are wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0454"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-v","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"An accredited laboratory analyzes the samples.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Who does ACR 2025 recommend should analyze Method 3 samples?","trap":"An accredited lab is recommended. Analysis by NIOSH 0500 is the SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0454"]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-w","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"CAN","text":"Sampling equipment is capable of transport onto the work site.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Can Method 3 sampling equipment be transported onto the work site?","trap":"CAN states ability. It is neither a permission nor a requirement.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.4.2-x","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"NOTE","text":"Samples generally go to a laboratory. On-site sampling is still permitted.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2","prompt":"Where do Method 3 samples generally go, and is on-site sampling permitted?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:5.6-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"Documentation of compliance with this Standard covers all work performed.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6","prompt":"What does ACR 2025 recommend that post-project documentation of compliance cover?","trap":"Overall documentation is recommended. Lab results after a Vacuum Test and chain of custody for outside labs are SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0455"]},{"id":"acr:5.6-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"CAN","text":"Documentation includes organized and legible written records and visual records.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6","prompt":"In broad terms, what two kinds of records can post-project documentation include, and in what condition?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0580"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.1-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"A copy of the lab results goes into the documentation after a NADCA Vacuum Test.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.1","prompt":"When a NADCA Vacuum Test was performed, what must go into the post-project documentation?","trap":"One of only two SHALLs in 5.6. Photos, plans and submittals are only recommended.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0456","ascs-0572"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.2-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"SHALL","text":"When outside laboratories or agencies do the work, chain of custody documentation applies.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.2","prompt":"When is chain of custody documentation required in post-project documentation?","trap":"Required only when outside laboratories or agencies do the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0457"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.3-a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"The documentation includes photo images and HVAC plans.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.3","prompt":"Which images and drawings does ACR 2025 recommend including in post-project documentation?","trap":"Photos are recommended, not required. Before and after photos are not a SHALL.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0458"]},{"id":"acr:5.6.3-b","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-method-3-results","tag":"REC","text":"The documentation includes submittal forms for materials, warrantees, and guarantees.","src":"ACR 2025 §5.6.3","prompt":"What submittal forms does ACR 2025 recommend including in post-project documentation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0458"]},{"id":"acr:D-1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Cleaning is the removal of non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Cleaning?","trap":"2021 said removal of visible particulate and contaminants. 2025 says removal of non-adhered substances.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0732"]},{"id":"acr:D-11","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 edition defined Cleaning as removal of visible particulate and contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How did the 2021 edition define Cleaning?","trap":"That is the old wording. Pick \"removal of non-adhered substances\" for the 2025 exam.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-2","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanical Cleaning removes contaminants and debris not intended to be present on internal surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Mechanical Cleaning remove, per the ACR 2025 definition?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-3","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Contaminant is any substance not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Contaminant?","trap":"Contaminant is ANY unwanted substance. Debris is limited to non-adhered substances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0032"]},{"id":"acr:D-4","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Debris is non-adhered substances not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Debris?","trap":"Debris adds \"non-adhered\" to the Contaminant wording. Particulate adds \"removable by an agitation device\".","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0033"]},{"id":"acr:D-5","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Particulate is any non-adhered substance that an agitation device can remove.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Particulate?","trap":"The agitation-device clause is unique to Particulate. Contaminant and Debris say nothing about how it is removed.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0031"]},{"id":"acr:D-10","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"NOTE","text":"The agitation-device clause is what separates Particulate from Contaminant and Debris.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which clause separates the definition of Particulate from Contaminant and Debris?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-6","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Non-Adhered Substance is material not intended or designed to be present in the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Non-Adhered Substance?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-6a","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Section 4 cleaning and restoration procedures can remove a non-adhered substance.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What can remove a non-adhered substance, per its ACR 2025 definition?","trap":"Non-adhered: Section 4 procedures can remove it. Adhered: Section 4 procedures do not.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0028"]},{"id":"acr:D-7","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"An Adhered Substance is material that Section 4 procedures do not remove.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Adhered Substance?","trap":"Visibly clean does not require removing adhered substances. It requires freedom from non-adhered substances.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0028","ascs-0029","ascs-0731"]},{"id":"acr:D-8","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Examples of adhered substances are paint, mastic, sealant, and stains.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of adhered substances?","trap":"Stains count as adhered. A stained surface can still be visibly clean.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0029","ascs-0045"]},{"id":"acr:D-9","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Visibly Clean means the interior surfaces are free of non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does the Definitions section define Visibly Clean?","trap":"Not \"free of all contaminants\". Only non-adhered substances must be gone.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0517"]},{"id":"acr:D-12","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Non-Porous Component contacts the air stream and resists penetration by water or air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Non-Porous Component?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-13","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Non-porous examples are sheet metal, aluminum foil, and polymetric flexible duct film.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of non-porous components?","trap":"Flex duct film is non-porous, so Method 3 can apply to it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0438"]},{"id":"acr:D-14","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"A Porous Component contacts the air stream and allows penetration by water or air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Porous Component?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-15","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Porous examples are fibrous glass duct liner and fibrous glass duct board.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which fibrous glass products does ACR 2025 list as porous components?","trap":"Porous means Method 3 never applies. A disputed result on liner or duct board ends at Method 2.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-16","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"DEF","text":"Sound attenuated duct, wood, and concrete are also porous components.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Besides fibrous glass liner and duct board, what else does ACR 2025 list as porous?","trap":"Wood and concrete are porous even though they feel hard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-17","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-substances","tag":"NOTE","text":"Wood and concrete are the two porous examples that candidates forget.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which two porous examples do candidates most often forget?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-18","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 1 is a normal fungal ecology for a similar indoor environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 1 defined?","trap":"Condition 1 is not sterile. It can include settled spores and traces of actual growth.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0293","ascs-0736"]},{"id":"acr:D-19","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 1 can have settled spores, fungal fragments, or traces of actual growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What can a Condition 1 environment still contain?","trap":"Settled spores alone do not make Condition 2. Condition 2 spores come from a Condition 3 area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0736"]},{"id":"acr:D-20","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 2 is contamination primarily by settled spores or fungal fragments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 2 defined?","trap":"Condition 2 is set by where the settled spores came from: directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0294","ascs-0317","ascs-0734"]},{"id":"acr:D-20a","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"A Condition 2 environment may also have traces of actual growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Can a Condition 2 environment have traces of actual growth?","trap":"Traces of actual growth can appear in Condition 1 and Condition 2. Traces alone do not mean Condition 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-21","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Those spores or fragments come directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where do the settled spores or fragments of a Condition 2 environment come from?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0294","ascs-0317","ascs-0734"]},{"id":"acr:D-22","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Condition 3 is the presence of actual microbial growth, spores, and fungal fragments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How is Condition 3 defined?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0295","ascs-0735"]},{"id":"acr:D-23","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Actual growth includes active or dormant growth, and visible or hidden growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What forms of growth count as actual growth for Condition 3?","trap":"Dormant and hidden growth still count. Condition 3 does not need visible, active growth.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0735"]},{"id":"acr:D-24","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S520 is the source of all three condition definitions.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which standard is the source of the Condition 1, 2 and 3 definitions?","trap":"S520 is mold remediation. S500 is water damage. S590 is HVAC assessment after damage.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-25","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Microbial Contamination is past or current active indoor microbial growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Microbial Contamination?","trap":"2025 says \"microbial\" throughout where 2021 said \"mold\". Past growth counts, not only current growth.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-26","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"acr-microbial-conditions","tag":"DEF","text":"Its identity, location, and amplification do not reflect a normal microbial ecology.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What makes indoor microbial growth count as Microbial Contamination rather than normal ecology?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-27","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"HEPA means 99.97% filtration efficiency at 0.3 micron-sized particles.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What filtration efficiency and particle size define HEPA in ACR 2025?","trap":"HEPA is rated at 0.3 micron. The Method 3 filter pore sizes are 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer.","delta":false,"num":{"q":"HEPA means 99.97% filtration efficiency at __ particles.","a":"0.3 micron","d":["0.45 micron","0.7 micron","0.8 micron"]},"qs":["ascs-0338"]},{"id":"acr:D-28","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The US Department of Energy is the source of the HEPA definition.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which agency is the source of the HEPA definition in ACR 2025?","trap":"The US Department of Energy, not EPA, OSHA or NIOSH.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-29","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Filtration Device is a self-contained blower assembly with particulate filtration.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Filtration Device (AFD)?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-30","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Scrubber is an AFD with HEPA filtration that recirculates air in a defined space.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Scrubber?","trap":"Scrubber recirculates air in the space. Negative air machine controls pressure differentials.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0073","ascs-0103","ascs-0121"]},{"id":"acr:D-31","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Negative Air Machine is a HEPA-filtered AFD that controls workspace pressure differentials.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Negative Air Machine?","trap":"Negative air machine controls pressure differentials. Scrubber recirculates air in the space.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0073","ascs-0074","ascs-0102","ascs-0121","ascs-0692"]},{"id":"acr:D-32","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Negative Air Machine can have ducting outside the building envelope, or no ducting.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Must a Negative Air Machine be ducted outside the building envelope?","trap":"Ducting outdoors is optional in the definition. A negative air machine can have no ducting.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0074"]},{"id":"acr:D-33","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Collection Device is a HEPA machine that discharges air to the indoor environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where may a HEPA collection device discharge its air?","trap":"Only a HEPA collection device may discharge indoors. A non-HEPA one discharges outside the building envelope.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0733"]},{"id":"acr:D-34","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Collection Device is also a fan-driven non-HEPA machine that discharges outside the envelope.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where does a fan-driven non-HEPA collection device discharge its air?","trap":"No HEPA, exhaust outdoors. Only a HEPA collection device may discharge to the indoor environment.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0733"]},{"id":"acr:D-35","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Contact Vacuum is a collection device, usually portable, with a nylon brush nozzle.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Contact Vacuum?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0078","ascs-0118"]},{"id":"acr:D-36","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Agitation Device dislodges or moves non-adhered substances in the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Agitation Device?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-37","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Agitation device examples are brushes, whips, compressed air, and contact vacuum attachments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of agitation devices?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-38","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Source Removal is mechanical cleaning that eliminates contaminants at their point of origin.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Source Removal?","trap":"New term in 2025, along with Damp Wiping, Open Air Plenum and Duct Wrap.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0582","ascs-0740"]},{"id":"acr:D-39","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-equipment-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Damp Wiping is a process for wiping component surfaces on restoration projects.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Damp Wiping?","trap":"New term in 2025, along with Source Removal, Open Air Plenum and Duct Wrap.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0764"]},{"id":"acr:D-40","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Duct is a passageway for distribution and extraction of air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Duct?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0119"]},{"id":"acr:D-41","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"The Air Duct definition excludes plenums that do not follow SMACNA Standards.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which plenums does the Air Duct definition exclude?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0272"]},{"id":"acr:D-42","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Board is rigid insulation board with a finishing material on one or both sides.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Board?","trap":"Duct board is porous, so Method 3 never applies to it.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-43","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Liner is insulation, usually fibrous glass, applied inside a metal duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Liner?","trap":"Liner is inside the duct and porous. Wrap and covering are on the outside.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-44","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct Wrap is insulation for the exterior of ducts, with sealed seams and joints.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Duct Wrap?","trap":"Duct Wrap is a new 2025 term. Wrap is exterior insulation; liner is interior.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-45","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air Duct Covering is insulation and banding on the external surface of a duct.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Air Duct Covering?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-46","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Service Panel closes a service opening in an HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Service Panel do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-47","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Duct Access Door is a fabricated metal hatch for permanent installation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Duct Access Door?","trap":"Prefabricated door gauge follows the system pressure class. A field-made panel follows the duct gauge.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-48","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Flush Mount door extends into the duct and sits flush with the outside duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does a Flush Mount access door sit in the duct?","trap":"Flush mount extends into the duct. Surface mount extends out from the duct wall.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0113"]},{"id":"acr:D-49","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Surface Mount door extends out from the surface of the outside duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does a Surface Mount access door sit on the duct?","trap":"Surface mount extends out from the duct wall. Flush mount extends into the duct.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-50","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Hinged door has a door and a doorframe with a hinge between them.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Hinged access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-51","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Sandwich door has two parts, fastened on both sides of the duct wall.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Sandwich access door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0077","ascs-0115"]},{"id":"acr:D-52","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Spin Door is round. The frame spins into a round opening.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What defines a Spin Door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0114"]},{"id":"acr:D-53","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Closure is an access door or panel that creates a permanent seal.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Closure?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-54","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Permanent means the life of the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How long is \"permanent\" in ACR 2025?","trap":"Permanent is tied to the life of the system, not to a set number of years.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0738"]},{"id":"acr:D-55","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mastic caulks, seals, or cements gaps and cracks in duct connections and joints.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Mastic do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"Mastic is an adhered substance. Closure mastics must meet UL 181A/B, not UL 181.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-56","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanically Fasten means to affix objects with screws, clamps, locks, or straps.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What four fastener types does the Mechanically Fasten definition allow?","trap":"Service panels are narrower: screws or rivets only, at minimum every 4 inches on center (4.2.5.2).","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0076"]},{"id":"acr:D-57","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-duct-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The definition allows four fastener types. Clause 4.2.5.2 narrows panels to screws or rivets.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions; §4.2.5.2","prompt":"How does clause 4.2.5.2 narrow the Mechanically Fasten definition for service panels?","trap":"Rivets are not in the definition list yet are allowed for panels. Clamps, locks and straps are not.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-70","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Air-Handling Unit moves air. It can also clean and condition the air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does an Air-Handling Unit do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0095"]},{"id":"acr:D-71","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Central-Station AHU is a factory-made encased assembly with fans and other equipment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Central-Station air-handling unit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0110"]},{"id":"acr:D-72","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Central-Station AHU does not include a heating source or a cooling source.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Does a Central-Station AHU include a heating source or a cooling source?","trap":"A central-station AHU has no heating or cooling source of its own. A cooling-heating unit provides both.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-73","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Cooling-Heating Unit gives both cooling and heating.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Cooling-Heating Unit provide?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-74","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Cooling-Heating examples are mini-splits, PTACs, wall units, window units, and fan coil units.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the ACR 2025 examples of Cooling-Heating Units?","trap":"Split systems also count as cooling-heating units.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0081","ascs-0111"]},{"id":"acr:D-74a","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Split systems are also Cooling-Heating Units.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which unit type do split systems fall under in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-75","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Cooling Unit gives cooling. A Heating Unit gives heating.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Cooling Unit and a Heating Unit?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0117"]},{"id":"acr:D-76","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Make-Up Air Unit supplies tempered fresh air that replaces exhausted air.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Make-Up Air Unit supply?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-77","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Ventilating Unit gives ventilation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Ventilating Unit provide?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-78","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Coils temper or dehumidify the air that the system handles.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What do coils do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-79","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Coil media are water, ethylene glycol solution, brine, volatile refrigerant, or steam.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What media can flow through coils, per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-80","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"acr-ahu-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Total cooling is sensible cooling plus latent cooling.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What makes up total cooling?","trap":"Sensible cooling changes temperature; latent cooling removes moisture. Total cooling is the sum.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-58","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Airside Surface is the internal surface that air impacts during travel through the system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Airside Surface?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0108"]},{"id":"acr:D-59","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Airside Surface excludes open air plenums and interstitial cavities.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does the Airside Surface definition exclude?","trap":"Changed in 2025. Open air plenums and interstitial cavities are not airside surfaces.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0075"]},{"id":"acr:D-60","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Open Air Plenum is a non-ducted air pathway in building cavities, voids, and spaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Open Air Plenum?","trap":"A new 2025 term. Open air plenums are not part of the HVAC system and are not airside surfaces.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0079","ascs-0139","ascs-0140"]},{"id":"acr:D-61","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Open air plenums are outside the occupied zone of the building.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Where are open air plenums located relative to the occupied zone of the building?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-62","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The HVAC System runs from the point of air entry to the point of air discharge.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What are the boundaries of the HVAC System in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-63","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"The HVAC System includes return grilles, return ducts, and the air-handling unit.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which return-side parts and central equipment does the HVAC System definition include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0105"]},{"id":"acr:D-64","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"It includes the mixing box, coil compartment, and condensate drain pans.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which AHU sections and pans does the HVAC System definition include?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-65","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"It includes humidifiers, dehumidifiers, supply ducts, fans, fan housing, and fan blades.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which moisture-control devices, supply-side air paths and fan parts does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-66","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"It includes air wash systems, spray eliminators, and turning vanes.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which water-spray air treatment parts and duct fittings that redirect airflow does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-67","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"It includes filters, filter housings, reheat coils, and supply diffusers.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which filtration parts, secondary heating coils and supply-air outlets does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-68","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"It includes dedicated exhaust components, ventilation components, and make-up air systems.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which parts that remove air from the building or bring outdoor air in does the HVAC System definition list?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0763"]},{"id":"acr:D-69","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Non-ducted open air plenums of all types are not part of the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Are non-ducted open air plenums part of the HVAC system in ACR 2025?","trap":"Changed in 2025. A non-ducted ceiling return plenum is not part of the HVAC system as ACR defines it.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0080","ascs-0494"]},{"id":"acr:D-81","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An ASCS holds the NADCA designation after a pass on a written certification examination.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an ASCS?","trap":"ASCS oversight of a project is only recommended. Training and qualification of workers are mandatory.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-82","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP is qualified by education, training, and experience to assess fungal ecology.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What qualifies an Indoor Environmental Professional (IEP), per ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-83","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP creates a sampling strategy and interprets laboratory data.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What two sampling and lab tasks does an IEP perform?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0296"]},{"id":"acr:D-84","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An IEP determines Condition 1, 2, and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Who determines Condition 1, 2 and 3 status and verifies the return to Condition 1?","trap":"The IEP, not Section 5 verification. Cleanliness verification does not determine microbial contamination.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0296","ascs-0737"]},{"id":"acr:D-85","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Inspection is a gathering of information for determinations and assessments.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Inspection?","trap":"Inspection gathers information. Assessment reviews and evaluates. A preliminary determination is the first conclusion.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-86","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"An Assessment is a review and evaluation of the system or representative portions.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Assessment?","trap":"Assessment reviews and evaluates. Inspection only gathers the information.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-87","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"A Preliminary Determination is a conclusion from information at an initial inspection.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Preliminary Determination?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-88","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Standard of Care is the practice common to reasonably prudent members of the trade.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Standard of Care?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0741"]},{"id":"acr:D-89","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Restoration means to bring back to a former or original state.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Restoration?","trap":"Restoration does not include sealing duct air leaks or repairing malfunctioning mechanical devices.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-90","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-scope-roles","tag":"DEF","text":"Chain of Custody Documentation shows seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Chain of Custody Documentation show?","trap":"Required under 5.6.2 only when outside laboratories or agencies do the work.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0742"]},{"id":"acr:D-91","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Environmental Engineering Controls are modifications to the work environment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Environmental Engineering Controls?","trap":"ACR is an engineering-controls document. It never specifies personal protective equipment.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-92","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"They permit safe operations and prevent escape or transfer of contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What two purposes do Environmental Engineering Controls serve?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-93","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Containment Area is an engineered space that controls migration of contaminants.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Containment Area?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-94","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Appropriate Negative Pressure prevents debris entry to occupied space.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Appropriate Negative Pressure prevent from reaching occupied space?","trap":"No numeric value is given. Any answer in inches of water column for negative pressure is wrong.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0692","ascs-0744"]},{"id":"acr:D-95","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"It also prevents debris exit from the contained area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Besides keeping debris out of occupied space, what else does Appropriate Negative Pressure prevent?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-96","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard gives no numeric value for negative pressure anywhere.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Does ACR 2025 set a numeric value for negative pressure?","trap":"The 80 in. WC figure is Method 2 vacuum lift, not a containment pressure.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0744"]},{"id":"acr:D-97","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Negative Duct Pressurization is a differential inside the duct against the indoor non-work area.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Negative Duct Pressurization?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-98","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Ambient Air Cleaning removes particulate from indoor air outside of the HVAC system.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Ambient Air Cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0743"]},{"id":"acr:D-99","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Pressure Drop is a loss in pressure from friction, static, or heat.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Pressure Drop?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-100","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-controls-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Frictional resistance to fluid flow usually causes a pressure drop.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What usually causes a pressure drop?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-101","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Friable means easily crumbled or pulverized.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Friable mean in ACR 2025?","trap":"Porous fire-damaged surfaces are checked for friability and odor after cleaning.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0761"]},{"id":"acr:D-102","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Abrasion is a surface loss of material from friction.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Abrasion?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-103","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Antimicrobial describes an agent that kills or inactivates microorganisms, or suppresses growth.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does Antimicrobial describe in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-104","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Surface Treatment repairs surface defects or modifies surface characteristics.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does a Surface Treatment do, per ACR 2025?","trap":"Surface treatments go on only after cleaning and a PASS on verification, and must meet UL 181, not 181A/B.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0739"]},{"id":"acr:D-105","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Wet Process Cleaning uses water or liquid chemicals as part of the process.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Wet Process Cleaning?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-106","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Bioaerosols are airborne particles of biological origin.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Bioaerosols?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-107","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"SDS means Safety Data Sheet.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does SDS stand for in ACR 2025?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-109","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Agitation is a process that uses an agitation device to dislodge non-adhered substances.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Agitation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-110","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Mechanical Agitation is the same as Agitation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Mechanical Agitation in relation to Agitation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-111","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Visual Inspection is examination of cleanliness with the naked eye.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Visual Inspection?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-112","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Surface Comparison Testing determines the cleanliness of porous component surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which surfaces does the 2025 Definitions entry say Surface Comparison Testing evaluates?","trap":"The Definitions entry says porous only, but Section 5.3 says porous and non-porous. The 5.3 body text governs.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-113","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"This Definitions entry still says porous. Section 5.3 says porous and non-porous.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions; §5.3","prompt":"How does the Surface Comparison Testing definition conflict with Section 5.3?","trap":"","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-114","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Access is the ability to gain entry to the interior of the air duct or component.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Access?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-115","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"An Access Door is a fabricated metal barrier that opens or closes a service opening.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define an Access Door?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-116","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"A Panel is a fabricated metal section that forms the structural shell of equipment.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define a Panel?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-117","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"To Seal is to make secure against leakage by a fastener, a coating, or a filler.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What does To Seal mean in ACR 2025?","trap":"Service panel sealing is a SHALL in 2025. It was only recommended in 2021.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-118","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"DEF","text":"Fibrous Glass Insulation is a synthetic material for thermal retention and sound attenuation.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"How does ACR 2025 define Fibrous Glass Insulation?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-119","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The Standard defines SHALL, RECOMMEND, MAY, CAN, and Requirement. See G-19 through G-23.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"Which obligation words does ACR 2025 define?","trap":"MAY is permission. CAN is ability. That pair is the most-missed definition in the Standard.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:D-108","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"acr-process-terms","tag":"NOTE","text":"The 2021 term \"Authorities Having Jurisdiction\" has no definition in 2025.","src":"ACR 2025, Definitions","prompt":"What happened to the 2021 definition of Authorities Having Jurisdiction in ACR 2025?","trap":"AHJ was deleted. 2025 refers to applicable local, regional, provincial or national codes instead.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 covers professional water damage restoration.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S500 cover?","trap":"S500 water, S520 mold, S590 HVAC assessment after damage. Flood water is categorized per S500.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0745"]},{"id":"acr:R-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S520 covers professional mold remediation.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S520 cover?","trap":"S520 projects inspect all components, not the 10% minimum. S520 also defines Conditions 1, 2 and 3.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0773","ascs-0797"]},{"id":"acr:R-3","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S590 covers assessment of HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IICRC S590 cover?","trap":"New reference in 2025. S590 assesses the HVAC system; S500 and S520 restore and remediate.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0746"]},{"id":"acr:R-4","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 covers fire-related particulate on HVAC interior surfaces.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-5","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 covers inspection and maintenance of commercial HVAC systems.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180 cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-6","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"NFPA 90A covers installation of air-conditioning and ventilating systems.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does NFPA 90A cover?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0774"]},{"id":"acr:R-7","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 applies to surface treatments.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does UL 181 apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"UL 181 is for surface treatments. UL 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-8","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181A/B applies to tapes and mastics for closure of service openings.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does UL 181A/B apply to in ACR 2025?","trap":"UL 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics. UL 181 is for surface treatments.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0623"]},{"id":"acr:R-9","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 723 determines the flame-spread rating and the smoke-developed rating.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"Which referenced standard determines flame-spread and smoke-developed ratings?","trap":"Door and panel materials in the airstream: flame-spread not over 25, smoke-developed not over 50. The pair inverts often.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0783"]},{"id":"acr:R-10","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"NIOSH Method 0500 covers total nuisance dust for the Method 3 analysis.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"Which referenced method covers the Method 3 lab analysis?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":["ascs-0568"]},{"id":"acr:R-11","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"The ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual is the source of Table 2.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What is the source of Table 2 in ACR 2025?","trap":"Table 2 gives recommended duct velocities by type of contaminant.","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-12","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IKECA C10-2021 covers cleaning methodology for commercial kitchen exhaust systems.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"What does ANSI/IKECA C10-2021 cover?","trap":"New reference in 2025, along with S590.","delta":true,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"acr:R-13","dom":"Standards","lesson":"acr-references","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA and NAIMA govern installation of thermal-acoustic insulation in the air stream.","src":"ACR 2025, References","prompt":"Which organizations govern installation of thermal-acoustic insulation in the air stream?","trap":"","delta":false,"num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:energy-claim-honest","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"When a customer asks about energy savings, say that restoring airflow and coil heat transfer can improve efficiency, but savings vary. Never quote a fixed or guaranteed percentage.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"How should you answer a homeowner who asks whether cleaning will lower the power bill?","trap":"EPA publishes no average savings figure, so 'EPA says 15 percent' or '20 to 30 percent' claims are unsupportable.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0000","ascs-0034","ascs-0047","ascs-0062"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-energy-evidence","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says research suggests cleaning coils, fans and heat exchangers may improve system efficiency, but little evidence shows that cleaning only the ducts improves it.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"According to EPA, which cleaning work has some evidence of improving efficiency, and which has little?","trap":"The efficiency case rests on components such as coils and fans, not on duct cleaning alone.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0000","ascs-0034"]},{"id":"ascs:fouling-mechanism","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Debris on coils and in ducts restricts airflow and insulates heat-transfer surfaces. Removing it restores designed airflow and heat transfer, which is how cleaning can save energy.","src":"NADCA, Benefits of HVAC System Cleaning","prompt":"How does removing accumulated particulate improve HVAC system efficiency?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0014"]},{"id":"ascs:fouled-coil","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"A fouled cooling coil raises pressure drop and insulates the fins, which cuts airflow, heat transfer and total cooling capacity. Reduced airflow and capacity are its documented consequences, not better dehumidification.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What does fouling on a cooling coil do to pressure drop, airflow and capacity?","trap":"Distractors claim fouling improves dehumidification or latent capacity. The answer is reduced airflow and capacity.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0001"]},{"id":"ascs:fouled-blower-wheel","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Debris on a blower wheel spoils the aerodynamics of its blades, so the fan delivers less air at the same speed.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What does debris on a blower wheel do to delivered airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0009"]},{"id":"ascs:resistance-pairs","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"NOTE","text":"Pairs that the exam runs backwards: a restricted return lowers airflow, a higher MERV filter usually adds resistance and lowers airflow, and a clean coil has less pressure drop than a fouled one.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What do a restricted return, a higher MERV filter and a clean coil each do to airflow or pressure drop?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0009"]},{"id":"ascs:strongest-benefit","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"The strongest benefit to present to an owner is restored design airflow and capacity, because it can be measured, for example as coil pressure drop before and after cleaning.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Which cleaning benefit is the strongest to present to a building owner, and why?","trap":"Guaranteed cuts in sick days, comfort complaints or utility cost are not defensible benefits.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0004","ascs-0052"]},{"id":"ascs:equipment-life","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Cleaning can support longer equipment life because removing fouling reduces the strain on blowers and compressors across the air handler and system. State it as support, never as a guaranteed number of years.","src":"NADCA, Benefits of HVAC System Cleaning","prompt":"Why can cleaning extend HVAC equipment life, and how should you state that benefit?","trap":"Coatings, SEER upgrades and part replacement are not how cleaning extends life; reduced strain is.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0016","ascs-0038","ascs-0068"]},{"id":"ascs:benefit-themes","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Proactive cleaning within a maintenance, energy or IAQ plan supports three benefits: air quality, energy efficiency and equipment life. None of them is an absolute guarantee.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"Which three benefits does proactive HVAC cleaning support?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0025","ascs-0042"]},{"id":"ascs:health-claim","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"The most defensible health-related claim is that cleaning removes contaminants from the system. Claims that it cures allergy or asthma symptoms, or eliminates airborne mold in the building, are unsupportable.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is the most defensible claim about duct cleaning and occupant health?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0002"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-health-evidence","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA states that duct cleaning has never been shown to actually prevent health problems, so a health outcome is not something a contractor can promise.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What does EPA say about duct cleaning and preventing health problems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0002","ascs-0063"]},{"id":"ascs:iaq-claim","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Improved indoor air quality is a core benefit of cleaning, but cleaning supports IAQ rather than guaranteeing it. Never promise a specific IAQ result, on any type of job.","src":"NADCA, Benefits of HVAC System Cleaning","prompt":"How should an ASCS describe the IAQ benefit of cleaning?","trap":"Residential and healthcare jobs are not exceptions. No job type allows an IAQ guarantee.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0013","ascs-0026","ascs-0035"]},{"id":"ascs:allergy-response","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"When a customer wants cleaning to end allergy symptoms, document the conditions, define the scope and explain what source removal does. Do not promise a medical outcome; refer medical questions to a physician.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"How do you respond to a homeowner who wants a guarantee that cleaning will eliminate allergy symptoms?","trap":"Adding an antimicrobial or including the whole supply side never makes a health guarantee acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0043","ascs-0063"]},{"id":"ascs:odor-scope","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Cleaning removes odor sources inside the HVAC system. Odors that originate elsewhere in the building will survive even a perfect duct cleaning.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What can you accurately tell an owner who expects cleaning to solve a persistent odor?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0005"]},{"id":"ascs:fire-fuel-load","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Accumulated lint and debris, as in dryer or laundry exhaust, are a fuel load. Cleaning reduces the fire risk; it does not eliminate it.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"How should fire risk be framed when discussing HVAC and exhaust cleaning?","trap":"Fire risk is not limited to commercial kitchens, and no cleaning eliminates it.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0007","ascs-0066"]},{"id":"ascs:no-stay-clean-guarantee","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Do not guarantee that a system will stay clean for years. Reloading depends on filtration, use and conditions you do not control, and a duct coating does not change that.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"How should you answer an owner who wants a five-year guarantee that the system will stay clean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0008"]},{"id":"ascs:expectations-scope","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Set expectations before the job: cleaning removes non-adhered contaminants. It does not repair mechanical faults, replace filter maintenance, or guarantee odor or IAQ numbers.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Why must customer expectations be set before an HVAC cleaning project?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0017","ascs-0041"]},{"id":"ascs:proposal-language","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Proposal language that manages expectations states the findings, the components in scope, the exclusions, and how cleanliness will be verified.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should proposal language state to manage customer expectations?","trap":"Price, schedule and crew size do not manage expectations; promising resolved complaints overpromises.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0056"]},{"id":"ascs:before-after-photos","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"The strongest proof of value delivered is before and after photographs of the components. Energy estimates, product certificates and signed statements are assertions, not evidence.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is the strongest way to document the value delivered to a customer?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0010"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-routine-position","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA does not recommend cleaning air ducts routinely on a schedule. It recommends cleaning only as needed, when specific conditions exist.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What is EPA's position on routine duct cleaning?","trap":"EPA sets no annual interval and asks for no documented complaint; it ties cleaning to conditions such as mold, vermin or heavy debris.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0003"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-conditions","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says to consider cleaning when there is substantial visible mold growth inside hard surface ducts, the ducts are infested with vermin, or they are clogged with debris or releasing particles into the home.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"Under which three conditions does EPA say to consider having ducts cleaned?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0003"]},{"id":"ascs:condition-drives-recommendation","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"Base a cleaning recommendation on inspection findings, such as loose debris, visible discharge or pest debris. Elapsed time, system age, a new owner or a general request are not conditions.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should a recommendation to clean be based on?","trap":"Adhered stains, discoloration, faded labels and an old serial number are cosmetic, not contamination.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0006","ascs-0044","ascs-0048","ascs-0054"]},{"id":"ascs:maintenance-program-decision","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"A formal inspection and maintenance program can trigger an assessment, but the cleaning decision still rests on the findings and the program's requirements, not on cleaning at every inspection.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"How should a formal inspection and maintenance program affect the decision to clean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0055"]},{"id":"ascs:source-removal-why","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-benefits-claims","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA favors source removal because it eliminates the contaminant at its origin. Sanitizing or encapsulating without removal leaves the source in place to be re-entrained.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"Why does NADCA emphasize source removal over sanitizing alone?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0040"]},{"id":"ascs:sbs-vs-bri","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Sick building syndrome: occupants report discomfort symptoms with no known cause, and most get relief soon after leaving. Building-related illness: a clinically defined illness with an identifiable cause, such as Legionnaires' disease, and recovery can take longer.","src":"EPA Indoor Air Facts No. 4, Sick Building Syndrome","prompt":"How does sick building syndrome differ from building-related illness?","trap":"Distractors swap the pair. SBS clears after leaving; BRI has a diagnosable cause.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0058"]},{"id":"ascs:soot","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Soot is a byproduct of incomplete combustion. It deposits in plenums and ductwork near combustion equipment such as boilers and generators.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII; industry practice","prompt":"Which contaminant is a byproduct of incomplete combustion found near boilers and generators?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0059"]},{"id":"ascs:biological-vs-particulate","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Biological contaminants include bacteria, viruses, mold, and animal or insect byproducts. Drywall dust, fiberglass fragments and soot are non-biological particulate.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII (contaminant list); industry practice","prompt":"Which contaminants are biological rather than particulate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0060"]},{"id":"ascs:respirable-particulate","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Respirable particulate is small enough to stay suspended in air and reach the deep lung, which is why HEPA filtration and containment matter during source removal.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What are particles small enough to stay airborne and reach the deep lung called?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0061"]},{"id":"ascs:voc-sources","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"VOCs in an HVAC system mostly off-gas from solvents, adhesives, paints, coatings and cleaning chemicals.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Where do VOCs entering an HVAC system most commonly come from?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0064"]},{"id":"ascs:infestation-contaminates","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Bird, rodent and insect infestation matters because droppings, carcasses and nesting material contaminate the airstream and create health hazards.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Why is bird, rodent or insect infestation a reason to clean HVAC components?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0065"]},{"id":"ascs:construction-particulate","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Construction and remodel particulate typically includes gypsum dust, sawdust and silica, which sets it apart from ordinary household dust.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What materials make construction particulate different from ordinary dust?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0069"]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-infiltration","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","tag":"FACT","text":"Open air plenums are not fully sealed, so humidity and unconditioned outside air can infiltrate, leading to condensation, odors and microbial growth.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Why can condensation form inside an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0067"]},{"id":"ascs:osha-governs","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA sets and enforces worker safety regulations on U.S. HVAC cleaning job sites. EPA handles environmental rules, not worker safety.","src":"Occupational Safety and Health Act; 29 CFR 1910 and 1926","prompt":"Which agency's regulations govern worker safety on U.S. HVAC cleaning job sites?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0678"]},{"id":"ascs:loto-sequence","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA energy control runs in a fixed order: know the energy, shut down, isolate, apply locks or tags, relieve stored energy, then verify isolation before work starts.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d)","prompt":"What is the required order of steps when applying lockout/tagout?","trap":"Locking after work starts, or bleeding stored energy before isolating, breaks the sequence.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0689"]},{"id":"ascs:loto-authorized","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"The authorized employee is the person who applies the lockout or tagout devices in order to service or maintain the equipment.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b)","prompt":"Who is the authorized employee under lockout/tagout?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0688"]},{"id":"ascs:loto-affected","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"An affected employee operates or uses the equipment being serviced, or works in the area, but does not apply the lockout. Affected employees are notified before locks go on and after they come off.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b), (c)(9)","prompt":"Who is an affected employee under lockout/tagout?","trap":"The worker who applies the device and verifies isolation is authorized, not affected.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0695"]},{"id":"ascs:loto-verify","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"After locking and tagging, verify isolation by trying a normal start to confirm the equipment will not run, then return the operating control to neutral or off.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d)(6); Appendix A","prompt":"How do you verify isolation after locking and tagging an AHU disconnect?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0715"]},{"id":"ascs:loto-removal","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Each lockout or tagout device is removed only by the employee who applied it. The only exception is a documented employer procedure used when that employee is not available.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(e)(3)","prompt":"Who may remove a personal lockout device?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0714"]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-147","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.147, the control of hazardous energy standard, is OSHA's lockout/tagout rule.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147","prompt":"Which OSHA standard is the lockout/tagout rule?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's lockout/tagout standard is 29 CFR 1910.__ .","a":"147","d":["146","134","1200"]},"qs":["ascs-0693"]},{"id":"ascs:gfci-tools","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"On construction sites, workers using portable electric tools must be protected by GFCIs or by an assured equipment grounding conductor program.","src":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1)","prompt":"What must protect workers using portable electric tools on a job site?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0680","ascs-0709"]},{"id":"ascs:double-insulated","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"A listed tool protected by double insulation, and marked as such, need not be grounded, so it may lack a grounding prong. Every other safe-use rule still applies.","src":"29 CFR 1926.404(f)(7)(iv)(C)(6)","prompt":"What may a tool marked double insulated legitimately do?","trap":"Double insulation does not approve a tool for wet locations, removing guards or skipping cord inspection.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0710"]},{"id":"ascs:cord-handling","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Never raise, lower or carry a tool by its cord, and unplug by the plug body, never by yanking the cord.","src":"29 CFR 1910.334(a)(1); OSHA 3080 Hand and Power Tools","prompt":"Which cord handling practices are prohibited with portable electric tools?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0711"]},{"id":"ascs:damaged-cord","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Inspect cords before use on each shift. A cord with damaged outer insulation is removed from service until properly repaired and tested; wrapping it with tape does not restore it.","src":"29 CFR 1910.334(a)(2)","prompt":"What must happen to an extension cord with cracked insulation exposing conductors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0712"]},{"id":"ascs:extension-cord-type","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-loto-electrical","tag":"REG","text":"Extension cords used with portable electric tools on construction sites must be three-wire type and designed for hard or extra-hard usage.","src":"29 CFR 1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(J)","prompt":"What type of extension cord is required for portable tools on a job site?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0713"]},{"id":"ascs:confined-space-def","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A confined space is large enough to bodily enter and work in, has limited or restricted entry or exit, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. A walk-in AHU can meet all three.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"What three conditions make a space a confined space under OSHA?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0670","ascs-0675"]},{"id":"ascs:confined-not-permit","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Meeting the confined space definition does not by itself require a permit. The space must be evaluated, and whether it is permit-required depends on the hazards found.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b), (c)(1)","prompt":"Is an enterable AHU automatically a permit-required confined space?","trap":"Locking out the unit does not exempt the space from evaluation.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0670"]},{"id":"ascs:permit-space-triggers","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A confined space is permit-required if it has any one of four hazards: a hazardous atmosphere, material that can engulf, an entrapping internal configuration, or any other recognized serious hazard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Which four characteristics make a confined space permit-required?","trap":"Size, entry frequency and crew size are never triggers.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0702"]},{"id":"ascs:entrapment-config","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Inwardly converging walls, or a floor that slopes down and tapers to a smaller cross-section, make a space permit-required because they can trap or asphyxiate an entrant.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Why do inwardly converging walls or a tapering floor make a space permit-required?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0703"]},{"id":"ascs:o2-deficient","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA defines an oxygen deficient atmosphere as one containing less than 19.5 percent oxygen by volume.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Below what oxygen level is an atmosphere oxygen deficient?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's cutoff for an oxygen deficient atmosphere is __ oxygen by volume.","a":"19.5 percent","d":["23.5 percent","20.9 percent","18 percent"]},"qs":["ascs-0690","ascs-0705"]},{"id":"ascs:o2-enriched","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"An atmosphere with more than 23.5 percent oxygen by volume is oxygen enriched, which raises the fire and combustion hazard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"Above what oxygen level is an atmosphere oxygen enriched?","trap":"","num":{"q":"An atmosphere with more than __ oxygen by volume is oxygen enriched.","a":"23.5 percent","d":["19.5 percent","20.9 percent","22 percent"]},"qs":["ascs-0696","ascs-0705"]},{"id":"ascs:flammable-10-lfl","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"A flammable gas, vapor or mist above 10 percent of its lower flammable limit (LFL, also called LEL) makes a confined space atmosphere hazardous.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"What concentration of flammable gas makes a confined space atmosphere hazardous?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's hazardous atmosphere cutoff for flammable gas, vapor or mist is __ of its lower flammable limit.","a":"10 percent","d":["1 percent","50 percent","100 percent"]},"qs":["ascs-0706"]},{"id":"ascs:test-order","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Test a permit space atmosphere in this order: oxygen first, then combustible gases and vapors, then toxic gases and vapors. Combustible gas meters need oxygen to read correctly.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d)(5)(iii)","prompt":"In what order do you test the atmosphere before permit space entry?","trap":"The order is written into the regulation; 'any order if all three are tested' is wrong.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0704"]},{"id":"ascs:attendant-duty","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"The attendant stays outside the permit space for the whole entry, keeps count of entrants, monitors conditions inside and out, and orders evacuation when conditions turn unsafe.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(i)","prompt":"What is the attendant's primary duty during a permit-required entry?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0707"]},{"id":"ascs:attendant-rescue","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"If an entrant collapses, the attendant summons rescue services and attempts non-entry rescue with the retrieval system. Untrained would-be rescuers who go in often become added fatalities.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(i)(4), (i)(7), (i)(9), (k)(3)","prompt":"What should the attendant do first when an entrant collapses inside a permit space?","trap":"An attendant may enter only if trained and equipped for rescue and relieved by another attendant.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0708"]},{"id":"ascs:permit-flow","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"Before entry, the permit records completed preparations and test results, the entry supervisor signs it, and it is posted at the portal. The supervisor cancels it when the work ends; cancelled permits are kept at least 1 year.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(e), (f)","prompt":"How is a confined space entry permit authorized, displayed, closed out and retained?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's minimum retention period for cancelled confined space entry permits is __ .","a":"1 year","d":["30 days","3 years","6 months"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-146","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-confined-spaces","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.146 is OSHA's permit-required confined spaces standard for general industry. Confined spaces in construction fall under 29 CFR 1926 Subpart AA.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(a); 29 CFR 1926 Subpart AA","prompt":"Which OSHA standard covers permit-required confined spaces?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's general industry permit-required confined spaces standard is 29 CFR 1910.__ .","a":"146","d":["147","134","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:engineering-first","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA's first line against airborne contaminants is engineering controls such as enclosure and ventilation. Respirators are required when those controls are not feasible or while they are being put in place.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(a)(1)","prompt":"When does OSHA require respirators instead of relying on engineering controls alone?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0676"]},{"id":"ascs:resp-hazard-selection","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"Respirator selection is based on the respiratory hazard present and on workplace and user factors, found through an assessment. Contract terms, company minimums and exposure duration are inputs, not the basis.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d)(1)(i)","prompt":"What drives respirator selection in a contaminated system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0672","ascs-0676"]},{"id":"ascs:resp-program","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"Required respirator use needs a written program with hazard-based selection, medical evaluation, training and, for tight-fitting facepieces, fit testing.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(c), (d), (e), (f), (k)","prompt":"What must be in place before an employee is required to wear a tight-fitting respirator?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0691"]},{"id":"ascs:resp-order","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"For a required tight-fitting respirator, medical evaluation comes first, then fit testing, repeated at least annually, and a user seal check every time the respirator is put on.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(e)(1), (f)(2), (g)(1)(iii)","prompt":"In what order do medical evaluation, fit testing and user seal checks happen, and how often?","trap":"Fit testing is at least annual; the user seal check is every donning. Do not swap them.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0716"]},{"id":"ascs:facial-hair","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A tight-fitting respirator may not be worn when facial hair comes between the facepiece sealing surface and the face, or interferes with valve function.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(g)(1)(i)(A)","prompt":"Which condition makes wearing a required tight-fitting respirator unacceptable?","trap":"Corrective glasses are allowed if they do not disturb the facepiece seal.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0697"]},{"id":"ascs:apf-half-mask","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A half-mask air-purifying respirator, including an N95 filtering facepiece, has an OSHA assigned protection factor of 10.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d)(3)(i)(A), Table 1","prompt":"What is OSHA's assigned protection factor for a half-mask or filtering facepiece respirator?","trap":"A full facepiece air-purifying respirator is 50; a quarter mask is 5.","num":{"q":"A half-mask air-purifying respirator has an assigned protection factor of __ .","a":"10","d":["5","25","50"]},"qs":["ascs-0717"]},{"id":"ascs:sds-content","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"A Safety Data Sheet gives a chemical's hazards, safe handling and storage, exposure controls and PPE, and first-aid measures in 16 standard sections. Employers keep one readily accessible for each hazardous chemical used.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)","prompt":"What does a Safety Data Sheet give workers, and how must it be kept?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0677"]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-134","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.134 is OSHA's respiratory protection standard.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134","prompt":"Which OSHA standard covers respiratory protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's respiratory protection standard is 29 CFR 1910.__ .","a":"134","d":["146","147","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-1910-1200","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-respirators-sds","tag":"REG","text":"29 CFR 1910.1200 is OSHA's hazard communication standard, which requires container labels, Safety Data Sheets and employee training on hazardous chemicals.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(e), (f), (g), (h)","prompt":"Which OSHA standard is hazard communication, and what does it require?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's hazard communication standard is 29 CFR 1910.__ .","a":"1200","d":["134","146","147"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:risk-starts-hazards","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Risk management on an HVAC project begins by identifying job-site hazards, then applying controls such as engineering controls, PPE and procedures throughout the work.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA Safety Manual)","prompt":"What does risk management on an HVAC project begin with?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0684"]},{"id":"ascs:exposure-effects","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Microbial and particulate contaminants disturbed during cleaning can harm both workers and occupants, even when they are not visible, so exposure must be controlled.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA Safety Manual)","prompt":"Why must worker and occupant exposure be controlled during HVAC cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0683"]},{"id":"ascs:suspect-acm-action","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"When you find suspect asbestos-containing material, treat it as asbestos, stop any work that would disturb it, and notify the owner. Do not bag, wet, encapsulate or clean it yourself.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; industry practice","prompt":"What do you do when suspect asbestos-containing insulation is found on a job?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0671","ascs-0718"]},{"id":"ascs:acm-licensed-only","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Sampling, assessing or abating suspect asbestos is work for qualified, licensed asbestos professionals, not for the duct cleaning crew.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; state asbestos licensing","prompt":"Who samples or abates suspect asbestos-containing material?","trap":"Collecting a bulk sample yourself is the classic wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0671","ascs-0718"]},{"id":"ascs:pacm-1980","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA presumes that thermal system insulation and surfacing material in buildings built no later than 1980 contain asbestos (PACM) unless testing rebuts it.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b); 29 CFR 1910.1001(b)","prompt":"In buildings of what age does OSHA presume thermal system insulation contains asbestos?","trap":"1978 is the lead paint cutoff; 1980 is the asbestos presumption.","num":{"q":"The last construction year covered by OSHA's PACM presumption for thermal system insulation is __ .","a":"1980","d":["1978","1989","1970"]},"qs":["ascs-0719"]},{"id":"ascs:osha-asbestos-lead-map","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"In construction work, OSHA's asbestos standard is 29 CFR 1926.1101 and its lead standard is 29 CFR 1926.62. Paid renovation in pre-1978 homes also falls under EPA's RRP rule, 40 CFR 745.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101; 29 CFR 1926.62; 40 CFR 745 Subpart E","prompt":"Which federal rules cover asbestos and lead exposure during construction and renovation work?","trap":"","num":{"q":"OSHA's construction asbestos standard is 29 CFR 1926.__ .","a":"1101","d":["62","501","1200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-hazmat","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"In older buildings, open air plenums may contain asbestos and other hazardous materials, so PPE and job site safety are evaluated before anyone enters.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V, §VII","prompt":"Which hazardous materials does NADCA flag as possible in older-building open air plenums?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0698"]},{"id":"ascs:lead-presume","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"In housing and child-occupied facilities built before 1978, treat painted surfaces as lead-based paint unless a certified inspector, risk assessor or renovator has tested them free of lead-based paint.","src":"40 CFR 745.82(a); 745.103","prompt":"How do you treat painted surfaces in pre-1978 housing before cutting access holes?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EPA's target housing cutoff year is __ ; housing built before that year is covered.","a":"1978","d":["1980","1989","1970"]},"qs":["ascs-0673"]},{"id":"ascs:rrp-scope","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's Renovation, Repair and Painting rule covers paid work that disturbs paint in pre-1978 housing and child-occupied facilities. Cutting holes in painted surfaces to gain access counts as renovation.","src":"40 CFR 745.82, 745.83","prompt":"Which rule applies when HVAC access work disturbs paint in a pre-1978 home?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0720"]},{"id":"ascs:rrp-minor-repair","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"REG","text":"Minor HVAC work falls outside RRP renovation if it disturbs 6 square feet or less of painted surface per room inside, or 20 square feet or less outside, with no prohibited practices.","src":"40 CFR 745.83 (minor repair and maintenance)","prompt":"How much painted surface can minor HVAC work disturb before RRP renovation rules apply?","trap":"Window replacement and demolition of painted surfaces never qualify as minor repair.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:osha-mold-guide","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"OSHA publishes 'A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace', the OSHA reference on the ASCS reading list.","src":"OSHA SHIB 03-10-10; ASCS Candidate Guide","prompt":"Which OSHA publication on the ASCS reference list addresses mold?","trap":"EPA, not OSHA, publishes Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0687","ascs-0799"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-mold-guide","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA publishes 'Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings' (EPA 402-K-01-001), which appears in ACR's referenced documents.","src":"EPA 402-K-01-001","prompt":"Which agency publishes Mold Remediation in Schools and Commercial Buildings?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0799"]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-rmp","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Technicians cleaning an open air plenum shall follow risk-reduction protocols, and NADCA recommends a Risk Management Plan or Job Hazard Analysis, because plenums hold essential electrical, plumbing, gas, fire protection, sewer and telecom systems.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VI","prompt":"What does NADCA recommend developing before working in an open air plenum, and why?","trap":"The risk-reduction protocols are 'shall'; the RMP or JHA itself is only recommended.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0699"]},{"id":"ascs:rmp-contents","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"A plenum Risk Management Plan communicates each risk item near essential systems, the likelihood and severity of a risk event, risk-reduction actions, emergency-response actions, and an emergency contact.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VI","prompt":"What should an open air plenum Risk Management Plan communicate?","trap":"Payment terms and change-order pricing are not part of it.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0700"]},{"id":"ascs:plenum-training","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Extra training that may be required before entering an open air plenum includes confined space, air monitoring, rig to rescue or rope work, ladder safety, fall protection and combustible dust.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII","prompt":"What additional training may be required before entering an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0701"]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90A, the standard for installing air-conditioning and ventilating systems, drives fire damper placement, smoke detection, and flame and smoke limits for materials in air-handling spaces.","src":"NFPA 90A","prompt":"Which standard governs fire dampers and smoke detection in air distribution systems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0792","ascs-0796"]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90B is the standard for installing warm air heating and air-conditioning systems.","src":"NFPA 90B","prompt":"What does NFPA 90B cover?","trap":"90A is air-conditioning and ventilating systems; 90B adds warm air heating.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0748"]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-90a-90b-split","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 90B applies to one- and two-family dwellings and to spaces of 25,000 cubic feet or less. NFPA 90A applies to larger spaces, typically commercial and institutional buildings.","src":"NFPA 90A §1.3; NFPA 90B §1.3","prompt":"How is the scope split between NFPA 90A and NFPA 90B?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0792"]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-96","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA 96 is the standard for ventilation control and fire protection of commercial cooking operations.","src":"NFPA 96","prompt":"What does NFPA 96 cover?","trap":"The kitchen exhaust cleaning methodology is IKECA C10; NFPA 96 is the fire protection standard.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0748"]},{"id":"ascs:surface-burning-tests","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ASTM E84, UL 723 and the former NFPA 255 are the same surface burning characteristics test, giving a material's flame spread and smoke developed ratings.","src":"ASTM E84; UL 723; NFPA 255 (withdrawn)","prompt":"What do ASTM E84, UL 723 and NFPA 255 have in common?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0748","ascs-0756","ascs-0783"]},{"id":"ascs:ul-181","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 181 is the standard for factory-made air ducts and air connectors.","src":"UL 181","prompt":"What is UL 181 the standard for?","trap":"Closure systems are UL 181A and 181B, not UL 181 itself.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0749"]},{"id":"ascs:ul-181a-181b","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"UL 181A covers closure systems such as tapes and mastics for rigid air ducts and connectors. UL 181B covers closure systems for flexible air ducts and connectors.","src":"UL 181A; UL 181B","prompt":"What is the difference between UL 181A and UL 181B?","trap":"A is rigid, B is flexible. The exam swaps them.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0750","ascs-0782"]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-006","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 is the HVAC Duct Construction Standards, Metal and Flexible. Duct cleanliness for new work is covered by a separate SMACNA guideline.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 006","prompt":"What is ANSI/SMACNA 006?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0794"]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction Guidelines set three levels, in order of increasing restriction: basic, intermediate and advanced. Basic is assumed when the specifications name no level.","src":"SMACNA, Duct Cleanliness for New Construction Guidelines","prompt":"What cleanliness levels do SMACNA's new construction guidelines set, and which applies by default?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0772","ascs-0795"]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah116","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA AH116 is the Fibrous Glass Duct Construction Standards.","src":"NAIMA AH116","prompt":"What is NAIMA AH116?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0752"]},{"id":"ascs:astm-c1071","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"ASTM C1071 is the standard specification for fibrous glass duct lining insulation.","src":"ASTM C1071","prompt":"What is ASTM C1071?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0752","ascs-0756"]},{"id":"ascs:nfpa-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"NFPA, the National Fire Protection Association, publishes fire and life-safety standards such as NFPA 90A, 90B and 96.","src":"NFPA","prompt":"Which organization publishes NFPA 90A and related fire and life-safety standards?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0789"]},{"id":"ascs:smacna-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-fire-duct","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA is the Sheet Metal and Air Conditioning Contractors' National Association, publisher of the HVAC Duct Construction Standards and other sheet metal duct guidance.","src":"SMACNA","prompt":"What does SMACNA stand for, and what does it publish?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0776","ascs-0788"]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-62-1","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE Standard 62.1 covers ventilation and acceptable indoor air quality in commercial and other non-residential buildings.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1","prompt":"What does ASHRAE Standard 62.1 cover?","trap":"62.1 is commercial, 62.2 is residential, 170 is healthcare, 180 is maintenance.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0238","ascs-0758","ascs-0857"]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-62-2","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE Standard 62.2 covers ventilation and acceptable indoor air quality in residential buildings.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.2","prompt":"What does ASHRAE Standard 62.2 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0758","ascs-0857"]},{"id":"ascs:ashrae-170","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 covers ventilation of health care facilities.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170","prompt":"What does ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0751"]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah122","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA, the North American Insulation Manufacturers Association, publishes AH122, the recommended practice for cleaning fibrous glass insulated air duct systems.","src":"NAIMA AH122","prompt":"Which organization and document cover cleaning fibrous glass insulated air duct systems?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0752","ascs-0775","ascs-0787"]},{"id":"ascs:naima-ah110","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NAIMA AH110 addresses fiber glass HVAC insulation and indoor environmental quality.","src":"NAIMA AH110","prompt":"What does NAIMA AH110 address?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0752"]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-std-05","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA Standard 05 sets the requirements for installing service openings in HVAC systems.","src":"NADCA Standard 05","prompt":"What does NADCA Standard 05 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0753"]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-std-03","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA Standard 03 sets the requirements for testing vacuum collection equipment.","src":"NADCA Standard 03","prompt":"What does NADCA Standard 03 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0754"]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-position-paper","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ACR 2025's reference list names two NADCA position papers: Using Chemical Products in HVAC Systems, and Inspection and Cleaning of Open Air Plenums in HVAC Systems. NADCA Standard 05, NAIMA AH122 and IESO/RIA 6001 are not position papers.","src":"ACR 2025, Reference Documents and Resources","prompt":"Which two NADCA documents in ACR 2025's reference list are position papers?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0755"]},{"id":"ascs:nmam-5th","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"The NIOSH publication ACR references is the NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, Fifth Edition, which contains Method 0500 used for Method 3 analysis.","src":"NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, 5th ed.; ACR Referenced Documents","prompt":"Which NIOSH publication does ACR reference for Method 3 analysis?","trap":"Industrial Ventilation is ACGIH's manual; the Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards is a different NIOSH book.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0757"]},{"id":"ascs:nyc-mold-guidelines","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"The city health department mold guidance in ACR's references is New York City's Guidelines on Assessment and Remediation of Fungi in Indoor Environments.","src":"NYC DOHMH; ACR Referenced Documents","prompt":"Which city's health department mold guidelines are in ACR's references?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0759"]},{"id":"ascs:prelim-determination-s520","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"DEF","text":"ACR's definition of preliminary determination comes from ANSI/IICRC S520: a conclusion from an initial inspection about moisture intrusion, actual or potential growth, and the need for other specialized experts.","src":"ACR Definitions (citing ANSI/IICRC S520)","prompt":"Which document is the source of the term preliminary determination, and what does it mean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0762"]},{"id":"ascs:acca-org","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"ACCA is the Air Conditioning Contractors of America, which co-sponsors ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 with ASHRAE.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180","prompt":"What is ACCA, and which standard does it co-sponsor?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0784","ascs-0793"]},{"id":"ascs:iep-acronym","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"IEP stands for Indoor Environmental Professional, the qualified party who assesses contamination.","src":"ASCS Candidate Guide acronym list","prompt":"What does IEP stand for?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0800"]},{"id":"ascs:mua-acronym","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-reference-docs","tag":"FACT","text":"MUA stands for make up air, the outdoor air brought in to replace air exhausted from a space.","src":"ASCS Candidate Guide acronym list","prompt":"What does MUA stand for?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0801"]},{"id":"ascs:acr-consensus","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"ACR is a consensus industry standard, not a regulation. It becomes binding when a contract specifies it or an authority adopts it.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"How is ACR best described, and when does it become binding?","trap":"ACR is not an OSHA code or an EPA regulation.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0765"]},{"id":"ascs:code-over-standard","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"An industry standard never overrides an adopted code. When ACR and the local mechanical code conflict, the code governs.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is the relationship between ACR and the local mechanical code when they conflict?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0766"]},{"id":"ascs:spec-conflict","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"When a project specification and ACR conflict on a method, resolve it with the specifier before proceeding. Silently following either one leaves you exposed.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should you do when a project specification and ACR conflict on a cleaning method?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0768"]},{"id":"ascs:epa-role","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's role in duct cleaning is to register antimicrobial products under FIFRA and publish guidance. It does not certify or license duct cleaners, set cleaning intervals, or enforce ACR.","src":"FIFRA; EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What is EPA's role relative to duct cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0767"]},{"id":"ascs:chemical-governing-set","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"REG","text":"A chemical application in an HVAC system is governed by the product label, the manufacturer's instructions, applicable law and the SDS exposure controls, not by customer requests, schedules or a signed release.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems; 29 CFR 1910.1200","prompt":"What should govern a chemical application in an HVAC system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0790","ascs-0791"]},{"id":"ascs:hepa-type","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"HEPA is defined by performance, not by name. Filters sold as 'HEPA-type' or 'HEPA-like' do not meet the HEPA definition and are not acceptable where ACR requires HEPA.","src":"DOE HEPA definition; industry practice","prompt":"Is a filter marketed as HEPA-type acceptable where ACR requires HEPA?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0771"]},{"id":"ascs:nadca-founded","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA was formed in 1989 by air duct cleaning companies who agreed that source removal was the only acceptable way to clean ductwork and wanted to discourage encapsulation.","src":"NADCA, History","prompt":"When was NADCA formed, and what cleaning method did it form to promote?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NADCA was formed in __ to promote source removal.","a":"1989","d":["1995","1980","1978"]},"qs":["ascs-0011"]},{"id":"ascs:certifies-individuals","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA certifies individuals, not companies. An ASCS who changes companies must complete a certification transfer form and pay a transfer fee.","src":"NADCA, ASCS certification page","prompt":"Who is ASCS certification issued to, and what happens when an ASCS changes companies?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0769"]},{"id":"ascs:ascs-renewal","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"ASCS certification is renewed every year, before June 30, for a fee.","src":"NADCA, ASCS certification page","prompt":"How often, and by what date, is ASCS certification renewed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:membership-ascs","dom":"Standards","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"A NADCA member company must have at least one full-time ASCS on staff for each member location, and one ASCS may not be assigned to more than one member or location.","src":"NADCA, Regular and Supplemental Membership requirements","prompt":"What ASCS staffing does NADCA membership require?","trap":"Not every technician must be an ASCS, and a CVI is not a membership condition.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0770"]},{"id":"ascs:ascs-roles","dom":"Benefits","lesson":"ascs-std-nadca-law","tag":"FACT","text":"Per the candidate guide, an ASCS is qualified to serve as project manager for residential and commercial HVAC cleaning and restoration, and as a ventilation system mold remediator.","src":"NADCA ASCS Candidate Guide","prompt":"Besides project manager for HVAC cleaning, what role is an ASCS qualified for?","trap":"ASCS does not make someone a licensed HVAC installer, engineer or industrial hygienist.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0012"]},{"id":"ascs:p-mechanical-plans-walkthrough","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Mechanical drawings are the plan set that shows AHU locations, duct routing, terminal units and zoning, which is what an inspection walk-through needs. Structural, electrical and site plans do not show these.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Which drawing set best locates AHUs, duct routing and terminal units before an inspection?","trap":"ACR 1.3 requires using both floor and mechanical plans if they exist; this card is about which set shows the equipment.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0328"]},{"id":"ascs:p-black-fallout-liner","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Black particulate settling from supply diffusers classically points to deteriorating internal fibrous glass liner or insulation shedding into the airstream, so the liner's condition is the first thing to assess.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A client reports black particulate falling from the diffusers onto desks. What should the inspection check first?","trap":"External duct wrap and jacketing sit outside the airstream and cannot shed into the space.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0327"]},{"id":"ascs:p-filter-bypass","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Filter bypass is gaps around a filter rack or frame that let unfiltered air go around the media, carrying particulate to the coil and ductwork no matter what MERV the filter has.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is filter bypass, and why does a higher MERV filter not fix it?","trap":"Not filter loading (dirt held in the media) or face velocity (air speed at the filter face).","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0326"]},{"id":"ascs:p-multizone-damper-leak","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"On a multizone unit, leaking zone dampers blend hot deck and cold deck air unintentionally, causing simultaneous heating and cooling, wasted energy and temperature complaints across the zones.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"During inspection of a multizone unit, what do leaking zone dampers most directly cause?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0325"]},{"id":"ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Standing water and slimy biofilm in a drain pan usually trace to a clogged drain line, a missing or wrong P-trap, or poor pan slope. Clean the pan and verify drainage works, or it comes back.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A drain pan holds standing water and biofilm. What is the likely root cause, and what must the scope include?","trap":"An antimicrobial tablet, a new pan, or cleaning alone leaves the drainage fault in place.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0331","ascs-0493"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-growth-remediation","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Visible microbial growth on porous duct liner makes the job remediation, with its own scope, plus containment and an IEP where the situation warrants. A modest area such as 40 sq ft does not make it routine cleaning.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Visible growth covers about 40 sq ft of porous duct liner. How is the job treated?","trap":"Under 100 sq ft is not a routine cleaning cutoff, and antimicrobial plus cleaning does not fix growth on liner.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0330"]},{"id":"ascs:p-suspect-acm-scope","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","tag":"FACT","text":"Suspect asbestos-containing material found during an assessment stops the work and changes the scope, bringing in other trades. Heavy dust, a loaded blower wheel or an undersized filter rack are ordinary findings.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Which assessment finding most clearly changes the scope of work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0283"]},{"id":"ascs:p-inspect-before-quote","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Before quoting a commercial job, inspect it in person. Access, system configuration and existing conditions decide the equipment, labor, containment and risk, and estimating without inspection produces unworkable scopes.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is the main reason to physically inspect a commercial project before quoting it?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0332"]},{"id":"ascs:p-scope-by-component","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Scope follows each component's documented condition. A heavily fouled AHU with visually clean supply ducts is scoped and reported component by component, so the customer can make an informed decision.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"The AHU is heavily fouled but the supply ducts look clean. How is the scope set?","trap":"Not an automatic single whole-system scope, not deferral, and not replacing the clean ducts.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0333"]},{"id":"ascs:p-whole-system-recontamination","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Failing to clean one component of a contaminated system can recontaminate the entire system and negate the benefits, so cleaning is meant to cover all components, including equipment and terminal devices.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"Why should cleaning a contaminated system cover all of its components?","trap":"Components upstream of the filter and non-removable terminal devices are not automatically excluded.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0496"]},{"id":"ascs:p-limited-scope-documented","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"A customer may limit the scope, such as supply side only. Do the work, document the limited scope and its implications, and never present it as a whole-system cleaning.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A customer wants the supply side cleaned and declines the return. What should you do?","trap":"Refusing the job is wrong too; the violation is issuing a standard system cleaning certificate.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0497"]},{"id":"ascs:p-create-access","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"When a duct section has no access, create access as needed to clean it. Leaving it out without saying so, or assuming nearby cleaning covers it, misrepresents the cleaning.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"During assessment you find a duct section with no access. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0281"]},{"id":"ascs:p-representative-sampling","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"When a specification calls for gravimetric verification, take the samples from representative locations. Picking only the dirtiest or only the easiest spots invalidates the result.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A specification calls for gravimetric verification. Where are the samples taken?","trap":"Every branch run or the air handler only are also wrong answers.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0282"]},{"id":"ascs:p-reclean-visual-failure","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"If post-cleaning visual inspection shows a light film or residue, reclean those surfaces. Method 2 or Method 3 testing is for a disputed or inconclusive visual, not a way to test past a clear visual failure.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A post-cleaning visual inspection shows a light film on interior surfaces. What is correct?","trap":"Coating to bind the residue, or accepting some residue as normal, are both wrong.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0285"]},{"id":"ascs:p-closeout-documentation","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","tag":"FACT","text":"Closeout documentation records the scope, methods, before and after conditions, findings and verification results, so the work and its proof can be reconstructed. Invoices, timesheets and satisfaction statements are not that record.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should the closeout documentation for a cleaning project record?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0284","ascs-0666"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-return-indicators","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Transfer ducts, transfer grilles, fire dampers or louvered doors may indicate that a ceiling or other building cavity is working as an open air return plenum.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","prompt":"Which features may indicate that a ceiling cavity is an open air return plenum?","trap":"Condensate traps, duct wrap and VAV boxes do not indicate a plenum return.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0139","ascs-0321"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-ceiling-tiles","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"In a ceiling return plenum, removing ceiling tiles disrupts the designed airflow. Keep the ceiling and adjacent walls intact, especially around spaces designed to be exhausted, such as bathrooms, supply closets and chemical storage.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IV","prompt":"Why must ceiling tile removal be managed carefully when inspecting a plenum-return building?","trap":"The reason is airflow, not a presumption that the tiles contain asbestos.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0322"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-unfiltered-air","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moving through an open air plenum is generally unfiltered, so dust and dirt drop out of the airstream and build up on everything in the plenum over time.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Why does particulate accumulate throughout an open air plenum?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0323"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-non-hvac-contents","dom":"Assessments","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"Unlike sealed ductwork, open air plenums often hold many items unrelated to HVAC, such as light fixtures, fire suppression, data cabling, wiring, plumbing, insulation and other construction materials.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §V","prompt":"Compared with sealed ductwork, what do open air plenums typically contain?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0324"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-controls-basis","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"In an open air plenum, engineering controls such as containment are used per ACR to prevent cross-contamination, and their form depends on the type of conditioned space and the contaminants present.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII","prompt":"What decides the engineering controls, such as containment, used when cleaning an open air plenum?","trap":"Not the contractor's equipment inventory or the building's age.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0649"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-iep-consult","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"When open air plenum contaminants cannot be readily identified and the scope is unclear, a consultation with an IEP may be necessary to identify the contaminants and develop the scope of work.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VII","prompt":"Who may need to be consulted when open air plenum contaminants and scope are unclear?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0648"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-type1-dry","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"The white paper's Type 1 (dry) plenum methods are dry surface dusting, brushing, wiping, agitating, blasting or compressed air washing under negative air, and HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VIII","prompt":"Which cleaning methods does the Open Air Plenums white paper list as Type 1 (dry)?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0645"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-type2-wet","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"The white paper's Type 2 (wet) plenum methods are wet wiping, water washing, pressure washing, steam cleaning and chemical cleaning.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VIII","prompt":"Which cleaning methods does the Open Air Plenums white paper list as Type 2 (wet)?","trap":"Wet wiping counts as Type 2 even though it seems gentle.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0646"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-min-cleanliness","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"FACT","text":"A contracted open air plenum shall be cleaned at minimum to visibly clean or to the cleanliness level agreed in the scope of work. Other verification may be considered if specified.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §IX","prompt":"What minimum cleanliness level applies when an open air plenum is contracted for cleaning?","trap":"The 0.75 mg/100 cm2 vacuum test threshold is not the plenum default.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0647"]},{"id":"ascs:p-type-method-level","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"NOTE","text":"NADCA numbers three different things: Type 1 and Type 2 are dry and wet cleaning methods, Methods 1 to 3 are cleanliness verification, and Levels 1 to 4 are containment.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.9 to 3.12, §4.11, §5; NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper §VIII","prompt":"What do NADCA's Type, Method and Level numbers each refer to?","trap":"There is no Method 2 wet or Level 2 wet; wet cleaning is always Type 2.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0645","ascs-0646"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-clean-in-place","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Dry, intact fibrous glass liner that is only loaded with settled dust may be cleaned in place with HEPA contact vacuuming. Porous liner is not replaced just for being porous or dusty.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A lined return plenum is dry, intact and heavily loaded with settled dust. How may it be handled?","trap":"Replacing, encapsulating or air washing only are the wrong answers for dry intact liner.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0479"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Moisture, microbial growth or physical damage drive the decision to remove porous liner. Dust load, liner age or occupant odor reports alone do not.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What drives removal of porous duct liner, and what does not?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0050","ascs-0479","ascs-0480","ascs-0481"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-water-damaged","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Water-damaged, delaminating liner is removed and replaced. Cleaning it, applying an antimicrobial or sealing it under a coating does not restore it, even when the owner wants it cleaned.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"The liner is water damaged and delaminating, and the owner still wants it cleaned. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0480"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-fuzzing-ok","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Minor surface fuzzing on dry, well-adhered liner after cleaning is cosmetic, not a failure, so the liner stays in service. No coating is required; ACR treats resurfacing as optional.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"After cleaning, dry and adhered liner shows minor surface fuzzing. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0500"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-soft-tools","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Clean fibrous glass liner with contact vacuuming and soft-bristle agitation, using tools and methods that do not erode or tear the surface. NAIMA AH122 covers cleaning fibrous glass insulated duct.","src":"NAIMA AH122","prompt":"What approach and guide apply to cleaning a system with interior fibrous glass liner?","trap":"Aggressive metal brushing, wet washing, or encapsulating before agitation are all wrong.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0653"]},{"id":"ascs:p-liner-mechanical-agitation","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Mechanically driven agitation devices are not banned in fibrous glass lined duct. They may be used if they will not damage the liner; the limit is tool selection and technique.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"May mechanically driven agitation devices be used in fibrous glass lined duct?","trap":"No outright ban, owner approval rule or lockout condition applies.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0491"]},{"id":"ascs:p-flex-torn-core","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"Flexible duct with a torn or damaged inner core is replaced. A breached core sheds material and cannot be restored by cleaning, taping, sleeving or coating.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Flexible duct with a torn inner core is found during cleaning. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0490","ascs-0664"]},{"id":"ascs:p-failed-coating","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"A previously applied duct coating peeling into the airstream is a contaminant source. Remove the failed coating; recoating or encapsulating over it repeats the failure.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A previously applied duct coating is peeling into the airstream. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0504"]},{"id":"ascs:p-replace-when-failed","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"The strongest reason to replace a duct section rather than clean it is loss of physical integrity, such as corrosion through. Age, cost, or never having been cleaned are not condition criteria.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is the strongest justification for replacing a duct section instead of cleaning it?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0502"]},{"id":"ascs:p-fix-contamination-source","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","tag":"FACT","text":"When a defect keeps loading the duct, such as oily residue from a kitchen exhaust cross-connection, correct that source as part of the work. Cleaning alone just lets the duct reload.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Duct interiors are coated with oily residue from a kitchen exhaust cross-connection. How is the cleaning handled?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0501"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-epa-registration","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"REG","text":"All antimicrobial pesticides used in HVAC systems must be EPA-registered, and products without specific HVAC directions on the label are not to be used on HVAC surfaces.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"What does NADCA's chemical position paper require of antimicrobial products used in HVAC systems?","trap":"Registration is not a license to apply anywhere; the label still limits sites, surfaces and methods.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0482","ascs-0654"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-label-is-law","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"REG","text":"Using a pesticide in any way inconsistent with its label violates federal law (FIFRA). An owner's consent or signed waiver does not make an off-label application legal.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems; FIFRA label misuse statement","prompt":"Can an owner's written consent authorize an off-label antimicrobial application?","trap":"Applying at half strength is still off-label use.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0483"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-label-surfaces","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"REG","text":"A label lists the sites and surfaces a product may be used on, and it may be used only there. A product labeled for non-porous HVAC surfaces may not be applied to internal duct liner.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"A product label says for use on non-porous HVAC surfaces and the duct is internally lined. May it be applied to the liner?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0483"]},{"id":"ascs:p-epa-no-fiberglass-biocide","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA states that no biocides are currently registered for use on fiberglass duct board or fiberglass-lined ducts, so find out whether the system has these materials before permitting any biocide.","src":"EPA, Should You Have the Air Ducts in Your Home Cleaned?","prompt":"What does EPA say about biocides on fiberglass duct board or fiberglass-lined duct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0483"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-after-cleaning","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"FACT","text":"Antimicrobials are applied only after the surfaces have been cleaned, because surface soil interferes with how well the product works. Fogging or treating before source removal is inappropriate.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"When may an antimicrobial be applied in an HVAC system, and why?","trap":"EPA registration does not make a pre-cleaning application acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0051","ascs-0482","ascs-0655"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-not-needed","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"FACT","text":"Source removal is the single best way to clean an HVAC system, and chemical products are not needed to achieve it. Use a chemical only when its use is justified and the label permits it.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"Are chemical products needed to achieve source removal?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0051","ascs-0655"]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-client-release","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"FACT","text":"NADCA recommends the client sign a release authorizing the specific chemical products and acknowledging that the risks were explained. A release never makes an off-label use legal.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"What client document does NADCA recommend before chemical products are applied?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-chem-applicator-license","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-chemical-products","tag":"FACT","text":"When an EPA-registered pesticide product is used, a state pesticide applicator's license may be required for the owner, the firm, a supervisor or the worker.","src":"NADCA Position Paper, Chemical Product Applications in HVAC Systems","prompt":"What license may be required to apply an EPA-registered antimicrobial in an HVAC system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"ascs:p-no-fixed-order","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"No single cleaning order for the air handler, supply and return is mandated. The requirement is to keep already cleaned components from being recontaminated.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Is there a required order for cleaning the air handler, supply and return?","trap":"Air handler first, or return then supply then air handler, are invented fixed orders.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0488"]},{"id":"ascs:p-fire-damper-midrun","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"A fire damper in a run being cleaned is accessed from both sides, cleaned without damage, checked for operation and left in service. Never remove it, wire it open or skip it.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A fire damper sits mid-run in a duct you must clean. What is correct?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0489"]},{"id":"ascs:p-multizone-dampers","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"When cleaning a multizone unit, position the zone dampers so every zone gets cleaned without damaging the dampers or their actuators. Disconnecting actuators or locking dampers open risks both.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should be done with the zone dampers when cleaning a multizone unit?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0495"]},{"id":"ascs:p-coil-leaving-side","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"Clean a fouled coil from the leaving-air side, back through the coil against normal airflow, so debris is pushed out the way it came in.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"From which side is a fouled evaporator coil cleaned, and why?","trap":"Cleaning from the entering side is the common wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0492"]},{"id":"ascs:p-filters-at-completion","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"At project completion, install new filters of the correct size and efficiency for the system's design. Never leave the rack empty, reuse or wash loaded filters, or jump to the highest MERV that fits.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What is done with filters at the completion of a cleaning project?","trap":"Upgrading MERV beyond the design is not the answer; match the design.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0506","ascs-0650"]},{"id":"ascs:p-access-doors-airtight","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"Existing access doors found damaged or missing gaskets are repaired or replaced so the system is returned airtight. Leaking doors hurt performance and let contaminants migrate.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What should be done with existing access doors found damaged or missing gaskets?","trap":"Reusing them as found with a note in the record is the wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0660"]},{"id":"ascs:p-fire-rated-closure","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","tag":"FACT","text":"An opening cut in a fire-rated duct or assembly must be closed so the assembly keeps its listed rating, for example with an access door listed to the same rating. A sheet metal patch or tape does not.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training); fire-rated assembly listings","prompt":"How is an access opening cut in a fire-rated duct or assembly closed?","trap":"The rating belongs to the duct assembly too, not only to the shaft.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0484","ascs-0485","ascs-0652"]},{"id":"ascs:p-nam-not-holding","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"If a negative air machine is running but the system will not hold negative pressure, first check for unsealed openings and a loaded HEPA filter restricting the machine, before adding equipment.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A negative air machine is running but the system is not holding negative pressure. What do you check first?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0651"]},{"id":"ascs:p-pressure-lost-stop","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"If negative pressure is lost while technicians are disturbing contamination, stop the disturbing work and restore the required pressure before continuing.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Negative pressure is lost while technicians are disturbing contamination. What is the immediate action?","trap":"Raising the supply fan or finishing the branch first spreads debris into the building.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0619"]},{"id":"ascs:p-exhaust-no-reentry","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"When non-HEPA negative air or collection equipment is exhausted outdoors, place the discharge where it cannot re-enter the building.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"Where outdoors should the exhaust from non-HEPA negative air equipment be directed?","trap":"An unoccupied room, the containment or the return plenum are all indoors.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0620"]},{"id":"ascs:p-mech-room-is-indoors","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"NOTE","text":"A mechanical room is inside the building. An unfiltered portable vacuum exhausting into it is exhausting indoors, so it is not an acceptable debris collection setup.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.1.2 and §4.1.3 (applied)","prompt":"Is a portable vacuum exhausting unfiltered into the mechanical room an acceptable collection method?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0499"]},{"id":"ascs:p-fuel-equipment-co","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"The key hazard of fuel-powered vacuums, generators and compressors staged for a job is carbon monoxide drawn into outside air intakes, openings or occupied areas, so place them and control CO exposure.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.2; industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What specific hazard does fuel-powered vacuum or compressor equipment staged for a job create?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0656","ascs-0686"]},{"id":"ascs:p-occupied-building-scope","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"When a building stays fully occupied during business hours, the most important scope items are engineering controls, work sequencing and often off-hours scheduling that keep contaminants away from occupants.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"A building stays fully occupied during business hours. What is the most important scope consideration?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0667"]},{"id":"ascs:p-healthcare-containment-first","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"In an occupied healthcare space, containment is established before any system opening is made, because contaminant release begins when the first access is cut.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"When should containment be established for work in an occupied healthcare space?","trap":"Not after the first access is cut, and not only in areas rated for infection control.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0503"]},{"id":"ascs:p-healthcare-barriers-purpose","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"On an occupied hospital project, critical barriers and negative pressure exist mainly to keep contaminants from migrating into sensitive areas. Occupancy and space sensitivity set the level of engineering control.","src":"Industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"On an occupied hospital corridor project, what is the primary reason for critical barriers and negative pressure?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0657"]},{"id":"ascs:p-power-tools-in-duct","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"FACT","text":"Portable electric tools used inside a metal duct need GFCI protection (or an assured grounding program) plus a grounded or double-insulated tool, because a conductive metal enclosure makes shock more likely.","src":"29 CFR 1926.404(b)(1), (f)(7)(iv)(C); industry practice (NADCA training)","prompt":"What protection do portable electric tools used inside a metal duct require?","trap":"Battery-only rules or a dedicated 20 amp circuit are not the requirement, and double insulation does not replace GFCI protection.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0498"]},{"id":"ascs:p-loto-before-contact","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","tag":"REG","text":"Before technicians contact moving parts or energized components, the equipment is shut down and locked out and tagged out per site safety requirements.","src":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147 (lockout/tagout)","prompt":"What condition must equipment be in before technicians contact its moving parts or energized components?","trap":"ACR itself names lockout/tagout only for electric resistance coils (4.11.8); the general rule comes from OSHA.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0668","ascs-0669","ascs-0693"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-types","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"DEF","text":"The NADCA white paper sorts open air plenums by pressure. Supply plenums run positive, as in raised-floor data centers and some clean rooms. Return or exhaust plenums run negative: ceiling and attic plenums, wall voids, closets, raised floors and pipe chases.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper (2023), plenum types","prompt":"How does the NADCA open air plenum white paper classify plenums, and which spaces count as return or exhaust?","trap":"A ceiling or attic plenum used by design is a return/exhaust plenum, not an accidental one.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0142"]},{"id":"ascs:p-oap-accidental","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","lesson":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","tag":"DEF","text":"Leaky or disconnected ducts can turn a building cavity into an unintended supply or return air path. The NADCA white paper calls that an accidental air plenum.","src":"NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper (2023), plenum types","prompt":"What does NADCA call a cavity that becomes an air path because ducts leak or disconnect?","trap":"A transfer grille or transfer duct makes a designed path, not an accidental plenum.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0143"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-55","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE 55 defines acceptable thermal conditions using six factors: metabolic rate, clothing insulation, air temperature, radiant temperature, air speed, and humidity. It does not address air quality; ventilation belongs to ASHRAE 62.1.","src":"ASHRAE 55, purpose and scope","prompt":"Which ASHRAE standard defines thermal comfort, what six factors does it use, and does it cover air quality?","trap":"Outdoor air ventilation rate is not one of the six factors.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0856"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-901","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"ASHRAE/IES 90.1 is the energy standard for sites and buildings other than low-rise residential. It sets minimum efficiency for the envelope, lighting and HVAC. Ventilation is 62.1, comfort is 55, and filter testing is 52.2.","src":"ANSI/ASHRAE/IES 90.1-2022, Energy Standard for Sites and Buildings Except Low-Rise Residential Buildings","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 90.1 cover, and which building type is outside its scope?","trap":"Mixing up the ASHRAE numbers: 90.1 is energy, 62.1 ventilation, 55 comfort, 52.2 filter testing.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0825"]},{"id":"cvi:ashrae-522","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-ashrae","tag":"DEF","text":"The MERV rating comes from the ASHRAE 52.2 test, which measures removal efficiency by particle size. ISO 16890 rates filters in ePM classes instead, and ASHRAE 62.1 sets minimum MERV values without testing for them.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2","prompt":"Which test standard produces a filter's MERV number?","trap":"ASHRAE 62.1 names minimum MERV values but is not the test method.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:fd-interval","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 80 requires fire damper testing and inspection 1 year after installation, then every 4 years.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19 (2010 §19.4.1)","prompt":"How often does NFPA 80 require fire damper testing after installation?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Outside hospitals, NFPA 80 retests fire dampers every __ after the first-year test.","a":"4 years","d":["1 year","5 years","6 years"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-hospital","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Hospitals are the exception: after the first-year test, NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 allow hospital fire and smoke dampers to be tested every 6 years.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19; NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2)","prompt":"What damper test cycle do NFPA 80 and 105 allow hospitals after the first-year test?","trap":"Six years is hospitals only. A 5-year-old test in an office is overdue.","num":{"q":"After the first-year test, hospitals may test fire dampers every __.","a":"6 years","d":["4 years","5 years","1 year"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:sd-interval","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 105 tests smoke dampers 1 year after installation, then every 4 years, the same pattern NFPA 80 uses for fire dampers.","src":"NFPA 105 (2010 §6.5.2)","prompt":"When does NFPA 105 require smoke damper testing and inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-combination","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"A combination fire/smoke damper is both a fire damper and a smoke damper, so its inspection and testing must meet NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 together.","src":"NFPA 80 Ch. 19; NFPA 105","prompt":"Which standards govern testing of a combination fire/smoke damper?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-code-chain","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 101 sends HVAC ductwork to NFPA 90A or 90B as applicable, and NFPA 90A sends fire dampers to NFPA 80 and smoke dampers to NFPA 105. NFPA 90A sets no periodic test procedure itself.","src":"NFPA 101 (2012) §9.2.1; NFPA 90A (2012) §5.4.8","prompt":"What is the chain of references from the Life Safety Code to fire damper testing?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-fusible-test","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"To test a fusible-link fire damper under NFPA 80, release or remove the link with the damper fully open. It must close fully on its own, latch if designed to, then be reset with the link reinstalled.","src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.2","prompt":"How is a fusible-link fire damper tested under NFPA 80?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-command-test","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Fire dampers without fusible links and NFPA 105 smoke dampers are tested by commanding them fully closed or open, as designed, then restoring them. A position indicator counts only under the remote inspection rules.","src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.3; NFPA 105","prompt":"How are fire dampers without fusible links and smoke dampers tested, and when does a position indicator count?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ul-555-family","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 555 lists fire dampers, UL 555S smoke dampers, UL 555C ceiling dampers, and UL 33 heat responsive links. A combination damper carries both UL 555 and UL 555S.","src":"UL 555; UL 555S","prompt":"Which UL standards list fire dampers, smoke dampers, ceiling dampers, and fusible links?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-rating","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"A fire damper is rated 1½ hours in an assembly rated under 3 hours, and 3 hours in an assembly rated 3 hours or more. It is not matched to the wall's own rating.","src":"IBC 717; IMC 607; UL 555","prompt":"What fire protection rating does a fire damper need in a 2-hour wall?","trap":"Matching the damper to the 2-hour wall.","num":{"q":"A fire damper in a 2-hour rated wall needs a __ rating.","a":"1½ hours","d":["2 hours","3 hours","45 minutes"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-dynamic","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Where fans keep running during a fire, fire dampers must be dynamic-rated: tested to close against airflow and marked with velocity and pressure limits. Static dampers suit only systems whose fans shut down automatically.","src":"UL 555 (dynamic rating); ICC CodeNotes","prompt":"When must a fire damper be dynamic-rated rather than static?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:damper-label","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"Damper access doors must be permanently labeled FIRE DAMPER, SMOKE DAMPER, or FIRE/SMOKE DAMPER in letters at least 1/2 inch high. An unlabeled access panel is a finding.","src":"IMC 607.4; NFPA 90A (2012) §4.3.5.2","prompt":"How must a damper access panel be labeled?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Damper access labels need letters at least __ high.","a":"1/2 inch","d":["1/4 inch","1 inch","4 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90B covers warm air heating and air-conditioning systems in one- and two-family dwellings and in spaces of 25,000 cubic feet or less.","src":"NFPA 90B §1.3","prompt":"Which NFPA standard covers the forced-air system in a single-family house?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90A covers systems serving spaces over 25,000 cubic feet, plus Type III, IV, and V buildings over three stories regardless of volume.","src":"NFPA 90A §1.3","prompt":"Above what space volume does NFPA 90A apply instead of 90B?","trap":"2,000 and 15,000 are 90A duct detector airflow thresholds in cfm, not volumes.","num":{"q":"NFPA 90A applies to systems serving spaces over __.","a":"25,000 cubic feet","d":["2,000 cubic feet","15,000 cubic feet","50,000 cubic feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 90A requires a duct smoke detector on systems over 2,000 cfm, in the supply downstream of the filters and ahead of any branch takeoff.","src":"NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1","prompt":"Where does NFPA 90A require a duct smoke detector on a single-floor unit over 2,000 cfm?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NFPA 90A requires a supply duct smoke detector on systems over __.","a":"2,000 cfm","d":["400 cfm","15,000 cfm","50 cfm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"When a return system over 15,000 cfm serves more than one story, NFPA 90A adds detectors at each story ahead of the connection to the common return.","src":"NFPA 90A §6.4.2.1","prompt":"When does NFPA 90A add return detectors at each story?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:imc-return-det","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC 606.2.1 return detector goes upstream of any filters, exhaust air connections, outdoor air connections, or decontamination equipment.","src":"IMC 606.2.1","prompt":"Where does IMC 606.2.1 place the return-side duct smoke detector?","trap":"Upstream of filters in the return is the IMC rule; downstream of filters in the supply is the 90A rule.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:nfpa-96-intervals","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 96 inspects kitchen exhaust for grease monthly for solid-fuel cooking, quarterly for high volume (24-hour, charbroiling, wok), semiannually for moderate volume, and annually for low volume such as churches and seasonal businesses.","src":"NFPA 96-2024 Table 12.4 (Table 11.4 in 2021 and earlier)","prompt":"What are the NFPA 96 kitchen exhaust inspection frequencies by cooking type?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NFPA 96 inspects exhaust for 24-hour or charbroiling cooking every __.","a":"3 months","d":["1 month","6 months","12 months"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ikeca-map","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IKECA I10 is the kitchen exhaust inspection methodology (mechanical condition, structural integrity, fire safety, cleanliness). C10 is the cleaning methodology ACR cites, and M10 covers end-user maintenance between cleanings.","src":"ANSI/IKECA I10-2020, C10-2021, M10-2023 (ikeca.org)","prompt":"Which IKECA standard sets the kitchen exhaust inspection methodology, and what do C10 and M10 cover?","trap":"ANSI/IICRC S590 assesses HVAC after water, fire, or mold damage, not kitchen exhaust.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:irc-scope","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The IRC covers detached one- and two-family dwellings and townhouses up to three stories above grade plane with a separate means of egress, including their mechanical systems.","src":"IRC R101.2","prompt":"Which I-code governs the mechanical system of a three-story townhouse with its own egress?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:imc-irc-exception","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"The 2024 IMC 101.2 exception lets IRC-scope buildings comply with either the IMC or the IRC. A fourth story or shared egress moves the building to the IBC and IMC.","src":"IMC 101.2 (2024)","prompt":"What moves a townhouse out of the IRC and into the IBC and IMC?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:flex-connector","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-nfpa-codes","tag":"CODE","text":"A flexible air connector (round Listed Air Connector label) is limited to 14 feet and cannot pass through walls, floors, or ceilings.","src":"IMC 603.6.2","prompt":"What length and routing limits apply to a flexible air connector?","trap":"A flexible air duct (rectangular Listed Air Duct label) has no IMC length limit. Read the label.","num":{"q":"The IMC limits a flexible air connector to __ in length.","a":"14 feet","d":["8 feet","25 feet","6 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:smacna-seal-classes","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class A seals transverse joints, longitudinal seams, and duct wall penetrations. Class B seals transverse joints and longitudinal seams. Class C seals transverse joints only.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Stds, Table 1-2","prompt":"What does each SMACNA seal class (A, B, C) require sealed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:smacna-016","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"The SMACNA HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual (ANSI/SMACNA 016) gives leakage test procedures, apparatus, setup, and expected rates. The Duct Construction Standards set gauge, reinforcement, and seal class and defer testing to it.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 016-2012","prompt":"Which SMACNA document gives duct leakage test procedures and expected rates?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:smacna-008","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA's IAQ Guidelines for Occupied Buildings Under Construction (ANSI/SMACNA 008) organizes construction IAQ into HVAC protection, source control, pathway interruption, housekeeping, and scheduling. LEED's construction IAQ credit points to it.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 008 (2nd ed. 2007)","prompt":"Which document organizes construction IAQ control into five measures, and what are they?","trap":"SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction is the sibling guideline with three cleanliness levels for new ductwork.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:astm-c1071","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"ASTM C1071 is the specification for fibrous glass duct lining insulation, the thermal and sound-absorbing liner used in ducts, plenums, and air handlers carrying air up to 250°F.","src":"ASTM C1071","prompt":"What does ASTM C1071 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ul-181","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 181 classifies factory-made air ducts and air connectors, such as Class 1 duct board. It lists the duct, not the closure tape.","src":"UL 181","prompt":"What does a UL 181 listing classify?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ul-181a","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"CODE","text":"Duct board keeps its UL 181 Class 1 air duct rating only when its joints use UL 181A closures: 181A-P pressure-sensitive tape, 181A-H heat-activated tape, or 181A-M mastic. Cloth duct tape voids the rating.","src":"UL 181A; UL 181B; Owens Corning EnduraGold","prompt":"What closures keep duct board's UL 181 Class 1 rating?","trap":"UL 181B (such as 181B-FX tape) lists flexible duct closures, the wrong listing for rigid board.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ul723-e84","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 723 and ASTM E84 are the same tunnel test for surface burning characteristics, and NFPA 255 uses the same method. An E84 flame-spread or smoke-developed index is a UL 723 result.","src":"UL 723 = ASTM E84 (Intertek)","prompt":"How do UL 723 and ASTM E84 relate?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:class-a-450","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"NOTE","text":"A 450 smoke-developed index is the ASTM E84 cap for interior finish Classes A, B, and C (Class A flame spread 0 to 25). ACR limits airstream door and panel materials to flame spread 25, smoke-developed 50.","src":"ASTM E84 classes (Intertek); ACR 2025 §4.2.7","prompt":"Where does a 450 smoke-developed limit apply, and does it satisfy airstream material rules?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASTM E84 interior finish classes allow a smoke-developed index up to __.","a":"450","d":["50","25","200"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ul-900","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-duct-listings","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 900 rates the combustibility and smoke generated by air filter units. Particle removal efficiency comes from ASHRAE 52.2, not UL 900.","src":"UL 900","prompt":"What property does a UL 900 filter classification address?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:s500-categories","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S500 water categories: Category 1 is from a sanitary source, Category 2 is significantly contaminated, and Category 3 is grossly contaminated, such as sewage or backflow from beyond the toilet trap.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S500","prompt":"What are the three S500 water categories?","trap":"Classes 1 to 4 rate drying difficulty (Class 4 is specialty drying), not contamination.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:s500-time","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"S500 categories follow the contamination present, not just the source. Time and temperature amplify contaminants, so clean water left warm for days can degrade to Category 2 or 3.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S500","prompt":"Can a potable-water leak stay Category 1 after sitting warm for five days?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:s590-purpose","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IICRC S590 (first edition 2023) is an assessment-only standard for HVAC systems after water, fire, or mold damage. The assessor sorts internal HVAC surfaces into impacted and non-impacted.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023","prompt":"What core call does an S590 assessor make on each internal HVAC surface?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:s590-output","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"Under S590 the assessor writes a report and a Restoration Work Plan (RWP) for the remediation contractor. Water categories are defined in S500 and mold Conditions in S520, not in S590.","src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023","prompt":"What does an S590 assessment produce for the restorer?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:epa-duct-triggers","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA's home duct guide lists three reasons to consider cleaning: substantial visible mold inside hard-surface ducts, vermin infestation, and ducts clogged with debris or releasing particles into the home.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What three conditions does EPA list as reasons to consider duct cleaning?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:epa-duct-health","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA states duct cleaning has never been shown to prevent health problems, studies do not conclusively show dirty ducts raise home particle levels, and ducts should be cleaned as needed rather than routinely.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What does EPA say about duct cleaning, health, and cleaning schedules?","trap":"ACR Table 1 sets inspection intervals, not cleaning intervals.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"FACT","text":"EPA says no biocides are registered for fiber glass duct board or lined ducts, a biocide on other surfaces goes on only after proper cleaning, and routine use of sealants to encapsulate contaminants is not recommended.","src":"EPA 402-K-97-002","prompt":"What does EPA say about biocides on fiberglass-lined ducts and about encapsulating contamination?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:epa-baq-guide","dom":"Standards","lesson":"cvi-restoration-epa","tag":"DEF","text":"Building Air Quality: A Guide for Building Owners and Facility Managers (EPA and NIOSH, 1991) covers IAQ profiles, management plans, and diagnosing IAQ problems in commercial buildings.","src":"EPA 402-F-91-102","prompt":"Which EPA guide, co-published with NIOSH, covers IAQ problems in commercial buildings?","trap":"IAQ Tools for Schools is EPA's school kit; A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace is an OSHA bulletin.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:cs-entry","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Under 1910.146, entry into a permit-required confined space occurs as soon as any part of the body breaks the plane of an opening. A head and arm through an access door is entry.","src":"29 CFR 1910.146(b)","prompt":"When does entry into a permit space occur under 1910.146?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:fall-gi-4","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"General industry fall protection starts at 4 feet above a lower level (1910.28(b)(1)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(1)","prompt":"At what height does general industry require fall protection at an unprotected edge?","trap":"","num":{"q":"General industry fall protection starts at __ above a lower level.","a":"4 feet","d":["6 feet","24 feet","3 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:fall-cons-6","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Construction fall protection starts at 6 feet (1926.501(b)(1)). General industry is 4 feet.","src":"29 CFR 1926.501(b)(1)","prompt":"At what height does construction require fall protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Construction fall protection starts at __ above a lower level.","a":"6 feet","d":["4 feet","10 feet","15 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:break-19","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"In construction, a personnel access point with a break in elevation of 19 inches or more needs a stairway or ladder, unless a ramp, runway, sloped embankment, or personnel hoist is provided.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1051(a)","prompt":"What break in elevation requires a stairway or ladder in construction?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Construction requires a stairway or ladder at a break in elevation of __ or more.","a":"19 inches","d":["30 inches","36 inches","42 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:fixed-ladder-24","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"A fixed ladder extending more than 24 feet above a lower level needs fall protection. Ladders installed since November 19, 2018 need a ladder safety or personal fall arrest system; older ladders may keep a cage or well until 2036.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(9)","prompt":"Above what height does a fixed ladder need fall protection, and which systems qualify on ladders installed since November 2018?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:roof-under-6","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"On a low-slope roof, work less than 6 feet from the edge needs a guardrail, safety net, travel restraint, or personal fall arrest system (1910.28(b)(13)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(i)","prompt":"What fall protection does work within 6 feet of a low-slope roof edge need?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:roof-6-15","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"From 6 to under 15 feet from a low-slope roof edge, a designated area may replace conventional fall protection when the work is infrequent and temporary.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(ii)","prompt":"When may a designated area be used on a low-slope roof?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:roof-15","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"At 15 feet or more from a low-slope roof edge, work that is both infrequent and temporary needs no fall protection if the employer enforces a rule keeping workers out of the 15-foot zone.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(b)(13)(iii)","prompt":"When may low-slope roof work proceed with no fall protection?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Infrequent, temporary work at least __ from a low-slope roof edge may skip fall protection under a work rule.","a":"15 feet","d":["6 feet","4 feet","10 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:prework-exempt","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"1910.28 exempts inspecting or assessing conditions before work starts or after all work ends, unless compliant fall protection is installed and available. Construction has a similar exception (1926.500(a)(1)) without that installed-equipment condition.","src":"29 CFR 1910.28(a)(2)(ii); 1926.500(a)(1)","prompt":"Does 1910.28 apply to a CVI's pre-work walk of a roof with no fall protection installed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-36","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Portable ladder side rails must extend at least 3 feet (36 inches) above an upper landing (1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.23(c)(11); 1926.1053(b)(1)","prompt":"How far must portable ladder side rails extend above the roof edge?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A portable ladder's side rails must extend at least __ above the upper landing.","a":"36 inches","d":["42 inches","30 inches","19 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-fixed","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Fixed through or side-step ladders extend their side rails 42 inches above the access level, and a fixed ladder without a cage or well needs 30 inches of clearance on the climbing side.","src":"29 CFR 1910.23(d)(4), (d)(13)","prompt":"What side-rail extension and climbing clearance apply to fixed ladders?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-4to1","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"A non-self-supporting ladder's foot goes out about one-quarter of its working length (the 4-to-1 rule), so 24 feet of working length puts the foot about 6 feet from the wall.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1053(b)(5)(i)","prompt":"How far from the wall should a ladder with 24 feet of working length sit?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A ladder with 24 feet of working length sets its foot about __ from the wall.","a":"6 feet","d":["8 feet","12 feet","4 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:powerline-10","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"An unqualified worker and any conductive object he handles stay at least 10 feet from unguarded overhead lines of 50 kV or less, plus 4 inches for every 10 kV above 50 kV.","src":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(3)","prompt":"How close may an unqualified worker bring an aluminum ladder to a 50 kV line?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Unqualified workers keep conductive objects at least __ from overhead lines of 50 kV or less.","a":"10 feet","d":["3 feet","15 feet","20 feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:ladder-nonconductive","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-access","tag":"REG","text":"Portable ladders used where they could contact exposed energized parts need nonconductive side rails (1910.333(c)(7)).","src":"29 CFR 1910.333(c)(7)","prompt":"What kind of side rails must a ladder have near exposed energized parts?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:loto-inspecting","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA 1910.147 counts inspecting as servicing, so reaching into an AHU fan section just to photograph the wheel needs lockout. A helper at the disconnect is no substitute.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(a)(1), (b)","prompt":"Does lockout apply when a CVI reaches into a fan section only to take a photo?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:loto-control-circuit","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"Push buttons, selector switches, and other control circuit devices, such as a thermostat or BAS command, are not energy isolating devices. The lock goes on a disconnect or breaker that physically blocks energy.","src":"29 CFR 1910.147(b)","prompt":"Why is setting the thermostat to OFF and commanding the unit off at the BAS not lockout?","trap":"Unplugged cord-and-plug equipment under the worker's exclusive control is an exception, not the limit of the standard.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:fit-test","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"1910.134 requires a respirator fit test before first use, whenever a different facepiece size, style, model, or make is used, and at least annually.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134(f)(2), (e)(7)","prompt":"When does 1910.134 require a respirator fit test?","trap":"A new make of facepiece triggers a fit test, not a new medical evaluation.","num":{"q":"1910.134 repeats respirator fit testing at least every __.","a":"12 months","d":["6 months","24 months","3 months"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:seal-check","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"A user seal check is done every time a respirator is put on, but it never replaces a fit test.","src":"29 CFR 1910.134","prompt":"Can a user seal check stand in for a fit test after switching respirator brands?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:sds-8","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"HazCom requires a 16-section SDS, and Section 8 covers exposure controls and personal protection, including gloves and respirators.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200(g)(2), App. D","prompt":"Which SDS section lists exposure controls and PPE?","trap":"","num":{"q":"SDS Section __ lists exposure controls and personal protection.","a":"8","d":["2","4","7"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:sds-2-4-7","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"SDS Section 2 identifies hazards, Section 4 gives first-aid measures, and Section 7 covers handling and storage.","src":"29 CFR 1910.1200 App. D","prompt":"What do SDS Sections 2, 4, and 7 cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:heat-stroke","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"FACT","text":"Confusion, slurred speech, and hot skin signal heat stroke: call 911, stay with the worker, and cool him quickly with cold water, wet cloths, or ice.","src":"NIOSH heat-related illnesses","prompt":"What should a CVI do first when a helper in a hot attic becomes confused with hot skin?","trap":"Sips of water and rest in shade are heat exhaustion first aid; for heat stroke that delay can be fatal.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:noise-85","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"1910.95 requires a hearing conservation program (monitoring, audiometric testing, hearing protectors) at an 8-hour TWA of 85 dBA, the action level.","src":"29 CFR 1910.95(c)","prompt":"At what exposure does 1910.95 require a hearing conservation program?","trap":"","num":{"q":"1910.95's hearing conservation action level is an 8-hour TWA of __.","a":"85 dBA","d":["90 dBA","80 dBA","130 dBA"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:noise-90","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-osha-health","tag":"REG","text":"The 1910.95 PEL is 90 dBA for 8 hours; above it, feasible controls are required. A 10 dB standard threshold shift triggers follow-up, not the program itself.","src":"29 CFR 1910.95(b), (g)(10)","prompt":"What is the 1910.95 PEL, and what does a 10 dB threshold shift trigger?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:owner-acm-survey","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under 1926.1101(k)(2), building owners must determine the presence, location, and quantity of ACM and PACM before work begins and inform employers who will do the work.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(k)(2)","prompt":"What must a building owner provide before inspection ports are cut in insulated ductwork in a 1975 building?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:pacm","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA presumes thermal system insulation and surfacing material in buildings built no later than 1980 to be asbestos (PACM). It is treated as ACM until rebutted, without waiting for a lab result.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b)","prompt":"Which materials does OSHA presume contain asbestos, and in which buildings?","trap":"Over 1% asbestos defines confirmed ACM; presumed material needs no sample to be treated as ACM.","num":{"q":"OSHA presumes TSI and surfacing material contain asbestos in buildings built no later than __.","a":"1980","d":["1978","1970","1991"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:tsi-breeching","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"OSHA's thermal system insulation definition expressly includes breeching insulation, along with pipes, fittings, boilers, tanks, and ducts.","src":"29 CFR 1926.1101(b)","prompt":"Does OSHA's TSI definition include boiler breeching insulation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:neshap-notice","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under the asbestos NESHAP, the owner or operator of a covered demolition or renovation gives the EPA Administrator written notice at least 10 working days before asbestos removal or demolition begins.","src":"40 CFR 61.145(b)","prompt":"Who files a NESHAP notice, with whom, and how far ahead?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:neshap-thresholds","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"NESHAP renovation rules apply when regulated asbestos disturbed totals at least 260 linear feet on pipes, 160 square feet on other components, or 35 cubic feet where it could not be measured.","src":"40 CFR 61.145(a)(4)","prompt":"What amounts of regulated asbestos bring a renovation under NESHAP?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-scope","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"EPA's RRP rule applies to renovation for compensation in target housing (built before 1978) and child-occupied facilities.","src":"40 CFR 745.82; 745.103","prompt":"Where does the EPA lead RRP rule apply?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-minor","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"RRP excludes minor repair, including minor HVAC work, disturbing 6 square feet or less of painted surface per room inside or 20 outside, with no prohibited practices, window replacement, or demolition.","src":"40 CFR 745.83","prompt":"What makes HVAC work in pre-1978 housing exempt minor repair under RRP?","trap":"The exemption applies whether or not a child under 6 lives there.","num":{"q":"RRP minor repair covers disturbing __ or less of interior painted surface per room.","a":"6 square feet","d":["20 square feet","160 square feet","4 square feet"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-demolition","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Cutting painted drywall out cleanly is not demolition, but knocking the same hole out with a hammer is, and it loses the RRP minor repair exemption.","src":"40 CFR 745.83; EPA RRP FAQ 23002-18515","prompt":"Does knocking an opening in painted drywall with a hammer keep the RRP minor repair exemption?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-30-day","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Non-emergency jobs in the same room within 30 days count as one job for the RRP minor repair limit, and a removed painted component counts in full.","src":"40 CFR 745.83","prompt":"How does RRP count several small jobs in one room?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rrp-firm","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"REG","text":"Under RRP, firms that perform renovations for compensation must be EPA-certified. The certification belongs to the renovating firm, not the building owner.","src":"40 CFR 745.89","prompt":"Who must hold RRP certification, the renovating firm or the owner?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:histo-source","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"Histoplasma grows in soil enriched with bird or bat droppings, and people get sick by breathing spores released when dry material is disturbed. NIOSH names HVAC installers and service workers as at risk.","src":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis; CDC histoplasmosis","prompt":"What causes histoplasmosis, and why are HVAC workers at risk?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:histo-wet","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"NIOSH advises wetting bird or bat droppings rather than dry sweeping before removal. It describes a school outbreak spread through courtyard air intakes.","src":"NIOSH Histoplasmosis: Protecting Workers at Risk","prompt":"How should caked pigeon droppings at an intake be handled before removal?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:hanta-source","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"Hantavirus comes from rodent urine, droppings, and nesting material, not bird droppings. Cooling tower mist is a Legionella source, not a hantavirus source.","src":"CDC hantavirus; CDC Legionella","prompt":"Which HVAC contaminant sources carry hantavirus, histoplasmosis, and Legionella?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rodent-cleanup","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"CDC says never sweep or vacuum rodent droppings or nests. Spray them with bleach solution or an EPA-registered disinfectant until very wet, let soak about 5 minutes, then wipe up with paper towels.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance","prompt":"How does CDC say to clean fresh mouse droppings from a return plenum?","trap":"The normal ACR agitate-and-HEPA-vacuum method is wrong for fresh droppings before they are wetted.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"cvi:rodent-pros","dom":"Health & Safety","lesson":"cvi-hazmat-bio","tag":"FACT","text":"For rodents in ventilation systems, CDC points to a professional exterminator and duct cleaning specialists.","src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance","prompt":"Whom does CDC point to for rodents in a ventilation system?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-single-zone","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"A single-zone system has one thermostat controlling one unit for one space. A constant-volume single-zone unit needs only trunk and branch ducts to diffusers downstream, with no VAV boxes, mixing boxes or zone dampers.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What defines a single-zone system, and what sits downstream of a constant-volume single-zone unit?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0169"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cav-control","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"A constant air volume (CAV) system holds airflow fixed and changes the supply air temperature to match the load.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a constant air volume (CAV) system control space temperature?","trap":"VAV is the reverse: fixed supply temperature, varying airflow.","qs":["ascs-0154"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cav-reheat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Constant-volume reheat sends a fixed volume of cold air down one main and adds heat at each zone terminal. It wastes energy because air cooled for the zone with the biggest cooling load gets reheated at every zone needing less.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide","prompt":"How does a constant-volume reheat system meet falling zone loads, and why does it waste energy?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0820"]},{"id":"hvac:s-simultaneous-heat-cool","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Constant-volume reheat, multizone and dual-duct systems inherently heat and cool at the same time, because each cools the air first and then reheats or remixes it.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Which system types inherently heat and cool at the same time?","trap":"Packaged rooftops with integrated economizers and VAV with static pressure reset are not on this list.","qs":["ascs-0848"]},{"id":"hvac:s-reheat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"Reheat is heating air downstream of the cooling coil, at the unit or at a zone terminal, to meet zone demand or to warm air overcooled for dehumidification. Reheat coils are supply-side components.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is reheat, and where does a reheat coil sit?","trap":"A coil in the intake ahead of the cooling coil is a preheat coil, not reheat.","qs":["ascs-0109","ascs-0128","ascs-0176"]},{"id":"hvac:s-trend-id","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"NOTE","text":"As zone load falls: VAV drops airflow while air stays near 55°F; constant-volume reheat holds airflow and warms air at the zone; a dual-duct box shows two inlets; a multizone blends at the unit.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide; PNNL VAV O&M","prompt":"How do trend readings tell VAV, constant-volume reheat, dual duct and multizone apart as zone load falls?","trap":"Constant airflow with warming air from one 55°F main is CV reheat, not VAV.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-doas","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"A dedicated outdoor air system (DOAS) conditions and dehumidifies only the outdoor air needed for ventilation. It has no return-air mixing box, and terminal units such as fan coils handle the space load.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN062","prompt":"What does a DOAS do, and what section does it lack compared with a rooftop unit?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0164","ascs-0845"]},{"id":"hvac:s-active-beam","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"An active chilled beam uses ducted primary air through nozzles to induce room air over its coil. A passive beam is a ceiling coil working by natural convection with no duct connection.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does an active chilled beam differ from a passive one?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0846"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ufad","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"FACT","text":"Underfloor air distribution (UFAD) supplies air through a pressurized raised-floor plenum and floor diffusers, so the raised floor works as a positive-pressure supply plenum.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; NADCA Open Air Plenums white paper","prompt":"How does underfloor air distribution (UFAD) deliver supply air?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0141","ascs-0168"]},{"id":"hvac:s-mua","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-system-types","tag":"DEF","text":"A make-up air unit supplies tempered outdoor air to replace air removed by large exhausts such as kitchens, labs and paint booths.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a make-up air unit do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0104","ascs-0126","ascs-0167"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-control","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A variable air volume (VAV) system holds supply air at a fixed temperature, typically about 55°F, and each box throttles airflow to match its zone load.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a VAV system control space temperature, and at what typical supply temperature?","trap":"CAV is the reverse: fixed airflow, varying supply temperature.","num":{"q":"A typical VAV cooling supply air setpoint is about __.","a":"55°F","d":["44°F","68°F","72°F"]},"qs":["ascs-0155","ascs-0222"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-fan-vfd","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"As VAV boxes close and duct static rises, a VFD slows the supply fan to hold duct static pressure at a sensor down the main. Supply air temperature is a separate control loop.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"As VAV boxes close, what is the supply fan controlled to hold, and how?","trap":"The fan does not hold a fixed rpm, a supply temperature or building static.","qs":["ascs-0156"]},{"id":"hvac:s-bypass-vav","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A bypass VAV system pairs a constant-volume fan with VAV boxes and dumps surplus supply air to the return through a bypass damper, keeping fan airflow steady. It is common on small systems without a VFD.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does the bypass damper in a bypass VAV system do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0811"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-box","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"DEF","text":"A VAV box is a supply-side terminal that varies conditioned airflow to one zone. A single-duct box is an inlet damper, actuator and controller, plus an inlet flow sensor if pressure-independent, sometimes with a reheat coil.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a single-duct VAV box and what does it contain?","trap":"An induction opening to the plenum belongs to fan-powered boxes, not a plain single-duct box.","qs":["ascs-0097","ascs-0124","ascs-0138","ascs-0813"]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-independent","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A pressure-independent VAV box reads a velocity-pressure flow sensor, a cross or ring of tubing at its own inlet collar, so it holds its airflow setpoint whatever the inlet static does.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a pressure-independent VAV box read to hold its airflow setpoint?","trap":"The duct static sensor two-thirds down the main controls the fan, not the box.","qs":["ascs-0802","ascs-0804"]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-dependent","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A pressure-dependent VAV box has no flow sensor, so at a fixed damper angle its airflow rises and falls with the inlet duct pressure.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a pressure-dependent VAV box's airflow behave?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0803"]},{"id":"hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"If a VAV inlet flow sensor is bent, coated or has plugged ports, for example from encapsulant, the controller misreads airflow and mispositions the damper, overfeeding or starving the zone. Protect flow sensors during cleaning.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What happens when a VAV box's inlet flow sensor is coated or plugged?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0804","ascs-0805"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-minimum","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"VAV boxes throttle to a set minimum airflow instead of closing fully, so the zone still gets ventilation air when its cooling load is gone.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why do VAV boxes stop at a minimum airflow instead of closing fully?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0806"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cooling-only-overcool","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A cooling-only VAV box at minimum flow can still overcool its zone, because the primary air is still about 55°F; volume drops but temperature does not. Such zones get reheat or a fan box.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why can a cooling-only VAV box at minimum flow still overcool its zone?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0818"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"As zone load falls, a single-duct VAV reheat box throttles to minimum airflow; at setpoint the zone is satisfied. If the zone keeps cooling below setpoint, the reheat coil energizes at that minimum flow.","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide","prompt":"What is the single-duct VAV reheat sequence as zone load falls?","trap":"Reheat never runs with the damper wide open or fully closed.","qs":["ascs-0157","ascs-0810","ascs-0817"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vav-damper-fail","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV damper that fails to close, stuck open or with a failed actuator, overfeeds its zone. A box reading minimum while its space keeps overcooling points to the damper or actuator.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a VAV box at minimum position with its space overcooling suggest?","trap":"Rule out design first: a cooling-only box still delivers 55°F air at a true minimum.","qs":["ascs-0089","ascs-0136"]},{"id":"hvac:s-leaking-reheat-valve","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"If air leaving a VAV box's coil is warmer than the primary air while the box passes maximum cooling airflow, the hot water reheat valve is leaking or stuck open.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What does air leaving a VAV reheat coil warmer than primary air, at maximum cooling airflow, indicate?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-open-box-cleaning","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-vav","tag":"FACT","text":"Before cleaning duct downstream of a VAV box, command its damper open through the BAS or box controller so air and tools can pass, note the original state, and restore it afterward.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How should a VAV box damper be set before cleaning the duct downstream of it?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0819"]},{"id":"hvac:s-primary-air","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"DEF","text":"Primary air is the air sent from the central AHU through the main duct. Secondary or induced air is what a fan-powered box pulls from the ceiling plenum.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does 'primary air' mean at a terminal unit?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0822"]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-fan","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In a series fan-powered box the fan sits in the primary air path, runs continuously through occupied hours, and moves all the air delivered to the zone.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Where does the fan sit in a series fan-powered box, and when does it run?","trap":"A fan that runs only on heating calls is the parallel box.","qs":["ascs-0158"]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-constant","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A series fan-powered box delivers constant discharge airflow; as primary air varies, the fan makes up the difference with induced plenum air, so only the primary-to-plenum ratio changes.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What discharge airflow does a series fan-powered box deliver as primary air varies?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0808"]},{"id":"hvac:s-parallel-fan","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In a parallel fan-powered box the fan sits beside the primary air path and runs only on a heating call. In cooling, discharge airflow simply tracks the primary air.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Where does the fan sit in a parallel fan-powered box, and when does it run?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0807"]},{"id":"hvac:s-parallel-backdraft","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A parallel fan-powered box has a backdraft damper on the fan outlet, so cold primary air cannot spill backward through the idle fan into the ceiling plenum during cooling.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What stops primary air from flowing backward through the idle fan of a parallel box?","trap":"The plenum inlet filter loads with dust but does not block reverse flow.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-low-static","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A central fan serving series boxes can run at lower duct static than one serving parallel boxes, because primary air only has to reach each box fan, which is sized for the total airflow.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"Why can the central fan run at lower duct static with series fan-powered boxes?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-plenum-free-heat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"In heating, a fan-powered box recovers free heat by pulling ceiling plenum air already warmed by lights and roof gain, before any reheat is needed.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where does the free heat a fan-powered box uses in heating mode come from?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0809"]},{"id":"hvac:s-induction-filter","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"A fan-powered box has a small filter on its plenum induction inlet. When it loads with dust the box fan starves and the zone can run cold or noisy.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What cleaning-relevant part does a fan-powered VAV box have, and what happens when it clogs?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0823"]},{"id":"hvac:s-series-backward","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fan-boxes","tag":"FACT","text":"If primary air reaches a series box while its fan is off, the forward-curved wheel spins backward. The box fan must be energized before any primary air flows.","src":"Krueger white paper, Anti-Backward Rotation for SFPTUs","prompt":"What prevents a series fan-powered box's fan from spinning backward?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A multizone AHU has a hot deck and a cold deck with zone dampers at the unit, mixing deck air in different proportions for each zone before it leaves the unit.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where and how does a multizone system mix air for its zones?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0127","ascs-0144","ascs-0159"]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-ducts","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"Each multizone zone damper set discharges into its own duct, so a multizone unit serving eight zones has eight supply ducts leaving it, each carrying air already mixed.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How many supply ducts leave a multizone unit serving eight zones?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A multizone unit serving eight zones has __ supply ducts leaving it.","a":"eight","d":["one","two","sixteen"]},"qs":["ascs-0146","ascs-0159"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A dual-duct system runs separate hot and cold ducts from the unit's decks to every zone, where a terminal mixing box blends them.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a dual-duct system deliver to each zone?","trap":"Multizone mixes at the unit and runs one duct per zone; dual duct mixes at the zone box.","qs":["ascs-0145","ascs-0160"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct-terminal","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A dual-duct terminal is recognized by two inlet collars, one fed from the hot deck and one from the cold deck; it blends them for the zone, and VAV versions also vary the volume.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"What makes a dual-duct terminal recognizable?","trap":"Fan-powered and reheat boxes have one inlet.","qs":["ascs-0812"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-duct-fault","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"In a dual-duct building, one zone running hot in summer while its neighbors are comfortable and the cold duct is at design points to that terminal's hot-air inlet damper stuck open.","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","prompt":"In a dual-duct building, what fault makes one zone run hot while its neighbors are fine?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-waste","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"Multizone and dual-duct systems waste energy because they make hot air and cold air and then blend them for each zone, heating and cooling at the same time.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why do multizone and dual-duct systems waste energy?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0824","ascs-0848"]},{"id":"hvac:s-multizone-leak","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-multizone","tag":"FACT","text":"A multizone zone commanded to full cooling that reads warmer than the cold deck has hot-deck air leaking past its damper. Hot share = (zone minus cold deck) divided by (hot deck minus cold deck).","src":"USACE ERDC-CERL Multizone Tech Note","prompt":"How do you spot and estimate hot-deck leakage into a multizone zone set to full cooling?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-coil","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A fan coil unit is a local blower, a hydronic or DX coil and a filter serving one space, with little or no duct.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a fan coil unit and what does it contain?","trap":"A compressor or a VAV damper is not part of a standard fan coil.","qs":["ascs-0094","ascs-0137","ascs-0163"]},{"id":"hvac:s-two-four-pipe","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"A two-pipe fan coil system carries either hot or chilled water, not both, so it changes over by season. A four-pipe system has separate hot and chilled water and can heat one unit while cooling another.","src":"Krueger Fan Coil Engineering","prompt":"How do two-pipe and four-pipe fan coil systems differ?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ptac","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC) is a self-contained through-wall unit serving one room, such as a hotel room, with its whole refrigeration circuit in the wall sleeve.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC)?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0233"]},{"id":"hvac:s-pthp","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A packaged terminal heat pump (PTHP) is a PTAC that uses reverse-cycle refrigeration as its main heat source. A plain PTAC heats with hot water, steam or electric resistance.","src":"10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"What distinguishes a PTHP from a PTAC?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ptac-slinger","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"Many PTACs have no drain: a slinger ring on the condenser fan throws base-pan condensate onto the hot condenser coil to evaporate, so some water in the outdoor-side pan is normal in cooling.","src":"Friedrich PTAC service manual","prompt":"What normally removes condensate from a PTAC's outdoor-side base pan?","trap":"Sizing a trap to negative static belongs to draw-through air handlers, not PTACs.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-wshp-loop","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"In a boiler-tower water-source heat pump system, the units share one closed water loop; the boiler adds heat when the loop runs cold and the tower rejects heat when it runs warm.","src":"Armstrong WSHP application guide; 10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"In a boiler-tower water-source heat pump system, what do the boiler and tower do?","trap":"The loop is a heat source and sink for the units, not chilled water for air coils.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-wshp-access","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 counts water-source heat pumps and fan-coil units as ventilation system components that need access doors or panels sized for inspection and maintenance, even above a hard ceiling.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.2","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 62.1 require for access to ceiling-hung water-source heat pumps?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-induction-unit","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"Induction units have no fans: high-pressure primary air through small nozzles creates a venturi that pulls room air over the unit's coil, so lint on the coil and nozzles is the inspection target.","src":"Energy Efficiency Manual 4.9, Induction Systems","prompt":"How does an induction unit move room air over its coil?","trap":"Fan coils, unit ventilators and PTACs all have their own fans.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-mini-split","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"DEF","text":"A ductless mini-split has an outdoor condensing unit and one or more wall or ceiling indoor heads, each with its own fan and coil, joined by refrigerant lines.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a ductless mini-split and its indoor unit?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0234"]},{"id":"hvac:s-mini-split-blower","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"In a ductless wall head the cross-flow blower wheel sits right behind the wet coil and pan, a classic growth site that can throw musty black specks into the room even when the mesh filters look clean.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the likely source of black specks and musty odor from a ductless wall head with clean filters?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-multi-split","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-room-units","tag":"FACT","text":"A multi-split has one outdoor unit and two or more indoor units that run independently, so each head counts as its own air-side unit with its own fan, coil and condensate pan.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"How should the indoor heads of a multi-split system be counted in a scope?","trap":"There is no shared duct system or zoning damper to count.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-ahu-path","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"Air through a typical draw-through AHU runs mixing box, filters, preheat coil if present, cooling coil, supply fan, then supply duct. Filters come ahead of the coils to protect them.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the order of air through a typical draw-through AHU?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0132","ascs-0172"]},{"id":"hvac:s-mixing-box","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The mixing box, or mixed air plenum, is where the outdoor air and return air dampers meet and the two streams blend before the filters.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What happens in an AHU mixing box?","trap":"Hot deck and cold deck air combining for each zone is a multizone unit, not a mixing box.","qs":["ascs-0107","ascs-0173"]},{"id":"hvac:s-preheat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A preheat coil sits ahead of the cooling coil in cold climates or high outdoor air units, warming cold intake air to protect the cooling coil from freezing.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a preheat coil do and where does it sit?","trap":"A coil downstream of the cooling coil is reheat.","qs":["ascs-0176","ascs-0177"]},{"id":"hvac:s-draw-through","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a draw-through AHU the fan sits downstream of the coils and pulls air through them, so the coil section and its drain pan run under negative pressure.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where is the fan in a draw-through AHU, and what pressure does the coil section see?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0170","ascs-0171"]},{"id":"hvac:s-blow-through","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a blow-through AHU the fan sits upstream of the coils and pushes air through them, so the coil section runs under positive pressure.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where is the fan in a blow-through AHU, and what pressure does the coil section see?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0170","ascs-0171"]},{"id":"hvac:s-blow-through-wet","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"In a blow-through unit, air leaving the cooling coil is at or near saturation and goes straight into whatever sits downstream, so final filters right after the coil can soak. In draw-through units, fan heat moves air away from saturation.","src":"HPAC Engineering, The Case of the Wet Filters","prompt":"Why can final filters just after the cooling coil in a blow-through AHU get soaked?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-return-relief-fans","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A return fan runs whenever the supply fan runs and pressurizes the return plenum, so leaks there push air out. A relief fan runs only when building pressure needs relief, usually in economizer mode.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN003","prompt":"How does a return fan differ from a relief fan?","trap":"With a relief fan system, the return plenum runs negative while the relief fan is off.","qs":["ascs-0915"]},{"id":"hvac:s-oa-intake","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"An outdoor air intake must sit away from exhaust discharges, cooling towers, loading docks and vents, or it pulls contaminated or moist air into the supply.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1 (outdoor air intake separation)","prompt":"Where should an outdoor air intake be located, and what is the risk of a bad location?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0237","ascs-0909"]},{"id":"hvac:s-humidifier-types","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A steam humidifier, the commercial norm, injects steam through dispersion tubes in the airstream. An evaporative wetted-media humidifier holds a wet pad and sump that can grow organisms into the airstream.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How do steam and evaporative AHU humidifiers work, and which is the growth concern?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0219","ascs-0907"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ultrasonic","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"Ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers spray dissolved tank minerals, and any microorganisms, into the air, leaving fine white dust; low-mineral water is the fix.","src":"EPA, Use and Care of Home Humidifiers","prompt":"What causes fine white dust near an ultrasonic humidifier fed with tap water?","trap":"Steam and evaporative types are not expected to disperse significant minerals.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-absorption-distance","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires turning vanes, volume dampers, duct offsets over 15 degrees and air cleaners downstream of a humidifier to sit at or beyond its absorption distance, or unabsorbed moisture wets them.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.8.2","prompt":"What ASHRAE 62.1 rule governs obstructions downstream of a humidifier?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-drain-pan","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"DEF","text":"The drain pan sits under the cooling coil to collect condensate and send it out the drain line. Pans and drain lines are AHU components inspected with the coil section.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a drain pan do, and which component group is it part of?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0107","ascs-0122","ascs-0131"]},{"id":"hvac:s-pan-slope","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires drain pans to slope at least 1/8 inch per foot toward the outlet, or otherwise drain freely, with the outlet at the pan's lowest point.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.1","prompt":"What slope does ASHRAE 62.1 require of a drain pan?","trap":"1-1/2 inch deep and 3 inches larger are the IRC auxiliary pan, not the 62.1 slope.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires drain pans to slope at least __ per foot toward the drain.","a":"1/8 inch","d":["1/2 inch","1-1/2 inches","3 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-draw-through-trap","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A draw-through pan sits below room pressure, so its drain needs a P-trap or other seal. Without one, suction pulls air up the drain, so the pan cannot drain while the fan runs and can overflow.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.7.3","prompt":"Why does a draw-through unit's drain pan need a trap?","trap":"Not because every gravity drain needs a trap by code.","qs":["ascs-0174"]},{"id":"hvac:s-trap-height","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A common coil-maker rule: for negative pressure, trap height H is the maximum negative static plus 1 inch, so a minus 2.0 in. w.c. pan needs 3.0 inches; for positive pressure, H is static plus 1/2 inch.","src":"Marlo Coil, Condensate Drain Trapping","prompt":"How tall must a condensate trap be for negative and for positive pressure pans?","trap":"","num":{"q":"By the coil-maker rule, the minimum trap height H for a draw-through pan section at minus 2.0 in. w.c. is __.","a":"3.0 inches","d":["1.5 inches","2.0 inches","2.5 inches"]},"qs":["ascs-0171"]},{"id":"hvac:s-float-switch","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"FACT","text":"A float switch in the drain pan locks the unit out when the water rises too high, before the pan overflows onto the ceiling.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a float switch in a condensate drain pan do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0175"]},{"id":"hvac:s-overflow-protection","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-ahu","tag":"CODE","text":"Where overflow would damage the building, the IRC requires an auxiliary pan at least 1-1/2 inches deep with its own drain, a separate overflow drain, or a water-level shutoff. An overflow tied into the primary drain does not count.","src":"IRC M1411 (auxiliary drain pan); IMC 307.2.3","prompt":"What overflow protection does the IRC require for a cooling coil above finished space?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The minimum depth the IRC sets for an auxiliary drain pan is __.","a":"1-1/2 inches","d":["1/8 inch","3/4 inch","3 inches"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-economizer","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"DEF","text":"An economizer modulates the outdoor and return air dampers to cool with outdoor air when conditions allow, which is called free cooling.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"What does an air-side economizer do?","trap":"Recovering exhaust heat into the supply is an ERV or HRV, not an economizer.","qs":["ascs-0165"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A dry-bulb economizer judges outdoor air by temperature alone, so it admits cool but very humid air. Differential enthalpy control compares the total heat, including moisture, of outdoor and return air.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"Why is a dry-bulb economizer weak in a humid climate, and what does enthalpy control add?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0840"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-integrated","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An integrated economizer keeps outdoor-air free cooling running alongside mechanical cooling: outdoor air is the first stage, and the compressor or chiller adds the rest while the damper stays open.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does an integrated economizer mean?","trap":"It does not lock out whenever the compressor runs.","qs":["ascs-0841"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-stuck-open","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An economizer or outdoor air damper stuck wide open in winter raises the heating load, and outdoor air below 32°F can freeze and split a hydronic coil.","src":"PNNL, Air-Side Economizer O&M Best Practices","prompt":"What does an economizer stuck at full outdoor air in winter cause?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0090"]},{"id":"hvac:s-oa-fraction","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"Mixed air temperature is the weighted average of outdoor and return air, so outdoor air fraction = (mixed minus return) divided by (outdoor minus return). Example: 20% outdoor air at 30°F with return at 70°F mixes to 62°F.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How do you find the outdoor air fraction from outdoor, return and mixed air temperatures?","trap":"Dividing by mixed minus outdoor is a common slip that overstates outdoor air.","qs":["ascs-0910"]},{"id":"hvac:s-econ-readings","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"Compare the outdoor air fraction calculated from temperatures with the damper command; they should track within about 25 percent. A damper commanded open while mixed air sits near return points to blades, linkage or actuator.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How can temperature readings show an economizer damper is not doing what the BAS commands?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-min-oa-fixed","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"In a VAV unit, a minimum outdoor air damper locked in one position lets outdoor airflow fall roughly in step with supply airflow as the fan slows. Holding the minimum takes active outdoor air control.","src":"CEC Nonres. Compliance Manual 2016 §4.3","prompt":"What happens to outdoor airflow through a fixed minimum damper as a VAV fan slows?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-relief-air","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"DEF","text":"Relief air is excess return air spilled outdoors through a relief damper or fan when the economizer brings in extra outdoor air. Exhaust air is pulled from restrooms and kitchens by dedicated fans.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does relief air differ from exhaust air?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0229"]},{"id":"hvac:s-erv-hrv","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An energy recovery ventilator (ERV) transfers moisture as well as sensible heat between exhaust and outdoor air. A heat recovery ventilator (HRV) transfers sensible heat only.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does an ERV differ from an HRV?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0166"]},{"id":"hvac:s-hrv-drain","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An HRV needs a condensate drain because its core moves only heat, so moisture in warm exhaust air condenses on the cold core in winter; many ERVs do not. Paper ERV cores are vacuumed, never washed.","src":"Broan HRV/ERV installation guide; RenewAire EV manual","prompt":"Why does an HRV need a condensate drain when many ERVs do not?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"An enthalpy wheel is a desiccant rotor that carries sensible heat and moisture between exhaust and outdoor air. Its purge section flushes the rotor with outdoor air to cut exhaust carryover into the supply.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does an enthalpy wheel transfer, and what does its purge section do?","trap":"Sealed aluminum plate cores transfer sensible heat only.","qs":["ascs-0843","ascs-0917"]},{"id":"hvac:s-wheel-leakage","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A wheel leaks exhaust air into the supply through seal leakage and carryover, and a higher static on the exhaust side forces more across. Keep the supply side at the higher pressure.","src":"ASHRAE, Practical Guidance for Epidemic Operation of ERVs","prompt":"Why would restroom odor appear in supply air from an enthalpy wheel?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-runaround","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-economizers","tag":"FACT","text":"A runaround loop is two coils joined by pumped fluid, one in each airstream, so the airstreams stay completely separate even in different air handlers; wheels always pass some air across.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Which energy recovery device keeps the two airstreams completely separate, even at a distance?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0844"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-function","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"DEF","text":"Coils are heat exchangers that temper, meaning heat or cool, and dehumidify the air passing through them.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What do coils in an HVAC system do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0096"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dx-coil","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A direct expansion (DX) coil is the evaporator itself: refrigerant boils inside its tubes. It is recognized by a distributor feeding many small tubes and a large insulated suction line.","src":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","prompt":"What is a DX coil and how is it recognized?","trap":"Chilled and hot water coils use supply and return headers, not a distributor.","qs":["ascs-0178","ascs-0187"]},{"id":"hvac:s-evap-cond","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"In cooling, the evaporator (indoor coil) boils low-pressure refrigerant at about 40°F, absorbing heat and moisture from the air. The condenser (outdoor coil) rejects that heat plus compressor work, turning vapor back to liquid.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What do the evaporator and the condenser do in the cooling cycle?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0884","ascs-0885"]},{"id":"hvac:s-chilled-water","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"In a chilled water system a central chiller makes water around 44°F that is pumped through the AHU coil (CHWS and CHWR). Condenser water (CWS and CWR) links the chiller and cooling tower and does not feed the AHU coil.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What feeds a chilled water cooling coil, and where does condenser water go?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0179"]},{"id":"hvac:s-water-steam-coils","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A hydronic heating coil carries hot water from a boiler. Steam coils are a separate type, and distributing (tube-in-tube) steam coils spread steam evenly to resist freezing where air can fall below 40°F.","src":"Marlo Coil, How to Identify the Four Main Types of Coils","prompt":"What feeds a hydronic heating coil, and why use a distributing steam coil for preheat?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0186"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ton","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"DEF","text":"One ton of cooling equals 12,000 BTU per hour, the heat needed to melt a ton of ice in 24 hours.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How many BTU per hour is one ton of cooling?","trap":"3,412 BTU per hour is one kilowatt, not one ton.","num":{"q":"One ton of cooling equals __.","a":"12,000 BTU/h","d":["3,412 BTU/h","24,000 BTU/h","60,000 BTU/h"]},"qs":["ascs-0183"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"The rule of thumb for DX coil airflow is about 400 CFM per ton, 350 to 450 by humidity design, so a 3-ton system moves about 1,200 CFM. Too little air freezes the coil; too much hurts dehumidification.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the rule-of-thumb airflow per ton across a DX cooling coil?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Rule-of-thumb airflow across a DX cooling coil is about __ per ton.","a":"400 CFM","d":["100 CFM","200 CFM","800 CFM"]},"qs":["ascs-0184","ascs-0852"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-delta-t","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A healthy DX cooling coil drops air temperature about 15 to 20°F. A bigger split at a similar load usually means low airflow, such as a loaded coil or dirty filter.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the typical temperature drop across a healthy DX coil, and what does a larger split suggest?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A healthy DX cooling coil typically drops air temperature about __.","a":"15 to 20°F","d":["5 to 8°F","30 to 35°F","40 to 45°F"]},"qs":["ascs-0185"]},{"id":"hvac:s-face-velocity","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Cooling coils are sized for roughly 400 to 550 FPM face velocity, about 500 FPM. Faster air blows condensate off the fins into the duct and liner past the drain pan.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why is cooling coil face velocity kept around 500 FPM?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Cooling coil face velocity is normally kept around __ to avoid condensate carryover.","a":"500 FPM","d":["50 FPM","900 FPM","4,005 FPM"]},"qs":["ascs-0900"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-rows","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Adding coil rows or fins per inch raises capacity and air pressure drop and gives dirt more places to bridge, so deep coils are harder to clean and are cleaned from both faces.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does adding rows or fins per inch to a coil do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0901"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fin-straightening","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"Fin straightening tools restore bent fins so air passes evenly across the coil; bent fins block airflow.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What do fin straightening tools do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0188"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-loading","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"As a coil loads, its air pressure drop rises and airflow falls in a constant-volume system, cutting capacity and risking coil freeze. Comparing coil pressure drop before and after cleaning shows the change.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a rising pressure drop across a cooling coil do, and how is cleaning improvement shown?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0083","ascs-0133"]},{"id":"hvac:s-leaking-heat-valve","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"FACT","text":"A temperature rise across an AHU heating coil whose valve is commanded closed means a leaking hot water valve; the cooling valve then opens to remove that heat, heating and cooling at once.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What does a temperature rise across a heating coil with its valve commanded closed indicate?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-access","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-coils","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires access to the upstream face of coils four rows deep or less, and to both faces above four rows. ACR 2025 requires access to both sides of every coil for cleaning.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.19.3; ACR 2025 §4.11","prompt":"What coil access does ASHRAE 62.1 require, and how does ACR 2025 differ?","trap":"A 62.1-compliant 4-row coil with one door can still need a second door for an ACR cleaning scope.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-split-system","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"DEF","text":"A split system has an indoor assembly holding the indoor coil, with its own blower or relying on a furnace blower, and a separate outdoor assembly with the outdoor coil, joined by refrigerant lines.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"What is a split system?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0161"]},{"id":"hvac:s-packaged-unit","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A packaged (single-package) unit, such as a rooftop unit, holds the compressor, both coils, the fan and often heat in one cabinet, so its evaporator coil, blower and drain pan are inspected at that outdoor cabinet.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"What does a packaged unit contain, and where are its evaporator coil and drain pan?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0162"]},{"id":"hvac:s-coil-only","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A cased coil on a furnace with no blower of its own relies on the furnace's circulating blower to move indoor air across it, so the blower compartment is part of the same air-side inspection.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M definitions","prompt":"On a split system with a coil-only cabinet on a furnace, what moves air across the coil?","trap":"The inducer moves flue gas and the condenser fan moves outdoor air; neither moves supply air.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-furnace-coil-location","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"The cooling coil goes on a furnace's supply side, since the IRC bars coils upstream of the heat exchanger unless listed. An upflow furnace has the coil on top; a downflow furnace, below it on the supply plenum.","src":"IRC M1411 (cooling coil location)","prompt":"Where is the cooling coil on an upflow versus a downflow furnace, and why?","trap":"The top of a downflow furnace is its return side.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-high-limit","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A furnace high-limit switch opens when the heat exchanger area overheats from low airflow, such as a dirty filter, closed registers or a failed blower; it shuts the gas valve while the blower keeps running.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"When does a furnace high-limit switch open, and why?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0895"]},{"id":"hvac:s-cracked-hx","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A cracked furnace heat exchanger is dangerous because it can pass flue gas, including carbon monoxide, into the supply air; the exchanger is what separates combustion gas from house air.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why is a cracked furnace heat exchanger dangerous?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0225"]},{"id":"hvac:s-zoning-bypass","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"In a zoned house, a bypass duct with a weighted damper dumps supply air into the return. With one small zone calling, cold air is reconditioned, return air cools and the indoor coil can freeze.","src":"JLC, Troubleshooting Zone Damper Systems","prompt":"How can a zoning bypass duct lead to a frozen indoor coil?","trap":"The bypass exists to relieve static when zones close, not to add it.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-reversing-valve","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A heat pump's reversing valve changes refrigerant flow direction. In heating the indoor coil becomes the condenser, so it runs warm and dry and its drain pan makes no condensate.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Air-Source Heat Pumps","prompt":"What does a heat pump's reversing valve do to the indoor coil in heating mode?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-defrost","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Defrost briefly switches a heat pump to cooling mode so hot gas melts frost off the outdoor coil. The outdoor fan stops, the coil may steam, and auxiliary heat keeps the supply air warm.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What happens during a heat pump defrost cycle?","trap":"Emergency heat shuts the heat pump off, so the outdoor coil would not steam.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-aux-heat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Auxiliary heat is the heat pump's electric strips, added automatically alongside the compressor when it falls behind below the balance point, and during defrost.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What is heat pump auxiliary heat and when does it run?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0182"]},{"id":"hvac:s-emergency-heat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"Emergency heat is a manual thermostat setting that shuts the heat pump's compressor off and runs the resistance strips alone, which costs far more to operate.","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat","prompt":"What does the EM HEAT setting on a heat pump thermostat do?","trap":"Aux heat is automatic and runs alongside the compressor; emergency heat is manual and replaces it.","qs":["ascs-0182"]},{"id":"hvac:s-balance-point","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"DEF","text":"A heat pump's balance point is the outdoor temperature where its heating output equals the building's heat loss. Below it, supplemental heat, strips or a furnace in dual fuel, is staged on.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a heat pump's balance point?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0847","ascs-0850"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dual-fuel","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"In a dual-fuel system the heat pump and gas furnace take turns: the heat pump carries mild weather and the furnace takes over when it gets cold, typically near the balance point.","src":"Trane, Dual Fuel Heat Pump glossary","prompt":"How do the heat pump and furnace share the load in a dual-fuel system?","trap":"They do not run together; running alongside the compressor is how electric aux strips work.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-geothermal","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-heat-pumps","tag":"FACT","text":"A typical water-to-air ground-source (geothermal) heat pump has no outdoor unit: buried loop pipes feed an indoor cabinet holding the compressor, coil, blower and drain pan, inspected like any air handler.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Geothermal Heat Pumps; 10 CFR 431.92","prompt":"What does a ground-source heat pump look like, and how is its air side inspected?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-fc","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A forward-curved centrifugal (squirrel cage) fan, with many small cupped blades curved toward rotation, is typical in residential furnaces and small air handlers; its blades load with dust.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"Where is a forward-curved centrifugal fan typically found?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0189"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-bi","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A backward-inclined fan has blades tilted away from rotation, flat, curved or airfoil, and is common in larger AHUs; its low angle of impingement lets dust build up on the blades.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"How is a backward-inclined fan recognized, and what cleaning issue does it have?","trap":"Radial blades are the type whose flat shape limits buildup.","qs":["ascs-0189"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-plenum","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A plenum (plug) fan is a centrifugal wheel with no scroll housing that pressurizes the whole surrounding cabinet section, so air can leave in several directions.","src":"Trane Engineers Newsletter ADM-APN036","prompt":"What is a plenum fan and what does it do to its cabinet?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0190"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-axial","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A vaneaxial fan is a tubeaxial fan with fixed outlet vanes that straighten swirl for medium to high pressure duty. A tubeaxial is a propeller in a cylinder; a propeller fan moves high flow at low pressure.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"How do vaneaxial, tubeaxial and propeller fans differ?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fc-overload","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A forward-curved fan's power rises steadily with airflow, so cleaning a loaded wheel or removing a duct restriction can raise motor amps toward nameplate and risk overload.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook, ch. 2","prompt":"Why can motor amps rise after a forward-curved blower is cleaned and restrictions removed?","trap":"Backward-inclined fans are non-overloading; their power falls off at high airflow.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-flow","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan airflow varies directly with fan speed: a fan moving 8,000 CFM at 900 RPM moves about 9,600 CFM at 1,080 RPM.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"How does fan airflow change with fan speed?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A fan moving 8,000 CFM at 900 RPM, sped up to 1,080 RPM, moves about __.","a":"9,600 CFM","d":["6,700 CFM","11,500 CFM","13,800 CFM"]},"qs":["ascs-0193"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-pressure","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan static pressure varies with the square of fan speed, so doubling speed doubles airflow but makes static pressure about four times higher.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, what does doubling fan speed do to static pressure?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling fan speed makes static pressure about __ as high.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","8 times","16 times"]},"qs":["ascs-0085","ascs-0193"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-law-power","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan power varies with the cube of speed: doubling speed takes about eight times the power, and even a 20 percent speed-up raises 5.0 bhp to about 8.6 bhp, which can overload the motor.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, how does fan power change with speed?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling fan speed raises fan power about __.","a":"8 times","d":["2 times","4 times","16 times"]},"qs":["ascs-0194"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fan-half-speed","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Per the fan laws, cutting fan speed in half cuts fan power to about one-eighth, which is why VFDs and VAV save fan energy as boxes throttle.","src":"DOE fan sourcebook","prompt":"Per the fan laws, what does halving fan speed do to fan power?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Cutting fan speed in half reduces fan power to about __ of the original.","a":"one-eighth","d":["one-half","one-quarter","one-sixteenth"]},"qs":["ascs-0814","ascs-0849"]},{"id":"hvac:s-vfd","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"DEF","text":"A variable frequency drive (VFD) controls fan output by changing the motor's supply frequency, so speed follows. Inlet guide vanes and discharge dampers are the older methods.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a variable frequency drive control fan output?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0192"]},{"id":"hvac:s-ecm","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A constant-airflow ECM is a variable-speed motor that ramps up to hold its programmed CFM as static rises, drawing more watts, until it hits its limit and airflow falls. A PSC motor simply loses airflow.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a constant-airflow ECM blower respond to rising static pressure?","trap":"Direct-drive ECMs have no belts or sheaves to blame.","qs":["ascs-0093","ascs-0195","ascs-0835"]},{"id":"hvac:s-belt-drive","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"On a belt-driven fan the adjustable motor sheave sets fan speed. Slipping or worn belts and worn or misaligned sheaves cut speed and airflow, so check them first when airflow is low.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What sets a belt-driven fan's speed, and what is checked first when its airflow is low?","trap":"Belt tension affects slip and wear, not the design drive ratio.","qs":["ascs-0084","ascs-0191","ascs-0899"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dirty-wheel","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"A dirty blower wheel moves less air at the same RPM because dust fills its cupped blades; cleaning the wheel is often the biggest airflow gain on a duct cleaning job.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a dirty blower wheel most directly cause?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0898"]},{"id":"hvac:s-system-resistance","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-fans","tag":"FACT","text":"Adding resistance, such as closing supply dampers to balance a constant-volume system, raises system static pressure and moves the fan back on its curve to lower airflow.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"In a constant-volume system, what does closing supply dampers do to the fan?","trap":"Fan power does not fall in proportion and total airflow does not rise.","qs":["ascs-0091"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dampers-balancing","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"Dampers are air-directional devices that control or direct airflow. A balancing damper is set during test and balance to fix a branch's airflow and then locked; it does not modulate zone temperature.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What do dampers do, and what is a balancing damper for?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0106","ascs-0200"]},{"id":"hvac:s-backdraft","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"A backdraft (gravity) damper is a flap with no actuator that lets air pass one way and stops reverse flow, used on exhaust discharges and intakes.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a backdraft damper?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0199"]},{"id":"hvac:s-opposed-blade","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"Opposed-blade dampers give a more linear flow response and are preferred for modulating duty; parallel-blade dampers are fine for two-position (open or closed) duty.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why are opposed-blade dampers preferred for modulating?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0198"]},{"id":"hvac:s-face-bypass","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"Face-and-bypass dampers control a coil's output by routing part of the air around the coil while the water or steam flow stays constant; they are common on preheat and steam coils.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How do face-and-bypass dampers control coil output?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0906"]},{"id":"hvac:s-fire-damper","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A fire damper closes when its fusible link melts at a set temperature. It responds to heat, not to a smoke detector, alarm or BAS signal.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What closes a fire damper?","trap":"Closing on a detector signal is the smoke damper.","qs":["ascs-0130","ascs-0196"]},{"id":"hvac:s-smoke-damper","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A smoke damper is closed by an actuator driven by a smoke detector or smoke-control signal, not by a fusible link.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What closes a smoke damper?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0130","ascs-0197"]},{"id":"hvac:s-combination-damper","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"DEF","text":"A combination fire and smoke damper does both jobs, closing on heat like a fire damper and on a detection signal like a smoke damper; plan legends often tag it FSD.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a combination fire and smoke damper do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0197"]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-dynamic","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"A static-rated fire damper is meant for systems whose fans shut down in a fire and is not rated to close against moving air. Where fans keep running, dynamic-rated dampers are needed.","src":"Ruskin IBD2 fire damper spec (UL 555)","prompt":"Why is a static-rated fire damper a concern where fans keep running during a fire?","trap":"","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-link-165","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"FACT","text":"The standard fire damper fusible link is rated 165°F, with 212°F links available as an option.","src":"Ruskin IBD2 fire damper spec (UL 555)","prompt":"What is the standard fire damper fusible link rating?","trap":"160°F is the IMC floor for the actuating device, not the standard link rating.","num":{"q":"The standard fire damper fusible link is rated __.","a":"165°F","d":["160°F","212°F","350°F"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-imc-160","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC sets a fire damper's heat-actuating device about 50°F above normal duct temperature, but not below 160°F, and not above 350°F in a smoke control system.","src":"IMC 607.3.3.1","prompt":"What temperature limits does the IMC set for a fire damper's heat-actuating device?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Per the IMC, a fire damper's heat-actuating device operates about 50°F above normal duct temperature, but not below __.","a":"160°F","d":["165°F","212°F","350°F"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-nfpa80-test","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-dampers","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 80 has fire dampers tested 1 year after installation and then every 4 years, except hospitals, which may go every 6 years. NFPA 105 sets the same cycle for smoke dampers.","src":"NFPA 80 §19.4.1 (2010 ed.); NFPA 105","prompt":"How often does NFPA 80 require fire dampers to be tested?","trap":"A 1-year repeat is ACR's recommended healthcare inspection interval; NFPA 80 uses 1 year only for the first damper test.","num":{"q":"Outside hospitals, NFPA 80 fire damper tests repeat every __ after the first-year test.","a":"4 years","d":["1 year","2 years","6 years"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-sensor","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A VAV duct static pressure sensor usually sits about two-thirds of the way down the main supply duct, a compromise between the fan and the worst-case box.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where is a VAV duct static pressure sensor usually placed?","trap":"Not at the fan discharge, not in the return, and not at the farthest diffuser.","qs":["ascs-0221"]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-reset","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Static pressure reset (trim and respond) keeps lowering the duct static setpoint until the most-demanding box damper is nearly wide open, so the fan does no more work than the worst zone needs.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does VAV static pressure reset lower the fan setpoint until?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0815"]},{"id":"hvac:s-static-too-low","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"If most VAV boxes sit over about 75 percent open in cooling, below their maximum airflow, with warm zones while the fan has speed to spare, the duct static setpoint is most likely too low.","src":"PNNL-29078 §4.3.8","prompt":"What does it mean when most VAV boxes sit nearly wide open with warm zones and spare fan speed?","trap":"A setpoint set too high shows the opposite: boxes mostly throttled.","qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:s-sat-reset","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Supply air temperature reset raises the cooling supply setpoint in mild weather, cutting reheat and cooling energy at the cost of more fan energy, because boxes then need more airflow.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does supply air temperature reset trade off?","trap":"It spends fan energy to save reheat and cooling, not the reverse.","qs":["ascs-0816"]},{"id":"hvac:s-sequence-of-operation","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"DEF","text":"The sequence of operation is the written description of how the controls run the fans, dampers and valves in each mode; get it before touching a BAS.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a sequence of operation?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0913"]},{"id":"hvac:s-freeze-stat","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A freeze stat (low-limit) has a capillary across the coil face that trips at about 35 to 38°F anywhere along its length, stopping the fan and closing the outdoor air damper to protect water coils.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a freeze stat across a coil face do?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0911"]},{"id":"hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct smoke detector senses smoke in the airstream and shuts the air handler fan down; it is a life safety device that cleaning work must not impair.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a duct smoke detector do?","trap":"Closing at a rated temperature is a fire damper's fusible link, not the detector.","qs":["ascs-0223","ascs-0553"]},{"id":"hvac:s-dcv","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"Demand control ventilation trims outdoor air based on CO2 when occupancy is low, subject to code minimums; it is common in conference rooms, gyms and assembly spaces.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does demand control ventilation save energy?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0842"]},{"id":"hvac:s-unoccupied","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"In unoccupied mode a typical commercial AHU closes the outdoor air damper and lets the space float to setback; some sequences add a pre-occupancy purge or optimal start.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a typical commercial AHU sequence do in unoccupied mode?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0914"]},{"id":"hvac:s-pressure-interlock","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-controls","tag":"FACT","text":"A supply fan interlocked with an exhaust fan and a motorized outdoor air damper is a pressure-control sequence against depressurization; defeating an interlock during cleaning changes building pressure.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is a supply fan interlocked with an exhaust fan and a motorized outdoor air damper?","trap":"","qs":["ascs-0239"]},{"id":"hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Bare galvanized sheet metal duct, rectangular or spiral round, is non-porous. It can take a wet process that damages nothing and keeps water and agents off electrical parts and porous materials, and the NADCA Vacuum Test can verify it.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions; §4.4; §5.3.3","prompt":"What makes bare galvanized sheet metal duct eligible for wet cleaning and the NADCA Vacuum Test?","trap":"Liner, duct board and attenuated duct are porous: no water, no Vacuum Test.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0099"]},{"id":"hvac:a-spiral-flat-oval","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Spiral duct is round duct wound from a metal strip with a continuous helical lock seam. Flat oval duct keeps much of round duct's low friction and stiffness in a lower profile where round will not fit.","src":"HVAC fundamentals (SMACNA duct construction)","prompt":"How are spiral duct and flat oval duct built, and when is flat oval used?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-board","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Fibrous glass duct board is rigid insulation board with a foil outer facing that serves as the vapor barrier. The board is formed into the duct itself.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is fibrous glass duct board?","trap":"Board IS the duct. Liner is glued inside a metal duct; wrap goes outside one.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0116","ascs-0207"]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-liner","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct liner is insulation, usually fibrous glass, applied inside a metal duct for thermal and sound control. It faces the airstream, so it is a porous airside surface.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is duct liner and where is it installed?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0100","ascs-0208"]},{"id":"hvac:a-lined-duct-openings","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"A service opening cut in internally lined duct, together with its closure, must leave no cut fibrous glass liner edges exposed to the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.1","prompt":"What liner condition at a service opening in lined duct is a deficiency?","trap":"4 in. screw spacing and 1 in. panel overlap are compliant; exposed liner edges are not.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-wrap","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Duct wrap is insulation applied to the outside of a duct with all seams and joints sealed to form a continuous vapor barrier. It never touches the airstream.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is duct wrap, and how does it differ from duct liner?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0082","ascs-0101"]},{"id":"hvac:a-wrap-repair","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"When a service opening disturbs external duct wrap, the wrap must be repaired so no break degrades its R-value, service rating, vapor barrier or air barrier.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.8.2","prompt":"What must be confirmed about external duct wrap after a service opening is closed?","trap":"Restoring the wrap is not left to the owner's option.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"Porous components can be penetrated by air or water: fibrous glass liner and duct board, sound attenuated duct, wood and concrete. Non-porous: sheet metal, aluminum foil and the polymeric film liner of flex duct.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"Which HVAC duct materials does ACR classify as porous and which as non-porous?","trap":"Flex duct's inner polymer film is non-porous even though flex is soft.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0071","ascs-0072","ascs-0098","ascs-0099"]},{"id":"hvac:a-porous-dry-only","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"Water and cleaning agents never contact fibrous glass or other porous duct. Duct board, liner and attenuators get dry mechanical cleaning, and Method 2, not the Vacuum Test, ends their verification.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.4; §4.17; §5.3.3","prompt":"How must porous duct such as duct board be cleaned and verified?","trap":"A low-pressure rinse with extraction is still water on porous board.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-friction","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Flex duct has more friction than rigid metal even when fully stretched. Slack, sag or compression can more than double it and cut airflow well below the chart value.","src":"ACCA Manual D; ADC installation standards","prompt":"How does flex duct compare with rigid metal for friction, and what does slack do?","trap":"Flex is never equal to metal, even pulled taut.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0206","ascs-0865"]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-support","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Support flex duct at intervals no greater than 4 ft, with sag no more than 1/2 in. per foot of spacing, on straps or saddles at least 1 1/2 in. wide.","src":"ADC Flexible Duct Installation Standards; DOE Building America","prompt":"What support spacing, sag limit and strap width apply to flex duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Horizontal flex duct supports go no more than __ apart, with sag held to 1/2 in. per foot of spacing.","a":"4 ft","d":["2 ft","6 ft","8 ft"]},"qs":["ascs-0866"]},{"id":"hvac:a-ul181-classes","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 Class 0 air duct has flame spread and smoke developed ratings of zero. Class 1 allows a flame spread index up to 25 and smoke developed up to 50.","src":"UL 181 (via DOE Building America)","prompt":"What separates UL 181 Class 0 from Class 1 flex duct?","trap":"Class 0 is the zero rating. UL 181A and 181B are closure listings, not duct classes.","num":{"q":"UL 181 Class 1 air duct allows a flame spread index up to __ and smoke developed up to 50.","a":"25","d":["0","50","75"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-ul181-family","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"DEF","text":"UL 181 lists factory-made air ducts and air connectors. UL 181A lists closures for rigid duct such as duct board; UL 181B lists closures for flexible duct and connectors.","src":"UL 181; UL 181A; UL 181B","prompt":"What do UL 181, UL 181A and UL 181B each cover?","trap":"UL 723 is the surface burning test (25/50), not a closure listing.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0749","ascs-0750","ascs-0782"]},{"id":"hvac:a-board-closures","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"CODE","text":"Duct board keeps its UL 181 Class 1 air duct rating only when joints are closed with UL 181A listed closures: 181A-P foil tape, 181A-H heat-activated tape or 181A-M mastic.","src":"UL 181A; duct board manufacturer instructions; ACR 2025 §4.2.5.4","prompt":"What closures must duct board joints use to keep the UL 181 Class 1 rating?","trap":"181B-FX is the flex duct listing; cloth duct tape voids the board's rating.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-board-pressure","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Fibrous glass duct board is a low-pressure UL 181 Class 1 air duct rated to about 2 in. w.g. static. Higher pressure classes are built in metal.","src":"NAIMA; Owens Corning EnduraGold data","prompt":"What static pressure is fibrous glass duct board limited to?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Fibrous glass duct board systems are limited to about __ static pressure.","a":"2 in. w.g.","d":["1/2 in. w.g.","6 in. w.g.","10 in. w.g."]},"qs":["ascs-0882"]},{"id":"hvac:a-flex-no-openings","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"SHALL","text":"No service opening is cut into flexible duct. Flex runs are accessed by disconnecting them at the ends and reattaching them per the manufacturer's instructions.","src":"ACR 2025 §4.2.9","prompt":"How is the inside of a flexible duct run reached for inspection or cleaning?","trap":"No UL 181B tape or gasketed panel makes a cut in flex acceptable.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0535"]},{"id":"hvac:a-fabric-duct","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-materials","tag":"FACT","text":"Fabric (textile) air dispersion duct, listed to UL 2518 and UL 723, is cleaned the way its manufacturer directs, usually taken down and laundered. It is not cut open for access or pressure washed in place.","src":"Manufacturer instructions; UL 2518","prompt":"How is soiled fabric air dispersion duct cleaned?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-equal-friction","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"The equal friction method sizes every section for the same pressure loss per 100 ft, often 0.08 to 0.10 in. w.g. per 100 ft on low-pressure work, read off a ductulator from each section's CFM.","src":"ACCA Manual D; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does the equal friction duct sizing method work?","trap":"Equal friction does not hold outlet static equal; that is static regain.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0858","ascs-0873"]},{"id":"hvac:a-static-regain","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Static regain sizing lets velocity drop after each takeoff so the velocity pressure regained as static offsets the next section's friction, keeping outlet static about even. It suits high-velocity and long commercial runs.","src":"HVAC fundamentals (ASHRAE Fundamentals, duct design)","prompt":"How does the static regain duct sizing method work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0859"]},{"id":"hvac:a-round-vs-rect","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"For the same airflow, round duct beats rectangular because a circle has the least perimeter per unit of area: less surface drag, less metal, stiffer under pressure and easier to seal.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why is round duct preferred over rectangular for the same airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0860"]},{"id":"hvac:a-aspect-ratio","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Keep rectangular duct aspect ratio at or below about 4 to 1; 1 to 1 is ideal. Flat, wide ducts add friction and metal and are harder to clean and seal.","src":"HVAC fundamentals (SMACNA)","prompt":"What aspect ratio limit applies to rectangular duct?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Rectangular duct aspect ratio should generally stay at or below __.","a":"4 to 1","d":["1 to 1","8 to 1","12 to 1"]},"qs":["ascs-0861"]},{"id":"hvac:a-equivalent-length","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"A fitting's equivalent length is the length of straight duct with the same pressure loss. Fitting allowances added to straight runs give the total effective length.","src":"ACCA Manual D","prompt":"What is the equivalent length of a duct fitting?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0862"]},{"id":"hvac:a-velocity-squared","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"In a given duct, friction loss rises roughly with velocity squared, so doubling the airflow roughly quadruples it. Shrinking a duct at constant CFM raises velocity, pressure loss and noise.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; DOE Fan Sourcebook","prompt":"What happens to friction loss when airflow through an existing duct doubles?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Doubling the airflow through an existing duct raises its friction loss about __.","a":"4 times","d":["2 times","8 times","1.5 times"]},"qs":["ascs-0150","ascs-0203","ascs-0867","ascs-0880"]},{"id":"hvac:a-critical-path","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Fan static is set by the most restrictive run, the critical path. Shorter runs get balancing dampers to throttle down to design airflow.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What sets the static pressure a duct system's fan must deliver?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0881"]},{"id":"hvac:a-takeoffs","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"A 45 degree or conical branch takeoff lets air enter the branch smoothly, with less turbulence and pressure loss than a straight tap.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Why use a 45 degree or conical takeoff instead of a straight tap?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0874"]},{"id":"hvac:a-turning-vanes","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Turning vanes guide air around hard rectangular elbows to cut turbulence. A mitered elbow without vanes loses far more pressure than a radius elbow. ACR lists turning vanes as HVAC components.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What do turning vanes do, and where are they installed?","trap":"Vanes belong in rectangular mitered elbows, not round elbows or flex.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0120","ascs-0210","ascs-0875"]},{"id":"hvac:a-vanes-humidifier","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"Turning vanes, volume dampers and duct offsets over 15 degrees downstream of a humidifier must sit at or beyond its absorption distance, or unabsorbed steam condenses on them.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.8.2","prompt":"How far downstream of a humidifier must turning vanes sit, and why?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-attenuator","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"A sound attenuator (silencer) is a duct section of perforated metal over fibrous glass fill that reduces fan and airflow noise carried by the duct. ACR counts it as porous.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What is a duct sound attenuator and how does ACR classify it?","trap":"The perforated metal face tempts people to call it non-porous; it is porous.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0112","ascs-0125","ascs-0209"]},{"id":"hvac:a-undersized-return","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"An undersized return is the usual choke point in a duct system. It shows up as high total external static and low airflow across the coil.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does an undersized return duct show up in readings?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0872"]},{"id":"hvac:a-transfer-path","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"FACT","text":"With one central return and no transfer path, closing a bedroom door pressurizes the room and depressurizes the rest of the house. Jumper ducts, transfer grilles or door undercuts relieve it.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What happens when a bedroom door closes in a house with one central return and no transfer path?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0879"]},{"id":"hvac:a-acca-manuals","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-design","tag":"DEF","text":"ACCA's residential design manuals run in order: Manual J calculates loads, Manual S selects equipment, Manual D designs the ducts, and Manual T selects registers and grilles.","src":"ACCA Manuals J, S, D, T","prompt":"What does each ACCA manual (J, S, D, T) cover, and in what order are they used?","trap":"Manual D is ducts, not loads; loads are Manual J.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0863","ascs-0864"]},{"id":"hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA duct pressure class is the static pressure a duct is built to hold, from 1/2 to 10 in. w.g. It sets sheet gauge and reinforcement.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 HVAC Duct Construction Standards","prompt":"What does a SMACNA duct pressure class describe?","trap":"Pressure class is construction strength; leakage class is tightness.","num":{"q":"SMACNA duct pressure classes run from 1/2 in. w.g. up to __.","a":"10 in. w.g.","d":["2 in. w.g.","4 in. w.g.","6 in. w.g."]},"qs":["ascs-0204","ascs-0794"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-a","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class A seals all transverse joints, all longitudinal seams and all duct wall penetrations.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class A?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0205"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-b","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class B seals all transverse joints and longitudinal seams, but not duct wall penetrations.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class B?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-seal-class-c","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"DEF","text":"SMACNA Seal Class C seals transverse joints only; longitudinal seams and penetrations are left unsealed.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards Table 1-2","prompt":"What must be sealed under SMACNA Seal Class C?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0205"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leakage-class","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"SMACNA leakage class (CL) is leakage in CFM per 100 sq ft of duct surface at 1 in. w.g. A lower CL is tighter, and round duct reaches a lower CL than rectangular at the same seal class.","src":"SMACNA HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual","prompt":"What does a SMACNA leakage class number mean?","trap":"A lower CL number means less leakage, not a lower pressure class.","num":{"q":"SMACNA leakage class is leakage in CFM per 100 sq ft of duct surface at __ static pressure.","a":"1 in. w.g.","d":["1/2 in. w.g.","2 in. w.g.","4 in. w.g."]},"qs":["ascs-0870"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leak-test","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct leakage test seals the openings, pressurizes the duct with a calibrated fan and meters the CFM needed to hold test pressure. SMACNA 016 gives the procedures and expected rates.","src":"ANSI/SMACNA 016 Leakage Test Manual","prompt":"How is duct leakage tested, and which SMACNA document gives the procedure?","trap":"SMACNA 006 is construction; it sends leakage testing to SMACNA 016.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0876"]},{"id":"hvac:a-leakage-calc","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: commercial duct leakage is supply fan airflow minus the sum of outlet airflows, divided by fan airflow. A 10,000 cfm traverse with 8,200 cfm at the diffusers is 18%.","src":"LBNL, Wray, Diamond and Sherman","prompt":"How is commercial supply duct leakage calculated from fan and outlet readings?","trap":"Divide by fan airflow, not the outlet total or the scheduled design CFM.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"Supply leaks push conditioned air out; into an attic, they depressurize the house so it pulls in hot attic air. Return leaks are under suction and pull in unconditioned air and debris, unfiltered when the leak is past the filter.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Minimizing Energy Losses in Ducts","prompt":"How do supply duct leaks differ from return duct leaks?","trap":"Insulation fibers at registers point to return leaks, not supply leaks.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0851"]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-heat-gain","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"FACT","text":"A supply trunk crossing a hot attic warms along its length by conduction through thin insulation. Because it runs positive, its leaks push air out rather than pulling attic air in.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Minimizing Energy Losses in Ducts","prompt":"Why does supply air warm along a trunk crossing a hot attic?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-afue-duct-loss","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"NOTE","text":"AFUE is measured at the furnace and leaves out duct losses, which DOE says can reach 35% of output when ducts run through attics or garages.","src":"DOE Energy Saver, Furnaces and Boilers","prompt":"Which heat loss does a furnace's AFUE rating leave out?","trap":"Flue loss and pilot fuel are inside the AFUE test; duct loss is not.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-attic-duct-r8","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-duct-leakage","tag":"CODE","text":"Since the 2021 IECC, supply and return ducts 3 in. and larger outside conditioned space, attics included, need at least R-8 insulation, and smaller ducts R-6. The 2018 edition allowed R-6 outside attics.","src":"IECC 2021 R403.3.1 (2024 R403.3.3; 2018 R403.3.1)","prompt":"What insulation does the IECC require for ducts in attics?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The IECC requires attic ducts 3 in. and larger to be insulated to at least __.","a":"R-8","d":["R-6","R-4.2","R-11"]},"qs":["ascs-0871"]},{"id":"hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"Under ACR, general, bathroom and heat recovery exhaust ducts are part of the ventilation system and the overall inspection. Kitchen grease exhaust falls under NFPA 96, with cleaning per ANSI/IKECA C10.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.5; ACR References (ANSI/IKECA C10); NFPA 96","prompt":"Are bathroom and general exhaust ducts part of an ACR system inspection?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0228"]},{"id":"hvac:a-relief-vs-exhaust","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Relief air is surplus return air spilled outdoors through a relief damper or fan when the economizer brings in extra outdoor air. Exhaust air is pulled from restrooms, kitchens and labs by dedicated exhaust fans.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does relief air differ from exhaust air?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0229"]},{"id":"hvac:a-nfpa96-scope","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"NFPA 96 covers ventilation control and fire protection of commercial cooking operations, including grease duct construction and exhaust inspection frequency.","src":"NFPA 96","prompt":"What does NFPA 96 cover?","trap":"NFPA 90A is general A/C and ventilating installation; 96 is commercial cooking.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0227","ascs-0748"]},{"id":"hvac:a-ikeca","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"DEF","text":"ANSI/IKECA C10 is the kitchen exhaust cleaning methodology that ACR cites. I10 is the inspection methodology and M10 covers maintenance between cleanings.","src":"ANSI/IKECA C10, I10, M10; ACR 2025 references","prompt":"What do IKECA C10, I10 and M10 each cover?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0227","ascs-0747"]},{"id":"hvac:a-grease-duct","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 96 grease duct is carbon steel at least No. 16 MSG or stainless steel at least No. 18 MSG, with a continuous liquid-tight external weld on all seams and joints.","src":"NFPA 96 §7.5","prompt":"What material and joints does NFPA 96 require for kitchen grease duct?","trap":"The heavier sheet (lower number, 16) goes with carbon steel; stainless is 18.","num":{"q":"NFPA 96 grease duct of carbon steel must be at least No. __ MSG.","a":"16","d":["18","20","24"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-nfpa96-frequency","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"NFPA 96 grease buildup inspection: monthly for solid-fuel cooking, quarterly for high volume (24-hour, charbroiling, wok), semiannually for moderate volume, annually for low volume.","src":"NFPA 96 Table 12.4 (11.4 in 2021 and earlier)","prompt":"How often does NFPA 96 require kitchen exhaust inspection for each cooking volume?","trap":"","num":{"q":"NFPA 96 inspects high-volume cooking exhaust (24-hour, charbroiling, wok) every __.","a":"3 months","d":["1 month","6 months","12 months"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-lab-exhaust-material","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Lab fume exhaust duct is chosen for the chemicals it carries. Welded 316L stainless serves many labs but not hydrochloric acid vapor; PVC-coated steel or FRP is used there.","src":"UC Lab Safety Design Manual, Ducting","prompt":"Which duct materials suit lab exhaust carrying hydrochloric acid vapor?","trap":"Lined galvanized and aluminum are not lab exhaust materials.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-lab-exhaust-hazard","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"REC","text":"Before fume hood exhaust ducts such as FRP runs are opened, get the chemical hazard data and consult a qualified IEP or health and safety professional, since residues may be hazardous.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.8; §3.5.1","prompt":"What should happen before lab fume hood exhaust ducts are opened?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-toilet-exhaust","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"CODE","text":"The IMC exhausts public toilet rooms at 50 cfm per water closet or urinal when the fan runs continuously while occupied, or 70 cfm when it runs intermittently.","src":"IMC 2024 Table 403.3.1.1, note e","prompt":"What exhaust rate does the IMC set for public toilet rooms?","trap":"Toilet rates are exhaust per fixture, not outdoor air per person.","num":{"q":"An IMC public toilet room exhausted continuously while occupied needs __ per water closet or urinal.","a":"50 cfm","d":["70 cfm","15 cfm","5 cfm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-dryer-duct","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Dryer exhaust duct should be smooth rigid metal, kept short, with no screws protruding inside to snag lint. Lint, restriction and heat together make a fire risk.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; dryer manufacturer instructions","prompt":"How should a dryer exhaust duct be built?","trap":"Foil or vinyl flex run at any length is the classic wrong answer.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0226"]},{"id":"hvac:a-intake-separation","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"Outdoor air intakes are kept away from exhaust outlets, cooling towers, loading docks and vents so exhaust, fumes and tower drift are not pulled back in. ASHRAE 62.1 sets separation distances.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"Why must outdoor air intakes be separated from exhausts and cooling towers?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0237","ascs-0909"]},{"id":"hvac:a-negative-building","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-exhaust","tag":"FACT","text":"When exhaust exceeds the air brought in, the building runs negative: doors are hard to open and air rushes in at every gap. Make-up air or rebalanced outdoor air fixes it.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a building with more exhaust than intake behave?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0104","ascs-0126","ascs-0167","ascs-0240"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-def","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"MERV (minimum efficiency reporting value) rates a filter's particle capture efficiency by size range, from ASHRAE 52.2 testing. Higher pressure drop often comes with it but is not the definition.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2","prompt":"What does a filter's MERV rating describe?","trap":"MERV is not airflow capacity, pressure drop or service life.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0086"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-range","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 52.2 rates general ventilation filters MERV 1 through 16. HEPA and ULPA filters are rated by separate test methods and sit above the scale.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2-2017","prompt":"What range do MERV ratings span under ASHRAE 52.2?","trap":"","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV ratings run from 1 through __.","a":"16","d":["8","12","20"]},"qs":["ascs-0151","ascs-0213"]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-not-others","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"NOTE","text":"Only ASHRAE 52.2 produces a MERV. ISO 16890 rates ePM classes, UL 900 tests filter combustibility and smoke, and ASHRAE 62.1 sets minimums without testing filters.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2; ISO 16890; UL 900; ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"Which document's test produces a MERV, and what do ISO 16890 and UL 900 rate instead?","trap":"UL 900 is a fire test, not an efficiency rating.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-e-ranges","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 52.2 builds MERV from three particle size ranges: E1 is 0.3 to 1.0 micron, E2 is 1.0 to 3.0 micron, and E3 is 3.0 to 10.0 micron.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 (NAFA summary)","prompt":"What particle sizes do ASHRAE 52.2 ranges E1, E2 and E3 cover?","trap":"A single 0.3 micron point is the HEPA test, not range E1.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 52.2 range E1 covers particles from 0.3 to __ micron.","a":"1.0","d":["2.5","3.0","10.0"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv13","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"A filter earns the highest MERV whose minimums it meets in all three ranges. MERV 13 needs 50% E1, 85% E2, 90% E3; MERV 12 needs 35/80/90; MERV 11 needs 20/65/85.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"What minimum efficiencies does MERV 13 require, and how is a MERV assigned?","trap":"Qualifying in E2 and E3 alone does not earn MERV 13; E1 must reach 50%.","num":{"q":"MERV 13 requires at least __ efficiency in range E1 (0.3 to 1.0 micron).","a":"50%","d":["20%","35%","75%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv16","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"MERV 16 requires at least 95% in all three size ranges. That is still short of HEPA, so a MERV rating alone cannot show HEPA performance.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"What does MERV 16 require, and is it HEPA?","trap":"","num":{"q":"MERV 16 requires at least __ efficiency in each ASHRAE 52.2 size range.","a":"95%","d":["85%","90%","99.97%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-low","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"When E3 efficiency is under 20%, ASHRAE 52.2 assigns MERV 1 through 4 by average arrestance. MERV 5 starts at 20% E3, and E1 enters the table only at MERV 11.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 MERV table (NAFA)","prompt":"How does ASHRAE 52.2 assign MERV 1 through 4?","trap":"","num":{"q":"MERV 5 begins at __ efficiency in range E3.","a":"20%","d":["35%","50%","65%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv-a","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"MERV-A is an optional ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J rating taken after the media is conditioned with fine particles. A MERV-A well below the MERV warns that in-service capture may drop.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 Appendix J","prompt":"What does a MERV-A value tell you?","trap":"MERV-A is not efficiency at final resistance; it reflects fine-particle conditioning.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-hepa","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"DEF","text":"HEPA filtration captures at least 99.97% of particles at 0.3 micron, the most penetrating particle size.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions (DOE HEPA definition)","prompt":"What removal efficiency defines a HEPA filter?","trap":"","num":{"q":"HEPA means at least 99.97% capture at __ micron.","a":"0.3","d":["1.0","3.0","10.0"]},"qs":["ascs-0152"]},{"id":"hvac:a-hepa-type","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"NOTE","text":"A HEPA-type filter rated 99% at 1 micron is not HEPA. ACR requires true HEPA, 99.97% at 0.3 micron, on vacuum equipment that exhausts indoors.","src":"ACR 2025 §3.3","prompt":"Why is a HEPA-type filter rejected on a negative air machine exhausting indoors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-families","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"On 52.2 application charts, disposable fiberglass panels typically fall in MERV 1 to 4, pleated media in 5 to 8, and rigid box, cartridge and pocket filters in 9 to 16.","src":"ASHRAE 52.2 application chart","prompt":"Which MERV groups do common filter types typically fall in?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Disposable 1 in. fiberglass panel filters typically rate MERV __.","a":"1 to 4","d":["5 to 8","9 to 12","13 to 16"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-merv8-floor","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires at least MERV 8 (or the ISO ePM10 minimum) upstream of cooling coils and other wetted surfaces, except sensible-only coils. Editions before 2013 said MERV 6. ACR itself sets no MERV.","src":"ASHRAE 62.1-2022 §5.5","prompt":"What minimum filter does ASHRAE 62.1 require upstream of cooling coils?","trap":"MERV 11 is 62.1's outdoor air rule where PM2.5 standards are exceeded, not the coil rule.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 62.1 requires at least MERV __ upstream of cooling coils and wetted surfaces.","a":"8","d":["6","11","13"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-prefilter","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-merv","tag":"FACT","text":"A prefilter, often MERV 8, catches large particles so the final filter, such as MERV 13 to 14 or HEPA, lasts longer. Gases and odors need a separate carbon stage.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a prefilter ahead of a final filter do?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0216"]},{"id":"hvac:a-high-merv-effect","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"A filter much higher in MERV than the system was designed for adds resistance: static rises and airflow falls unless the fan can overcome it, risking coil freezing and comfort complaints.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a much higher MERV filter than designed do to the system?","trap":"Fitting the rack says nothing about the fan's pressure budget.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0087","ascs-0153","ascs-0214"]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-dp-loading","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"A media filter's pressure drop rises slowly as it loads. Read it on a gauge across the bank and replace at the manufacturer's final resistance.","src":"NAFA Technicians on Site guideline","prompt":"When should a loading media filter be replaced?","trap":"0.5 in. w.c. is a typical residential blower rating, not a filter change-out limit.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-filter-dp-falls","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"NOTE","text":"At steady airflow a filter's resistance only climbs as it loads. A reading that falls, especially below its clean rating, means air is slipping past gaps or through torn media.","src":"ACHR News, air filter gages; Donaldson","prompt":"What does a filter pressure drop reading that suddenly falls below its clean rating mean?","trap":"A lower reading is not recovery; check the bank, frames and gauge tubing.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-bypass","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Filter bypass is unfiltered air slipping through gaps, poor fit or failed seals at the rack. It loads the coil and ducts no matter how high the filter's MERV.","src":"EPA Building Air Quality App. B","prompt":"What is filter bypass, and why does MERV not fix it?","trap":"A higher MERV in a leaky rack changes nothing.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0129","ascs-0215","ascs-0326"]},{"id":"hvac:a-bypass-acr","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"SHALL","text":"The AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass. Dust streaks on the coil face lined up with rack gaps are recorded as filter bypass.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1","prompt":"What does ACR require the AHU inspection to include about filters?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-electronic-cleaner","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Electronic air cleaners, such as electrostatic precipitators and ionizers, use high voltage to charge and collect particles. They can produce ozone, a lung irritant, as a by-product.","src":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","prompt":"What IAQ drawback comes with electronic air cleaners?","trap":"Media filters, not electronic cleaners, carry the higher pressure drop.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-ozone-limit","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"CODE","text":"UL 867 limits air cleaner ozone emission to 0.050 ppm (50 ppb), the baseline California's CARB requires. UL 2998 zero-ozone validation is 0.005 ppm.","src":"UL 867; UL 2998; CARB air cleaner regulation","prompt":"What ozone limit does UL 867 set for air cleaners?","trap":"0.10 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour PEL; 0.070 ppm is the EPA outdoor standard.","num":{"q":"UL 867, the CARB baseline, holds air cleaner ozone emission to __.","a":"0.050 ppm","d":["0.005 ppm","0.070 ppm","0.10 ppm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-uvc","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"UV-C (UVGI) lamps, usually low-pressure mercury at 253.7 nm, inactivate microorganisms on coils and drain pans. They capture no particles, so they never replace filters.","src":"EPA Residential Air Cleaners, 2nd ed.","prompt":"What do UV-C lamps at a coil do, and can they replace filters?","trap":"Pulling filters for UV-C lets dust load the coil and ducts.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-uvc-loto","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Before entering an air handler with UV-C lamps, de-energize and lock out the lamp circuit. UV-C damages eyes and skin in seconds and degrades nearby plastics and some liners.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; lamp manufacturer instructions","prompt":"What must be done before entering an AHU fitted with UV-C lamps?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0908"]},{"id":"hvac:a-carbon","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-filters-performance","tag":"FACT","text":"Particle filters cannot control gases and some odors. Gas-phase filters use a sorbent, most commonly activated carbon, to adsorb them.","src":"EPA, Residential Air Cleaners","prompt":"What filtration targets gases and odors?","trap":"Higher MERV, UV-C and electrostatic units all target particles or microbes, not gases.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0216"]},{"id":"hvac:a-iwc-fpm","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"HVAC air pressures are read in inches of water column (in. w.c., IWC, also written in. w.g.). Air velocity is in feet per minute (FPM).","src":"NADCA ASCS acronym list","prompt":"What units are used for HVAC air pressure and air velocity?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0147","ascs-0149"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sp-vp-tp","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"Static pressure pushes outward on the duct walls, velocity pressure comes from the air's motion, and total pressure is their sum: TP = SP + VP.","src":"TSI-107; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does total pressure in a duct equal?","trap":"VP = TP - SP. It is never static minus total or a product.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0201"]},{"id":"hvac:a-pitot-ports","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"A pitot tube points upstream. Its tip senses total pressure and its side ports sense static, so piping tip and side ports across one manometer reads velocity pressure.","src":"TSI-107","prompt":"What does a manometer read when a pitot's tip and side ports are piped across it?","trap":"External static is a unit measurement at the plenums, not a pitot reading.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0201"]},{"id":"hvac:a-v-4005","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"For standard air, velocity in FPM = 4005 x the square root of velocity pressure in in. w.c. Rearranged, VP = (V / 4005) squared.","src":"TSI-107; MSHA pitot card 7000-59","prompt":"What formula converts velocity pressure to air velocity for standard air?","trap":"Square root first: VP times 4005 skips the root.","num":{"q":"For standard air, V in fpm = __ x the square root of VP in in. w.c.","a":"4005","d":["1.08","4.5","500"]},"qs":["ascs-0202","ascs-0868"]},{"id":"hvac:a-v-4005-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a pitot reads 1.10 in. w.c. total and 0.85 static, so VP = 0.25. The square root is 0.5, and 4005 x 0.5 is about 2,000 fpm.","src":"TSI-107","prompt":"A pitot reads 1.10 in. w.c. total and 0.85 in. w.c. static: what is the velocity?","trap":"Plugging in static or total pressure instead of VP gives 3,700 or 4,200 fpm.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-traverse-why","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Duct velocity is not uniform: wall friction slows air at the edges and fittings skew the profile. A pitot traverse averages readings on an equal-area or log-Tchebycheff grid across the duct.","src":"TSI AF-106; Dwyer duct traversing guide","prompt":"Why take a multi-point pitot traverse instead of one reading?","trap":"A lone centerline reading runs high because air moves fastest mid-duct.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0088","ascs-0231"]},{"id":"hvac:a-flow-hood","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"A flow hood (balometer) captures a diffuser or grille and reads CFM directly at the outlet. The pitot traverse is the in-duct method.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a flow hood measure?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0230"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"DEF","text":"Total external static pressure (TESP) is the supply static read just after the unit plus the return static read just before it, added with signs ignored.","src":"NCI static pressure guide; ACHR News","prompt":"How is total external static pressure measured on a furnace or air handler?","trap":"Subtracting the readings, or reading one side only, understates TESP.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0869"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: +0.55 in. w.c. supply and -0.35 return give a TESP of 0.90 in. w.c., nearly double the 0.50 in. w.c. rating of many residential blowers. Look for a loaded filter, coil or undersized duct.","src":"ACHR News; NCI static pressure guide","prompt":"A furnace reads +0.55 supply and -0.35 return in. w.c.: what is its TESP?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Many furnaces and residential blowers are rated for about __ total external static.","a":"0.50 in. w.c.","d":["0.10 in. w.c.","0.90 in. w.c.","2.0 in. w.c."]},"qs":["ascs-0869"]},{"id":"hvac:a-component-drop","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a component's pressure drop is its upstream reading minus its downstream reading. A filter between -0.08 and -0.52 in. w.c. drops 0.44; compare that with its rated drop.","src":"NCI, Measure and Interpret Static Pressures","prompt":"How do you find a single component's pressure drop from static readings?","trap":"The biggest single reading marks a location, not a restriction.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-resistance-airflow","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"On a fixed-speed fan, added resistance from a loaded filter, dirty coil or closed dampers raises static pressure and lowers airflow as the fan rides back on its curve.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; DOE Fan Sourcebook","prompt":"What does added system resistance do to a fixed-speed fan's static and airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0083","ascs-0091","ascs-0214"]},{"id":"hvac:a-dp-flow-squared","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-pressure","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: pressure drop across a coil scales roughly with airflow squared. A 0.80 in. w.c. drop at 8,000 cfm is about 0.45 at 6,000 cfm, so compare before and after at equal airflow.","src":"DOE Fan Sourcebook; ACR 2025 §4.11.6","prompt":"Why must before and after coil pressure drops be compared at the same airflow?","trap":"A lower drop at a slower fan speed proves nothing about the cleaning.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0133"]},{"id":"hvac:a-q-va","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"DEF","text":"Airflow in CFM = average velocity in FPM x duct area in square feet, so velocity = CFM / area. Convert inches to feet before multiplying.","src":"HVAC fundamentals (Q = V x A)","prompt":"How are airflow, velocity and duct area related?","trap":"CFM times area, or area divided by CFM, is the inverted-formula trap.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0148","ascs-0202"]},{"id":"hvac:a-q-va-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a 24 x 12 in. duct is 288 sq in / 144 = 2.0 sq ft. At an average 900 fpm it carries 2.0 x 900 = 1,800 CFM.","src":"Dwyer duct traversing guide (Q = A x V)","prompt":"A 24 x 12 in. duct averages 900 fpm: what is the airflow?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-round-area","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: round duct area = pi x radius squared, in feet. A 14 in. duct is pi x (7/12) squared = 1.07 sq ft, so a 1,050 fpm traverse average gives about 1,120 CFM.","src":"TSI AF-106; geometry","prompt":"A 14 in. round duct averages 1,050 fpm: what is the airflow?","trap":"Using the diameter as the radius, or treating it as a square, overstates the flow.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-face-velocity","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: coil face velocity = airflow / finned face area. 9,000 CFM across a 3 x 5 ft face is 600 fpm, above the 500 fpm rule of thumb for condensate carryover.","src":"Trane, Coil Selection and Optimization","prompt":"How is coil face velocity found, and what does 9,000 CFM on a 3 x 5 ft face give?","trap":"Divide by the finned coil face, not the cabinet or the duct.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0900"]},{"id":"hvac:a-ton","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"DEF","text":"One ton of cooling is 12,000 Btu/h, the heat to melt a ton of ice in 24 hours. One kilowatt is 3,412 Btu/h.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; ACHR News","prompt":"How many Btu/h equal one ton of cooling?","trap":"","num":{"q":"One ton of cooling equals __ Btu/h.","a":"12,000","d":["3,412","10,000","24,000"]},"qs":["ascs-0183"]},{"id":"hvac:a-cfm-per-ton","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"The rule-of-thumb airflow across a DX cooling coil is about 400 CFM per ton, 350 to 450 depending on humidity design. Too little freezes the coil; too much hurts dehumidification.","src":"ACCA guidance (ENERGY STAR); HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the rule-of-thumb airflow per ton across a DX cooling coil?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The rule-of-thumb airflow across a DX coil is about __ per ton.","a":"400 CFM","d":["100 CFM","200 CFM","800 CFM"]},"qs":["ascs-0184","ascs-0852"]},{"id":"hvac:a-tons-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: a 60,000 Btu/h nameplate is 60,000 / 12,000 = 5 tons, and 5 x 400 = 2,000 CFM nominal. A 3-ton split moves about 1,200 CFM.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What nominal airflow should a 60,000 Btu/h cooling unit move?","trap":"Treating a ton as 10,000 Btu/h gives 2,400 CFM.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0852"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-108","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Sensible heat in Btu/h = 1.08 x CFM x temperature difference in F, for standard air. 1.08 = 0.075 lb per cu ft x 0.24 Btu per lb F x 60 minutes per hour.","src":"Energy Vanguard; Belimo HVAC formulas","prompt":"What formula gives the sensible heat carried by an airstream?","trap":"","num":{"q":"Sensible heat in Btu/h = __ x CFM x delta T for standard air.","a":"1.08","d":["0.68","4.5","500"]},"qs":["ascs-0853"]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: 1,600 CFM cooled from 78 to 58 F carries 1.08 x 1,600 x 20 = 34,560 Btu/h of sensible cooling, about 2.9 tons.","src":"Energy Vanguard; arithmetic","prompt":"How much sensible cooling does 1,600 CFM with a 20 F drop represent?","trap":"Dropping the 1.08 gives 32,000; using 4.5 gives 144,000.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-heat-constants","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"NOTE","text":"Keep the constants straight: 1.08 x CFM x delta T is sensible, 4.5 x CFM x delta enthalpy is total, 0.68 x CFM x delta grains is latent, 500 x GPM x delta T is water side.","src":"Belimo HVAC formulas guide","prompt":"Which constant goes with sensible, total, latent and water-side heat formulas?","trap":"4.5 multiplies enthalpy change, never a temperature difference.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0853"]},{"id":"hvac:a-temp-split","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"The temperature drop (split) across a healthy DX cooling coil is typically about 15 to 20 F, but the target depends on return dry bulb and wet bulb; within about 3 F of target is normal.","src":"Proctor Engineering target split table; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What temperature split is normal across a DX cooling coil?","trap":"A universal 18 to 20 F target is wrong; cooler, drier return air lowers it.","num":{"q":"A healthy DX coil's temperature split is typically about __.","a":"15 to 20 F","d":["5 to 8 F","30 to 35 F","40 to 45 F"]},"qs":["ascs-0185"]},{"id":"hvac:a-split-meaning","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-airflow-math","tag":"FACT","text":"From Q = 1.08 x CFM x delta T, a bigger split at similar load means less airflow, such as a loaded coil or filter. A smaller split points to excess airflow or lost capacity, such as low charge.","src":"Sensible heat relationship; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What does a larger than normal temperature split across a DX coil usually mean?","trap":"Extra airflow shrinks the split; it never widens it.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0185"]},{"id":"hvac:a-dry-wet-bulb","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Dry bulb is ordinary air temperature. Wet bulb is read on a wetted wick; it equals dry bulb only at 100% RH, and the spread between them gives RH on a sling psychrometer.","src":"NWS definitions; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What are dry bulb and wet bulb, and when are they equal?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0919"]},{"id":"hvac:a-dew-point","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Dew point is the temperature to which air must be cooled, at constant moisture, to reach saturation. Dew point is at or below wet bulb, which is at or below dry bulb.","src":"NWS glossary","prompt":"What is the dew point of an air sample?","trap":"Dew point is not the coil temperature or the wet bulb.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0217"]},{"id":"hvac:a-condensation","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Any surface colder than the dew point of the air touching it condenses water. Cold ducts, jackets and drain pans below dew point sweat and can grow biofilm.","src":"NWS glossary","prompt":"When does condensation form on a duct or surface?","trap":"Room RH under 100% does not stop condensation on a colder surface.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0217"]},{"id":"hvac:a-rh-rises","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Cooling air without changing its moisture content raises its RH until it reaches 100% at the dew point.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What happens to RH when air is cooled without changing its moisture?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0918"]},{"id":"hvac:a-rh-misleads","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"NOTE","text":"Worked example: RH misleads for condensation. 80 F crawlspace air at 50% RH (dew point 60 F) sweats a 58 F duct, while 72 F office air at 55% RH (dew point 55 F) does not.","src":"NWS, dew point vs humidity","prompt":"Why can air at lower RH condense on a surface that higher-RH air does not?","trap":"EPA's below-60% RH advice is a room target, not a surface test.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-sensible-latent","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Sensible heat changes dry-bulb temperature; latent heat is moisture. Total cooling is sensible plus latent, and the sensible heat ratio is sensible divided by total.","src":"ACR 2025 Definitions; HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the difference between sensible and latent cooling, and what is sensible heat ratio?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0218","ascs-0922"]},{"id":"hvac:a-latent-needs-dew","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"A coil removes moisture only where its surface is below the air's dew point. A bone-dry pan under a running coil in humid weather means it is doing almost all sensible work.","src":"NWS glossary; ACR 2025 Definitions","prompt":"What does a dry condensate pan under a running DX coil in humid weather mean?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-reheat-dehumidify","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Reheat dehumidification overcools air below its dew point to condense moisture, then reheats it so it leaves drier at a comfortable temperature. Hot-gas reheat uses compressor heat; electric reheat pays for it.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How does a hot-gas or electric reheat dehumidification mode work?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0924"]},{"id":"hvac:a-latent-sources","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Latent load comes from people, cooking, showers and humid infiltration. Lights, computers and solar gain through glass are sensible load.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"Where does latent load in an occupied space come from?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0923"]},{"id":"hvac:a-enthalpy","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"DEF","text":"Enthalpy is total heat, sensible plus latent, per pound of dry air. It is read along the wet-bulb lines of a psychrometric chart.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What is the enthalpy of moist air?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0920"]},{"id":"hvac:a-grains","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"Air moisture content is given in grains per pound of dry air; 7,000 grains equal one pound of water.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"How many grains equal one pound of water?","trap":"","num":{"q":"__ grains equal one pound of water.","a":"7,000","d":["144","1,000","12,000"]},"qs":["ascs-0921"]},{"id":"hvac:a-oversized-ac","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-psychrometrics","tag":"FACT","text":"An oversized air conditioner satisfies the thermostat before the coil has removed much moisture, so its short cycles leave a cool, clammy space.","src":"ACCA Manuals J and S","prompt":"Why does an oversized air conditioner control humidity poorly?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0854"]},{"id":"hvac:a-mixed-air-temp","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: mixed air temperature is the flow-weighted average. 20% outdoor air at 30 F with 80% return at 70 F gives 0.2 x 30 + 0.8 x 70 = 62 F.","src":"HVAC fundamentals","prompt":"What mixed air temperature do 20% outdoor air at 30 F and 80% return air at 70 F make?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0910"]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Outdoor air fraction = (MAT - RAT) / (OAT - RAT). Multiply it by supply airflow to get outdoor air CFM.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What formula gives the outdoor air fraction from mixed, return and outdoor air temperatures?","trap":"Dividing by MAT - OAT is a common slip, printed even in some tip sheets.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: 12,000 cfm with OAT 30, RAT 72 and MAT 62 F gives (62 - 72) / (30 - 72) = 0.238, so about 2,860 cfm of outdoor air.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"At 12,000 cfm, OAT 30 F, RAT 72 F and MAT 62 F, how much outdoor air is the unit taking in?","trap":"9,140 cfm is the return-air share, not outdoor air.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-fraction-spread","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Use the mixed-air method only when outdoor and return air differ by more than about 5 F. With a smaller spread, a 1 F sensor error swings the answer wildly.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.2","prompt":"When is the mixed-air outdoor air fraction calculation reliable?","trap":"","num":{"q":"PNNL uses the mixed-air OA fraction only when OAT and RAT differ by more than about __.","a":"5 F","d":["1 F","3 F","20 F"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-oa-vs-damper","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Calculated outdoor air fraction should track the damper command within about 25%. OAT 55, RAT 75 and MAT 74 F mean 5% outdoor air; with the damper commanded open, check blades, linkage and actuator.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"How closely should calculated outdoor air fraction track the damper command?","trap":"A BAS damper command is not proof of airflow.","num":{"q":"PNNL says calculated OA fraction should track the damper command within about __.","a":"25%","d":["5%","10%","50%"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-fixed-min-oa","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: in VAV, a fixed minimum OA damper lets outdoor air fall about in step with supply airflow. 2,000 cfm at 10,000 cfm supply becomes about 1,100 cfm at 5,500.","src":"CEC Nonresidential Compliance Manual 2016 §4.3","prompt":"What happens to outdoor air through a fixed minimum damper as VAV supply airflow drops?","trap":"A fixed damper position is not a fixed outdoor airflow.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-mixing-leak","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: the mixing formula measures damper leakage too. Hot share = (zone - cold deck) / (hot deck - cold deck), so (62 - 55) / (90 - 55) = 20% leaking past a shut hot-deck blade.","src":"USACE ERDC-CERL Multizone Tech Note; mixing arithmetic","prompt":"How do deck and zone temperatures reveal hot-deck damper leakage?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-leaking-heat-valve","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"A temperature rise across a heating coil whose valve is commanded closed means the valve leaks. Cooling running while outdoor air is colder than the discharge setpoint is the PNNL clue.","src":"PNNL-29078 §9.4.1","prompt":"What does a temperature rise across a heating coil with its valve commanded closed mean?","trap":"MAT near OAT shows the economizer dampers are open, so they are not the fault.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-occupants","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Indoor CO2 comes mainly from occupants, so it tracks ventilation per person. It is not an overall IAQ indicator: VOCs from building materials do not track it.","src":"ASHRAE Position Document on Indoor CO2","prompt":"What does an indoor CO2 reading tell an inspector, and what does it miss?","trap":"Low CO2 says nothing about carpet or material emissions.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-700","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"At steady state, 15 cfm of outdoor air per sedentary adult holds indoor CO2 about 700 ppm above outdoors, with outdoor air near 420 ppm.","src":"NIST (Persily); ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2","prompt":"How far above outdoors does 15 cfm per person hold CO2 at steady state?","trap":"","num":{"q":"At steady state, 15 cfm of outdoor air per person holds CO2 about __ above outdoors.","a":"700 ppm","d":["420 ppm","1,000 ppm","5,000 ppm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-steady-state","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"NOTE","text":"The CO2 ventilation estimate works only after CO2 levels off. A reading taken while CO2 is still rising overestimates the ventilation rate.","src":"ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2 (2025)","prompt":"Why can't a still-rising CO2 reading be used to estimate ventilation?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-example","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Worked example: steady CO2 of 1,470 ppm with 420 ppm outdoors gives 15 x 700 / (1,470 - 420) = 10 cfm of outdoor air per person.","src":"ASHRAE PD on Indoor CO2; ASTM D6245","prompt":"Steady CO2 is 1,470 ppm with 420 ppm outdoors: about how much outdoor air per person?","trap":"Using the raw 1,470 reading instead of the difference gives 7 cfm.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-co2-no-limit","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"NOTE","text":"ASHRAE 62.1 sets no indoor CO2 limit for IAQ. 1,000 ppm is shorthand from old guidance, and 5,000 ppm is the OSHA 8-hour workplace exposure limit.","src":"NIST, Quit Blaming ASHRAE 62.1 for 1000 ppm CO2","prompt":"Does ASHRAE 62.1 set an indoor CO2 limit?","trap":"Newer 62.1 CO2 values are setpoints for demand-controlled ventilation, not IAQ limits.","num":{"q":"The OSHA 8-hour exposure limit for CO2 is __.","a":"5,000 ppm","d":["420 ppm","700 ppm","1,000 ppm"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-dcv","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","tag":"FACT","text":"Demand control ventilation trims outdoor air on CO2 readings when occupancy is low, within code minimums, so the unit conditions less unneeded outdoor air.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; ASHRAE 62.1","prompt":"How does demand control ventilation save energy?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0842"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seer2","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"SEER2 is seasonal cooling output in Btu divided by electrical input in watt-hours over a modeled cooling season.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1","prompt":"What does SEER2 express?","trap":"EER2 is the single-point rating; HSPF2 is heating; AFUE is fuel.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0826"]},{"id":"hvac:a-eer2","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"EER2 is a single-point cooling rating at 95 F outdoor (80 F dry bulb, 67 F wet bulb indoor). The Southwest adds an EER2 minimum for split ACs because peak heat matters there.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(6); App. M1","prompt":"How does EER2 differ from SEER2?","trap":"","num":{"q":"EER2 is rated at a single outdoor condition of __.","a":"95 F","d":["47 F","62 F","80 F"]},"qs":["ascs-0827"]},{"id":"hvac:a-hspf2","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"HSPF2 is a heat pump's seasonal heating output in Btu divided by all its watt-hours for the season, resistance strip heat included.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 §1.2, §4.2","prompt":"What does HSPF2 divide by what?","trap":"Strip heat is counted; a single-condition ratio is COP, not HSPF2.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0828"]},{"id":"hvac:a-afue","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"AFUE is the share of a furnace's annual fuel energy delivered as heat. About 80% is a standard non-condensing furnace; 90% and up needs a condensing secondary heat exchanger.","src":"DOE Energy Saver; 10 CFR 430 App. N","prompt":"What does AFUE rate, and what AFUE marks a condensing furnace?","trap":"","num":{"q":"A standard non-condensing furnace is about __ AFUE.","a":"80%","d":["35%","90%","96%"]},"qs":["ascs-0829"]},{"id":"hvac:a-cop","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"DEF","text":"COP is heat delivered divided by electrical energy used at one set of conditions, with no units. Resistance heat is COP 1.0; a COP 4 heat pump gives 4 units of heat per unit of electricity.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 §1.2","prompt":"What does a heating COP mean, and why does a heat pump beat resistance heat?","trap":"COP is not Btu per watt-hour over a season, and not kW per ton.","num":{"q":"Electric resistance heat has a COP of __.","a":"1.0","d":["0.8","3.0","4.0"]},"qs":["ascs-0830","ascs-0850"]},{"id":"hvac:a-seer2-test-static","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"FACT","text":"The SEER2 test raised external static for conventional ducted systems to 0.50 in. w.c.; the old test used as little as 0.10. The same unit therefore rates lower in SEER2 than in SEER.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 Table 4","prompt":"Why is a unit's SEER2 lower than its old SEER?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The SEER2 test uses __ external static for conventional ducted systems.","a":"0.50 in. w.c.","d":["0.10 in. w.c.","0.20 in. w.c.","0.90 in. w.c."]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-tesp-vs-rating","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"FACT","text":"A duct system measuring well above the 0.50 in. w.c. rating static cuts airflow or adds fan power, so delivered capacity and efficiency fall below the label.","src":"10 CFR 430 Subpart B App. M1 Table 4","prompt":"What happens to delivered efficiency when measured TESP is well above the SEER2 test static?","trap":"SEER2 does not cover whatever duct static the house has.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-hp-seer2","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"Federal minimums from January 1, 2023: split-system heat pumps need 14.3 SEER2 and single-package heat pumps 13.4 SEER2.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(5)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum SEER2 for split and packaged heat pumps?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The 2023 federal minimum for a split-system heat pump is __ SEER2.","a":"14.3","d":["11.9","12","13.4"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-hp-hspf2","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"Federal minimums from January 1, 2023: split-system heat pumps need 7.5 HSPF2, single-package heat pumps 6.7, and small-duct high-velocity 6.1. The pre-2023 split heat pump minimum was 8.2 HSPF.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(1), (c)(5)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum HSPF2 for a split-system heat pump?","trap":"8.2 is the old HSPF scale, not HSPF2.","num":{"q":"Since 2023 the federal minimum HSPF2 for a split-system heat pump is __.","a":"7.5","d":["6.1","6.7","8.2"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-min-ac","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-efficiency","tag":"REG","text":"The 2023 national minimum for split and single-package central air conditioners is 13.4 SEER2. The Southeast and Southwest raise split ACs under 45,000 Btu/h to 14.3 SEER2.","src":"10 CFR 430.32(c)(5), (c)(6)","prompt":"What is the 2023 federal minimum SEER2 for a split air conditioner, and where is it higher?","trap":"","num":{"q":"The 2023 national (North region) minimum for a split air conditioner is __ SEER2.","a":"13.4","d":["12","13.8","14.3"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-uds-sheet-types","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"In National CAD Standard (UDS) numbering, M is mechanical and the next digit is sheet type: 0 general, 1 plans, 2 elevations, 3 sections, 4 large-scale views, 5 details, 6 schedules and diagrams.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"What do the digits after M mean in UDS sheet numbers?","trap":"","num":{"q":"In UDS numbering, AHU and VAV schedules sit on the M-__ series.","a":"6","d":["0","3","5"]},"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-legend","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"Symbols vary between design offices, so each set defines its own in the legend and notes on the 0-series general sheet. Schedules and details use the symbols but do not define them.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Where do you confirm what an unfamiliar symbol on a mechanical plan means?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-duct-size-notation","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"Rectangular duct tags read width first, then depth: 30x14 is 30 in. wide as seen in plan and 14 in. deep. Check the legend for whether sizes are clear inside dimensions.","src":"Drawing convention; project legend","prompt":"How do you read a rectangular duct tag such as 30x14 on a plan?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-equipment-schedules","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"An equipment tag on the plans points to a schedule row with its design data: airflow, external static, capacity, filters and electrical.","src":"Drawing practice","prompt":"Where do you find the design airflow and external static for a tagged AHU?","trap":"Duct tags give sizes only; diffuser and VAV schedules list terminal airflows.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-air-device-schedule","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"FACT","text":"A diffuser schedule gives design CFM and a flow hood gives actual. 150 CFM at an outlet scheduled for 300 is half of design, so look upstream; neck size proves nothing.","src":"Drawing practice","prompt":"How do you judge a flow hood reading against the diffuser schedule?","trap":"Never resize the design to fit a bad reading.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-schedule-vs-field","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"The schedule is the design basis. A scheduled MERV 14 final bank missing in the field is a documented deficiency, even when the scheduled MERV 8 prefilters are present.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.7.1, §1.10; drawing practice","prompt":"How is a filter bank that differs from the AHU schedule recorded?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-plan-vs-section","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"A plan is a horizontal view from above. A section is a vertical cut, the view that shows a duct dropping through a shaft past several floors.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Which drawing view shows a vertical duct run through several floors?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-detail","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"A detail is a large-scale drawing of one assembly, such as a condensate trap, a damper installation or a duct support. An elevation shows a vertical face.","src":"NCS/UDS Module 1","prompt":"Which view gives an enlarged look at one connection, such as a drain trap?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-line-types","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"Line types differ by firm: return may be dashed and exhaust dot-dash, or both dashed. The set's legend and the RA and EA labels at the grilles settle it.","src":"Drawing practice (firm legends)","prompt":"How do you tell whether a dashed duct on a plan is return or exhaust?","trap":"No universal NCS rule makes dashed lines return or exhaust.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-damper-tags","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"Common legend tags: FD fire damper (fusible link, closes on heat), SD smoke damper (closes on a detection signal), FSD combination fire and smoke damper, VD volume damper.","src":"Typical mechanical legends (ASHRAE design set; ODOT M01)","prompt":"What do FD, SD, FSD and VD mean on a mechanical legend?","trap":"A duct smoke detector is not a damper; confirm every tag on the set's legend.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-other-tags","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"Other common tags: BDD backdraft damper, BD balancing damper, AD access door, FPB fan powered box, RHC reheat coil. Confirm each on the set's legend.","src":"Typical mechanical legends (ASHRAE design set; ODOT M01)","prompt":"What do BDD, BD, AD, FPB and RHC mean on a mechanical legend?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-piping-tags","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"DEF","text":"CHWS and CHWR are chilled water supply and return to AHU coils. CWS and CWR are condenser water between chiller and cooling tower. HWS and HWR serve heating coils.","src":"Typical mechanical legend (ASHRAE design set)","prompt":"Which piping tags feed an air-side cooling coil, and which run to the cooling tower?","trap":"Condenser water never reaches an air-side coil.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-acr-plans","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"SHALL","text":"If building floor plans and mechanical plans exist, ACR requires using them during the inspection and again during cleaning and restoration.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3","prompt":"What does ACR require about mechanical plans during an inspection?","trap":"It is a SHALL; plans in storage are retrieved, not skipped.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:a-field-controls","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-drawings","tag":"NOTE","text":"Plans inform the scope, but the assessment records what the inspection finds. A duct drawn as bare metal but found lined is scoped as porous duct.","src":"ACR 2025 §1.3, §1.10","prompt":"When plans and field conditions disagree, which controls the scope?","trap":"","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-fan-creates-airflow","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"The supply fan or blower is the component that creates airflow in an air-handling system. Coils, filters, attenuators and dampers only add resistance or control it, and diffusers only release it into the room.","src":"HVAC fundamentals; ACR 2025 definitions, air-handling unit","prompt":"Which component in an air handler creates the airflow, and what do the other components do to it?","trap":"A sound attenuator or damper changes airflow; it never creates it.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0134"]},{"id":"hvac:d-return-plenum","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A return plenum is a chamber or path that carries return air back to the air handler. It conveys air; it does not filter it. Filtration happens at the filter bank.","src":"ACR 2025 definitions; NADCA Open Air Plenums White Paper (2023)","prompt":"What does a return plenum do, and does it filter the air?","trap":"Being a plenum does not make it a filter location. Air is filtered at the filter bank.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0092"]},{"id":"hvac:d-diffuser","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A supply diffuser is the outlet that spreads supply air into the occupied space in a set pattern so it mixes with room air. A grille is a plain louvered face, most often used on returns.","src":"Titus Engineering Guidelines, grilles, registers and diffusers; ASHRAE Terminology","prompt":"What does a diffuser do that a plain grille does not?","trap":"A damper is not what makes a diffuser. A grille with a damper is a register.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0135","ascs-0211"]},{"id":"hvac:d-register","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"A register is a grille with an integral damper, so the airflow through it can be adjusted or shut off at the face.","src":"Titus Engineering Guidelines, grilles, registers and diffusers; ASHRAE Terminology","prompt":"What is a register?","trap":"A balancing damper out in the branch or trunk is not a register. The damper is built into the outlet.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0212"]},{"id":"hvac:d-throw","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"Throw is the distance a supply jet travels from the outlet face before its velocity falls to a stated terminal velocity. Catalogs list throw at 150, 100 and 50 fpm. Spread and drop are separate measures.","src":"Titus Engineering Corner, What is Throw? What is Terminal Velocity? (2017); CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"What is an outlet's throw, and how do catalogs state it?","trap":"Drop is how far the jet falls and spread is how wide it fans. Neither one is throw.","num":{"q":"Diffuser catalogs list throw to terminal velocities of 150, 100 and __ fpm.","a":"50","d":["25","75","200"]},"qs":["ascs-0877"]},{"id":"hvac:d-coanda","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"DEF","text":"The Coanda effect makes a jet cling to a nearby surface. A ceiling diffuser's jet hugs the ceiling, which keeps cold air up, lets it mix with room air, and carries it farther than a free jet.","src":"CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"How does the Coanda effect help a ceiling diffuser?","trap":"It holds cold air up at the ceiling. It does not push the jet down onto occupants.","num":null,"qs":["ascs-0878"]},{"id":"hvac:d-dumping","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"At low VAV airflow a cold jet can lose its grip on the ceiling and fall into the occupied space before it mixes. Engineers call it excessive drop; techs call it dumping. Occupants feel cold drafts under the outlet.","src":"CSE, Air Jet Dynamics of Air Diffusers","prompt":"What happens to a ceiling diffuser jet at very low VAV airflow?","trap":"Dumping comes from too little airflow at the outlet, not too much.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-smudging","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"Dark smudging around a ceiling diffuser usually comes from room air the jet induces along the ceiling, carrying lint and dust from the room. By itself it does not prove the ductwork is dirty.","src":"ACHR News, Smudging Around Air Distribution Diffusers (2012-12-03)","prompt":"What usually causes smudge rings around ceiling diffusers, and what do they prove about the ducts?","trap":"Blaming duct debris. The smudge alone proves nothing, so inspect the ducts before recommending cleaning.","num":null,"qs":[]},{"id":"hvac:d-laminar-or","dom":"Mechanical","lesson":"hvac-air-distribution","tag":"FACT","text":"ASHRAE 170 requires unidirectional, downward airflow from an operating room's primary supply array: nonaspirating or laminar flow diffusers averaging 25 to 35 cfm per sq ft, extending 12 in. past each side of the table.","src":"Price Industries, How Is ASHRAE Standard 170 Applied to Hospital Operating Rooms?; ANSI/ASHRAE/ASHE 170","prompt":"What does ASHRAE 170 require of the primary supply diffuser array over an operating table?","trap":"These diffusers are built not to mix. A high-induction mixing diffuser defeats the clean air curtain over the table.","num":{"q":"ASHRAE 170 sets the minimum extension of the OR primary diffuser array past each side of the surgical table at __.","a":"12 in.","d":["6 in.","18 in.","24 in."]},"qs":["ascs-0916"]}],"lessons":[{"id":"hvac-system-types","title":"Air system types: CAV, reheat, DOAS and more","dom":"Mechanical","order":50,"intro":"How the main central system types hold airflow or temperature and where heat is added. The exam asks what each type is and why some waste energy.","mnemonic":"VAV varies Volume; CAV varies the temperature. Reheat, multizone and dual duct all cool first, then heat or remix.","units":["hvac:s-single-zone","hvac:s-cav-control","hvac:s-cav-reheat","hvac:s-simultaneous-heat-cool","hvac:s-reheat","hvac:s-trend-id","hvac:s-doas","hvac:s-active-beam","hvac:s-ufad","hvac:s-mua"]},{"id":"hvac-vav","title":"VAV systems and VAV boxes","dom":"Mechanical","order":55,"intro":"How a VAV system and its boxes vary airflow, what the flow sensor does, and the faults a duct cleaner can cause or spot at a box.","mnemonic":"Box sequence as the zone cools off: throttle, minimum, then reheat. Never closed, never reheating wide open.","units":["hvac:s-vav-control","hvac:s-vav-fan-vfd","hvac:s-bypass-vav","hvac:s-vav-box","hvac:s-pressure-independent","hvac:s-pressure-dependent","hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling","hvac:s-vav-minimum","hvac:s-cooling-only-overcool","hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence","hvac:s-vav-damper-fail","hvac:s-leaking-reheat-valve","hvac:s-open-box-cleaning"]},{"id":"hvac-fan-boxes","title":"Series vs parallel fan-powered boxes","dom":"Mechanical","order":65,"intro":"The two fan-powered VAV terminals, where the fan sits, when it runs, and the parts that load with dust or fail.","mnemonic":"Series = Steady and in the Stream: fan always on, all air through it. Parallel = Part-time and off to the side: fan runs for heat only, backdraft damper stops spill.","units":["hvac:s-primary-air","hvac:s-series-fan","hvac:s-series-constant","hvac:s-parallel-fan","hvac:s-parallel-backdraft","hvac:s-series-low-static","hvac:s-plenum-free-heat","hvac:s-induction-filter","hvac:s-series-backward"]},{"id":"hvac-multizone","title":"Multizone and dual duct","dom":"Mechanical","order":70,"intro":"Two older hot-and-cold systems that are easy to confuse. The exam tests where the mixing happens and what that means at the zone.","mnemonic":"Multizone Mixes at the Machine, one duct per zone. Dual duct Delivers two Ducts to every zone box.","units":["hvac:s-multizone","hvac:s-multizone-ducts","hvac:s-dual-duct","hvac:s-dual-duct-terminal","hvac:s-dual-duct-fault","hvac:s-multizone-waste","hvac:s-multizone-leak"]},{"id":"hvac-room-units","title":"Fan coils, PTACs, water-source and ductless units","dom":"Mechanical","order":80,"intro":"The local units a tech meets in hotels, condos and offices, what is inside each, and where each one grows or holds dirt.","mnemonic":"Two pipes, one season; four pipes, both at once.","units":["hvac:s-fan-coil","hvac:s-two-four-pipe","hvac:s-ptac","hvac:s-pthp","hvac:s-ptac-slinger","hvac:s-wshp-loop","hvac:s-wshp-access","hvac:s-induction-unit","hvac:s-mini-split","hvac:s-mini-split-blower","hvac:s-multi-split"]},{"id":"hvac-ahu","title":"Inside the air handler","dom":"Mechanical","order":90,"intro":"The parts of an air handler in airflow order, draw-through vs blow-through, return and relief fans, humidifiers, and the drain pan and trap rules.","mnemonic":"Path: Mice Find Cheese First Sometimes = Mixing box, Filters, Coils, Fan, Supply. Traps: pulling needs a full inch, pushing needs half.","units":["hvac:s-ahu-path","hvac:s-mixing-box","hvac:s-preheat","hvac:s-draw-through","hvac:s-blow-through","hvac:s-blow-through-wet","hvac:s-return-relief-fans","hvac:s-oa-intake","hvac:s-humidifier-types","hvac:s-ultrasonic","hvac:s-absorption-distance","hvac:s-drain-pan","hvac:s-pan-slope","hvac:s-draw-through-trap","hvac:s-trap-height","hvac:s-float-switch","hvac:s-overflow-protection"]},{"id":"acr-what-acr-covers","title":"What ACR 2025 is and what it covers","dom":"Standards","order":100,"intro":"ACR 2025 is the industry standard of care: a performance standard with minimum procedures, applied to airside surfaces. Scope exclusions and the no-warranty lines are favorite distractors.","mnemonic":"Three P's are out of scope: non-ducted open air Plenums, Parts repair (electrical, pneumatic, high-pressure vessels, gas and oil controls), and OEM Preventative maintenance.","units":["acr:G-1","acr:G-2","acr:G-3","acr:G-4","acr:G-9a","acr:G-9b","acr:G-5","acr:G-6","acr:G-7","acr:G-8","acr:G-9","acr:G-13","acr:G-16","acr:G-17"]},{"id":"hvac-economizers","title":"Economizers, outdoor air and energy recovery","dom":"Mechanical","order":100,"intro":"How outdoor air enters and is checked with temperature readings, how economizers fail, and how ERVs, HRVs and wheels move heat between airstreams.","mnemonic":"Outdoor air fraction: how far the mix moved from return, over the whole outdoor-to-return gap. ERV = Everything (heat and moisture); HRV = Heat only.","units":["hvac:s-economizer","hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy","hvac:s-econ-integrated","hvac:s-econ-stuck-open","hvac:s-oa-fraction","hvac:s-econ-readings","hvac:s-min-oa-fixed","hvac:s-relief-air","hvac:s-erv-hrv","hvac:s-hrv-drain","hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel","hvac:s-wheel-leakage","hvac:s-runaround"]},{"id":"acr-obligation-words","title":"Shall, recommend, may, can","dom":"Standards","order":110,"intro":"Most ACR clauses carry an obligation word, and the exam tests the word as often as the fact. Learn what each word binds you to before you learn the clauses.","mnemonic":"Only SHALL binds. RECOMMEND is advice. MAY is 'Mother, may I?': permission given. CAN is 'I can': an ability you have. An answer that makes MAY, CAN, or RECOMMEND a requirement is wrong.","units":["acr:G-19","acr:G-20","acr:G-21","acr:G-22","acr:G-23","acr:G-24","acr:G-25"]},{"id":"hvac-coils","title":"Coils and refrigeration basics","dom":"Mechanical","order":110,"intro":"What the coil types are, the refrigeration cycle in plain words, the rule-of-thumb numbers, and what fouling does to a coil.","mnemonic":"12,000 BTU per ton, 400 CFM per ton, 500 FPM face, 15 to 20 degree split.","units":["hvac:s-coil-function","hvac:s-dx-coil","hvac:s-evap-cond","hvac:s-chilled-water","hvac:s-water-steam-coils","hvac:s-ton","hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","hvac:s-coil-delta-t","hvac:s-face-velocity","hvac:s-coil-rows","hvac:s-fin-straightening","hvac:s-coil-loading","hvac:s-leaking-heat-valve","hvac:s-coil-access"]},{"id":"acr-people-and-codes","title":"Qualified people, codes, and judgment","dom":"Standards","order":120,"intro":"Who may do work to ACR 2025, and which rules win when the Standard and the law disagree. Tested because required training sits right beside recommended certification.","units":["acr:G-10","acr:G-11","acr:G-12","acr:X-9","acr:G-14","acr:G-14a","acr:G-15","acr:G-18"],"mnemonic":"Trained is a must, certified is a wish: training, qualifications, knowledge and experience are SHALLs, but ASCS oversight is only recommended. Follow the codes and laws strictly, a microbial code beats contradictory text, and judgment MAY justify a deviation."},{"id":"hvac-heat-pumps","title":"Heat pumps and residential systems","dom":"Mechanical","order":120,"intro":"Split vs packaged equipment, furnace coil placement and safeties, and how heat pumps heat, defrost and use backup heat.","mnemonic":"Aux is Automatic and alongside the compressor; EM is Manual and alone. Dual fuel takes turns.","units":["hvac:s-split-system","hvac:s-packaged-unit","hvac:s-coil-only","hvac:s-furnace-coil-location","hvac:s-high-limit","hvac:s-cracked-hx","hvac:s-zoning-bypass","hvac:s-reversing-valve","hvac:s-defrost","hvac:s-aux-heat","hvac:s-emergency-heat","hvac:s-balance-point","hvac:s-dual-fuel","hvac:s-geothermal"]},{"id":"hvac-fans","title":"Fans, motors and the fan laws","dom":"Mechanical","order":130,"intro":"Fan types and where they are found, the fan laws, and what drives, motors and dirty wheels do to airflow.","mnemonic":"1-2-3: flow to the 1st power of speed, pressure to the 2nd, power to the 3rd. Double the speed: 2x air, 4x pressure, 8x power.","units":["hvac:s-fan-fc","hvac:s-fan-bi","hvac:s-fan-plenum","hvac:s-fan-axial","hvac:s-fc-overload","hvac:s-fan-law-flow","hvac:s-fan-law-pressure","hvac:s-fan-law-power","hvac:s-fan-half-speed","hvac:s-vfd","hvac:s-ecm","hvac:s-belt-drive","hvac:s-dirty-wheel","hvac:s-system-resistance"]},{"id":"hvac-dampers","title":"Dampers","dom":"Mechanical","order":140,"intro":"What each damper type does and what closes it, plus the fire damper temperatures and test intervals the exam uses as traps.","mnemonic":"Fire = Fusible (heat); Smoke = Signal (detector). 160 code floor, 165 standard link, 212 option, 350 smoke control ceiling. Test at 1 year, then every 4; hospitals 6.","units":["hvac:s-dampers-balancing","hvac:s-backdraft","hvac:s-opposed-blade","hvac:s-face-bypass","hvac:s-fire-damper","hvac:s-smoke-damper","hvac:s-combination-damper","hvac:s-static-dynamic","hvac:s-link-165","hvac:s-imc-160","hvac:s-nfpa80-test"]},{"id":"hvac-controls","title":"Controls","dom":"Mechanical","order":145,"intro":"The control points a duct cleaner works around: duct static and supply temperature loops, safeties, and outdoor air control.","mnemonic":"Static sensor: two-thirds down the main.","units":["hvac:s-static-sensor","hvac:s-static-reset","hvac:s-static-too-low","hvac:s-sat-reset","hvac:s-sequence-of-operation","hvac:s-freeze-stat","hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector","hvac:s-dcv","hvac:s-unoccupied","hvac:s-pressure-interlock"]},{"id":"acr-need-to-clean","title":"When cleaning is recommended: the 16 triggers","dom":"Benefits","order":150,"intro":"Sixteen conditions that, shown by an inspection or building history, make cleaning recommended. Tested for the wording of each trigger and for the fact that all 16 are recommendations, not requirements.","mnemonic":"4 + 7 + 5 = 16, all REC. Sense it (4): particulate present, performance hurt, odor, visible discharge. Events (7): fire, smoke, or water damage; pests; fire hazard; construction debris; dusty new install; microbial Condition 2 or 3; porous material breaking down. Programs (5): ASHRAE 180, OEM, energy, IAQ, LEED.","units":["acr:N-0","acr:N-1","acr:N-18","acr:N-2","acr:N-3","acr:N-4","acr:N-5","acr:N-6","acr:N-7","acr:N-8","acr:N-16","acr:N-9","acr:N-10","acr:N-11","acr:N-12","acr:N-13","acr:N-14","acr:N-15","acr:N-17"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-materials","title":"Duct types and materials","dom":"Mechanical","order":150,"intro":"Galvanized, spiral, flat oval, duct board, liner, wrap, flex and fabric duct: what each is, which are porous, and the UL 181 listings behind them. Exams test the porous list and the board, liner, wrap confusion.","mnemonic":"Board IS the duct, liner is IN it, wrap is ON it. UL 181A for rigid board, 181B for bendy flex. Class 0 is zero; Class 1 is 25/50. Flex: 4 ft supports, half-inch sag per foot.","units":["hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","hvac:a-spiral-flat-oval","hvac:a-duct-board","hvac:a-duct-liner","hvac:a-lined-duct-openings","hvac:a-duct-wrap","hvac:a-wrap-repair","hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous","hvac:a-porous-dry-only","hvac:a-flex-friction","hvac:a-flex-support","hvac:a-ul181-classes","hvac:a-ul181-family","hvac:a-board-closures","hvac:a-board-pressure","hvac:a-flex-no-openings","hvac:a-fabric-duct"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-design","title":"Duct sizing, fittings and noise","dom":"Mechanical","order":160,"intro":"How ducts are sized (equal friction, static regain), why round beats rectangular, and how elbows, vanes, takeoffs, returns and attenuators change pressure loss and noise.","mnemonic":"ACCA order J, S, D, T: loads, then equipment, then ducts, then terminals. Double the air, four times the friction. Keep rectangles at 4 to 1 or squarer.","units":["hvac:a-equal-friction","hvac:a-static-regain","hvac:a-round-vs-rect","hvac:a-aspect-ratio","hvac:a-equivalent-length","hvac:a-velocity-squared","hvac:a-critical-path","hvac:a-takeoffs","hvac:a-turning-vanes","hvac:a-vanes-humidifier","hvac:a-attenuator","hvac:a-undersized-return","hvac:a-transfer-path","hvac:a-acca-manuals"]},{"id":"hvac-air-distribution","title":"Outlets, throw and room air","dom":"Mechanical","order":165,"intro":"What moves the air and what releases it: the fan, the return plenum, diffusers, grilles and registers, plus how a supply jet throws, clings to the ceiling, dumps at low flow, and why smudge rings do not prove dirty ducts.","mnemonic":"Fan makes it, plenum carries it, diffuser spreads it, grille passes it, register adds a damper. Throw is quoted at 150, 100 and 50 fpm.","units":["hvac:d-fan-creates-airflow","hvac:d-return-plenum","hvac:d-diffuser","hvac:d-register","hvac:d-throw","hvac:d-coanda","hvac:d-dumping","hvac:d-smudging","hvac:d-laminar-or"]},{"id":"hvac-duct-leakage","title":"Duct leakage, SMACNA classes and location losses","dom":"Mechanical","order":170,"intro":"SMACNA pressure, seal and leakage classes at exam level, how leakage is tested and calculated, and what supply leaks, return leaks and attic runs cost.","mnemonic":"Seal classes: A adds All openings, B sits Between (joints and seams), C is Crossways joints only. Supply leaks push out; return leaks pull in.","units":["hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class","hvac:a-seal-class-a","hvac:a-seal-class-b","hvac:a-seal-class-c","hvac:a-leakage-class","hvac:a-leak-test","hvac:a-leakage-calc","hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks","hvac:a-duct-heat-gain","hvac:a-afue-duct-loss","hvac:a-attic-duct-r8"]},{"id":"hvac-exhaust","title":"Exhaust systems: grease, lab, toilet and dryer","dom":"Mechanical","order":180,"intro":"Which exhaust ducts fall under ACR and which move to NFPA 96 and IKECA, relief versus exhaust air, and the construction and material rules for grease, lab, toilet and dryer exhaust.","mnemonic":"NFPA 96 inspections: fuel monthly, heavy quarterly, normal twice a year, light once. IKECA: I inspects, C cleans, M maintains. Grease duct gauge: carbon 16, stainless 18.","units":["hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope","hvac:a-relief-vs-exhaust","hvac:a-nfpa96-scope","hvac:a-ikeca","hvac:a-grease-duct","hvac:a-nfpa96-frequency","hvac:a-lab-exhaust-material","hvac:a-lab-exhaust-hazard","hvac:a-toilet-exhaust","hvac:a-dryer-duct","hvac:a-intake-separation","hvac:a-negative-building"]},{"id":"hvac-filters-merv","title":"MERV, HEPA and filter types","dom":"Mechanical","order":190,"intro":"How ASHRAE 52.2 builds a MERV, the size ranges and key thresholds, what HEPA means, typical filter families, and the ASHRAE 62.1 MERV 8 floor.","mnemonic":"Size breaks 0.3, 1, 3, 10 (E1 fine, E2 mid, E3 coarse). Families: 1-4 flat fiberglass, 5-8 pleated, 9-16 rigid box and pocket. MERV 13 is 50/85/90. HEPA is 99.97 at 0.3. Coil floor MERV 8.","units":["hvac:a-merv-def","hvac:a-merv-range","hvac:a-merv-not-others","hvac:a-e-ranges","hvac:a-merv13","hvac:a-merv16","hvac:a-merv-low","hvac:a-merv-a","hvac:a-hepa","hvac:a-hepa-type","hvac:a-filter-families","hvac:a-merv8-floor","hvac:a-prefilter"]},{"id":"hvac-filters-performance","title":"Filter pressure drop, bypass and air cleaners","dom":"Mechanical","order":195,"intro":"What filter gauges tell you, why bypass beats any MERV, and what electronic cleaners, UV-C and carbon do and do not remove.","mnemonic":"Rising gauge means loading; falling gauge means leaking. Electronic makes ozone, UV-C kills but does not catch, carbon catches gases.","units":["hvac:a-high-merv-effect","hvac:a-filter-dp-loading","hvac:a-filter-dp-falls","hvac:a-bypass","hvac:a-bypass-acr","hvac:a-electronic-cleaner","hvac:a-ozone-limit","hvac:a-uvc","hvac:a-uvc-loto","hvac:a-carbon"]},{"id":"acr-inspection-purpose","title":"What inspections decide and what the assessment delivers","dom":"Assessments","order":200,"intro":"The inspection gathers the facts, before and after every project. The system component assessment turns them into a recommendation and a scope. Tested because the two lists overlap and are easy to swap.","mnemonic":"Inspect before AND after. The assessment answers five questions: Clean it? (need) What? (scope) How? (techniques) How is the space protected? (engineering controls) Anything special? (unique requirements).","units":["acr:1.0-a","acr:1.0-b","acr:1.0-c","acr:1.1-a","acr:1.1-b","acr:1.1-c","acr:1.10-a","acr:1.10-b","acr:1.10-c","acr:1.10-d","acr:1.10-e","acr:1.10-f"]},{"id":"hvac-air-pressure","title":"Static, velocity and total pressure","dom":"Mechanical","order":200,"intro":"Static, velocity and total pressure, the pitot tube and traverse, 4005, total external static and component pressure drops, with worked examples.","mnemonic":"Tip reads Total, Side reads Static, the difference is Velocity: TP = SP + VP. Then V = 4005 times root VP. TESP: add the two sides, ignore the signs.","units":["hvac:a-iwc-fpm","hvac:a-sp-vp-tp","hvac:a-pitot-ports","hvac:a-v-4005","hvac:a-v-4005-example","hvac:a-traverse-why","hvac:a-flow-hood","hvac:a-tesp","hvac:a-tesp-example","hvac:a-component-drop","hvac:a-resistance-airflow","hvac:a-dp-flow-squared"]},{"id":"hvac-airflow-math","title":"Airflow math: CFM, tons and heat","dom":"Mechanical","order":205,"intro":"CFM = FPM x area, tons and 400 CFM per ton, the 1.08 sensible heat formula and temperature split, each with a worked example.","mnemonic":"A ton is 12,000; figure 400 CFM per ton. 1.08 = 0.075 x 0.24 x 60: density, specific heat, minutes.","units":["hvac:a-q-va","hvac:a-q-va-example","hvac:a-round-area","hvac:a-face-velocity","hvac:a-ton","hvac:a-cfm-per-ton","hvac:a-tons-example","hvac:a-sensible-108","hvac:a-sensible-example","hvac:a-heat-constants","hvac:a-temp-split","hvac:a-split-meaning"]},{"id":"acr-inspector-prep","title":"Who inspects and how to prepare","dom":"Assessments","order":210,"intro":"The mandatory knowledge an inspector needs, the recommended credentials, and the steps around the inspection itself: risk assessment, plans, and engineering controls. The trap is required knowledge versus recommended certification.","mnemonic":"Don't Enter The System: Design, Engineering, Techniques, Standards, the four areas of verifiable working knowledge. Credentials (ASCS, CVI) are only recommended.","units":["acr:1.2-a","acr:1.2-h","acr:1.2-b","acr:1.2-c","acr:1.2-d","acr:1.2-e","acr:1.2-f","acr:1.2-g","acr:1.2.1-a","acr:1.2.1-b","acr:1.3-a","acr:1.3-b","acr:1.3-c","acr:1.4-a","acr:1.4-b","acr:1.4-c","acr:1.4-d"]},{"id":"hvac-psychrometrics","title":"Psychrometrics: dew point and latent heat","dom":"Mechanical","order":210,"intro":"Dry bulb, wet bulb, dew point, RH, sensible versus latent heat and why surfaces below dew point sweat. Tested through condensation and coil scenarios.","mnemonic":"Dew point, then wet bulb, then dry bulb, lowest to highest; all equal at saturation. Colder than dew point, it sweats.","units":["hvac:a-dry-wet-bulb","hvac:a-dew-point","hvac:a-condensation","hvac:a-rh-rises","hvac:a-rh-misleads","hvac:a-sensible-latent","hvac:a-latent-needs-dew","hvac:a-reheat-dehumidify","hvac:a-latent-sources","hvac:a-enthalpy","hvac:a-grains","hvac:a-oversized-ac"]},{"id":"acr-table-1","title":"Routine inspections and the Table 1 schedule","dom":"Assessments","order":220,"intro":"How often ACR 2025 recommends inspecting air handlers, supply ducts, and return and exhaust ducts in five building uses, and what changes during construction. Table 1 intervals are among the most drilled numbers on both exams.","mnemonic":"AHU = 1 year for everyone. Homes and boats (Residential, Marine) get a break on ducts: 1, 2, 2. The workplaces (Commercial, Industrial, Healthcare) are 1, 1, 1.","units":["acr:1.5-a","acr:1.5-b","acr:1.5-c","acr:1.5-d","acr:1.5.1-a","acr:1.5.1-b","acr:1.5.1-c","acr:1.5.1-d","acr:1.5.1-e","acr:1.5.1-f","acr:1.6-a","acr:1.6-b","acr:1.6-c"]},{"id":"hvac-mixed-air-co2","title":"Mixed air, outdoor air and CO2","dom":"Mechanical","order":220,"intro":"Reading mixed-air temperatures to find outdoor air fraction, spotting damper and valve faults from readings, and using CO2 as a ventilation indicator without overreaching.","mnemonic":"OA fraction: how far the mix moved from return, over the whole outdoor-to-return gap. CO2: 15 cfm per person holds about 700 over outdoors, once steady.","units":["hvac:a-mixed-air-temp","hvac:a-oa-fraction","hvac:a-oa-fraction-example","hvac:a-oa-fraction-spread","hvac:a-oa-vs-damper","hvac:a-fixed-min-oa","hvac:a-mixing-leak","hvac:a-leaking-heat-valve","hvac:a-co2-occupants","hvac:a-co2-700","hvac:a-co2-steady-state","hvac:a-co2-example","hvac:a-co2-no-limit","hvac:a-dcv"]},{"id":"acr-ten-percent-ahu","title":"The 10% rule and the air handler checklist","dom":"Assessments","order":230,"intro":"How much of a system a cleanliness inspection must cover, the S520 all-components exception, and the one component list in 1.7 written as SHALL. Expect the 10% figure, the exception, and the AHU list.","mnemonic":"Follow the air through the box: DAMPERS let it in, FILTERS (and bypass) clean it, heating and cooling COILS temper it, the CONDENSATE pan and drain line catch the drip, the HUMIDIFIER adds moisture, the FAN in its compartment pushes it, the ACOUSTIC insulation quiets it, and DOOR GASKETS and UNIT INTEGRITY keep it in.","units":["acr:1.7-a","acr:1.7-b","acr:1.7-c","acr:1.7-d","acr:1.7-e","acr:X-7","acr:1.7.1-a","acr:1.7.1-b","acr:1.7.1-c","acr:1.7.1-d","acr:1.7.1-e","acr:1.7.1-f","acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-h","acr:1.7.1-i"]},{"id":"hvac-efficiency","title":"Efficiency ratings and 2023 minimums","dom":"Mechanical","order":230,"intro":"SEER2, EER2, HSPF2, AFUE and COP, the SEER2 test static, and the federal minimums in force since January 1, 2023.","mnemonic":"SEER2 is the season, EER2 one 95 F point, HSPF2 the heating season. Split heat pump 14.3 and 7.5; package heat pump 13.4 and 6.7; split and package ACs start at 13.4.","units":["hvac:a-seer2","hvac:a-eer2","hvac:a-hspf2","hvac:a-afue","hvac:a-cop","hvac:a-seer2-test-static","hvac:a-tesp-vs-rating","hvac:a-min-hp-seer2","hvac:a-min-hp-hspf2","hvac:a-min-ac"]},{"id":"acr-other-components","title":"Supply, return, plenum, exhaust, and damaged components","dom":"Assessments","order":240,"intro":"What the supply, return, and exhaust inspections take in, how the new 2025 open air plenum clause works, and what to do with damaged parts. Tested for the new numbering and for may versus shall wording.","mnemonic":"1.7.1 to 1.7.6 follow the air: AHU, Supply out, Return back, Open plenum (new, a MAY), Exhaust leaves, Damage gets documented. 'All Supply Returns Open Every Door.'","units":["acr:1.7.2-a","acr:1.7.2-b","acr:1.7.2-c","acr:1.7.3-a","acr:1.7.3-b","acr:1.7.3-c","acr:1.7.3-d","acr:1.7.4-a","acr:1.7.4-b","acr:1.7.4-c","acr:1.7.4-d","acr:1.7.5-a","acr:1.7.5-b","acr:1.7.5-c","acr:1.7.6-a","acr:1.7.6-b"]},{"id":"hvac-drawings","title":"Reading mechanical drawings","dom":"Mechanical","order":240,"intro":"Sheet numbering, legends, duct size tags, schedules, plan versus section versus detail, and damper and piping symbols, plus what ACR says about using plans.","mnemonic":"UDS sheet digits: 0 legend, 1 plan, 2 elevation, 3 section, 5 detail, 6 schedule.","units":["hvac:a-uds-sheet-types","hvac:a-legend","hvac:a-duct-size-notation","hvac:a-equipment-schedules","hvac:a-air-device-schedule","hvac:a-schedule-vs-field","hvac:a-plan-vs-section","hvac:a-detail","hvac:a-line-types","hvac:a-damper-tags","hvac:a-other-tags","hvac:a-piping-tags","hvac:a-acr-plans","hvac:a-field-controls"]},{"id":"acr-hazmat-microbial","title":"Hazardous materials and suspect microbial growth","dom":"Assessments","order":250,"intro":"What to do when an inspection may expose people to hazardous materials or turns up suspect microbial growth, and who takes it from there. A mandatory same-visit duct inspection sits among recommendations that read mandatory.","mnemonic":"Suspect growth in the box? Check both ducts now: supply and return on the same visit, Table 1 clock ignored. That is the only SHALL in 1.8 and 1.9. Every other action there is only recommended.","units":["acr:1.8-a","acr:1.8-b","acr:1.8-c","acr:X-8","acr:1.9-a","acr:1.9-b","acr:1.9-c","acr:1.9.1-a","acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c","acr:1.9.1-d","acr:1.9.2-a","acr:1.9.2-b"]},{"id":"acr-work-plan","title":"The written work plan: what must be in it","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":300,"intro":"A written work plan is mandatory before any cleaning, but only some of its parts are. Covers its purpose, the required scope of work, and the recommended means, trades, schedule, and communication sections.","mnemonic":"Scope opens, Submittals and Safety close: 2.2, 2.7, and 2.8 are the SHALL sections. Everything between them (2.3 methods, 2.4 trades, 2.5 schedule, 2.6 communication) is only recommended.","units":["acr:2.0-a","acr:2.0-b","acr:2.0-c","acr:2.0-d","acr:2.1-a","acr:2.1-b","acr:2.2-a","acr:2.2-b","acr:2.2-c","acr:2.3-a","acr:2.4-a","acr:2.4-b","acr:2.5-a","acr:2.5-b","acr:2.6-a","acr:2.6-b"]},{"id":"acr-submittals-safety","title":"Product submittals, SDS, and the safety plan","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":310,"intro":"The mandatory product, coating, and SDS requirements, controls for chemical vapors, and the life safety plan for detectors taken off-line. Watch for the one CAN and the one REC among the SHALLs.","units":["acr:2.7-a","acr:2.7-b","acr:2.7-c","acr:2.7.1-a","acr:2.7.1-b","acr:2.7.1-c","acr:2.7.2-a","acr:2.7.2-b","acr:2.8-a","acr:2.8-b","acr:2.8-c","acr:2.8-d"],"mnemonic":"Client CAN, Representatives Recommended: the client submittal record is a CAN, and each designated representative's duties are only recommended. The rest are SHALL: product and coating lists, instructions always available, SDS in the plan and on site, vapor and odor controls, and life safety when detectors go off-line."},{"id":"acr-engineering-controls","title":"Engineering controls: what counts and when","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":350,"intro":"Section 3 opens by saying what engineering controls are for and closes by making them mandatory on every project. The exam tests the list, the fact that PPE is not on it, and which summary lines are only recommendations.","mnemonic":"The ten controls in 3.0 order: 'Some Inspectors Prefer Dust-free Homes Here, Despite The Humid South' = Source control, Isolation barriers, Pressure differentials, Dust suppression, HEPA vacuuming, HEPA filtration, Detailed cleaning, Temperature, Humidity, Sanitary approach. Nothing you wear is on the list.","units":["acr:3.0-a","acr:3.0-b","acr:3.0-c","acr:3.0-d","acr:3.0-e","acr:3.0-f","acr:3.0-g","acr:3.0-h","acr:X-12","acr:3.13-a","acr:3.13-b","acr:3.13-c","acr:3.13-d","acr:3.13-e"]},{"id":"acr-equipment-hygiene","title":"Contractor equipment: clean in, clean out","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":360,"intro":"Section 3.1 treats your own tools as a contamination source: what equipment it covers, and when it must be cleaned, serviced, opened, or sealed. The exam likes the one recommendation hiding among the shalls.","mnemonic":"Clean IN (3.1.1), service DURING (3.1.2), open a dirty vac only in containment or OUTSIDE (3.1.2.1), clean or seal before it MOVES or LEAVES (3.1.3). The on-site vac check (3.1.4) is the lone 'recommended'.","units":["acr:3.1-a","acr:3.1-b","acr:3.1-c","acr:3.1-d","acr:3.1-e","acr:3.1-f","acr:3.1-g","acr:3.1.1-a","acr:3.1.1-b","acr:3.1.2-a","acr:3.1.2-b","acr:3.1.2.1-a","acr:3.1.2.1-b","acr:3.1.2.1-c","acr:3.1.3-a","acr:3.1.3-b","acr:3.1.4-a","acr:3.1.4-b"]},{"id":"acr-site-controls","title":"Site controls: exhaust, pressure, waste and backup","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":370,"intro":"Clauses 3.2 to 3.8 cover site controls: where fuel-powered gear sits, HEPA on vacuums that exhaust indoors, negative pressure, containing waste, air scrubbing, chemicals, and backup equipment. Scrubbing and backup are only recommended here. Watch the 2025 wording changes.","mnemonic":"HEPA is 'three nines and a seven at point three': 99.97% at 0.3 micron. No HEPA? It exhausts outdoors.","units":["acr:3.2-a","acr:3.2-b","acr:3.2.1-a","acr:3.3-a","acr:3.3-b","acr:3.4-a","acr:3.4-b","acr:3.4-c","acr:3.4-d","acr:3.5-a","acr:3.5.1-a","acr:3.6-a","acr:3.6-b","acr:3.6-c","acr:3.7-a","acr:3.7-b","acr:3.8-a"]},{"id":"acr-containment-ladder-level-1","title":"The containment ladder and Level 1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":380,"intro":"Containment has four levels, and each level inherits everything below it. This lesson shows the whole ladder in one view, then the three parts of Level 1, the minimum every project stands on.","mnemonic":"Learn only what each rung ADDS. 1 = Pressure, Cover, Control. 2 = Barriers, two-layer 6-mil Floor, 4 ACH, Manometer, Wipe-down. 3 = one decon chamber plus live monitoring. 4 = two chambers plus a recording alarm. Instruments climb Validate, Monitor, Record (Levels 2, 3, 4).","units":["acr:3-L1","acr:3-L2","acr:3-L3","acr:3-L4","acr:3-Lx","acr:3.9-a","acr:3.9.1-a","acr:3.9.1-b","acr:3.9.2-a","acr:3.9.2-b","acr:3.9.3-a","acr:3.9.3-b","acr:3.9-b","acr:3.9-c","acr:3.9-d","acr:X-2"]},{"id":"acr-level-2-barriers","title":"Level 2: barriers, floor and entry flaps","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":390,"intro":"Level 2 is where temporary barriers arrive. This lesson covers the barrier specs, the two-layer floor, and the entry flaps, plus the split the exam loves: the same 6-mil material is recommended in one clause and mandatory in the next.","mnemonic":"'Walls required, wall specs are wishes, floors are laws': barriers themselves are required, but their specs (floor to ceiling, 6-mil, airtight seals) are recommended; the two-layer 6-mil floor running 6 inches up the wall is required. Zipper gets one flap, a slit gets two.","units":["acr:3.10.1-a","acr:3.10.2-a","acr:3.10.2.1-a","acr:3.10.2.2-a","acr:3.10.2.3-a","acr:3.10.2.4-a","acr:3.10.3-a","acr:3.10.3-b","acr:3.10.3-c","acr:3.10.4-a","acr:3.10.4.1-a","acr:3.10.4.1-b","acr:3.10-c","acr:X-3","acr:3.10-d"]},{"id":"acr-level-2-pressure","title":"Level 2: pressure, 4 ACH and teardown","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":400,"intro":"Once the Level 2 enclosure is up, it must stay negative, be proven with an instrument, be scrubbed at 4 air changes per hour, and be cleaned before it comes down. The 4 ACH figure is the only one in the book.","mnemonic":"Level 2 runs on 'M, 4, W': a Manometer to prove it, 4 ACH to scrub it, a Wipe or HEPA vac before it comes down (plus verification first on healthcare jobs).","units":["acr:3.10.5-a","acr:3.10.5-b","acr:3.10.6-a","acr:3.10.7-a","acr:3.10.7-b","acr:3.10.7-c","acr:X-1","acr:3.10.8-a","acr:3.10.8-b"]},{"id":"acr-level-3-4","title":"Levels 3 and 4: decon chambers and monitoring","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":410,"intro":"Level 3 adds a single decontamination chamber and live monitoring of pressure and particulate. Level 4 doubles the chamber and adds a recording monitor with an alarm. The exam checks what each level adds and the stop-work rule.","mnemonic":"3 = one room plus Watch (continuous pressure, particulate baseline, real-time counts). 4 = two rooms plus Record and Ring (recording monitor with alarm). Stop above background; restart only when Back to background AND the cause is fixed.","units":["acr:3.11-a","acr:3.11.1-a","acr:3.11.2-a","acr:3.11.2-b","acr:3.11.2-c","acr:3.11.2-d","acr:3.11.3-a","acr:3.11.3-b","acr:3.11.3-c","acr:3.11.3-d","acr:3.11.3-e","acr:3.11.3-f","acr:3.11.3-g","acr:3.11.3-h","acr:3.12-a","acr:3.12.1-a","acr:3.12.2-a","acr:3.12.2-b","acr:3.12.3-a","acr:3.12.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-cleaning-goal-pressure","title":"The cleaning goal and negative duct pressure","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":450,"intro":"Section 4 starts with the target, visibly clean unless the contract names a verification level, and with keeping the duct negative before and during cleaning. Watch the HEPA versus outdoor exhaust rule.","mnemonic":"HEPA may exhaust inside; no HEPA goes outside.","units":["acr:4.0-a","acr:4.0-b","acr:4.1-a","acr:4.1-b","acr:4.1-c","acr:4.1-d","acr:4.1.1-a","acr:4.1.2-a","acr:4.1.2-b","acr:4.1.3-a"]},{"id":"acr-service-openings","title":"Service openings: rules for every opening","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":460,"intro":"Cutting into a duct is part of cleaning it. These clauses say what any opening must not damage, how fibrous glass is repaired, the flex duct ban, and which codes apply. Watch the 2025 additions: duct wrap, IMC, provincial codes.","mnemonic":"Never cut flex, just unhook it: no service opening goes into flexible ductwork, and unhooking it by the manufacturer's instructions is the only recommendation; every other rule is SHALL. Cut fibrous glass goes back like for like: same thickness, no exposed edges in the airstream, no break that degrades R value, service rating, vapor or air barrier, external duct wrap included.","units":["acr:4.2.1-a","acr:4.2.1-b","acr:4.2.2-a","acr:4.2.3-a","acr:4.2.4-a","acr:4.2.4-b","acr:4.2.8.1-a","acr:4.2.8.2-a","acr:4.2.8.2-b","acr:4.2.8.2-c","acr:4.2.8.2-d","acr:4.2.8.2-e","acr:4.2.9-a","acr:4.2.9.1-a","acr:4.2.10-a","acr:4.2.10-b","acr:4.2.10-c"]},{"id":"acr-service-panels","title":"Service panels, access doors and the 25/50 rule","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":470,"intro":"The most number-dense clause in Section 4: panel gauge, fastener spacing, overlap, sealing, the UL listings, access door gauge, and the flame-spread and smoke-developed limits. Nearly every line is a shall.","mnemonic":"Panel = '4, 1, seal, 181A/B': screws or rivets every 4 inches on center, 1 inch overlap on all sides, sealed (a shall since 2025), tapes and mastics to UL 181A/B. Gauge: the PANEL matches or beats the duct gauge, the DOOR follows the pressure class. Flame 25, smoke 50: flame is the smaller number, smoke doubles it.","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a","acr:4.2.5.1-b","acr:4.2.5.2-a","acr:4.2.5.2-b","acr:4.2.5.2-c","acr:4.2.5.3-a","acr:4.2.5.3-b","acr:4.2.5.4-a","acr:X-13","acr:4.2.6-a","acr:4.2.6-b","acr:4.2.6-c","acr:X-15","acr:4.2.7-a","acr:4.2.7-b","acr:4.2.7-c","acr:4.2.7-d","acr:4.2.7-e","acr:X-14"]},{"id":"acr-agitate-capture","title":"The core method: agitate, capture, keep water off","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":480,"intro":"ACR's cleaning method in one line: agitate to dislodge, then capture with vacuum collection. This lesson covers that method, the limits on wet cleaning, and what a cleaned air duct must achieve.","mnemonic":"Knock it loose, catch it. Pressure alone is not cleaning. Water never touches wires or fiberglass, except electric coils done the 4.11.8 way.","units":["acr:4.3-a","acr:4.3-b","acr:4.3-c","acr:4.4-a","acr:4.4-b","acr:4.4-c","acr:4.4-d","acr:4.4-e","acr:4.7-a","acr:4.7-b","acr:4.7.1-a","acr:4.7.2-a","acr:4.7.3-a","acr:4.7.4-a"]},{"id":"acr-vacuum-velocities","title":"Vacuum collection and Table 2 velocities","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":490,"intro":"Vacuum collection runs the whole time you clean and must hold enough velocity and negative pressure. Table 2, taken from the ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual, lists recommended minimum duct design velocities; from fumes upward they climb 500 fpm per contaminant class.","mnemonic":"Minimums climb 500 fpm a step from 2,000: 'Fire Fighters Don't Avoid Heavy Mud' = Fumes 2,000, Fine light dust 2,500, Dry dusts 3,000, Average industrial 3,500, Heavy 4,000, Heavy or Moist 4,500 and up. Vapors, gases, smoke: any velocity (optimum 1,000 to 2,000).","units":["acr:4.5-a","acr:4.5-b","acr:4.5.1-a","acr:4.5.1-b","acr:4.5.1-c","acr:T2-a","acr:T2-b","acr:T2-r1","acr:T2-r2","acr:T2-r3","acr:T2-r4","acr:T2-r5","acr:T2-r6","acr:T2-r7"]},{"id":"acr-ahu-dampers-registers","title":"Air handlers, dampers, registers and detectors","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":500,"intro":"Short clauses with sharp obligation splits: wet cleaning of AHU parts is recommended but water control is required, dampers are marked and reset, registers come out if possible but always go back, and detection equipment is never impaired.","mnemonic":"Mark it, clean it, put it back. Pulling registers is recommended; putting them back is a must.","units":["acr:4.6-a","acr:4.6.1-a","acr:4.6.2-a","acr:4.6.2-b","acr:4.8-a","acr:4.8-b","acr:4.9-a","acr:4.9-b","acr:4.9-c","acr:4.10-a","acr:4.10-b"]},{"id":"acr-coil-inspect-type1","title":"Coil cleaning 1: access, inspection and Type 1","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":510,"intro":"Every coil job starts with access to both faces and a visual inspection of the coil and drain pan. That inspection decides Type 1 dry or Type 2 wet. This lesson covers the triggers, the replacement cases, and the Type 1 rules and tools.","mnemonic":"Coil path: Look, Dry, Look, Wet, Look, Repeat; Replace only when wet methods cannot remove it. Jump straight to Wet when microbes are suspected or the assessment says dry will not do it. Type 1 toolkit 'VBAF': HEPA contact Vacuum, Brushes, compressed Air, Fin straighteners.","units":["acr:4.11-a","acr:4.11-b","acr:4.11.1-a","acr:4.11.1-b","acr:4.11.1.1-a","acr:4.11.1.2-a","acr:4.11.1.3-a","acr:4.11.1.3-b","acr:4.11.2-a","acr:4.11.2-b","acr:4.11.2-c","acr:4.11.2-d","acr:4.11.2-e","acr:4.11.2-f","acr:4.11.2-g","acr:4.11.2-h","acr:4.11.3-a","acr:4.11.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-coil-type2","title":"Coil cleaning 2: Type 2 wet cleaning and the drain pan","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":520,"intro":"Type 2 is wet cleaning of the coil, drain pan, and drain line. This lesson covers its triggers, the permitted methods, the drain pan tests, product limits, and the repeat-or-replace rule, plus the big 2025 change: Type 1 no longer always comes first.","mnemonic":"Type 2 = 'water TOO': coil products, line-pressure water, pressure washer, hot water or steam. Three roads into Type 2: Microbes suspected, Assessment says dry will not do, Debris left after dry. Drain pan: Test, Clean and flush, Test again.","units":["acr:4.11.4-a","acr:4.11.4-b","acr:4.11.4-c","acr:4.11.4-h","acr:4.11.4-d","acr:4.11.4-e","acr:4.11.4-f","acr:4.11.4-g","acr:4.11.4-g2","acr:4.11.4-g3","acr:4.11.4.1-a","acr:4.11.4.1-b","acr:4.11.4.2-a","acr:4.11.4.2-b","acr:4.11.4.2-c","acr:4.11.5-a","acr:4.11.5-b","acr:4.11.5-c"]},{"id":"acr-coil-special","title":"Coil cleaning 3: measuring results, inline and electric coils","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":530,"intro":"Why a pressure drop reading beats your eyes, how to handle duct-mounted and inline coils without drain pans, and the four-step rule for wet cleaning electric resistance coils, the only exception to keeping water off electrical parts.","mnemonic":"Electric coil: 'Kill, Mild, Rinse, Dry': de-energize and lockout/tagout, non-corrosive detergent only, rinse free of chemicals, fully dry before power returns.","units":["acr:4.11.6-a","acr:4.11.6-b","acr:4.11.7-a","acr:4.11.7-b","acr:4.11.7-c","acr:4.11.8-a","acr:4.11.8-b","acr:4.11.8-c","acr:4.11.8-d"]},{"id":"acr-restoration-order","title":"Restoration after cleaning: order, repair and surface treatments","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":540,"intro":"Restoration never comes first. This lesson covers the order of operations, what happens to components that cannot take cleaning, the microbial remediation standard, and when surface treatments may go on.","mnemonic":"Clean, then restore. Clean, verify PASS, then coat. Coatings wear plain '181'; tapes and mastics wear '181A/B'.","units":["acr:4.12-a","acr:4.13-a","acr:4.13-b","acr:4.13-c","acr:4.14-a","acr:4.14.1-a","acr:4.14.2-a","acr:4.14.3-a","acr:4.14.4-a","acr:4.14.4-b","acr:4.14.4-c","acr:4.15-a","acr:4.15-b","acr:4.15-c","acr:4.15.1-a","acr:4.15.1-b","acr:X-10"]},{"id":"acr-fibrous-glass","title":"Fibrous glass: clean, resurface or replace","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":550,"intro":"Fibrous glass liner and duct board are cleaned like any duct, but gently. When cleaning is not enough, you may resurface if the surface will bond, and replace it if it will not. Cleaned non-porous surfaces can be restored too.","mnemonic":"Clean, then resurface, then replace. Cleaning is SHALL and gentle, with no abrasions, breaks, or tears; resurfacing is a MAY, and a surface that will not bond is not resurfaced; replacement is only recommended, once cleaning and resurfacing cannot restore the material.","units":["acr:4.17-a","acr:4.17.1-a","acr:4.18-a","acr:4.18-b","acr:4.18.1-a","acr:4.18.1-b","acr:4.18.2-a","acr:4.18.2-b","acr:4.18.2-c","acr:4.19-a","acr:4.19-b","acr:4.21-a"]},{"id":"acr-insulation-replacement","title":"Replacing insulation after microbial growth","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":560,"intro":"When porous material has Condition 3 growth, removal and replacement are recommended; the metal must then be mechanically cleaned, and on microbial jobs the base must reach Condition 1 first. Replacements must meet or exceed the original spec and be capable of passing verification.","mnemonic":"Out at 3, back in at 1: Condition 3 porous material should come out (recommended); the base must reach Condition 1 before new insulation goes in (required).","units":["acr:4.16-a","acr:4.16-b","acr:4.20-a","acr:4.20-b","acr:4.20.1-a","acr:X-11","acr:4.20.2-a","acr:4.20.2-b","acr:4.20.3-a","acr:4.20.4-a","acr:4.20.5-a","acr:4.20.5-b"]},{"id":"acr-damage-repair","title":"Water, fire and smoke damage, and pre-existing damage","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":570,"intro":"After a flood or a fire the job changes: water gets categorized under S500, fire-damaged parts are evaluated for friability and odor, and soot questions go to IESO/RIA 6001. Damage that was already there gets documented and reported.","mnemonic":"S500 for water, S520 for mold, 6001 for soot. Found old damage? Write it down, tell the client.","units":["acr:4.22-a","acr:4.22-b","acr:4.22-c","acr:4.22-d","acr:4.23-a","acr:4.23-b","acr:4.23.1-a","acr:4.23.2-a","acr:4.23.3-a","acr:4.23.4-a","acr:4.24-a","acr:4.24-b","acr:4.24.1-a","acr:4.24.1-b"]},{"id":"acr-verification-overview","title":"Verification: purpose, timing and the three methods","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":600,"intro":"What verification proves (particulate only), when it happens, the three methods and which surfaces each can judge, and how a disputed result escalates. The 2025 renumbering and the wider Method 2 scope are heavily tested.","mnemonic":"LOOK, BRUSH, WEIGH. Method 1 you look, Method 2 you brush with a contact vacuum, Method 3 you weigh a filter. Anything can be looked at or brushed; only a non-porous surface can be weighed. Escalation climbs 1, then 2 or 3, then 3 settles it. Porous tops out at the brush.","units":["acr:5.0-a","acr:5.0-b","acr:5.0-c","acr:5.0-d","acr:5.0-e","acr:5.0-f","acr:5.1-a","acr:5.1-b","acr:5-M1","acr:5-M2","acr:5-M3","acr:5.2-a","acr:5.2-b","acr:5.2-c","acr:5.2.1-a","acr:5.3-a","acr:5.3-c","acr:5.3-d","acr:5.3.3-a","acr:5.3.3-b","acr:5-M4","acr:5-M5"]},{"id":"acr-method-2","title":"Method 2: the Surface Comparison Test","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":610,"intro":"The Method 2 kit and procedure: a HEPA contact vacuum, the brush and hose sizes, four passes, and the before-and-after visual comparison. Every number here is a SHALL and a drill favorite.","mnemonic":"4-80, 2.5 on 1.5: four passes, 80 in. of static lift, a 2.5 in. brush on a 1.5 in. hose (the brush is one inch bigger than the hose). Pass means no significant visible difference.","units":["acr:5.3-b","acr:5.3.1-a","acr:5.3.1-b","acr:5.3.1-c","acr:5.3.1-d","acr:5.3.1-e","acr:5.3.1-f","acr:5.3.1-g","acr:5.3.2-a","acr:5.3.2-b"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-kit","title":"Method 3: how the vacuum test works and its kit","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":620,"intro":"How the NADCA Vacuum Test pulls particulate off a non-porous surface without touching it, and the exact kit it needs: pump, cassette, filters, calibrator and template. The Method 3 number set is the densest drill material in the Standard.","mnemonic":"TRIPLE 15: the pump draws at least 15 L/min, the calibrator is accurate at 15 L/min, the template is 15 mil. Filters are 37 mm, and the 3.7 cm maximum slot width is that 37 with the decimal moved. Slots are 2 x 25 cm = 50 cm2 each, two make 100 cm2, at least 2.5 cm apart; alternate slots stay 2.0 to 3.7 cm wide.","units":["acr:5.4-a","acr:5.4-b","acr:5.4-c","acr:5.4-d","acr:5.4-e","acr:5.4-f","acr:5.4-g","acr:5.4-h","acr:5.4-i","acr:5.4-j","acr:5.4.1-a","acr:5.4.1-b","acr:5.4.1-c","acr:5.4.1-d","acr:5.4.1-e","acr:5.4.1-f","acr:5.4.1-g","acr:5.4.1-h","acr:5.4.1-i","acr:5.4.1.1-a","acr:5.4.1.1-b","acr:5.4.1.2-b","acr:5.4.1.2-c","acr:5.4.1.2-d","acr:5.4.1.2-e"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-field","title":"Method 3: taking the sample in the field","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":630,"intro":"The Method 3 sampling protocol up to pump shutdown: preparing the surface and template, opening and connecting the cassette, setting the flow, and making the passes. Order, speed, pass count and the no-contact rules are tested.","mnemonic":"TAPE, FLAT, DRY, OFF, then PLUGS, OUTLET, 17. Tape the template at four corners, flat, on a dry surface with the air handler off. Plugs out, outlet to the pump, flow set to 17 plus or minus 2. Then 5 and 2: at most 5 cm per second, two passes per slot, edges on the template, no push, never touch the duct.","units":["acr:5.4.2-a","acr:5.4.2-b","acr:5.4.2-c","acr:5.4.2-d","acr:5.4.2-e","acr:5.4.2-f","acr:5.4.2-g","acr:5.4.2-h","acr:X-4","acr:5.4.2-i","acr:5.4.2-j","acr:5.4.2-k","acr:5.4.2-l","acr:5.4.2-m","acr:X-5","acr:5.4.2-n","acr:5.4.2-o","acr:5.4.2-y"]},{"id":"acr-method-3-results","title":"Method 3 results, the pass limit and project documentation","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":640,"intro":"What happens to the cassette after sampling, the scale and pass numbers, who should sample and analyze, and what the closing paperwork must hold. Several items here look mandatory but are only recommended.","mnemonic":"0.7 UP, 0.75 DOWN: scale sensitivity is 0.7 mg or more, and the net debris weight must be 0.75 mg/100 cm2 or less. Paperwork has only two SHALLs, both lab-driven: the lab results after a Vacuum Test, and chain of custody when an outside lab or agency is used. Photos, plans and submittals are recommendations.","units":["acr:5.4.2-p","acr:5.4.2-q","acr:5.4.2-r","acr:X-6","acr:5.4.2-s","acr:5.4.2-t","acr:5.5-a","acr:5.4.2-u","acr:5.4.2-v","acr:5.4.2-w","acr:5.4.2-x","acr:5.6-a","acr:5.6-b","acr:5.6.1-a","acr:5.6.2-a","acr:5.6.3-a","acr:5.6.3-b"]},{"id":"acr-substances","title":"The four substances, and porous vs non-porous","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":700,"intro":"The definitions the whole Standard runs on: what cleaning removes, the near-identical substance terms, and which materials are porous. They decide what visibly clean means and which verification method can apply.","mnemonic":"ANY, LOOSE, BRUSHABLE: Contaminant is any unwanted substance, Debris is the loose (non-adhered) kind, Particulate is loose material an agitation device can remove. Adhered (paint, mastic, sealant, stains) stays, and visibly clean ignores it. Porous is \"LBS of Wet Concrete\": Liner, Board, Sound-attenuated duct, Wood, Concrete. Non-porous is \"Metal, Foil, Film\".","units":["acr:D-1","acr:D-11","acr:D-2","acr:D-3","acr:D-4","acr:D-5","acr:D-10","acr:D-6","acr:D-6a","acr:D-7","acr:D-8","acr:D-9","acr:D-12","acr:D-13","acr:D-14","acr:D-15","acr:D-16","acr:D-17"]},{"id":"acr-microbial-conditions","title":"Microbial Conditions 1, 2 and 3","dom":"Assessments","order":710,"intro":"The S520 Condition 1, 2 and 3 definitions and what counts as microbial contamination. Questions test the small wording differences between the three levels.","mnemonic":"BASELINE, SPILLOVER, SOURCE. Condition 1 is the normal Baseline (traces allowed). Condition 2 is Spillover: settled spores or fragments that came from a 3. Condition 3 is the Source: actual growth, active or dormant, visible or hidden.","units":["acr:D-18","acr:D-19","acr:D-20","acr:D-20a","acr:D-21","acr:D-22","acr:D-23","acr:D-24","acr:D-25","acr:D-26"]},{"id":"acr-equipment-terms","title":"Equipment terms: HEPA, AFDs, scrubbers and collection devices","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":720,"intro":"Equipment words: HEPA, air filtration devices, scrubbers, negative air machines, collection devices and agitation devices, plus two terms new in 2025. The scrubber versus negative air machine split and the HEPA numbers are common questions.","mnemonic":"HEPA is three nines and a seven at point three: 99.97% at 0.3 micron, defined by the DOE. The Scrubber Stays (recirculates in the space); the Negative air machine controls pressure, ducted out or not. Collection device: HEPA may blow inside, no HEPA blows outside.","units":["acr:D-27","acr:D-28","acr:D-29","acr:D-30","acr:D-31","acr:D-32","acr:D-33","acr:D-34","acr:D-35","acr:D-36","acr:D-37","acr:D-38","acr:D-39"]},{"id":"acr-duct-terms","title":"Duct, door and closure terms","dom":"Mechanical","order":730,"intro":"Duct, insulation, access door and closure terms. Door types and the fastener definitions feed the service-opening questions in Section 4.","mnemonic":"Flush goes IN, Surface sticks OUT, Sandwich has two slices (both sides of the wall), Spin is round. Mechanically fasten means Screws, Clamps, Locks or Straps, but a service panel gets only screws or rivets.","units":["acr:D-40","acr:D-41","acr:D-42","acr:D-43","acr:D-44","acr:D-45","acr:D-46","acr:D-47","acr:D-48","acr:D-49","acr:D-50","acr:D-51","acr:D-52","acr:D-53","acr:D-54","acr:D-55","acr:D-56","acr:D-57"]},{"id":"acr-ahu-terms","title":"Air-handling unit types and coils","dom":"Mechanical","order":740,"intro":"The air-handling unit family as ACR defines it, plus coils and total cooling. Know which unit types carry their own heating or cooling source.","units":["acr:D-70","acr:D-71","acr:D-72","acr:D-73","acr:D-74","acr:D-74a","acr:D-75","acr:D-76","acr:D-77","acr:D-78","acr:D-79","acr:D-80"],"mnemonic":"Units do what their names say: Cooling cools, Heating heats, Cooling-Heating does both (window units to split systems), Ventilating ventilates, Make-Up replaces exhausted air. The odd one out is the Central-Station AHU: fans in a factory box, no heating or cooling source of its own. Total cooling is sensible plus latent."},{"id":"acr-scope-roles","title":"Scope boundaries, roles and project terms","dom":"Standards","order":750,"intro":"Where the Standard stops (airside surfaces, the HVAC system, open air plenums) and the defined roles and project terms: ASCS, IEP, inspection versus assessment, restoration and chain of custody.","mnemonic":"Walk the air from entry to discharge: every part it passes through inside the system is in; a non-ducted open air plenum is out. Chain of custody: Some Couriers Carefully Transfer All Deliveries (Seizure, Custody, Control, Transfer, Analysis, Disposition). Inspection gathers, assessment evaluates.","units":["acr:D-58","acr:D-59","acr:D-60","acr:D-61","acr:D-62","acr:D-63","acr:D-64","acr:D-65","acr:D-66","acr:D-67","acr:D-68","acr:D-69","acr:D-81","acr:D-82","acr:D-83","acr:D-84","acr:D-85","acr:D-86","acr:D-87","acr:D-88","acr:D-89","acr:D-90"]},{"id":"acr-controls-terms","title":"Engineering controls and pressure terms","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":760,"intro":"Engineering controls, containment and pressure terms. The Standard defines negative pressure by what it must do, never by a number, and the exam leans on that.","mnemonic":"Negative pressure has a job, not a number: keep debris out of occupied space and inside the contained area.","units":["acr:D-91","acr:D-92","acr:D-93","acr:D-94","acr:D-95","acr:D-96","acr:D-97","acr:D-98","acr:D-99","acr:D-100"]},{"id":"acr-process-terms","title":"Process, access and obligation-word terms","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":770,"intro":"Process, material and access terms, the obligation-word definitions, and two 2025 leftovers: the porous-only Method 2 definition and the deleted AHJ term.","mnemonic":"\"Mother, MAY I?\" asks permission. \"Yes, I CAN\" states ability. RECOMMEND advises. Only SHALL makes a requirement.","units":["acr:D-101","acr:D-102","acr:D-103","acr:D-104","acr:D-105","acr:D-106","acr:D-107","acr:D-109","acr:D-110","acr:D-111","acr:D-112","acr:D-113","acr:D-114","acr:D-115","acr:D-116","acr:D-117","acr:D-118","acr:D-119","acr:D-108"]},{"id":"acr-references","title":"Referenced documents: which standard covers what","dom":"Standards","order":780,"intro":"Which referenced standard covers what. Exam options often swap S500, S520 and S590, or UL 181 and UL 181A/B.","mnemonic":"Follow the damage: S500 water gets in, S520 mold grows, S590 assesses the HVAC afterward. Plain UL 181 is for surface treatments; UL 181 with the A/B letters is for closures, the tapes and mastics that seal service openings.","units":["acr:R-1","acr:R-2","acr:R-3","acr:R-4","acr:R-5","acr:R-6","acr:R-7","acr:R-8","acr:R-9","acr:R-10","acr:R-11","acr:R-12","acr:R-13"]},{"id":"ascs-benefits-performance","title":"Airflow, energy and equipment life","dom":"Benefits","order":1000,"intro":"What fouling does to coils, blowers and airflow, and how to describe the energy and equipment-life benefits of cleaning without overclaiming. The exam rewards the defensible answer, never the biggest promise.","mnemonic":"Mechanism first, number never: debris blocks air and insulates coils, cleaning restores both, savings vary. Added resistance usually lowers airflow; a clean coil has less pressure drop than a fouled one.","units":["ascs:energy-claim-honest","ascs:epa-energy-evidence","ascs:fouling-mechanism","ascs:fouled-coil","ascs:fouled-blower-wheel","ascs:resistance-pairs","ascs:strongest-benefit","ascs:equipment-life","ascs:benefit-themes"]},{"id":"cvi-ashrae","title":"ASHRAE standards and the 62.1 equipment rules","dom":"Standards","order":1000,"intro":"Which ASHRAE standard answers which question, plus the ASHRAE 62.1 filter, drain pan, maintenance and construction rules an inspector cites in findings. CVI distractors are almost always a sibling standard.","mnemonic":"Standard numbers: 55 feels (comfort), 62.1 breathes (62.2 at home), 52.2 grades filters, 170 heals, 180 maintains, 188 is water, 241 is germs in the air. Table 8-1 clock: towers 1 month, filters 3, louvers 6, pans and coils 12.","units":["cvi:ashrae-55","cvi:ashrae-901","cvi:ashrae-522"]},{"id":"ascs-benefits-claims","title":"Honest claims and when to recommend cleaning","dom":"Benefits","order":1010,"intro":"What you may promise about health, IAQ, odor and fire risk, what EPA actually says about duct cleaning, and why the recommendation to clean follows conditions found, not the calendar. Overpromising is the classic wrong answer.","mnemonic":"Claim what you do, not what you hope: you remove contaminants; you do not cure, guarantee or promise. Condition, not calendar: EPA says clean for mold, vermin or debris, never on a schedule.","units":["ascs:health-claim","ascs:epa-health-evidence","ascs:iaq-claim","ascs:allergy-response","ascs:odor-scope","ascs:fire-fuel-load","ascs:no-stay-clean-guarantee","ascs:expectations-scope","ascs:proposal-language","ascs:before-after-photos","ascs:epa-routine-position","ascs:epa-conditions","ascs:condition-drives-recommendation","ascs:maintenance-program-decision","ascs:source-removal-why"]},{"id":"cvi-dampers","title":"Fire and smoke dampers","dom":"Standards","order":1010,"intro":"Test intervals, listings, ratings, labels and the test method for fire, smoke and combination dampers. Damper compliance is a named CVI topic and the numbers are drilled hard.","mnemonic":"1, then 4, hospitals 6 (NFPA 80 fire, NFPA 105 smoke, combination = both). UL 555 fire, S for Smoke, C for Ceiling, 33 is the link. Rating: under 3 hours gets 1½, 3 hours gets 3.","units":["cvi:fd-interval","cvi:damper-hospital","cvi:sd-interval","cvi:damper-combination","cvi:damper-code-chain","cvi:damper-fusible-test","cvi:damper-command-test","cvi:ul-555-family","cvi:damper-rating","cvi:damper-dynamic","cvi:damper-label"]},{"id":"ascs-contaminants-iaq","title":"Contaminant types and IAQ terms","dom":"Benefits","order":1020,"intro":"The contaminant classes an ASCS must recognize and the IAQ vocabulary that shows up in benefit questions: SBS versus BRI, respirable particulate, VOCs, soot and biological contaminants.","mnemonic":"SBS Stops when you leave; BRI Brings a diagnosis. Biological means microbes and critters: bacteria, viruses, mold, animal and insect byproducts. Drywall dust, fiberglass and soot are non-biological particulate; VOCs are gases.","units":["ascs:sbs-vs-bri","ascs:soot","ascs:biological-vs-particulate","ascs:respirable-particulate","ascs:voc-sources","ascs:infestation-contaminates","ascs:construction-particulate","ascs:plenum-infiltration"]},{"id":"cvi-nfpa-codes","title":"NFPA scope, duct detectors, kitchen exhaust and the I-codes","dom":"Standards","order":1020,"intro":"Which NFPA document and which I-code governs a system, where duct smoke detectors go, kitchen exhaust inspection frequency, and the flex connector limit. Scope questions reward knowing the one number that splits two documents.","mnemonic":"B for the Bungalow: 90B is dwellings and 25,000 cu ft or less. Detectors: 2,000 cfm supply, 15,000 cfm multi-story return. NFPA 96: fuel monthly, heavy quarterly, normal twice, light once. IKECA: I inspects, C cleans, M maintains.","units":["cvi:nfpa-90b-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-scope","cvi:nfpa-90a-supply-det","cvi:nfpa-90a-return-det","cvi:imc-return-det","cvi:nfpa-96-intervals","cvi:ikeca-map","cvi:irc-scope","cvi:imc-irc-exception","cvi:flex-connector"]},{"id":"cvi-duct-listings","title":"Duct construction documents and material listings","dom":"Standards","order":1030,"intro":"SMACNA documents, seal classes, liner and duct listings, closure tapes and fire test names. These show up as submittal and field-finding questions where the wrong listing is the trap.","mnemonic":"Seal classes: A adds All openings, C is Crossways joints only, B sits Between. Closures: 181A for rigid board, 181B for bendy flex.","units":["cvi:smacna-seal-classes","cvi:smacna-016","cvi:smacna-008","cvi:astm-c1071","cvi:ul-181","cvi:ul-181a","cvi:ul723-e84","cvi:class-a-450","cvi:ul-900"]},{"id":"cvi-restoration-epa","title":"Restoration standards and EPA duct guidance","dom":"Standards","order":1040,"intro":"IICRC water categories and classes, what S590 assessment produces, and what EPA's duct cleaning and building guides actually say. Inspectors get tested on quoting EPA accurately and on picking the right IICRC document.","mnemonic":"Follow the loss: S500 is the Soaked system (water), S520 the Spores that follow (mold), S590 the Survey of the HVAC after either, 6001 the Soot test. EPA duct guide: 3 reasons (mold, vermin, clogged), no proven health benefit, no biocide on fiberglass.","units":["cvi:s500-categories","cvi:s500-time","cvi:s590-purpose","cvi:s590-output","cvi:epa-duct-triggers","cvi:epa-duct-health","cvi:epa-no-biocide-liner","cvi:epa-baq-guide"]},{"id":"ascs-loto-electrical","title":"Lockout/tagout and job-site electrical safety","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1100,"intro":"OSHA's energy control rule (who locks, in what order, how to verify, who removes) and the electrical rules for cords and portable tools on a cleaning job. These are regulation facts with fixed answers.","mnemonic":"People Should Isolate, Lock, Release, Verify: Prepare, Shut down, Isolate, Lock or tag, Release stored energy, Verify with a start attempt. The hand that hung the lock takes it off. 1-4-7, lock it or go to heaven.","units":["ascs:osha-governs","ascs:loto-sequence","ascs:loto-authorized","ascs:loto-affected","ascs:loto-verify","ascs:loto-removal","ascs:osha-1910-147","ascs:gfci-tools","ascs:double-insulated","ascs:cord-handling","ascs:damaged-cord","ascs:extension-cord-type"]},{"id":"ascs-confined-spaces","title":"Confined spaces and permit entry","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1110,"intro":"When an air handler or plenum is a confined space, what makes it permit-required, the atmospheric limits, the test order, and what the attendant does. Walk-in AHUs make this core ASCS safety content.","mnemonic":"Spaces before Switches: .146 is Spaces, .147 is Switches. Oxygen window 19.5 to 23.5 (normal air 20.9 sits inside it); flammables trip at one tenth (10 percent) of the LFL. Test O, C, T: Oxygen, Combustibles, Toxics.","units":["ascs:confined-space-def","ascs:confined-not-permit","ascs:permit-space-triggers","ascs:entrapment-config","ascs:o2-deficient","ascs:o2-enriched","ascs:flammable-10-lfl","ascs:test-order","ascs:attendant-duty","ascs:attendant-rescue","ascs:permit-flow","ascs:osha-1910-146"]},{"id":"ascs-respirators-sds","title":"Respirators and Safety Data Sheets","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1120,"intro":"When OSHA requires respirators, how they are selected, the program steps before a tight-fitting respirator is worn, and what an SDS gives the crew. Distractors swap the order and the intervals.","mnemonic":"Engineering first, respirator second. Doctor, fit, seal: medical evaluation, then fit test (yearly), then user seal check (every donning). APF ladder for air-purifying masks: quarter 5, half 10, full 50. 1-3-4, breathe some more; 1200 is HazCom, 16 SDS sections.","units":["ascs:engineering-first","ascs:resp-hazard-selection","ascs:resp-program","ascs:resp-order","ascs:facial-hair","ascs:apf-half-mask","ascs:sds-content","ascs:osha-1910-134","ascs:osha-1910-1200"]},{"id":"ascs-hazmat-plenums","title":"Asbestos, lead, mold guidance and plenum risk","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1130,"intro":"What to do when you meet suspect asbestos or old paint, the dates that trigger OSHA and EPA presumptions, the mold guidance documents on the reference list, and NADCA's risk management guidance for open air plenums.","mnemonic":"Lead 1978, asbestos 1980: lead comes first, asbestos two years later. Presume, stop, notify: you never sample it, bag it or wet it. RRP minor repair: 6 inside per room, 20 outside.","units":["ascs:risk-starts-hazards","ascs:exposure-effects","ascs:suspect-acm-action","ascs:acm-licensed-only","ascs:pacm-1980","ascs:osha-asbestos-lead-map","ascs:plenum-hazmat","ascs:lead-presume","ascs:rrp-scope","ascs:rrp-minor-repair","ascs:osha-mold-guide","ascs:epa-mold-guide","ascs:plenum-rmp","ascs:rmp-contents","ascs:plenum-training"]},{"id":"ascs-std-fire-duct","title":"Fire, UL and duct construction documents","dom":"Standards","order":1150,"intro":"Which NFPA, UL, ASTM, SMACNA and NAIMA document governs fire safety, closures, surface burning and duct construction. Questions give a title and ask for the designator, or the reverse.","mnemonic":"90A = Air-conditioning in big buildings; 90B = Bungalows (one- and two-family homes, or any space of 25,000 cubic feet or less); 96 = cooking. UL 181 = the duct, 181A = rigid closure (A for Angular), 181B = flexible closure (B for Bendy). E84 = UL 723 = old NFPA 255: one burn test, three names.","units":["ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety","ascs:nfpa-90b","ascs:nfpa-90a-90b-split","ascs:nfpa-96","ascs:surface-burning-tests","ascs:ul-181","ascs:ul-181a-181b","ascs:smacna-006","ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels","ascs:naima-ah116","ascs:astm-c1071","ascs:nfpa-org","ascs:smacna-org"]},{"id":"ascs-std-reference-docs","title":"Ventilation, insulation and reference documents","dom":"Standards","order":1160,"intro":"The ASHRAE ventilation standards, the NAIMA and NADCA documents, and the other references behind ACR, plus the acronyms the candidate guide expects you to know.","mnemonic":"ASHRAE by number: 62.1 is everyone else (commercial), 62.2 is home (residential), 170 is the hospital, 180 keeps it running (maintenance). NAIMA: AH116 builds, AH122 cleans, AH110 breathes (IEQ). NADCA: 03 pulls (vacuum testing), 05 opens (service openings).","units":["ascs:ashrae-62-1","ascs:ashrae-62-2","ascs:ashrae-170","ascs:naima-ah122","ascs:naima-ah110","ascs:nadca-std-05","ascs:nadca-std-03","ascs:nadca-position-paper","ascs:nmam-5th","ascs:nyc-mold-guidelines","ascs:prelim-determination-s520","ascs:acca-org","ascs:iep-acronym","ascs:mua-acronym"]},{"id":"ascs-std-nadca-law","title":"NADCA, codes and the law","dom":"Standards","order":1180,"intro":"How ACR relates to codes, contracts and regulators, what EPA does and does not do, and the NADCA facts about certification and membership that the exam tests.","mnemonic":"Code beats standard, contract makes a standard binding, conflicts go back to the specifier. EPA registers products; it never certifies cleaners. NADCA timeline: 1989 founded on source removal, 1995 first ASCS, renew every year by June 30.","units":["ascs:acr-consensus","ascs:code-over-standard","ascs:spec-conflict","ascs:epa-role","ascs:chemical-governing-set","ascs:hepa-type","ascs:nadca-founded","ascs:certifies-individuals","ascs:ascs-renewal","ascs:membership-ascs","ascs:ascs-roles"]},{"id":"ascs-p-inspection-findings","title":"What inspection findings point to","dom":"Assessments","order":1200,"intro":"Field clues an inspector reads and what each one means: black fallout, filter bypass, leaking zone dampers, wet drain pans, growth on liner and suspect asbestos. Scenario questions test the root cause and the next step.","units":["ascs:p-mechanical-plans-walkthrough","ascs:p-black-fallout-liner","ascs:p-filter-bypass","ascs:p-multizone-damper-leak","ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause","ascs:p-liner-growth-remediation","ascs:p-suspect-acm-scope"],"mnemonic":"Clues point to causes: black fallout to shedding liner, a slimy pan to the drain line, trap or slope, leaky zone dampers to mixed hot and cold decks, and bypass beats any MERV. Only two findings change the job: visible growth on porous liner makes it remediation, even at 40 sq ft, and suspect asbestos stops the work."},{"id":"ascs-p-scoping-closeout","title":"Scoping, access and closeout records","dom":"Assessments","order":1210,"intro":"How the scope is set from what you find, how to handle missing access and customer-limited scopes, and what the closeout record has to prove. These are judgment questions with one defensible answer.","units":["ascs:p-inspect-before-quote","ascs:p-scope-by-component","ascs:p-whole-system-recontamination","ascs:p-limited-scope-documented","ascs:p-create-access","ascs:p-representative-sampling","ascs:p-reclean-visual-failure","ascs:p-closeout-documentation"],"mnemonic":"No guessing, no silent skipping, no dressing up. Inspect before you quote and scope each component by its own condition; create access rather than skip a section, and document a customer-limited scope as limited; never cherry-pick sample spots, test past a failed visual, or treat an invoice as the closeout record."},{"id":"ascs-p-open-air-plenums","title":"Open air plenums (NADCA white paper)","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":1220,"intro":"ACR 2025 leaves non-ducted open air plenums out of scope, so NADCA's white paper guides them when they are contracted. The exam tests how to spot one, what is inside, the cleaning Types and the minimum cleanliness level.","mnemonic":"Return plenum clues: two Transfers (duct and grille), a Fire damper, a Louvered door. Then keep the numbers apart: Type cleans, Method checks, Level contains.","units":["ascs:p-oap-return-indicators","ascs:p-oap-ceiling-tiles","ascs:p-oap-unfiltered-air","ascs:p-oap-non-hvac-contents","ascs:p-oap-controls-basis","ascs:p-oap-iep-consult","ascs:p-oap-type1-dry","ascs:p-oap-type2-wet","ascs:p-oap-min-cleanliness","ascs:p-type-method-level","ascs:p-oap-types","ascs:p-oap-accidental"]},{"id":"ascs-p-liner-flex-coatings","title":"Liner, flex duct and failed coatings","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":1230,"intro":"When porous liner is cleaned in place, left alone or replaced, plus torn flex cores, failed coatings and defects that keep reloading a duct. The answers turn on condition, not on age or dust load.","mnemonic":"Wet or Growth: take it out. Damaged: resurface if it still bonds, replace if not. Dust alone: clean it in place.","units":["ascs:p-liner-clean-in-place","ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers","ascs:p-liner-water-damaged","ascs:p-liner-fuzzing-ok","ascs:p-liner-soft-tools","ascs:p-liner-mechanical-agitation","ascs:p-flex-torn-core","ascs:p-failed-coating","ascs:p-replace-when-failed","ascs:p-fix-contamination-source"]},{"id":"ascs-p-chemical-products","title":"Antimicrobials and the label","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":1240,"intro":"What NADCA's chemical position paper and EPA say about antimicrobials in HVAC systems. The label is the law, consent never overrides it, and antimicrobials only ever follow cleaning.","mnemonic":"Clean first, label always, consent never overrides.","units":["ascs:p-chem-epa-registration","ascs:p-chem-label-is-law","ascs:p-chem-label-surfaces","ascs:p-epa-no-fiberglass-biocide","ascs:p-chem-after-cleaning","ascs:p-chem-not-needed","ascs:p-chem-client-release","ascs:p-chem-applicator-license"]},{"id":"ascs-p-cleaning-practice","title":"Field cleaning practice","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":1250,"intro":"Practical calls on the job beyond what ACR spells out: cleaning order, fire dampers, multizone dampers, coil direction, filters, access doors and fire-rated closures.","units":["ascs:p-no-fixed-order","ascs:p-fire-damper-midrun","ascs:p-multizone-dampers","ascs:p-coil-leaving-side","ascs:p-filters-at-completion","ascs:p-access-doors-airtight","ascs:p-fire-rated-closure"],"mnemonic":"Leave it working as designed: fire dampers cleaned from both sides and left in service, zone dampers positioned rather than disconnected, new filters of the design size and efficiency, doors airtight, fire-rated openings closed to the same rating. Only the coil goes backwards, from the leaving-air side. No order is mandated; just never recontaminate what is already clean."},{"id":"ascs-p-negative-air-safety","title":"Negative air, occupied spaces and job safety","dom":"Cleaning & Restoration","order":1260,"intro":"Troubleshooting negative pressure, where exhaust may go, protecting occupants in offices and hospitals, and the electrical and lockout basics for working in and around ducts.","units":["ascs:p-nam-not-holding","ascs:p-pressure-lost-stop","ascs:p-exhaust-no-reentry","ascs:p-mech-room-is-indoors","ascs:p-fuel-equipment-co","ascs:p-occupied-building-scope","ascs:p-healthcare-containment-first","ascs:p-healthcare-barriers-purpose","ascs:p-power-tools-in-duct","ascs:p-loto-before-contact"],"mnemonic":"Seal before you add, stop when it slips, contain before you cut: check for unsealed openings and a loaded HEPA filter before adding machines, stop disturbing work if negative pressure is lost, and contain a healthcare space before the first opening. A mechanical room is indoors, non-HEPA discharge goes where it cannot re-enter, and fuel-powered equipment stays away from intakes."},{"id":"cvi-osha-access","title":"OSHA: confined spaces, falls, ladders and power lines","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1310,"intro":"The OSHA numbers for getting to the equipment safely: permit space entry, fall protection triggers, low-slope roofs, ladders and overhead lines.","mnemonic":"4 in the plant, 6 on the site, 19 inches needs a ladder. Roof edge: under 6 conventional, 6 to 15 designated area, 15 plus a work rule. Ladder: 3 feet past the landing, 1 out for every 4 up. Power lines: 10 feet.","units":["cvi:cs-entry","cvi:fall-gi-4","cvi:fall-cons-6","cvi:break-19","cvi:fixed-ladder-24","cvi:roof-under-6","cvi:roof-6-15","cvi:roof-15","cvi:prework-exempt","cvi:ladder-36","cvi:ladder-fixed","cvi:ladder-4to1","cvi:powerline-10","cvi:ladder-nonconductive"]},{"id":"cvi-osha-health","title":"OSHA: lockout, respirators, HazCom, heat and noise","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1320,"intro":"Energy control, respirator fit testing, SDS sections, heat illness first aid and noise limits that apply to inspectors and their crews.","mnemonic":"Lock the disconnect, not the thermostat. Fit test: first use, new facepiece, every year. SDS 8 is the gear section. Noise: 85 starts the program, 90 starts the controls.","units":["cvi:loto-inspecting","cvi:loto-control-circuit","cvi:fit-test","cvi:seal-check","cvi:sds-8","cvi:sds-2-4-7","cvi:heat-stroke","cvi:noise-85","cvi:noise-90"]},{"id":"cvi-hazmat-bio","title":"Asbestos, lead and biological hazards","dom":"Health & Safety","order":1330,"intro":"Presumed asbestos, owner surveys, NESHAP notice, lead RRP limits, and the droppings and water hazards found in HVAC systems. Inspectors must spot these before anything is cut or disturbed.","mnemonic":"PACM: TSI and surfacing, built by 1980. RRP: 6 in, 20 out, pre-1978 housing. Birds and bats = histoplasmosis (wet it), rodents = hantavirus (soak it 5 minutes), warm standing water = Legionella.","units":["cvi:owner-acm-survey","cvi:pacm","cvi:tsi-breeching","cvi:neshap-notice","cvi:neshap-thresholds","cvi:rrp-scope","cvi:rrp-minor","cvi:rrp-demolition","cvi:rrp-30-day","cvi:rrp-firm","cvi:histo-source","cvi:histo-wet","cvi:hanta-source","cvi:rodent-cleanup","cvi:rodent-pros"]}],"sequences":[{"id":"seq-acr-project-flow","title":"ACR 2025 project flow, first inspection to final inspection","steps":["Inspect the system before the project starts","Document and evaluate findings in the system component assessment","Give a written work plan before any cleaning starts","Mechanically clean the components","Verify each component right after cleaning, before the system runs","Inspect the system again after the project"],"src":"ACR 2025 §1.1, §1.10, §2.0, §4, §5.1"},{"id":"seq-acr-containment-levels","title":"Containment levels, lowest to highest: what each level adds","steps":["Negative pressure, protective coverings, cross-contamination control","Temporary barriers, two-layer 6-mil floor, 4 ACH scrubbing, manometer","Single-chamber decon unit, continuous pressure monitoring, particulate baseline","Second decon chamber, constant recording pressure monitor with alarm"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.9 to 3.12"},{"id":"seq-acr-pressure-instruments","title":"Negative pressure instrument rule as containment level rises","steps":["Validate negative pressure with a manometer or airflow measuring device","Monitor negative pressure continuously with an instrument that detects loss","Record pressurization constantly, with an appropriate alarm"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.10.6, 3.11.3, 3.12.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-level3-particulate","title":"Level 3 particulate monitoring and the stop-work rule","steps":["Measure background total particulate before containment set-up","Monitor total particulate in real time during the work","Stop work when levels rise above background","Correct the cause and let levels return to background","Restart the work"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.11.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-equipment-project","title":"Contractor equipment through a project","steps":["Clean and inspect equipment before it enters the work site","Verify vacuum equipment on site before work starts (recommended)","Clean or seal equipment before relocating it within the building","Clean or seal equipment before it leaves the building"],"src":"ACR 2025 §3.1.1 to 3.1.4"},{"id":"seq-acr-coil-path","title":"Coil cleaning path when Type 1 is tried first","steps":["Visually inspect the coil and the drain pan","Clean with Type 1 dry methods, negative air running, coil isolated","Inspect the coil after Type 1","Debris remains or coil impacted: clean with Type 2 wet methods","Inspect the coil after Type 2","Debris still remains: repeat the process","Type 2 cannot remove the debris: replacement may be necessary"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.1 to 4.11.5"},{"id":"seq-acr-drain-pan","title":"Drain pan handling during Type 2 coil cleaning","steps":["Inspect the drain pan and test its drainage","Clean and flush the drain pan and drain line","Inspect the drain pan and test its drainage again"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.4.1"},{"id":"seq-acr-electric-coil","title":"Wet cleaning an electric resistance coil","steps":["De-energize the power source and apply lockout/tagout","Wash with a non-corrosive detergent only","Rinse the coil free of chemicals","Dry the coil thoroughly","Return the coil to an energized state"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.11.8"},{"id":"seq-acr-dampers","title":"Handling dampers and air-directional devices","steps":["Mark the damper position","Do the cleaning work","Return the damper to its marked position"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.8"},{"id":"seq-acr-restoration-order","title":"Order of operations before a surface treatment","steps":["Mechanically clean: agitate, then capture with vacuum collection","Confirm clean with a PASS on the specified cleanliness verification","Apply the surface treatment, which must meet UL 181"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.3, 4.14, 4.15"},{"id":"seq-acr-fibrous-glass","title":"Restoring damaged fibrous glass","steps":["Mechanically clean the fibrous glass per Section 4.7","Assess whether the cleaned surface gives a strong bondable surface","Resurface it, or replace it if it cannot be restored"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.17 to 4.19"},{"id":"seq-acr-insulation-replacement","title":"Replacing insulation removed for microbial growth","steps":["Remove the porous material with Condition 3 growth","Mechanically surface clean and scrape the metal clean","Bring the base surface to Condition 1, free of loose debris","Install replacement insulation to original spec, per manufacturer instructions"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.16, 4.20"},{"id":"seq-acr-table2-velocities","title":"Table 2 contaminant classes, lowest to highest duct velocity","steps":["Vapors, gases, smoke","Fumes and metal smokes, such as welding","Fine light dust, such as cotton lint","Dry dusts and powders, such as soap dust","Average industrial dust, such as grinding dust","Heavy dusts, such as wet sawdust","Heavy or moist dusts, such as moist cement dust"],"src":"ACR 2025 Table 2"},{"id":"seq-acr-verification-escalation","title":"Verification escalation, from cleaning to system start-up","steps":["Clean the component","Verify immediately with Method 1, visual inspection","Method 1 inconclusive or disputed: use Method 2 or Method 3","Method 2 disputed: Method 3 decides if non-porous; porous stops at Method 2","Put the system back into operation after verification"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.1, §5.2.1, §5.3.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-method2-procedure","title":"Method 2 Surface Comparison Test procedure","steps":["Note the visible characteristics of the test area before vacuuming","Pass the HEPA contact vacuum brush over the area four times","Compare the visible characteristics after vacuuming","No significant visible difference: the surface is clean","Inconclusive or disputed on a non-porous surface: Method 3 decides"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.3"},{"id":"seq-acr-method3-sampling","title":"Method 3 NADCA Vacuum Test sampling steps","steps":["Tape the template flat on the dry surface, air handler off","Remove the protective plugs from the new cassette","Attach the cassette outlet end to the vacuum pump tubing","Calibrate the flow to 17 L/min, plus or minus 2","Vacuum each slot twice, once each direction, at 5 cm/s or less","Turn the pump off and put the plugs back","Label the cassette and record the sampled surface area","Weigh per NIOSH 0500 and compare with 0.75 mg/100 cm2"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.4.2, §5.5"},{"id":"seq-acr-method3-airflow","title":"How Method 3 moves particulate onto the filter","steps":["Template holds the cassette above the sampled surface","Air accelerates through the narrow gap between template and surface","Increased velocity dislodges latent particulate onto the filter media","Cassette is prepared and weighed for total debris","Net weight is compared with 0.75 mg/100 cm2"],"src":"ACR 2025 §5.4, §5.5"},{"id":"seq-ascs-lockout-tagout","title":"Applying lockout/tagout before servicing an air handler","steps":["Learn the type, size and hazards of every energy source","Shut the equipment down in an orderly way","Operate the isolating devices to cut off each energy source","Apply a lock or tag to each isolating device","Relieve, disconnect or restrain stored energy","Try a normal start to verify it will not run","Return the controls to off, then begin work"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.147(d); Appendix A"},{"id":"seq-ascs-permit-entry","title":"Permit-required confined space entry, start to finish","steps":["Identify and evaluate the hazards of the permit space","Isolate the space and ventilate to control atmospheric hazards","Test the atmosphere: oxygen, then combustibles, then toxics","Record the preparations and test results on the entry permit","Entry supervisor signs the permit; post it at the portal","Attendant takes station outside, then entrants go in","Supervisor cancels the permit when the work is complete"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d), (e), (f)"},{"id":"seq-ascs-atmos-test","title":"Atmospheric testing order before confined space entry","steps":["Test for oxygen","Test for combustible gases and vapors","Test for toxic gases and vapors"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.146(d)(5)(iii)"},{"id":"seq-ascs-respirator","title":"Putting a worker in a required tight-fitting respirator","steps":["Select the respirator from the hazard assessment","Complete the medical evaluation","Fit test with the same make, model, style and size","Perform a user seal check every time it is put on"],"src":"29 CFR 1910.134(d), (e), (f), (g)"},{"id":"seq-ascs-p-source-removal","title":"Source removal on a duct section","steps":["Establish continuous negative pressure and vacuum collection","Agitate surfaces to dislodge debris into the collection stream","Verify cleanliness immediately after cleaning","Return the system to operation"],"src":"ACR 2025 §4.1, §4.3, §5.1"},{"id":"seq-ascs-p-job-flow","title":"A typical commercial cleaning job, start to finish","steps":["Inspect the site in person for access, layout and conditions","Assess and document conditions, then define the scope","Deliver the written work plan before any cleaning","Set up containment and controls before cutting any opening","Clean by agitation and vacuum collection under negative pressure","Verify cleanliness before the system operates","Close up, install correct filters, return the system to service","Deliver closeout documentation with the verification results"],"src":"ACR 2025 §1.1, §1.10, §2.0, §4.1, §4.3, §5.1, §5.6; industry practice (NADCA training)"},{"id":"seq-cvi-s590-assessment","title":"ANSI/IICRC S590 post-loss HVAC assessment","steps":["Assess the HVAC system after the water, fire, or mold loss","Sort each internal surface into impacted or non-impacted","Write the assessment report and Restoration Work Plan (RWP)","Remediation contractor carries out the Restoration Work Plan"],"src":"ANSI/IICRC S590-2023 (IICRC; C&R Magazine summary)"},{"id":"seq-cvi-fusible-damper-test","title":"NFPA 80 fusible-link fire damper test","steps":["Confirm the fire damper is fully open","Remove or release the fusible link","Confirm the damper closes fully on its own","Confirm it latches, if designed to latch","Reset the damper and reinstall the link"],"src":"NFPA 80-2019 §19.5.2.2"},{"id":"seq-cvi-damper-code-chain","title":"Life Safety Code reference chain to damper testing","steps":["NFPA 101 sends HVAC ductwork to NFPA 90A, or 90B as applicable","NFPA 90A sends fire dampers to NFPA 80 and smoke dampers to NFPA 105","NFPA 80 and NFPA 105 set the test and inspection schedule"],"src":"NFPA 101 (2012) §9.2.1; NFPA 90A (2012) §5.4.8"},{"id":"seq-cvi-rodent-cleanup","title":"CDC cleanup of rodent droppings and nests","steps":["Spray droppings and nest with bleach or EPA-registered disinfectant until very wet","Let the material soak about 5 minutes","Wipe it up with paper towels"],"src":"CDC rodent clean-up guidance"},{"id":"seq-ahu-airflow","title":"Air path through a typical draw-through air handler","src":"HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Return air travels from the rooms back toward the AHU","Return and outdoor air blend in the mixing box","Filters clean the mixed air ahead of the coils","Preheat coil, if present, warms cold mixed air","Cooling coil cools and dehumidifies the air","Supply fan pulls air through the coils","Supply duct carries air to terminals and diffusers"]},{"id":"seq-vav-zone-satisfies","title":"VAV reheat box response as a zone's cooling load falls","src":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide; HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Zone is warm: box passes maximum cooling airflow","Cooling load falls: damper throttles the primary air","Zone reaches setpoint: damper sits at minimum airflow","Zone keeps cooling below setpoint: reheat coil turns on at minimum"]},{"id":"seq-parallel-box-heating","title":"Parallel fan-powered box going from cooling to heating","src":"Titus engineering guide, terminal units","steps":["Cooling: box fan off, primary air damper modulates","Load drops: primary air throttles to minimum","Heating call: box fan starts, pulling warm plenum air","More heat needed: reheat coil energizes"]},{"id":"seq-heat-pump-defrost","title":"Heat pump defrost cycle","src":"Carrier, What Is Auxiliary Heat; DOE Energy Saver, Air-Source Heat Pumps","steps":["Frost builds on the outdoor coil during heating","Reversing valve shifts to cooling mode and the outdoor fan stops","Hot gas melts frost while aux strips warm the supply air","Frost cleared: system returns to heating mode"]},{"id":"seq-refrigeration-cycle","title":"Refrigerant path through the cooling cycle, starting at the compressor","src":"HVAC fundamentals","steps":["Compressor squeezes low-pressure vapor into hot high-pressure gas","Condenser rejects heat and the gas turns to liquid","Metering device drops the pressure into a cold mist","Evaporator boils the refrigerant, absorbing heat from the air"]},{"id":"seq-hvac-read-mech-sheet","title":"Reading a mechanical drawing set","steps":["Read the legend, abbreviations and general notes on the M-0 sheet","Trace ducts, tags and devices on the M-1 floor plans","Use M-3 sections for vertical runs through shafts and floors","Check M-5 details for enlarged views of specific connections","Look up each plan tag's design data on the M-6 schedules"],"src":"NCS/UDS Module 1; drawing practice"},{"id":"seq-hvac-pitot-traverse","title":"Pitot tube duct traverse for airflow","steps":["Choose a straight duct section away from elbows and fittings","Lay out equal-area or log-Tchebycheff points across the duct","Point the pitot upstream, tip and side ports across one manometer","Read velocity pressure at every traverse point","Convert each reading to fpm with 4005 times root VP","Average the point velocities","Multiply average velocity by duct area in square feet for CFM"],"src":"TSI AF-106; TSI-107"},{"id":"seq-hvac-acca-manuals","title":"ACCA residential design order","steps":["Manual J: calculate heating and cooling loads","Manual S: select equipment to match the loads","Manual D: design and size the duct system","Manual T: select registers and grilles"],"src":"ACCA Manuals J, S, D, T"},{"id":"seq-hvac-oa-fraction","title":"Checking outdoor air with mixed-air temperatures","steps":["Read OAT, RAT and MAT with the unit running steady","Confirm OAT and RAT differ by more than about 5 F","Compute fraction = (MAT - RAT) / (OAT - RAT)","Multiply the fraction by supply airflow for outdoor air CFM","Compare with the scheduled minimum and the damper command"],"src":"PNNL-29078 §9.2, §9.4.1"}],"questions":[{"id":"ascs-0000","stem":"A homeowner asks whether cleaning will lower the power bill. Best answer?","options":["Restoring airflow and coil heat transfer can improve efficiency, but savings vary","EPA documents an average fifteen percent reduction in energy consumption","There is no energy benefit; the value of cleaning is strictly air quality","Cleaning typically saves twenty to thirty percent on the utility bill"],"expl":"Defensible and honest. Guaranteed percentages are unsupportable and EPA publishes no such average.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:energy-claim-honest","ascs:epa-energy-evidence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0001","stem":"Which is a documented consequence of a fouled cooling coil?","options":["Higher pressure drop that cuts airflow and capacity","Higher coil airflow at a constant fan speed","Better heat transfer across the fouled fins","Lower pressure drop across the coil face"],"expl":"Fouling raises pressure drop, cuts airflow, and cuts capacity. It does not improve performance in any mode.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:fouled-coil"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0002","stem":"What is the most defensible claim about duct cleaning and occupant health?","options":["It removes contaminants from the system","It has no demonstrated relationship to health","It eliminates airborne mold in the building","It cures allergy and asthma symptoms"],"expl":"You can claim what you actually do. Curative health claims are unsupportable; so is claiming no relationship at all.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:health-claim","ascs:epa-health-evidence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0003","stem":"How is EPA's published position on routine duct cleaning best described?","options":["Recommended as needed, when specific conditions exist","Recommended annually for residential and commercial systems","Required before any coil, blower, or equipment replacement","Recommended on a fixed schedule set by system age"],"expl":"EPA does not recommend routine cleaning on a schedule; it points to conditions like growth, vermin, or heavy debris.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:epa-routine-position","ascs:epa-conditions"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0004","stem":"Which is the strongest documented benefit to present to a commercial building owner?","options":["Restored design airflow and system capacity","Reduced frequency of scheduled filter replacement","Extended manufacturer warranty coverage on the compressor","Guaranteed reduction in occupant sick days and absenteeism"],"expl":"Airflow and capacity restoration is measurable and defensible. The others are unsupported or not yours to promise.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:strongest-benefit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0005","stem":"A restaurant owner claims cleaning will solve a persistent odor. What is the accurate response?","options":["Cleaning removes odor sources within the system","Cleaning eliminates all building odors regardless of their origin","Odor complaints are unrelated to the condition of HVAC systems","Odor always requires an EPA-registered antimicrobial application"],"expl":"Scope it to the system. Odor sourced elsewhere in the building will survive a perfect duct cleaning.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:odor-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0006","stem":"Which is a legitimate reason to recommend cleaning?","options":["Substantial visible debris is seen in the ducts on inspection","The occupant requests improved indoor air quality","The system has not been cleaned in over two years","A coil replacement is scheduled for next month"],"expl":"Condition drives the recommendation. Elapsed time and general requests are not conditions.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:condition-drives-recommendation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0007","stem":"Fire risk and HVAC cleaning. Which is correct?","options":["Accumulated lint and debris are a fuel load","Fire risk applies only to commercial kitchens","Cleaning eliminates fire risk in dryer exhaust","Duct cleaning is a recognized fire code requirement"],"expl":"Debris is fuel. That is the honest framing. Cleaning reduces risk, it does not eliminate it, and it is not a code mandate.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:fire-fuel-load"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0008","stem":"An owner asks for a guarantee that the system will stay clean for five years. What should you do?","options":["Explain that reloading depends on the filters, use, and site conditions","Decline the job rather than accept the long-term liability of a guarantee","Provide the guarantee, backed by annual reinspection at no charge","Provide the guarantee if a coating, sealant, or biocide is applied"],"expl":"Reloading is driven by variables you do not control. Coatings, sealants, and biocides do not create a five-year guarantee.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:no-stay-clean-guarantee"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0009","stem":"Which correctly pairs a system condition with its consequence?","options":["Fouled blower wheel, reduced airflow","Restricted return, increased supply airflow","Higher MERV filter, increased airflow","Clean coil, increased pressure drop"],"expl":"Debris on the wheel destroys its aerodynamics and cuts delivered air. The other three are backwards.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:fouled-blower-wheel","ascs:resistance-pairs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0010","stem":"What is the strongest way to document value delivered to a customer?","options":["Before and after photographs of system components","A written estimate of projected annual energy savings","A certificate listing every product applied during the work","A signed statement attesting that the system is now clean"],"expl":"Visual documentation of actual condition change is evidence. Estimates and certificates are assertions.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:before-after-photos"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0011","stem":"NADCA's founding mission promoted which method as the only acceptable way to clean an HVAC system?","options":["Source removal","Chemical encapsulation","Sanitizing fogging","Filter replacement"],"expl":"NADCA was formed in 1989 to promote source removal, physically dislodging and removing contaminants, as the only acceptable cleaning method.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nadca-founded"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0012","stem":"An ASCS is qualified to serve as project manager for residential and commercial HVAC cleaning. What other role is an ASCS qualified for?","options":["Ventilation system mold remediator","Licensed HVAC installer","Licensed structural engineer","Certified industrial hygiene consultant"],"expl":"Per the candidate guide, an ASCS is qualified to serve as project manager for cleaning/restoration and as a ventilation system mold remediator.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ascs-roles"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0013","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, which is a primary benefit customers gain from proper HVAC system cleaning?","options":["Improved indoor air quality","Guaranteed removal of allergens","A permanent equipment warranty","An end to filter changes"],"expl":"Improved indoor air quality is a core benefit. NADCA and the ACR standard specifically avoid guaranteeing IAQ or energy outcomes.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:iaq-claim"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0014","stem":"How does removing accumulated particulate from HVAC components primarily improve system efficiency?","options":["Restoring designed airflow and heat transfer","Increasing the refrigerant charge for better cooling","Raising the thermostat setpoint","Eliminating the need for coils"],"expl":"Debris on coils and in ducts restricts airflow and insulates heat-transfer surfaces; removing it restores designed performance, which can save energy.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:fouling-mechanism"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0015","stem":"Which outcome does the ACR Standard explicitly refuse to warrant or guarantee will result from cleaning?","options":["Energy savings or correction of IAQ concerns","That the work will meet the ACR standard of care","That NADCA project documentation will be produced","That HEPA negative pressure will be used"],"expl":"The ACR disclaimer states NADCA does not warrant that adherence will result in energy savings or correct any IAQ-related concerns.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-16","acr:G-17"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0016","stem":"Cleaning HVAC components can extend equipment lifespan mainly because it ____.","options":["Reduces blower and compressor strain from restricted airflow","Adds a protective antimicrobial barrier coating","Replaces worn belts and bearings during the service","Upgrades the equipment to a higher SEER rating"],"expl":"Fouled coils and blocked ducts make blowers and compressors work harder; removing that load reduces wear and can extend service life.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:equipment-life"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0017","stem":"Managing customer expectations before a project is important because HVAC cleaning ____.","options":["Cannot correct defects outside the scope","Guarantees a specific measured IAQ number","Invariably eliminates odors permanently","Substitutes for filter maintenance"],"expl":"Cleaning removes non-adhered contaminants; it does not repair mechanical malfunctions or guarantee odor/IAQ numbers. Setting expectations avoids disputes.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:expectations-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0018","stem":"A newly installed duct system became contaminated with construction dust. What is this a recognized reason to do?","options":["Clean the HVAC components before placing them in service","Void the HVAC equipment manufacturer's warranty","Skip the HVAC inspection since the system is new","Replace the HVAC ductwork rather than clean"],"expl":"ACR 2025 lists construction dust in a newly installed component or duct as its own reason to clean, and recommends an inspection for cleanliness before the system operates (1.6). The 2021 edition required verification as clean; 2025 asks only for the inspection.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-16"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0019","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, which of the following is a recognized cause of HVAC system contamination?","options":["Bird, rodent, or insect infestation","Filter media sized, seated, and sealed","Supply and return airflows properly balanced","Visual inspection on a routine schedule"],"expl":"Infestation by birds, rodents, or insects and their byproducts is one of the ACR-listed conditions indicating a need to clean.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-6"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0020","stem":"The ACR Standard lists fire, smoke, and water damage as conditions that ____.","options":["May indicate a need for cleaning and restoration","May be addressed by deodorizing alone","May be referred out as beyond NADCA scope","May require full system replacement"],"expl":"Contamination from fire, smoke, and/or water damage is an ACR-listed condition indicating the components should be cleaned and restored.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0021","stem":"Which is NOT one of the ACR-listed conditions that indicate a need to clean HVAC components?","options":["The building recently changed ownership","Presence of particulate on components","Components discharging visible dirt into the space","Microbial contamination reaching Condition 2 or 3"],"expl":"A change in ownership is not a listed trigger. Particulate, visible discharge, and Condition 2/3 microbial contamination all are.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-1","acr:N-4","acr:N-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0022","stem":"Cleaning HVAC components can be pursued as a component to achieve which building certification?","options":["LEED green building certification","OSHA VPP Star recognition","UL component listing","ISO 9001 registration"],"expl":"The ACR lists achieving LEED Certification among the reasons a component may be cleaned.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-15"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0023","stem":"When particulate accumulation compromises component performance, the ACR treats this as ____.","options":["A recognized reason to clean","A cosmetic housekeeping issue","A reason to increase fan speed","Outside the standard"],"expl":"Compromised performance due to particulate accumulation is one of the listed conditions indicating a need for cleaning.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0024","stem":"The ACR addresses odors traced to HVAC components as ____.","options":["A condition that may warrant cleaning","Requiring an antimicrobial coating in each case","A condition that may be excluded from scope","A condition that may be cleared by filter changes"],"expl":"When components are determined to be a source of unacceptable odors, that is a listed condition indicating a need to clean.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0025","stem":"Proactively cleaning as part of an energy management program reflects which benefit theme?","options":["Energy savings and efficiency","Regulatory penalty avoidance","Energy code compliance reporting","Energy rebate documentation"],"expl":"Cleaning as part of a proactive energy management program ties to the energy-savings benefit of maintaining clean, unobstructed components.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-13","ascs:benefit-themes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0026","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, which statement about HVAC cleaning and indoor air quality is most defensible?","options":["Cleaning can support IAQ but is not a guarantee","Cleaning can guarantee healthy IAQ","Cleaning can show no measurable link to IAQ","IAQ can be unaffected by particulate loading"],"expl":"Cleaning supports improved IAQ by removing contaminant sources, but neither NADCA nor the ACR guarantees an IAQ result.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:iaq-claim"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0027","stem":"What does the ACR list a proactive indoor air quality management program as a reason to do?","options":["Clean HVAC components","Avoid the ACR-recommended inspections","Disable the HVAC filtration","Increase HVAC duct velocity"],"expl":"Cleaning as part of a proactive IAQ management program is one of the ACR's listed conditions.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-14"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0028","stem":"How are contaminants that can be removed by an agitation device and vacuum classified?","options":["Non-adhered substances","Adhered substances","Structural duct materials","Coatings"],"expl":"Non-adhered substances are those removable by cleaning/restoration; adhered substances (paint, mastic, stains) are not.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-6a","acr:D-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0029","stem":"Which is an example of an ADHERED substance that cleaning will not remove?","options":["Paint or mastic","Settled surface dust","Loose debris","Lint"],"expl":"Adhered substances include paint, mastic, sealant, and stains, materials not removed by standard cleaning procedures.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-8","acr:D-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0030","stem":"Deterioration of fibrous glass duct liner or duct board is listed as a condition that ____.","options":["Indicates a need to address it","Requires no corrective action","Improves airflow through the run","Falls outside the ACR's scope"],"expl":"Deterioration of fibrous glass liner, duct board, or other porous components is an ACR-listed condition indicating a need to clean.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0031","stem":"Which best describes 'particulate' as defined by the ACR Standard?","options":["A non-adhered substance that is removed by an agitation device","An adhered substance bonded to the duct surface","A structural component that carries duct loads","A sealant applied along the duct seams and joints"],"expl":"Particulate is any non-adhered substance in the system that can be removed using an agitation device.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0032","stem":"What is a 'contaminant' under the ACR Standard?","options":["Any substance not intended to be present","Solely microbial growth","Solely visible dust","Solely construction-related debris and dust"],"expl":"A contaminant is any substance not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0033","stem":"How is 'debris' defined under the ACR?","options":["Non-adhered, unintended substances","Adhered mastic bonded to the duct joints","Insulation banding, screws, and fasteners","Coil fins, tubes, headers, and drain pans"],"expl":"Debris is non-adhered substances not intended to be present within the HVAC system.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0034","stem":"Which is a defensible way to describe the energy benefit of coil cleaning to a customer?","options":["Better heat transfer can improve efficiency","It can cut the energy bill by exactly 30%","It can replace the need for a new unit","It can guarantee LEED energy points"],"expl":"Honest expectation-setting: clean coils restore heat transfer and airflow, which can improve efficiency, but specific savings are not guaranteed.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:energy-claim-honest","ascs:epa-energy-evidence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0035","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, when should an ASCS avoid promising a specific indoor air quality result?","options":["In all cases; IAQ outcomes are not guaranteed","On residential jobs, where IAQ varies most","On healthcare jobs, where IAQ is regulated","In no case; better IAQ can be promised"],"expl":"IAQ outcomes are influenced by many factors; the ACR explicitly declines to guarantee them, so an ASCS should not promise a specific result.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:iaq-claim"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0036","stem":"Construction, retrofit, and remodel activity is significant to HVAC cleaning because components ____.","options":["Often collect significant debris during construction","Stay free of significant construction debris","Are typically self-cleaning during construction","Cannot be inspected during construction"],"expl":"HVAC components often collect significant debris during construction; the ACR recommends inspecting before the system operates.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0037","stem":"Which is a legitimate driver for HVAC cleaning tied to occupant comfort and health?","options":["Visible dirt discharging into the space","Lower thermostat setpoints year-round","Adding refrigerant to the circuit","Painting the exterior of the ductwork"],"expl":"Components discharging visible dirt/debris into the conditioned space is a listed condition and a comfort/health driver.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0038","stem":"The benefit of increased equipment lifespan is realized primarily on which components?","options":["The air handler and broader HVAC system","The thermostat and its HVAC controls","The exterior surface of the HVAC ductwork","The filters serving the HVAC equipment"],"expl":"Reducing fouling load benefits the air handling unit and HVAC system as a whole, supporting longer service life.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:equipment-life"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0039","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, which of these is a 'cause and type of contamination' an ASCS should understand?","options":["Construction dust and microbial growth","Refrigerant types and charge levels","Thermostat make and model number","Duct color and exterior finish"],"expl":"Understanding causes/types of contamination (construction particulate, microbial growth, infestation, fire/smoke/water) is part of the Benefits domain.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-8","acr:N-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0040","stem":"Why does NADCA emphasize source removal over sanitizing alone?","options":["Source removal eliminates the contaminant at its origin","Source removal needs no containment or HEPA equipment","Sanitizing is prohibited under federal pesticide law","Sanitizing alone meets the ACR visibly clean standard"],"expl":"Source removal eliminates contaminants at their origin, preventing re-entrainment; sanitizing without removal leaves the source in place.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:source-removal-why"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0041","stem":"A customer asks whether duct cleaning will 'fix' a broken blower motor. What is the best answer?","options":["No, mechanical repair is a separate scope of work","Yes, cleaning also repairs mechanical parts","Yes, if a coating is applied after the cleaning","Yes, in commercial buildings with service plans"],"expl":"Cleaning addresses contaminants, not mechanical repair. Repair/replacement of malfunctioning devices is outside the cleaning scope (managing expectations).","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:expectations-scope","acr:4.24.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0042","stem":"Which is the most accurate benefits statement for a proactive HVAC maintenance program?","options":["It supports IAQ, efficiency, and equipment life","It removes the need for HVAC servicing","It guarantees dust, mold, and odor removal","It ends the need for filter changes"],"expl":"Proactive cleaning as part of a maintenance/energy/IAQ plan supports air quality, efficiency, and equipment life, without absolute guarantees.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:benefit-themes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0043","stem":"A homeowner asks for a written guarantee that cleaning will eliminate allergy symptoms. Which response is most appropriate?","options":["Document conditions, define scope, avoid promising a result.","Guarantee relief when dust is visible, and note it in the scope.","Promise better air quality, if the supply, return, and AHU are included.","Guarantee relief after cleaning, if an antimicrobial is applied."],"expl":"Cleaning can address documented contamination, but a contractor should not guarantee a specific medical or IAQ outcome.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:allergy-response"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0044","stem":"Which finding provides the strongest direct basis for recommending cleaning of an HVAC component?","options":["The component is visibly discharging loose particulate into the space.","The duct surface has a cosmetic stain that is firmly adhered.","The customer reports the ducts have not been cleaned recently.","The component is older than its typical manufacturer warranty."],"expl":"A recommendation should be tied to inspection findings and actual conditions, not age or preference alone.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:condition-drives-recommendation"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0045","stem":"Which material is best classified as an adhered substance rather than removable particulate?","options":["Paint overspray bonded to the duct surface.","Rodent nesting material lying inside a branch duct.","Loose lint collected near the face of a return grille.","Dry construction dust resting on the duct surface."],"expl":"Paint, mastic, sealant, and stains are examples of adhered substances.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0046","stem":"Which sequence best describes source removal?","options":["Dislodge contamination, then capture it with vacuum collection","Raise airflow, loosen debris, discharge to registers","Coat the contamination, run the fan, verify visually","Mist with disinfectant, dwell, return to service"],"expl":"Source removal combines agitation with capture by vacuum collection equipment.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.3-c","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0047","stem":"A salesperson wants to advertise a fixed percentage of energy savings after every cleaning. What is the best correction?","options":["Describe potential benefits without promising a set figure.","Use the figure where cooling coils may be heavily loaded.","Use the figure when pre-clean static is documented.","Use the figure if the customer signs a written acknowledgment."],"expl":"Cleaning may restore performance, but a specific energy result should not be guaranteed.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:energy-claim-honest"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0048","stem":"Which situation most strongly supports further assessment and possible cleaning?","options":["An inspection confirms loose debris at the outlets.","Occupants dislike the look of a galvanized trunk.","The system ran six years without a cleaning project.","The owner plans to swap out the thermostats next cycle."],"expl":"Debris discharge plus confirmed loose material is a condition directly tied to cleaning need.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:condition-drives-recommendation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0049","stem":"After a smoke event, what is the best basis for deciding whether HVAC cleaning is warranted?","options":["Inspect, document the damage, then define the scope.","Replace the materials, note it, then decide on inspection.","Clean each component, since smoke means full cleaning.","Deodorize, run the system, then inspect if odor remains."],"expl":"Fire and smoke damage can justify cleaning, but the project scope should follow inspection and assessment.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.0-a","acr:1.10-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0050","stem":"Inspection finds deteriorated porous liner that cannot be restored without further damage. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","options":["The liner may need removal rather than cleaning.","Wet cleaning may be repeated until discoloration lifts.","It may remain in service if the metal duct is clean.","It may stay in service once an antimicrobial is applied."],"expl":"Deteriorated porous material that cannot be restored may require replacement.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.19-a","ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0051","stem":"A client requests antimicrobial fogging before any mechanical cleaning. Which response best follows NADCA principles?","options":["Use source removal first, chemicals for lawful need.","Fog first, so the cleaning needs less containment.","Fog first, if the client signs the work order.","Fog first, whenever microbial growth is suspected."],"expl":"Chemical application is not a substitute for source removal and must follow the label and applicable law.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-chem-after-cleaning","ascs:p-chem-not-needed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0052","stem":"A heavily loaded coil has an elevated pressure drop. Which post-cleaning benefit is most supportable?","options":["A possible return of airflow and heat transfer, as shown by measurement.","An end to occupant comfort complaints throughout the served zone.","A possible 20 percent utility saving, calculated from the coil's age.","An extension of equipment life equal to the years it has been in service."],"expl":"Performance may improve after effective cleaning, but fixed savings, comfort, or lifespan claims are not defensible.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:strongest-benefit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0053","stem":"New duct sections contain visible construction debris before startup. What is the most appropriate conclusion?","options":["Assess and clean components before operation","Coat the debris in place if the duct is undamaged","Leave it; new ductwork is exempt from cleanliness","Run the system so airflow carries it to the filters"],"expl":"Construction debris is a recognized reason for inspection and possible cleaning before operation.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-16"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0054","stem":"Which combination most strongly indicates a contamination condition rather than a cosmetic issue?","options":["Pest debris and visible discharge at a register.","Minor corrosion, faded labels, and an older serial.","Discoloration, intact mastic, and normal airflow.","Adhered paint, sealed panels, and a new thermostat."],"expl":"Pest debris, loose particulate, and debris discharge are direct contamination indicators.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:condition-drives-recommendation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0055","stem":"A system is included in a formal inspection and maintenance program. How should that affect the cleaning decision?","options":["Use the findings and program requirements to decide","Clean once the equipment passes its service life","Skip cleaning; the program replaces source removal","Clean at each inspection regardless of condition"],"expl":"A maintenance program can trigger assessment, but the cleaning decision still rests on conditions and requirements.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:maintenance-program-decision"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0056","stem":"Which proposal language best manages customer expectations?","options":["State the findings, the components in scope, the exclusions, and the verification.","State the price, the schedule, the crew size, and the start date.","State that odors, comfort complaints, and IAQ concerns will resolve.","State that dust will be sterilized, sealed, and kept from returning."],"expl":"Clear findings, scope, limits, and verification reduce overpromising and ambiguity.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:proposal-language"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0058","stem":"What is the key difference between Sick Building Syndrome (SBS) and Building-Related Illness (BRI)?","options":["SBS symptoms clear after leaving the building, while BRI has diagnosable medical causes","SBS has clinically diagnosable causes, while BRI symptoms clear after leaving the building","SBS applies to industrial buildings, while BRI applies to residential occupancies","SBS and BRI are interchangeable terms for the same set of occupant complaints"],"expl":"Sick Building Syndrome (SBS) describes non-specific health complaints that improve away from the building. Building-Related Illness (BRI) refers to specific diagnosable illnesses directly tied to building bioaerosols (like Legionella or hypersensitivity pneumonitis). E.g. Legionnaires' disease.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:sbs-vs-bri"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0059","stem":"Which contaminant class is a byproduct of incomplete combustion and commonly found in plenums near boilers or generators?","options":["Soot","Pollen","Fiberglass","Gypsum dust"],"expl":"Soot is a combustion byproduct that deposits in plenums and ductwork near combustion equipment and is listed among plenum contaminants.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:soot"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0060","stem":"Which of the following is a BIOLOGICAL contaminant rather than a particulate?","options":["Bacteria","Drywall dust","Fiberglass fragments","Soot"],"expl":"Bacteria, viruses, mold, and animal/insect byproducts are biological contaminants; drywall dust, fiberglass, and soot are particulate.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:biological-vs-particulate"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0061","stem":"How are particles small enough to remain suspended and reach the deep lung generally described?","options":["Respirable particulate","Settleable dust","Adhered substances","Bulk debris"],"expl":"Respirable particulate stays airborne and penetrates the lower respiratory tract, which is why HEPA filtration and containment matter during source removal.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:respirable-particulate"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0062","stem":"A customer asks whether cleaning will lower their power bill by a specific amount. Under the NADCA ACR Standard, what should the ASCS do?","options":["Explain savings vary and are not guaranteed","Guarantee a 20% reduction in cost","Decline to discuss energy performance","State that cleaning has no energy effect"],"expl":"The ACR declines to warrant energy savings. Explaining the mechanism honestly, without a guaranteed number, is the correct expectation management.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:energy-claim-honest"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0063","stem":"Under the NADCA ACR Standard, a homeowner reports allergy symptoms and wants cleaning to eliminate them. What should the ASCS do?","options":["Explain cleaning cannot guarantee a health outcome","Commit to measurable relief of the reported symptoms","Recommend an ozone generator as a finishing step","Decline the project as outside the ACR scope"],"expl":"Health outcomes are outside the ASCS scope. Describe what source removal does, avoid medical claims, and refer medical questions to a physician.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:epa-health-evidence","ascs:allergy-response"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0064","stem":"Where do VOCs entering an HVAC system most commonly originate?","options":["Solvents, adhesives, paints, coatings, and cleaning agents","Galvanized steel, zinc, rivets, hangers, and straps","Road sand, concrete dust, and masonry grit","Pollen grains, dust mites, and insect parts"],"expl":"Volatile organic compounds off-gas from solvents, adhesives, paints, coatings, and cleaning products, and are a recognized IAQ contaminant class.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:voc-sources"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0065","stem":"Why is insect and rodent infestation primarily an ACR-listed cleaning trigger?","options":["Nesting and byproducts contaminate the airstream","It voids the equipment warranty terms","It changes the duct pressure class","It requires refrigerant recovery"],"expl":"Birds, rodents, insects, and their byproducts are a listed condition; the droppings, carcasses, and nesting material contaminate the airstream and create hazards.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:infestation-contaminates"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0066","stem":"Lint accumulation in a dryer exhaust or laundry return is a cleaning trigger primarily tied to ____.","options":["Fire hazard risk","LEED credit eligibility","Refrigerant loss","Noise transfer"],"expl":"Components determined to be at risk for fire hazard are a listed condition; combustible lint loading is the classic example.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:fire-fuel-load"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0067","stem":"Which statement about condensation inside an open air plenum is accurate?","options":["Humid infiltration can cause condensation","It can be ignored because plenums are unconditioned","It can form in supply ducts but not in plenums","It cannot form where the return air is filtered"],"expl":"Plenums are not sealed, so humidity and unconditioned outside air infiltrate, creating condensation, odors, microbial growth, and other conditions requiring attention.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:plenum-infiltration"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0068","stem":"The most defensible way to describe HVAC cleaning's effect on equipment lifespan is that it ____.","options":["Reduces strain from restricted airflow and fouled heat transfer surfaces","Doubles the service life of a typical residential split system","Removes the need for scheduled preventive maintenance visits","Guarantees a twenty-year service life when done to the ACR"],"expl":"Removing the fouling load reduces blower and compressor strain. Frame it as supporting longer life, not as a guaranteed number.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:equipment-life"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0069","stem":"Construction particulate is treated differently from ordinary dust primarily because it ____.","options":["Includes gypsum, sawdust, silica, and other fine debris","Is microbial, rather than mineral, in nature","Resists vacuuming, brushing, air washing, and wiping","Falls under NFPA 90A rather than the ACR Standard"],"expl":"Construction and remodel work loads new systems with gypsum, sawdust, silica and other fine debris. ACR 2025 lists that contamination as its own reason to clean and recommends an inspection for cleanliness before the system operates (1.6).","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:construction-particulate"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0070","stem":"The ACR lists a proactive HVAC maintenance program per ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 as ____.","options":["A reason cleaning may be performed","A condition that may prohibit cleaning","A refrigerant requirement that may apply","A fire code violation that may be cited"],"expl":"Cleaning performed as part of an HVAC maintenance program as defined in Standard 180 is one of the ACR's listed conditions supporting cleaning.","domain":"Benefits","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-11"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0071","stem":"Which is defined as a non-porous HVAC component?","options":["Polymeric film lining flexible duct","Sound attenuated duct sections in the return","Poured concrete plenum walls below grade","Fibrous glass duct board with foil facing"],"expl":"Non-porous examples: sheet metal, aluminum foil, polymeric flex liner. Porous: liner, board, sound attenuated duct, wood, concrete.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0072","stem":"Which is defined as a porous HVAC component?","options":["Sound attenuated duct sections","Galvanized sheet metal","Polymeric flex duct liner","Aluminum foil facing"],"expl":"Porous: fibrous glass liner and board, sound attenuated duct, wood, concrete.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0073","stem":"What is an air filtration device that filters and recirculates air within a space called?","options":["An air scrubber","A negative air machine","A portable air mover","A portable contact vacuum"],"expl":"AFD definition: filters and recirculates = air scrubber; filters and creates negative pressure = negative air machine.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-30","acr:D-31"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0074","stem":"Per ACR, what is a negative air machine?","options":["A HEPA-filtered AFD that controls pressure, ducted outside or not","A non-HEPA fan unit ducted outside the building envelope","A HEPA air scrubber that recirculates air in the space","Any blower, HEPA or not, that exhausts air outdoors"],"expl":"Negative Air Machine: a HEPA-filtered AFD for collecting particulate and controlling pressure; may or may not be ducted outside.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-31","acr:D-32"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0075","stem":"Per ACR, what does 'airside surface' not include?","options":["Open air plenums or interstitial cavities","Turning vanes, reheat coils, and filter housings","Fan blades and housings","Return grilles, return ducts, and supply diffusers"],"expl":"Airside surface excludes open air plenums and interstitial cavities. The others are listed as HVAC components.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-59"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0076","stem":"Per ACR, to 'mechanically fasten' means to use ____.","options":["Screws, clamps, locks, or straps","Any UL 181A/B listed closure system","Welds, brazed seams, or solder joints","Mastic, tape, gaskets, or duct sealant"],"expl":"Mechanically fasten: screws, clamps, locks, or straps, contrasted with mastic or tape.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-56"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0077","stem":"What is a 'sandwich' duct access door?","options":["Two parts fastened together on both sides of the duct wall","A round door frame that spins into a round opening","A door and doorframe attached together by a hinge","A door and frame flush with the outside duct wall"],"expl":"Sandwich: two-part closure fastened together on both sides of the duct wall. Flush mount, spin door, hinged are the others.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-51"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0078","stem":"Per ACR, what does a 'contact vacuum' use?","options":["A nylon brush nozzle on the inlet hose","A HEPA filter cassette on a calibrated pump","A compressed-air skipper head","A rotary steel brush on a flexible drive shaft"],"expl":"Contact Vacuum: portable collection device with a nylon brush nozzle applied directly to the surface.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-35"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0079","stem":"Per ACR, what is an 'open air plenum'?","options":["Intended non-ducted air paths in building cavities and voids","A lined supply plenum attached directly to the AHU","A ducted return plenum built to SMACNA construction standards","A mixing box with an outside air intake damper"],"expl":"Open Air Plenum: intended non-ducted air pathways formed in building cavities, voids, and spaces outside the occupied zone.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-60"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0080","stem":"Per ACR's HVAC component definition, non-ducted open air plenums are ____.","options":["Not considered part of the HVAC system","Considered supply-side components of the system","Part of the system when lined","Considered return-side components of the system"],"expl":"HVAC System/Components: for purposes of this Standard, non-ducted open air plenums of all types are not part of the HVAC system.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-69"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0081","stem":"Which unit type does ACR list under 'cooling-heating unit'?","options":["Packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC)","Make-up air unit with direct gas-fired heater","Ventilating unit with means for ventilation","Central-station air-handling unit (AHU)"],"expl":"Cooling-Heating Unit examples: mini-splits, PTAC, window units, fan coil units, split systems.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-74"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0082","stem":"What is 'duct wrap'?","options":["Exterior duct insulation with sealed seams and joints","Rigid insulation board with a foil vapor facing","Interior fibrous glass liner used for sound control","The outer vapor jacket of flexible ductwork"],"expl":"Definition: insulation applied on the exterior of ducts with all seams and joints sealed for a continuous vapor barrier.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-wrap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0083","stem":"Pressure drop across a cooling coil is rising over time in a constant volume system. Direct effect?","options":["Airflow falls","Fan amp draw rises with airflow","Latent capacity increases","Supply temperature rises at constant load"],"expl":"Added resistance moves the operating point back on the fan curve. Less air, less capacity, possible coil freeze at low load.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-coil-loading","hvac:a-resistance-airflow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0084","stem":"A belt-driven fan is delivering low airflow. Which is checked first?","options":["Belt tension and sheave condition","Variable frequency drive programming","Motor winding resistance","Blade pitch angle"],"expl":"Slipping belts and worn sheaves are the common, cheap, mechanical cause. Check the simple thing first.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-belt-drive"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0085","stem":"By roughly how much does doubling fan speed in a given system change static pressure?","options":["Four times, following the square of speed","Two times, in direct ratio to speed","Eight times, with the cube of speed","No change in a fixed duct system"],"expl":"Fan laws: flow scales with speed, pressure with speed squared, power with speed cubed.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-law-pressure"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0086","stem":"What does a MERV rating describe about a filter?","options":["Its particle capture efficiency","Its rated airflow capacity","Its initial pressure drop at rated flow","Its service life before changeout"],"expl":"MERV is efficiency by particle size band. Higher MERV usually costs pressure drop, but that is a consequence, not the definition.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-merv-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0087","stem":"What does installing a much higher MERV filter than the system was designed for typically cause?","options":["Reduced airflow and capacity","No change if the filter fits the rack","Lower total external static pressure","Improved airflow through better media"],"expl":"Higher resistance, less air. Fitting the rack says nothing about the system's pressure budget.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-high-merv-effect"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0088","stem":"Which is measured to determine air velocity in a duct?","options":["Velocity pressure with a pitot traverse","Static pressure at a wall tap","Differential pressure across the filter bank","Total pressure at the fan outlet"],"expl":"Velocity pressure, traversed across the section, is what converts to FPM and then to CFM.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-traverse-why"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0089","stem":"What does a VAV box at minimum position with the space overcooling suggest?","options":["A failed damper or actuator","Excess airflow through the box","High supply temperature","Undersized ductwork upstream"],"expl":"At minimum position it should not be overcooling. Something is not closing.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-damper-fail"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0090","stem":"What will an economizer stuck at full outside air in winter most directly cause?","options":["Increased heating load","Reduced fan power","Reduced heating load","Added space humidity"],"expl":"You are conditioning outdoor air you did not need. Coil freeze is the follow-on risk.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-econ-stuck-open"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0091","stem":"In a constant volume system, what will closing supply dampers to balance zones do?","options":["Increase the system static pressure","Reduce the fan power in direct proportion","Increase the total system airflow","Have no effect on the fan's operation"],"expl":"You added resistance. Static rises and the fan rides back up its curve.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-system-resistance","hvac:a-resistance-airflow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0092","stem":"What is the primary function of a return air plenum?","options":["Convey air back to the air handler","Provide thermal storage","Equalize supply zone pressures","Filter particulate ahead of the coil"],"expl":"It is a conveyance path. Filtration happens at the filter bank, not by virtue of being a plenum.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-return-plenum"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0093","stem":"A direct-drive ECM blower is not reaching design CFM despite a clean filter and coil. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Duct static exceeds the motor's range","Belt slippage between the motor sheave and the blower","Incorrect blade pitch on the forward-curved wheel","Sheave misalignment causing drive losses at the shaft"],"expl":"ECMs hold torque against static up to a limit, then fall off. Belts and sheaves do not exist on direct drive.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ecm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0094","stem":"What components would an ASCS expect to find in a fan coil unit?","options":["Blower, coil and filter","Diffuser, damper and grille","Blower, valve and filter","Damper, compressor and evaporator coil"],"expl":"A fan coil unit contains a blower, a coil, and a filter. (NADCA official practice question.)","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0095","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, how is an air-handling unit (AHU) best defined?","options":["A packaged assembly that moves and may condition air","A passageway that may distribute air through a building","A portable scrubber that may filter workspace air","An access door that may be fitted to a duct"],"expl":"The ACR defines an AHU as a packaged assembly, usually connected to ductwork, that moves air and may also clean and condition it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-70"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0096","stem":"What do coils in an HVAC system do?","options":["Temper and/or dehumidify the air","Seal duct joints","Provide structural support for ducts","Filter particulate"],"expl":"Coils are heat exchangers that temper (heat/cool) and/or dehumidify the air handled by the system.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-coil-function"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0097","stem":"A VAV box is a component associated with which part of the system?","options":["Supply air distribution to the zones","The condensate drain and trap","The exterior duct wrap insulation","The fuel-powered backup generator"],"expl":"Variable Air Volume (VAV) boxes are supply-side terminal devices; supply duct inspections include VAV boxes and fan-powered terminal boxes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-box"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0098","stem":"Which of the following is a POROUS HVAC component?","options":["Fibrous glass duct liner","Galvanized sheet metal duct","Aluminum foil facing","Polymeric film flex-duct core"],"expl":"Porous components can be penetrated by water or air, fibrous glass duct liner/board, sound-attenuated duct, wood, concrete.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0099","stem":"Which of the following is a NON-POROUS HVAC component?","options":["Sheet metal duct","Fibrous glass duct board","Sound-attenuated duct","Wood plenum"],"expl":"Non-porous components (sheet metal, aluminum foil, polymeric film flex liner) cannot be penetrated by water or air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-galvanized-nonporous","hvac:a-porous-vs-nonporous"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0100","stem":"A 'duct liner' is typically made of what material and applied where?","options":["Fibrous glass inside a metal duct","Sheet metal fastened to the duct exterior","Rubber gasket material at the joints","Mastic compound applied at the seams"],"expl":"Duct liner is insulation, usually fibrous glass, applied to the inside of a metal duct for thermal retention and sound attenuation.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-liner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0101","stem":"'Duct wrap' differs from duct liner in that duct wrap is ____.","options":["Applied to the exterior as a vapor barrier","Applied to the interior airside surface","Installed as a coil section liner","Used as a mechanical fastening strip"],"expl":"Duct wrap is exterior insulation with all seams/joints sealed to provide a continuous vapor barrier.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-wrap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0102","stem":"How is a negative air machine best described?","options":["A HEPA-filtered AFD that controls workspace pressure","A non-HEPA exhaust blower vented indoors","An AFD that adds humidity to the workspace","A HEPA-rated coil cleaning chemical applicator"],"expl":"A negative air machine is a HEPA-filtered air filtration device that collects particulate and controls workspace pressure; may or may not be ducted outside.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-31"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0103","stem":"What is an 'air scrubber'?","options":["An AFD that recirculates air through HEPA within the space","An AFD that exhausts air to create negative pressure","An AFD that heats or cools the supply air stream","An AFD that dehumidifies air in the work area"],"expl":"An air scrubber is an AFD using HEPA filtration configured to re-circulate air within a defined space (contrast: negative air machine creates negative pressure).","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-30"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0104","stem":"What does a make-up air (MUA) unit do?","options":["Supplies tempered air to replace exhausted air","Recirculates return air within the occupied space","Dehumidifies air in a crawlspace or basement","Captures debris during duct cleaning work"],"expl":"A make-up air unit supplies tempered fresh air to replace air that is exhausted, using direct gas-fired, electric, or water heater sections.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mua","hvac:a-negative-building"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0105","stem":"Which components are part of the return side of an HVAC system?","options":["Return grilles, return ducts, and return plenums","Supply diffusers, VAV boxes, and reheat coils","Supply trunks, branch runs, and register boots","Outside-air louvers, hoods, and rain caps"],"expl":"Return grilles, return ducts and ducted return plenums carry air back to the AHU. ACR treats a non-ducted open air plenum as outside the HVAC system, which is why the key says return plenums and not ceiling cavities.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 definitions","units":["acr:D-63","acr:1.7.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0106","stem":"What do dampers and other air-directional mechanical devices do?","options":["Control or direct airflow","Filter particulate","Provide thermal insulation","Seal service openings"],"expl":"Dampers are air-directional devices controlling airflow; their position must be marked before cleaning and restored afterward.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dampers-balancing"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0107","stem":"Which is considered part of the HVAC system per the ACR definition?","options":["The condensate drain pans and mixing box","Adjacent building structural columns","Exterior grounds and site landscaping","Roof membrane and parapet flashing"],"expl":"The HVAC system includes AHU interior, mixing box, coil compartment, condensate drain pans, supply/return ducts, fans, etc. Non-ducted open air plenums are excluded.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mixing-box","hvac:s-drain-pan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0108","stem":"What does 'airside surface' refer to?","options":["The internal surface the airstream contacts","The outer surface of the duct wrap","The roof-mounted condenser coil face","The face of the wall thermostat"],"expl":"The airside surface is the internal surface of the system, ductwork, and components that air impacts while traveling through, excluding open air plenums.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-58"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0109","stem":"A reheat coil is typically found on which side of the system?","options":["Supply air distribution","Return solely","Exhaust solely","Outside the building envelope"],"expl":"Reheat coils are part of supply air distribution and are inspected with supply ducts, mixing boxes, and VAV/terminal boxes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-reheat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0110","stem":"Which describes a central-station air-handling unit?","options":["A factory-made encased fan assembly","A ductless mini-split wall unit","A portable air scrubber on a cart","A condensate removal pump unit"],"expl":"A central-station AHU is a factory-made encased assembly performing one or more functions of circulating, cleaning, conditioning, and mixing air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-71"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0111","stem":"A ductless mini-split, PTAC, or window unit falls under which ACR definition?","options":["Cooling-heating unit","Dedicated make-up air unit","Non-ducted open air plenum","Central-station air-handling unit"],"expl":"A cooling-heating unit includes means for cooling and heating and covers ductless mini-splits, PTACs, wall/window units, fan coil units, and split systems.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-74"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0112","stem":"What do sound attenuators in a duct system primarily do?","options":["Reduce noise carried by the duct","Increase duct air velocity to terminals","Filter growth from the duct airstream","Provide structural bracing for the duct"],"expl":"Sound attenuators (and sound-attenuated duct/liner) reduce noise; they are addressed in inspection manuals and can be porous.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-attenuator"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0113","stem":"A duct access door with cam locks mounts flush and extends into the duct, externally flush with the wall. What type is it?","options":["Flush Mount door","Surface Mount door","Spin-in door","Sandwich door"],"expl":"A Flush Mount access door and frame extends into the duct and is externally flush with the outside duct wall.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-48"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0114","stem":"What is a round access door installed by spinning the frame into a round opening called?","options":["Spin door","Hinged door","Sandwich door","Surface Mount door"],"expl":"A spin door is a round access door and frame installed by spinning the frame into a round opening.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-52"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0115","stem":"What is a two-part closure device mechanically fastened on both sides of the duct wall called?","options":["Sandwich door","Flush Mount door","Spin door","Hinged door"],"expl":"A sandwich access door is a two-part closure fastened together on both sides of the duct wall at the service-opening perimeter.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-51"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0116","stem":"How is 'duct board' best described?","options":["Rigid insulation board faced with a finishing material","A sheet metal duct panel lined with fibrous glass","Flexible insulation wrapped outside a metal duct","Canvas flexible connector fitted at the fan inlet"],"expl":"Duct board is rigid insulation material faced with a finishing material, the outer facing normally serving as a vapor (and possibly air) barrier.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-board"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0117","stem":"Which unit type includes means for cooling only but may include other air-handling functions?","options":["Cooling unit","Heating unit","Heat pump","Ventilating unit"],"expl":"A cooling unit includes means for cooling and may also include other air-handling unit functions.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-75"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0118","stem":"What characterizes a contact vacuum?","options":["A brush nozzle used on the surface","A HEPA exhaust ducted to the outdoors solely","A two-chamber decontamination unit","A fuel-powered engine"],"expl":"A contact vacuum is a portable collection device with a nylon brush nozzle applied directly to a surface for mechanical agitation and cleaning.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-35"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0119","stem":"Which best defines an 'air duct' per the ACR?","options":["A passageway for air distribution","Any air cavity within the structure","An air handling coil compartment","An air filter housing section"],"expl":"An air duct is a passageway for distribution and extraction of air, excluding plenums not installed per SMACNA standards.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-40"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0120","stem":"Turning vanes, filter housings, and fan blades are ____.","options":["Part of the HVAC system components","Not part of the HVAC system","Parts of non-ducted open air plenums","Structural elements of the building"],"expl":"Turning vanes, filters, filter housings, fan blades, and fan housings are all listed as HVAC system components.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-turning-vanes"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0121","stem":"What is an air filtration device (AFD) that filters and creates negative pressure called?","options":["Negative air machine","Recirculating HEPA air scrubber","Portable HEPA contact vacuum","Portable make-up air unit"],"expl":"An AFD that filters air and creates negative pressure is a negative air machine; one that filters and re-circulates within a space is an air scrubber.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-31","acr:D-30"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0122","stem":"Condensate drain pans and drain lines are associated with which component group?","options":["The air-handling unit","Return air grilles and boots","External duct wrap","Sound attenuators"],"expl":"Condensate pans and drain lines are AHU/coil-section components inspected during AHU cleanliness inspections.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-drain-pan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0123","stem":"Which statement about mixing boxes is correct?","options":["Inspected with the supply ducts","They fall outside the defined HVAC system","They filter refrigerant","They replace VAV boxes"],"expl":"The mixing box is part of the HVAC system; supply duct inspections include mixing boxes, VAV boxes, and fan-powered terminal boxes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0124","stem":"A terminal device modulates airflow to a zone and may include reheat. Which component is being described?","options":["A variable air volume box.","A make-up air unit.","A fire damper assembly.","A sound attenuation section."],"expl":"A VAV box varies zone airflow and may include reheat.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-box"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0125","stem":"What is the primary function of a sound attenuator installed in ductwork?","options":["Reduce fan and airflow noise carried through the duct.","Reduce outdoor air volume entering the duct system.","Reduce moisture and condensation inside the duct.","Reduce static pressure loss across the duct system."],"expl":"Sound attenuators reduce duct-borne noise.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-attenuator"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0126","stem":"A large exhaust system removes air from a building. Which equipment primarily replaces that exhausted volume?","options":["A make-up air unit.","A sound attenuation section.","A return air plenum.","A variable air volume box."],"expl":"Make-up air replaces air removed by exhaust systems.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mua","hvac:a-negative-building"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0127","stem":"How does a traditional multi-zone system serve zones with different load requirements?","options":["It mixes deck air in different proportions per zone.","It reheats each zone equally after a single cooling process.","It varies outdoor-air volume while supply temperature is fixed.","It cycles a separate compressor for each zone it serves."],"expl":"A multi-zone system mixes hot and cold deck air in varying proportions.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-multizone"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0128","stem":"Which description best defines reheat in an air distribution system?","options":["Heating air downstream of cooling to meet zone demand.","Heating return air upstream of the filter section.","Heating condensate so the drain stays free of growth.","Heating outdoor air while the exhaust fan runs."],"expl":"Reheat commonly warms air downstream after cooling or dehumidification.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-reheat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0129","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, dust is visible downstream of a newly installed filter. Which filter-section condition should be investigated first?","options":["Poor fit, damaged seals, or bypass at the filter frame.","A fire damper closed in a remote supply branch, restricting flow.","Standing condensate, algae, or scale in the coil drain pan.","A sound attenuator set close to the fan discharge, shedding fiber."],"expl":"Downstream contamination can result from filter bypass, poor fit, or failed seals.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-bypass"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0130","stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a fire damper from a smoke damper?","options":["A fire damper responds to heat, a smoke damper to detection.","A fire damper sets zone airflow, a smoke damper drainage.","A fire damper responds to smoke, a smoke damper to heat.","A fire damper cuts noise, a smoke damper balances pressure."],"expl":"Fire dampers are heat actuated, while smoke dampers operate through smoke-control or detection logic.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fire-damper","hvac:s-smoke-damper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0131","stem":"Which component is positioned to collect condensate beneath a cooling coil?","options":["The drain pan.","The sound attenuator.","The filter rack.","The balancing damper."],"expl":"The drain pan captures condensate for removal through the drain line.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-drain-pan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0132","stem":"Which airflow path normally carries air from occupied rooms back toward the air handler?","options":["The return-air path.","The supply-air path.","The exhaust path.","The make-up-air path."],"expl":"Return air travels from the conditioned space back toward the AHU.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ahu-path"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0133","stem":"Which measurement is most useful for demonstrating a change in coil airflow resistance after cleaning?","options":["Static pressure drop across the coil at equal airflow.","Relative humidity measured inside the drain pan.","Supply voltage measured at the fan disconnect.","Sound level measured downstream of the coil."],"expl":"Comparing static pressure drop before and after cleaning can demonstrate coil-cleaning effectiveness.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-coil-loading","hvac:a-dp-flow-squared"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0134","stem":"Which component directly creates airflow through an air-handling system?","options":["The fan or blower assembly.","The sound attenuator assembly.","The drain pan assembly.","The diffuser neck assembly."],"expl":"Fans and blowers move air through the system.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-fan-creates-airflow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0135","stem":"Which outlet component is designed to distribute supply air into an occupied space?","options":["A supply diffuser.","A supply smoke detector.","A supply smoke damper.","A supply air plenum."],"expl":"A diffuser distributes supply air into the occupied space.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-diffuser"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0136","stem":"A zone receives excessive airflow because a terminal damper is stuck open. Which component is most likely involved?","options":["The variable air volume box serving that zone.","The sound attenuator serving that zone's trunk.","The make-up air unit serving that zone's exhaust.","The drain pan serving that zone's cooling coil."],"expl":"A VAV box controls zone airflow through its damper and controller.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-damper-fail"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0137","stem":"What typical primary components would an Air Systems Cleaning Specialist (ASCS) expect to find inside a standard Fan Coil Unit (FCU)?","options":["Blower, heat exchange coil, and filter","Diffuser, balancing damper, and return grille","Compressor, condenser coil, and expansion valve","VAV damper, reheat coil, and controller"],"expl":"A standard fan coil unit (FCU) consists primarily of a circulation blower/fan assembly, a heating/cooling coil, and a filter assembly.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0138","stem":"In a commercial building HVAC system, what is the primary function of a Variable Air Volume (VAV) terminal box?","options":["Regulate airflow volume to a zone","To produce chilled water for the cooling tower","To filter outdoor fresh air before entering the plenum","To mix exhaust air with outside air"],"expl":"A VAV box controls the volume of conditioned supply air delivered to individual building thermal zones using a motorized damper inside the box.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-box"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0139","stem":"What is a ceiling return air plenum?","options":["An open space above the drop ceiling","An enclosed metal duct carrying return air","A flex hose from a bathroom exhaust fan","A sealed box housing the evaporator coil"],"expl":"A return air plenum uses the architectural space above a dropped ceiling (or below raised flooring) as a pathway to convey return air back to the air handler rather than fully ducted pipe.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-60","ascs:p-oap-return-indicators"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0140","stem":"Per NADCA's Open Air Plenums white paper, how is an open air plenum best defined?","options":["Non-ducted air pathways in building cavities and voids","A sealed sheet metal duct run above the ceiling grid","A pressurized sheet metal chamber at the AHU supply outlet","Ceiling spaces containing fire or smoke dampers"],"expl":"Open air plenums are intended non-ducted air pathways in building cavities, voids, and spaces outside the occupied zone that facilitate airflow between HVAC equipment and the occupied space.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-60"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0141","stem":"A raised-floor air distribution system in a data center is an example of which plenum type?","options":["Supply plenum (positive)","Return/exhaust plenum (negative)","Accidental air plenum","Transfer plenum (neutral)"],"expl":"Supply plenums operate positive and are used by design in raised-floor distribution for data centers, certain clean rooms, and similar spaces.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ufad"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0142","stem":"How are ceiling/attic plenums, wall voids, closets, and pipe chases used for air movement classified?","options":["Return/exhaust plenums","Supply plenums (positive)","Accidental plenums","Mixing plenums"],"expl":"Return/exhaust plenums operate negative and by design include ceiling and attic plenums, voids between walls, closets, raised floor, and pipe chases.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0143","stem":"What does leaky or disconnected ductwork that unintentionally turns a cavity into an air pathway create?","options":["Accidental air plenum","Return air transfer plenum","Auxiliary supply air plenum","Ducted return air chase"],"expl":"Leaky or disconnected ducts can create an unintentional open air supply or return plenum, which the white paper calls an accidental air plenum.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-accidental"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0144","stem":"In a multizone air handling unit, where does the mixing of hot and cold air occur?","options":["At the unit's hot and cold decks","At a mixing box in each occupied zone","In the return plenum, before the filters","At the terminal diffuser on each branch"],"expl":"A multizone AHU has a hot deck and a cold deck with zone dampers at the unit; each zone gets its own duct leaving the unit already mixed.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-multizone"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0145","stem":"The key distinction between a dual-duct system and a multizone system is that a dual-duct system ____.","options":["Runs hot and cold ducts to a zone mixing box","Mixes air at dampers on the unit's decks","Serves each zone with a dedicated return path","Operates without heating or cooling coils"],"expl":"Dual-duct systems carry separate hot and cold ducts out to terminal mixing boxes; multizone systems mix at the AHU and run one duct per zone.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dual-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0146","stem":"A multizone unit serving eight zones will have how many supply ducts leaving the unit?","options":["Eight, one per zone","One, with zone dampers","Two, a hot and cold deck","Sixteen, two per zone"],"expl":"Each zone damper set discharges into its own duct, so a multizone unit has one supply duct per zone leaving the casing.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-multizone-ducts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0147","stem":"On the ASCS acronym list, what does FPM stand for?","options":["Feet Per Minute","Filters Per Month","Fan Pressure Measurement","Fine Particulate Matter"],"expl":"FPM is feet per minute, the unit for air velocity, and appears on NADCA's approved exam acronym list.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-iwc-fpm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0148","stem":"How is air velocity in a duct calculated?","options":["CFM divided by duct cross-sectional area in square feet","CFM multiplied by the duct cross-sectional area","Static pressure divided by fan speed in RPM","Duct area in square feet divided by the CFM"],"expl":"Velocity (FPM) equals volumetric flow (CFM) divided by the duct's cross-sectional area in square feet.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-q-va"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0149","stem":"What does IWC on the acronym list refer to?","options":["Inches of Water Column","Internal Wall Coating","Insulated Wiring Conduit","Indoor Water Content"],"expl":"IWC is inches of water column, the pressure unit used for static and negative pressure in HVAC work.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-iwc-fpm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0150","stem":"What will reducing a duct's cross-sectional area while holding CFM constant do?","options":["Increase velocity","Reduce velocity","Have no effect on velocity","Decrease static pressure"],"expl":"Velocity is inversely proportional to area at constant flow, so a smaller duct produces higher FPM.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-velocity-squared"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0151","stem":"What range do MERV ratings under ASHRAE Standard 52.2 span?","options":["1 through 16","1 through 8","1 through 20","10 through 30"],"expl":"ASHRAE 52.2 rates general-ventilation filters MERV 1 through 16. HEPA and ULPA filters are rated under separate test methods.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-merv-range"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0152","stem":"What removal efficiency defines a HEPA filter?","options":["99.97% at 0.3 micron","95% at 1.0 micron","99.999% at 0.12 micron","90% at 0.1 micron"],"expl":"HEPA filtration removes at least 99.97% of particles at the most-penetrating 0.3 micron size, which is why HEPA vacuums and AFDs are specified for source removal.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-hepa"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0153","stem":"What does installing a much higher MERV filter than the system was designed for most commonly cause?","options":["Increased pressure drop and reduced system airflow","Improved airflow through the filter bank","Lower fan energy at the same static","Freeze protection at the cooling coil"],"expl":"Higher MERV media raises resistance; if the fan cannot overcome it, airflow drops, which can cause coil freezing, comfort complaints, and equipment strain.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-high-merv-effect"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0154","stem":"How does a constant air volume (CAV) system control space temperature?","options":["Changing supply air temperature at a fixed airflow","Changing airflow at a fixed supply air temperature","Modulating a damper in each zone's terminal box","Slowing the supply fan with a VFD as the zone load falls"],"expl":"CAV: airflow fixed, discharge temperature changes. VAV is the reverse.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-cav-control"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0155","stem":"How does a variable air volume (VAV) system control space temperature?","options":["Changing airflow at a fixed supply air temperature","Changing supply air temperature at a fixed airflow","Cycling the supply fan on and off from the zone stat","Blending hot deck and cold deck air at the unit"],"expl":"VAV: ~55°F supply, each box throttles cfm to match load.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-control"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0156","stem":"As VAV boxes close, what is the supply fan controlled to hold?","options":["Duct static pressure at a sensor down the main","A fixed fan rpm set during commissioning","A fixed supply temperature at the cooling coil","Building static pressure relative to the outdoors"],"expl":"Static pressure control: a VFD slows the fan as boxes close. Discharge temperature is a separate loop.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-fan-vfd"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0157","stem":"What is the state of a reheat VAV box serving a zone below setpoint?","options":["At minimum airflow, reheat running","Damper wide open, reheat coil energized","Damper fully closed, reheat coil energized","At minimum airflow, reheat coil shut off"],"expl":"Reheat sequence: box goes to minimum (ventilation floor), then reheat warms that air. Never fully closed.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0158","stem":"In a series fan-powered VAV box, the fan ____.","options":["Runs continuously and moves all air to the zone","Runs only in heating, pulling warm ceiling plenum air","Sits upstream of the primary air damper","Is mounted in the zone's return air duct"],"expl":"Series: fan in line, runs all the time. Parallel: side-path fan runs in heating only.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-series-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0159","stem":"In a multizone air handler, where are hot and cold air mixed?","options":["At the unit's zone dampers, with one supply duct per zone","In each zone's box, at the ceiling, from two ducts","In the return plenum, upstream of both coils","In a zone mixing box, fed by hot and cold ducts"],"expl":"Multizone: mixing dampers at the unit, one duct per zone. Dual duct: two trunks to each zone.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-multizone","hvac:s-multizone-ducts"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0160","stem":"What does a dual-duct system deliver to each zone?","options":["Separate hot and cold ducts blended at a zone box","One cold duct with a reheat coil at the zone","One duct per zone already mixed at the unit","One cold duct throttled by a VAV damper"],"expl":"Dual duct: hot deck and cold deck trunks run to every zone. Distractors are reheat VAV and multizone.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dual-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0161","stem":"A split system is one where ____.","options":["Indoor and outdoor coils are in separate cabinets","Supply and return are moved by separate fans","Heating and cooling come from different fuel sources","One air handler serves two duct systems"],"expl":"Split = indoor coil/air handler plus outdoor condensing unit joined by refrigerant lines.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-split-system"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0162","stem":"What does a packaged rooftop unit (RTU) contain?","options":["The whole refrigeration circuit and fan in one cabinet","An indoor coil matched to a remote condenser","A supply fan and filters, with both coils indoors","A chilled water coil fed from a central plant"],"expl":"Packaged: compressor, both coils, fan, often heat, all in one box.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-packaged-unit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0163","stem":"How is a fan coil unit best described?","options":["A local fan and hydronic or DX coil serving one space","A central AHU fan and coil section serving many spaces","A terminal unit that throttles primary air from a central AHU","A ceiling diffuser with a fan and filter serving one space"],"expl":"Fan coil: local fan, local coil, little or no duct. ACR lists it under cooling-heating units.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0164","stem":"What is a dedicated outdoor air system (DOAS) meant to do?","options":["Condition ventilation air apart from the space load","Replace all return air with exhaust air","Supply 100 percent recirculated air to interior core zones","Pressurize stairwells during a fire alarm"],"expl":"DOAS dehumidifies and tempers the OA; terminal units handle the space load.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-doas"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0165","stem":"What does an economizer do?","options":["Cool with outside air when conditions allow","Recover exhaust heat into the supply airstream","Reduce compressor cycling with hot gas bypass","Hold minimum outside air during occupied hours"],"expl":"Economizer modulates OA/RA dampers for free cooling. Heat recovery is an ERV/HRV.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-economizer"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0166","stem":"An ERV differs from an HRV in that the ERV ____.","options":["Transfers moisture as well as sensible heat","Uses a refrigerant loop instead of an enthalpy wheel","Operates only during the heating season","Discharges exhaust through the supply fan"],"expl":"Energy recovery = sensible plus latent. Heat recovery = sensible only.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-erv-hrv"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0167","stem":"What does a make-up air unit do?","options":["Replace air removed by exhaust fans","Recirculate return air through a finer filter","Pre-cool outside air ahead of the economizer","Boost supply pressure to the farthest zones"],"expl":"Big exhausts (kitchens, labs, booths) pull air out; MUA brings tempered replacement air in.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mua","hvac:a-negative-building"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0168","stem":"What does underfloor air distribution (UFAD) supply air through?","options":["A pressurized raised-floor plenum and floor diffusers","Ceiling diffusers, with returns low on the wall","Sidewall registers fed from a crawlspace plenum","Displacement diffusers fed from a ceiling plenum"],"expl":"The raised floor is a positive-pressure open air plenum, the data center example in the NADCA white paper.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ufad"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0169","stem":"What does a single-zone system mean?","options":["One thermostat controls the unit for one space","One AHU with a VAV box in every room","One supply trunk with a balancing damper on every branch","One building served by one central plant"],"expl":"Single zone = one control point. Residential splits are the common case.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-single-zone"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0170","stem":"In a draw-through air handler, where is the fan?","options":["Downstream of the coils, pulling air through","Upstream of the coils, pushing air through them","In the return duct, ahead of the filters and coils","In the mixing box, ahead of the filters and coils"],"expl":"Draw-through: coil section is under negative pressure, so the condensate trap matters.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-draw-through","hvac:s-blow-through"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0171","stem":"What does a blow-through air handler put the coil section under?","options":["Positive pressure from the fan, so pan traps can be shallow","Negative pressure, so pan traps can be shallow","No pressure difference relative to the space","Return plenum pressure, matched to the ceiling"],"expl":"Blow-through: fan first. Draw-through: fan last, negative at the pan.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-draw-through","hvac:s-blow-through","hvac:s-trap-height"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0172","stem":"What is the order of air through a typical draw-through AHU?","options":["Mixing box, filters, coils, fan, then supply duct","Filters, fan, coils, mixing box, supply duct","Fan, filters, coils, mixing box, supply duct","Mixing box, cooling coil, filters, fan, then supply duct"],"expl":"Mixed air, filters protect the coils, heating then cooling, fan, supply.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ahu-path"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0173","stem":"What happens in a mixing box in an AHU?","options":["Return air and outside air blend before the filters","Hot deck and cold deck air combine for each zone served","Supply air splits between two main trunks","Refrigerant and condensate lines terminate"],"expl":"Mixing box (mixed air plenum): OA and RA dampers meet ahead of the filters.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mixing-box"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0174","stem":"Why does a draw-through unit's drain pan need a trap?","options":["Negative pressure would pull air up the drain","Refrigerant could migrate into the drain line","The pan must hold water to humidify supply air","Every gravity drain requires a trap by code"],"expl":"Untrapped drain on a negative-pressure pan sucks air backward; pan overflows. Trap depth is sized to the static.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-draw-through-trap"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0175","stem":"What does a float switch in a condensate drain pan do?","options":["Locks the unit out when pan water rises too high","Starts the condensate pump when the pan runs dry","Tells the BAS to open the pan drain valve","Meters the condensate volume leaving the drain pan"],"expl":"Overflow protection: float rises, unit shuts down before water hits the ceiling.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-float-switch"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0176","stem":"Where is a reheat coil located?","options":["After the cooling coil, at the unit or terminal","Before the cooling coil, ahead of the filters","In the return duct to temper return air","In the outside air intake to stop freezing"],"expl":"Reheat warms air already cooled for dehumidification or zone control. Intake coil is preheat.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-reheat","hvac:s-preheat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0177","stem":"What does a preheat coil do?","options":["Warm cold intake air to protect the cooling coil","Reheat supply air after the cooling coil dehumidifies","Warm return air before the mixing box and coils","Keep the coil condensate pan from freezing in winter"],"expl":"Preheat sits ahead of the cooling coil in cold climates or high-OA units.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"AHU anatomy","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-preheat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0178","stem":"A DX cooling coil is one that ____.","options":["Boils refrigerant inside its tubes","Carries chilled water from a central chiller","Runs glycol from a rooftop dry cooler","Wets a media pad the air passes through"],"expl":"DX = direct expansion: the coil is the evaporator.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dx-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0179","stem":"In a chilled water system, what feeds the cooling coil?","options":["A central chiller through a pumped loop","Refrigerant from a rooftop condensing unit","City water through a three-way mixing valve","The cooling tower loop run direct to the coil"],"expl":"Chiller makes ~44°F water. The tower cools the chiller's condenser, not the coils.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-chilled-water"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0182","stem":"What do electric heat strips in a heat pump air handler serve as?","options":["Auxiliary heat and emergency heat","Primary heat in mild weather","Compressor crankcase heat","Outdoor coil defrost heat"],"expl":"Strips stage on below the balance point and during defrost; emergency heat runs strips alone.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-aux-heat","hvac:s-emergency-heat"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0183","stem":"What does one ton of cooling equal?","options":["12,000 BTU per hour","1,000 BTU per hour","3,412 BTU per hour","288,000 BTU per hour"],"expl":"Heat to melt a ton of ice in 24 hours. 3,412 BTU/h is one kilowatt.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ton","hvac:a-ton"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0184","stem":"What is the rule-of-thumb airflow across a DX cooling coil?","options":["400 cfm per ton","200 cfm per ton","1,200 cfm per ton","40 cfm per ton"],"expl":"350-450 cfm/ton. Too little freezes the coil; too much loses dehumidification.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","hvac:a-cfm-per-ton"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0185","stem":"What is the typical temperature drop across a healthy DX cooling coil?","options":["About 15 to 20°F","About 5 to 8°F","Around 30 to 35°F","About 40 to 45°F"],"expl":"Rule of thumb for a healthy DX coil at normal airflow is about a 15 to 20°F drop (some techs use 18 to 22). A bigger split points to low airflow, such as a loaded coil or dirty filter; a smaller split points to low charge or too much airflow.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-coil-delta-t","hvac:a-temp-split","hvac:a-split-meaning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0186","stem":"What is a hydronic heating coil fed with?","options":["Hot water from a boiler","Chilled water from a chiller","Hot gas from a compressor","Refrigerant gas from a heat pump"],"expl":"Hydronic means water: a hydronic heating coil runs on hot water from a boiler. Chilled water feeds cooling coils, and hot gas and refrigerant belong to the refrigeration side.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-water-steam-coils"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0187","stem":"On a split system, what is the insulated refrigerant line?","options":["Suction line, the larger cold line","Liquid line, the smaller warm line","Discharge line at the compressor outlet","Condensate line off the drain pan"],"expl":"Suction line carries cold vapor; insulation stops sweating and heat gain.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dx-coil"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0188","stem":"What do fin straightening tools do?","options":["Restore bent fins so air passes evenly","Trim fins back to expose the coil tubes","Measure fins per inch across the coil face","Strip fins from a corroded coil for scrap"],"expl":"Bent fins block airflow. Fin straightening is a Type 1 dry method in ACR 4.11.2.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Coils and refrigeration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fin-straightening"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0189","stem":"Where is a forward-curved centrifugal fan typically found?","options":["Residential furnaces and small air handlers","Large plenum fan arrays in custom-built commercial AHUs","Axial roof-mounted exhaust fans","Industrial dust collection cyclones"],"expl":"Squirrel cage: cheap, quiet, small units. Backward inclined and airfoil in large AHUs.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-fc","hvac:s-fan-bi"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0190","stem":"A plenum fan differs from a housed centrifugal fan in that it ____.","options":["Discharges into the cabinet with no scroll housing","Uses axial blades on a belt-driven shaft","Is always installed in the return duct","Needs a discharge damper to control flow"],"expl":"Plenum fans: wheel spins in the open section, pressurizing it. Common in modern AHUs.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-plenum"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0191","stem":"A belt-driven blower delivers low airflow. What should you check first?","options":["Belt tension, wear, and sheave alignment","Compressor amperage, voltage, and contactor","Refrigerant subcooling, superheat, and charge","Condensate trap depth, slope, and blower wheel"],"expl":"Slipping or glazed belts and misaligned sheaves are the classic belt-drive failure.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-belt-drive"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0192","stem":"How does a variable frequency drive (VFD) control fan output?","options":["Changing the supply frequency to the fan motor","Throttling a damper at the fan discharge","Switching the fan motor between two windings","Modulating the fan's inlet guide vanes"],"expl":"VFD varies Hz, speed follows. Vanes and dampers are the older methods.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vfd"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0193","stem":"Per the fan laws, doubling fan speed makes airflow ____.","options":["Double, while static pressure quadruples","Quadruple, while static pressure doubles","Double, while static pressure also doubles","Stay the same, while power doubles"],"expl":"CFM ∝ rpm, SP ∝ rpm², power ∝ rpm³.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-law-flow","hvac:s-fan-law-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0194","stem":"Per the fan laws, by how much does doubling fan speed raise motor power?","options":["Eight times","Two times","Four times","Sixteen times"],"expl":"Power varies with the cube of speed.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-law-power"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0195","stem":"What is an ECM blower motor?","options":["A variable-speed motor holding programmed cfm","A belt-drive induction motor with a run capacitor","A two-speed PSC motor switched by a blower relay","A single-speed motor locked to the 60 Hz supply"],"expl":"ECM ramps up to hold cfm against rising static. PSC is the older single-speed type.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Fans and motors","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ecm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0196","stem":"What closes a fire damper?","options":["A fusible link melting at a set temperature","A signal from the duct smoke detector or fire alarm","A BAS command during unoccupied hours","A loss of pneumatic control air"],"expl":"Fire damper = fusible link. Smoke damper = actuator on a detector signal.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fire-damper"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0197","stem":"What closes a smoke damper?","options":["An actuator driven by a smoke detector signal","A fusible link melting at a set temperature","Airflow reversal sensed by a duct switch","A pull station signal routed to the damper"],"expl":"Smoke dampers are actuated; fire dampers are thermal. Combination dampers do both.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-smoke-damper","hvac:s-combination-damper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0198","stem":"Opposed-blade dampers are preferred for modulating because they ____.","options":["Give a more linear flow response","Seal more tightly when fully closed","Cost less than parallel-blade dampers","Fit into round duct without a transition"],"expl":"Parallel blade is fine for two-position. Opposed gives a better modulating curve.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-opposed-blade"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0199","stem":"What is a backdraft damper?","options":["A gravity flap that stops reverse airflow","A motorized damper interlocked with the fan","A balancing damper locked at its set point","A fire damper mounted at the intake louver"],"expl":"Backdraft (gravity) dampers on exhaust discharges and intakes. No actuator.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-backdraft"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0200","stem":"What does a balancing damper do?","options":["Set a fixed branch airflow during test and balance","Modulate zone temperature continuously","Isolate the duct run during a fire event or smoke alarm","Stop reverse flow through an exhaust fan"],"expl":"Locked after T&B. ACR 4.8: mark before cleaning, restore after.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dampers-balancing"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0201","stem":"What does total pressure in a duct equal?","options":["Static pressure plus velocity pressure","Static pressure minus velocity pressure","Velocity pressure times static pressure","Velocity pressure divided by static pressure"],"expl":"TP = SP + VP. A pitot tube reads both; VP is the difference.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-sp-vp-tp","hvac:a-pitot-ports"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0202","stem":"How do you find air velocity in a duct?","options":["cfm divided by area in square feet","cfm multiplied by area in square feet","Static pressure divided by 4,005","Area in square feet divided by cfm"],"expl":"Q = A × V, so V = Q ÷ A. 4,005 × √VP converts a pitot reading.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-v-4005","hvac:a-q-va"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0203","stem":"What will shrinking a duct's cross-section at constant cfm do?","options":["Raise velocity and raise pressure loss","Lower velocity and pressure loss","Leave velocity and pressure loss unchanged","Lower velocity and raise pressure loss"],"expl":"Same air through a smaller pipe moves faster and fights more friction.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-velocity-squared"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0204","stem":"What does SMACNA duct pressure class describe?","options":["The static pressure the duct is built to hold","The MERV rating of the filters the duct serves","The gauge of access doors set in the duct","The maximum velocity allowed in the duct trunk"],"expl":"½ to 10 in. w.g. classes govern gauge and reinforcement. ACR 4.2.6: access door gauge follows it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0205","stem":"Under SMACNA seal class A, what gets sealed?","options":["All joints, seams, and duct wall penetrations","Transverse joints, and nothing else","Transverse joints, seams, and nothing else","Transverse joints and wall penetrations only"],"expl":"A = everything. B = joints and seams. C = joints only.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-seal-class-a","hvac:a-seal-class-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0206","stem":"Compared with rigid metal duct, what does flexible duct have?","options":["Higher friction loss and sags if unsupported","Lower friction loss and higher velocity","Equal friction loss when fully stretched","No length limit under the International Mechanical Code"],"expl":"Pull it taut, keep it short. ACR 4.2.9: no service openings in it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-flex-friction"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0207","stem":"What is fibrous glass duct board?","options":["Rigid board with a foil facing, formed into the duct","Blanket insulation, foil faced, wrapped around the duct","Fibrous glass liner, foil faced, glued inside the duct","A polymer film, foil laminated, lining a flexible duct"],"expl":"Duct board IS the duct. Wrap is outside a metal duct; liner is inside it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-board"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0208","stem":"Where is duct liner installed?","options":["Inside a metal duct, facing the airstream","Outside a metal duct, under a foil vapor jacket","Outside a metal duct, held on with metal banding","Between the inner core and jacket of flex duct"],"expl":"Liner faces the air, so it is porous airside surface. Wrap is outside.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-duct-liner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0209","stem":"What is a sound attenuator in a duct system?","options":["A lined section that reduces fan and airflow noise","A damper that reduces velocity in the branch","A flexible connector installed at the fan","A turning vane assembly in the elbow"],"expl":"Attenuators are porous-lined. ACR lists sound attenuated duct as porous.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-attenuator"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0210","stem":"Where are turning vanes installed?","options":["Rectangular elbows to cut turbulence","Round elbows to raise the air velocity","Flexible duct to keep it from collapsing","Diffuser necks to adjust the air throw"],"expl":"Vanes guide air around a hard corner. They are a listed HVAC component in ACR.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-turning-vanes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0211","stem":"A diffuser differs from a grille in that a diffuser ____.","options":["Spreads supply air in a pattern into the space","Is a plain louvered opening used on returns","Always includes a damper behind the face","Is only installed in a sidewall, never in a ceiling"],"expl":"Diffuser = supply, spreads. Grille = plain louvers, typically return. Register = grille plus damper.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-diffuser"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0212","stem":"What is a register?","options":["A grille with an integral damper to adjust airflow","A ceiling diffuser with no damper behind it","A louvered return opening with no damper","A balancing damper set out in the branch duct"],"expl":"Register = grille + damper. ACR 4.9 treats registers, grilles, and diffusers together.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Dampers and ducts","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-register"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0213","stem":"What is the range of MERV ratings under ASHRAE 52.2?","options":["1 to 16, with HEPA above the scale","1 to 8, with HEPA above the scale","1 to 12, with HEPA above the scale","5 to 25, with HEPA included as MERV 25"],"expl":"MERV 1-16. HEPA (99.97% at 0.3 µm) is tested to a different standard and sits above 16.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-merv-range"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0214","stem":"What does a much higher MERV filter than the system was designed for cause?","options":["Higher static pressure and lower airflow","Coil freezing from the higher airflow","Compressor overload from higher suction pressure","Lower static pressure for the system fan"],"expl":"Tighter media = more resistance. Airflow drops unless the fan can overcome it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-high-merv-effect","hvac:a-resistance-airflow"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0215","stem":"Air bypass around a filter rack ____.","options":["Lets unfiltered air load the coil and duct","Improves airflow with no real downside","Is normal on a V-bank filter arrangement in an AHU","Is prevented by a higher MERV rating"],"expl":"Bypass is why ACR's AHU inspection list includes 'filters and air bypass'.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-bypass"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0216","stem":"What does a prefilter ahead of a final filter do?","options":["Catch large particles so the final filter lasts","Remove gases and odors that the final filter misses","Give HEPA capture ahead of the final filter","Add resistance so the final filter loads evenly"],"expl":"MERV 8 prefilter protects a MERV 13-14 or HEPA final. Carbon handles gases.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-prefilter","hvac:a-carbon"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0217","stem":"What is the dew point of an air sample?","options":["The temperature at which it becomes saturated","The dry-bulb temperature at 50 percent RH","The surface temperature of the cooling coil","The wet-bulb temperature at sea level"],"expl":"Any surface colder than the dew point sweats. That is why cold ducts and pans grow biofilm.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-dew-point","hvac:a-condensation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0218","stem":"What is the difference between sensible cooling and latent cooling?","options":["Sensible lowers temperature; latent removes moisture","Sensible removes moisture; latent lowers the temperature","Sensible cools the supply air; latent cools the return air","Sensible reads on the wet bulb; latent reads on the dry bulb"],"expl":"A coil below dew point does both. Latent load is why cfm/ton and coil temperature matter.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-sensible-latent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0219","stem":"What does a steam humidifier in an AHU do?","options":["Injects steam through a dispersion tube in the airstream","Sprays water over a wetted media pad the air passes across","Vibrates water into mist with ultrasonic plates","Adds moisture by opening the outside air damper"],"expl":"Steam is the commercial norm. ACR's AHU inspection list includes humidification systems.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-humidifier-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0221","stem":"Where is a VAV duct static pressure sensor usually placed?","options":["About two-thirds down the main supply duct","Right at the supply fan discharge","In the return air plenum near the unit","At the neck of the farthest diffuser"],"expl":"Two-thirds down is the compromise between fan and worst-case box.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-static-sensor"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0222","stem":"What is a typical VAV cooling supply air setpoint?","options":["55°F (13°C)","45°F (7°C)","65°F (18°C)","72°F (22°C), room setpoint"],"expl":"~55°F supply dehumidifies and lets boxes throttle. Reset warmer at low load.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-control"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0223","stem":"What does a duct smoke detector do?","options":["Shut the fan down when smoke is sensed in the duct","Modulate the economizer damper on outside air quality","Close the fire damper at the rated temperature","Trip the VAV box to minimum position"],"expl":"Duct detectors shut down the AHU. ACR 2.8 and 4.10: address life safety if offline, never impair them.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0225","stem":"A cracked furnace heat exchanger is dangerous because it ____.","options":["Can pass flue gas including CO into supply air","Restricts airflow across the cooling coil","Trips the high-limit switch on every cycle","Lets refrigerant escape into the flue"],"expl":"The exchanger separates flue gas from house air. ACR 4.24: document and report; repair is out of scope.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-cracked-hx"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0226","stem":"How should a dryer exhaust duct be built?","options":["Short, smooth rigid metal, no screws inside","Flexible foil, any length, with as many bends as needed","Vinyl flex, with sheet metal screws at each joint","Fibrous glass duct board, sealed with foil tape"],"expl":"Lint plus restriction plus heat = fire. NADCA's CDET cert covers this lane.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Exhaust","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-dryer-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0227","stem":"What governs commercial kitchen grease exhaust duct?","options":["NFPA 96, cleaned per IKECA C10","NFPA 90A, cleaned per ACR","ASHRAE 62.1, cleaned per NADCA 05","UL 181, cleaned per NAIMA AH 122"],"expl":"Grease duct is its own lane. ACR references both 96 and C10 for it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Exhaust","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-nfpa96-scope","hvac:a-ikeca"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0228","stem":"Under ACR, bathroom and general exhaust fans are ____.","options":["Part of the overall system inspection","Outside the Standard's scope entirely","Inspected only under IKECA C10","Inspected only on the healthcare interval"],"expl":"1.7.5: exhaust ducts are in. Kitchen grease exhaust is what jumps to NFPA 96/C10.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Exhaust","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-exhaust-in-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0229","stem":"Relief air differs from exhaust air in that relief air ____.","options":["Is excess return air spilled outdoors when the economizer operates","Is pulled from restrooms and kitchens by dedicated exhaust fans","Is outdoor air drawn in through the economizer damper","Bypasses the return fan through a transfer duct to the space"],"expl":"Relief leaves through a relief damper or return fan when the economizer brings in more OA.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Exhaust","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-relief-air","hvac:a-relief-vs-exhaust"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0230","stem":"What does a flow hood (balometer) measure?","options":["Airflow in cfm at a diffuser or grille","Static pressure inside the duct run","Refrigerant pressures at the service ports","Duct leakage under test pressure"],"expl":"Capture hood over the outlet reads cfm directly. Pitot traverse is the in-duct method.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-flow-hood"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0231","stem":"What does a pitot tube traverse measure?","options":["Velocity pressure at points across the duct","Static pressure at the fan discharge only","Airflow at the face of a ceiling diffuser","Superheat at the evaporator outlet"],"expl":"Traverse averages velocity across the duct area. Q = A × V.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-traverse-why"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0233","stem":"What is a packaged terminal air conditioner (PTAC)?","options":["A self-contained through-wall unit for one room","A rooftop unit dedicated to a single floor of the building","A VAV terminal box fitted with its own DX cooling coil","A fan coil unit fed from a central chilled water loop"],"expl":"Hotel-room wall sleeve unit, full refrigeration circuit inside. ACR lists it under cooling-heating units.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ptac"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0234","stem":"What is a ductless mini-split's indoor unit?","options":["A wall or ceiling head with its own fan and coil","A small furnace cabinet with a coil on the outlet side","A VAV box with an integral fan","A ceiling diffuser with a built-in filter"],"expl":"Outdoor condensing unit, one or more indoor heads on refrigerant lines. ACR lists it under cooling-heating units.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-mini-split"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0237","stem":"Where should an outside air intake be located?","options":["Away from exhaust outlets, cooling towers, loading docks, and vents","As near the exhaust discharge as possible to save duct","At grade level, near loading docks, parking, and dumpsters","Directly above the kitchen hood exhaust outlet on the roof"],"expl":"Intake placement is an IAQ fundamental and a common inspection finding.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-oa-intake","hvac:a-intake-separation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0238","stem":"What does ASHRAE 62.1 address?","options":["Ventilation and IAQ in commercial buildings","Ventilation and IAQ in dwelling units","Ventilation of health care facilities","Test methods for air cleaning devices"],"expl":"62.1 commercial, 62.2 residential, 170 healthcare, 52.2 filters.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ashrae-62-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0239","stem":"What is a supply fan interlocked with an exhaust fan and a motorized OA damper?","options":["A control sequence against depressurization","A fire damper installation arrangement per NFPA 90A","A test and balance procedure performed per NEBB","A refrigerant safety circuit for packaged units"],"expl":"Interlocks keep pressure relationships. Cleaning that defeats one changes building pressure.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-pressure-interlock"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0240","stem":"How does negative building pressure usually show up?","options":["Doors hard to open, air rushing in when they do","Doors that blow outward, opening on their own","Frost forming on the return grilles","High humidity readings at the diffusers"],"expl":"More exhaust than intake pulls air in through every crack. Fix is MUA or OA balance.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Filters and IAQ","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-negative-building"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0241","stem":"Inspections before and after a cleaning project are ____.","options":["Required","Optional","Advised","Not addressed"],"expl":"1.1: inspections shall be performed before and after. Routine inspections are the recommended part.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.1-a","acr:1.1-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0242","stem":"What is the minimum inspector qualification ACR makes mandatory?","options":["Working knowledge of HVAC and standards","Licensure as an indoor environmental professional","Current ASCS or CVI certification","Employment by a NADCA member company location"],"expl":"1.2: ASCS/CVI is recommended; at minimum personnel shall have verifiable working knowledge. Licensing applies to microbial determinations where law requires.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2-h","acr:1.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0243","stem":"What must anyone determining Condition 1, 2, or 3 during an inspection be?","options":["Qualified by training and experience, and licensed where law requires it","Certified by NADCA as an ASCS or a CVI, with no license needed","A certified industrial hygienist holding the CIH credential","An IEP retained directly by the building owner or the client"],"expl":"1.2: qualified through training and experience, and licensed where applicable by law.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2-g","acr:1.2-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0244","stem":"Building floor plans and mechanical plans, when available, shall be used during ____.","options":["Inspection, cleaning, and restoration","Cleanliness verification and documentation","Bidding, estimating, and contract closeout","Scope development, but not the field work"],"expl":"1.3: both floor plans and mechanical plans, if available, shall be used during inspection, cleaning, and restoration.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.3-b","acr:1.3-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0245","stem":"During an inspection, appropriate engineering controls to manage the workspace are ____.","options":["Required","Banned","Optional","Recommended only"],"expl":"1.4: during an inspection, appropriate engineering controls shall be used.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0246","stem":"Per Table 1, what is the recommended inspection interval for a residential supply duct?","options":["Two years","One year","Five years","Three years"],"expl":"Residential and marine ducts: 2 years. AHUs everywhere: 1 year. Commercial, industrial, healthcare ducts: 1 year.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0247","stem":"Per Table 1, what is the recommended AHU inspection interval for a marine vessel?","options":["One year","Six months","Two years, as for ducts","Every drydock"],"expl":"AHU interval is one year in every classification, including marine.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-d"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0248","stem":"Per Table 1, which building type has a two-year return duct interval?","options":["Marine","Healthcare","Commercial","Industrial"],"expl":"Residential and marine share the 2-year duct interval; commercial, industrial, and healthcare ducts are annual.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-e","acr:1.5.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0249","stem":"Inspecting a system for cleanliness before it operates after a remodel is ____.","options":["Recommended","May be waived by owner","Required and verified","Not addressed"],"expl":"1.6: recommended that new and existing components in construction, retrofit, or remodel be inspected before operation.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0250","stem":"What is the minimum share of HVAC system components a cleanliness inspection shall include?","options":["10 percent","5 percent","100 percent","25 percent"],"expl":"1.7: at minimum 10% of components.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0251","stem":"Which condition changes the inspection requirement from 10% to all components?","options":["A microbial remediation project per S520","A Category 3 water loss","A healthcare facility under ASHRAE/ASHE 170","A post-fire evaluation under IESO/RIA 6001"],"expl":"1.7: when the inspection is part of a microbial remediation project under ANSI/IICRC S520, all components shall be inspected.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0252","stem":"Which component group is listed under the AHU cleanliness inspection?","options":["Door gaskets and humidification systems","Return plenums, return dampers, and grilles","Reheat coils and VAV boxes","Bathroom, general, and heat recovery exhaust"],"expl":"1.7.1 AHU list includes door gaskets and humidification systems. Reheat/VAV are supply-side; return plenums are return-side; exhaust is 1.7.5.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0253","stem":"Reheat coils are inspected as part of which inspection?","options":["Supply air duct inspection","Return duct inspection","Exhaust air duct inspection","Air-handling unit inspection"],"expl":"1.7.2 lists reheat coils under supply duct inspection.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0254","stem":"How does ACR treat open air plenum inspections?","options":["May or may not be within contract scope","Required in full under S520 and may not be sampled","Required at the 10 percent minimum sample rate","Excluded from any inspection under the Standard"],"expl":"1.7.4: open air plenum inspections may or may not be part of the contractual scope; refer to the NADCA white paper.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.4-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0255","stem":"How are general exhaust, bathroom exhaust, and heat recovery exhaust treated?","options":["Part of the overall inspection","Inspected only when under contract","Inspected only under IKECA C10","Outside the Standard's scope"],"expl":"1.7.5: considered part of the ventilation system and part of the overall inspection.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.5-b","acr:1.7.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0256","stem":"Damaged or unsafe components observed during inspection shall be ____.","options":["Documented","Tagged out of service","Replaced before cleaning","Repaired before cleaning"],"expl":"1.7.6: shall be documented. Repair of malfunctioning devices is outside the Standard (4.24.1).","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.6-a","acr:1.7.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0257","stem":"If suspect mold is found in the AHU during inspection, the supply and return ducts ____.","options":["Shall be inspected at that same time","Are inspected on the Table 1 interval","Shall be inspected within 30 days","Shall be sampled by an IEP"],"expl":"1.9.1: inspected during that same inspection rather than per Table 1 intervals.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0258","stem":"What handles suspect mold found on furnishings or building products?","options":["S520 and applicable regulations","ACR Section 4.13 and the work plan","The IEP under Section 1.8 and Table 1","S500 water categories and this Standard"],"expl":"1.9.2: not within ACR's scope; refer to ANSI/IICRC S520 and applicable law.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9.2-b","acr:1.9.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0259","stem":"What does the Standard say about noting a preliminary Condition 1, 2, or 3 when suspect mold is observed?","options":["Recommended","May be deferred","Not addressed","Required by the Standard"],"expl":"1.9: recommended that a preliminary determination be noted; further assessment by an IEP is also recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0260","stem":"Which is NOT one of the five items the system component assessment shall include?","options":["A cost estimate for the work","A recommendation on the need to clean","A clearly defined scope of work","The cleaning techniques"],"expl":"1.10: need to clean, scope, techniques, engineering controls, unique requirements. Cost is not listed.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.10-b","acr:1.10-c","acr:1.10-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0261","stem":"What does the Standard say about consulting an IEP when inspectors may be exposed to hazardous materials?","options":["Recommended","Mandatory under 1.8","May be omitted","Not addressed"],"expl":"1.8: consultation with a qualified IEP or other health and safety professional is recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.8-a","acr:X-8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0262","stem":"What does the Standard say about a written work plan from the contractor before cleaning begins?","options":["Required","Owner's choice","Not addressed","Advised"],"expl":"2.0: the contractor shall provide a written work plan prior to commencement.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.0-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0263","stem":"Which element shall appear in the work plan?","options":["The scope of work","The means and methods","The schedule","The site contacts"],"expl":"2.2 scope is shall; 2.3 means/methods, 2.5 schedule, 2.6 communication plan are recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.2-a","acr:2.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0264","stem":"What does the Standard say about listing the specific means and methods of cleaning in the work plan?","options":["Recommended","Required under 2.3","Not addressed","May be waived"],"expl":"2.3: it is recommended that the written work plan identify means and methods.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0265","stem":"What does the Standard require for Safety Data Sheets covering project chemicals?","options":["In the plan and kept on site for the project","Filed with the AHJ before the first opening is cut","Posted at the containment","Submitted to the client for signature before work"],"expl":"2.7.1: SDS shall be in the work plan and maintained on site for the duration. Client submittal proof can be included.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7.1-a","acr:2.7.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0266","stem":"What does the Standard require for manufacturer's instructions for chemical products?","options":["Available at all times to workers and others","Attached to the invoice and the work plan","Approved in writing by the IEP before use","Read aloud at the daily safety briefing"],"expl":"2.7: the manufacturer's instructions for use and application shall be available at all times.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0267","stem":"When duct smoke detectors must be taken off line, what shall the work plan do?","options":["Address life safety concerns","Notify the fire marshal","Schedule a fire watch","Require a Level 3 containment"],"expl":"2.8: the plan shall address life safety concerns, which will likely require input from others.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0268","stem":"What does the Standard say about naming each organization's designated representative in the work plan?","options":["Recommended","Not addressed","Required by the Standard","May be deferred"],"expl":"2.8: recommended that the plan define responsibilities of each organization's designated representative.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.8-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0269","stem":"Where applicable, what shall the work plan describe engineering controls for?","options":["Chemical vapors and odors","Combustion emissions from generators","Waste water capture and disposal","Noise and vibration"],"expl":"2.7.2: a description of engineering controls to control worker and occupant exposure to chemical vapors and odors.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0270","stem":"What does the Standard say about routine cleanliness inspection of HVAC components by visual means?","options":["It is required, though the intervals are recommended","It is recommended, along with the Table 1 intervals","It is recommended when occupants report IAQ complaints","It is not addressed and is deferred to ASHRAE/ACCA 180"],"expl":"1.5.1: components shall be routinely inspected by visual means. Only the Table 1 intervals are recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-a","acr:1.5.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0271","stem":"How does the Standard treat a return plenum built to SMACNA and connected to the AHU?","options":["Included in the return air duct inspection","Excluded as a non-ducted open air plenum","Included in the AHU inspection under 1.7.1","Governed by the NADCA open air plenum white paper"],"expl":"1.7.3: return plenums are return components. The exclusion is non-ducted open air plenums.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.3-b","acr:1.7.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0272","stem":"Per the definition, what does an 'air duct' exclude?","options":["Plenums not installed per SMACNA","Flexible duct and its air connectors","Exhaust ducts serving bathrooms and kitchens","Return plenums, any type"],"expl":"Air Duct: a passageway for air, excluding plenums not installed in accordance with SMACNA Standards.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-41"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0273","stem":"Before an inspection, what shall the inspector have a clear understanding of?","options":["The impact of the inspection on the building and occupants","The containment level the IEP has specified","The Condition status of each building material","The client's approved budget for the cleaning"],"expl":"1.2.1 Risk Assessment: the inspector shall understand what impact the inspection may have on the building environment and occupants.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2.1-a","acr:1.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0274","stem":"What does the Standard say about more frequent inspections when geographic, human, or mechanical conditions warrant them?","options":["Recommended, shortening the Table 1 intervals","Required for marine and healthcare systems","Required, shortening Table 1 intervals","May be waived by the building owner"],"expl":"1.5.1.1: recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0275","stem":"What shall the AHU cleanliness inspection include?","options":["Filters and air bypass","Return dampers, return grilles, and plenums","Bathroom exhaust fans and heat recovery units","Reheat coils and VAVs"],"expl":"1.7.1 lists filters and air bypass among the AHU items. Reheat/VAV are supply, dampers/plenums are return, exhaust is 1.7.5.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Inspections","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0276","stem":"Which products shall be listed on the work plan?","options":["All chemical type products and coatings","Chemical type products only, not coatings","Products the client has approved in writing","Only coatings, sealants, and mastics used"],"expl":"2.7: all general use and/or chemical type products and coatings shall be clearly listed.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7-a","acr:2.7-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0277","stem":"Documentation that products were submitted to the client for review ____.","options":["Can also be included in the written work plan","Shall be signed off by the client's IEP","Shall be attached to the closeout report","Shall be included in the written work plan"],"expl":"2.7.1: can also be included. Permissive.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"Work Plans","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0278","stem":"Recognized method to verify duct surface cleanliness after cleaning?","options":["NADCA Vacuum Test with gravimetric analysis","ATP surface swab read on a field luminometer","Airborne particle count taken at the supply diffuser","Tape lift sample examined under direct microscopy"],"expl":"The vacuum test with gravimetric analysis is the standard's quantitative surface method.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5-M3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0279","stem":"What is the primary verification method under the standard?","options":["Visual inspection of cleaned components","Owner sign-off at the closeout walk","Airborne sampling before and after","Gravimetric testing of duct surfaces"],"expl":"Visual inspection is primary. Gravimetric is the quantitative backup when visual is disputed or specified.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2.1-a","acr:5-M1"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0280","stem":"A pre-cleaning assessment is performed primarily to do what?","options":["Determine system condition and scope","Establish a baseline particle count","Price the job accurately","Satisfy insurance documentation"],"expl":"Condition determines scope and method. Pricing is a downstream consequence, not the purpose.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.10-c","acr:1.0-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0281","stem":"During assessment you find no access to a section of duct. Correct?","options":["Create access as needed to clean the system","Recommend replacement of the inaccessible run","Clean adjacent runs and assume migration","Exclude the section and note it"],"expl":"Creating access is normal work. Excluding it silently misrepresents the cleaning.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-create-access"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0282","stem":"A specification calls for gravimetric verification. How should samples be taken?","options":["From representative locations","From the dirtiest visible location","From the easiest spots to reach","At the air handler, not the ducts"],"expl":"Representative sampling. Cherry-picking the worst or the easiest both invalidate the result.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-representative-sampling"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0283","stem":"Which finding during assessment most clearly changes the scope of work?","options":["Material suspected to contain asbestos in the system","An undersized filter rack at the air handler","A blower wheel heavily loaded with debris","Heavy settled dust throughout the return side"],"expl":"Suspect ACM stops work and brings in other trades. The rest are ordinary findings.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-suspect-acm-scope"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0284","stem":"What should post-cleaning documentation record?","options":["The work scope, cleaning methods, findings, and verification results","The invoice totals, crew timesheets, labor hours, and mileage","The customer's stated satisfaction with the completed work","A list of the cleaned components, without verification"],"expl":"The record has to reconstruct what you did and how you proved it.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-closeout-documentation"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0285","stem":"A visual inspection after cleaning shows a light film on interior surfaces. Correct?","options":["Reclean the affected surfaces, then reinspect","Coat the surfaces with a sealant to bind the residue","Verify by gravimetric test before deciding","Accept it, since some residue is expected"],"expl":"Visual is the primary standard and it failed. You reclean rather than testing your way past a visual failure.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-reclean-visual-failure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0286","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what is the minimum interval for visually inspecting HVAC systems in HEALTHCARE facilities for cleanliness?","options":["1 year","6 months","2 years","2.5 years"],"expl":"Table 1 recommends a 1-year interval for the AHU, supply, and return ducts in healthcare (and commercial and industrial) classifications.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0287","stem":"For a RESIDENTIAL building, what is the ACR's recommended minimum inspection interval for supply and return ducts?","options":["2 years","1 year","3 years","6 months"],"expl":"Residential: AHU every 1 year, but supply and return/exhaust ducts every 2 years (Table 1).","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0288","stem":"The cleanliness inspection shall include, at minimum, what portion of the HVAC system components?","options":["Ten percent","Twenty percent","Twenty-five percent","One hundred percent"],"expl":"The cleanliness inspection shall include at minimum 10% of the components, unless it's a microbial remediation project per S520, when ALL components are inspected.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0289","stem":"When an inspection is part of a microbial remediation project under ANSI/IICRC S520, how much of the system must be inspected?","options":["All components","10% of components","25% of components","50% of components"],"expl":"If conducted as part of a microbial remediation project per S520, ALL components of the HVAC system shall be inspected (not just 10%).","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7-d","acr:1.7-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0290","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, inspections shall be performed at which points relative to a cleaning project?","options":["Before and after","Solely before the work","Solely after the work","Neither before nor after"],"expl":"Inspections shall be performed both before and after HVAC cleaning and restoration; routine inspections are also recommended.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.1-a","acr:1.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0291","stem":"Which certification is specifically named by the ACR as a qualified HVAC inspector?","options":["NADCA Ventilation Inspector (CVI)","EPA 608 Universal certification","OSHA 30-hour Construction card","LEED AP building certification"],"expl":"The ACR names ASCS, CVI, or equivalent as qualified to determine preliminary component cleanliness.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0292","stem":"If suspect microbial contamination (mold) is observed during inspection, what should the ASCS do?","options":["Note a preliminary Condition","Ignore it if minor","Immediately fog with biocides","Shut the building down"],"expl":"It's recommended a preliminary Condition determination be noted and the cause/extent further assessed by an IEP or appropriate professional.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9-a","acr:1.9-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0293","stem":"What does Condition 1 describe in the ACR/S520 framework?","options":["Normal fungal ecology for that space","Actual active microbial growth on surfaces","Settled spores from a Condition 3 area","Category 3 water from flooding"],"expl":"Condition 1 is a normal fungal ecology, may have settled spores or traces, but reflective of normal for a similar indoor environment.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-18"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0294","stem":"Condition 2 describes an environment primarily contaminated with what?","options":["Settled spores from elsewhere","Actual visible microbial growth","Solely construction dust","Refrigerant vapor"],"expl":"Condition 2 is primarily settled spores/fungal fragments dispersed (directly or indirectly) from a Condition 3 area, possibly with traces of growth.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-20","acr:D-21"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0295","stem":"Condition 3 describes an environment with what?","options":["Actual microbial growth, active or dormant","Normal fungal ecology for that space","Settled spores, but no actual growth","No detectable fungal contamination"],"expl":"Condition 3 is actual microbial growth, active or dormant, visible or hidden, with associated spores and fragments.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-22"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0296","stem":"Which task is an Indoor Environmental Professional (IEP) NOT qualified to do?","options":["Perform the mechanical duct cleaning as the ASCS","Determine Condition 1, 2, and 3 status","Create a sampling strategy and interpret lab data","Verify return of fungal ecology to Condition 1"],"expl":"An IEP assesses fungal ecology, samples, interprets data, and determines Conditions, a role distinct from the ASCS performing the cleaning.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-84","acr:D-83"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0297","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, which set lists only items an air handling unit cleanliness inspection shall include?","options":["Filters, coils, fans, and dampers","Filters, refrigerant charge, and controls","Coils, compressor, and refrigerant charge","Dampers, controls, wiring, and the compressor"],"expl":"ACR 1.7.1: the AHU inspection shall include filters and air bypass, heating and cooling coils, condensate pans and drains, humidification, acoustic insulation, fans and fan compartments, dampers and door gaskets, and general unit integrity. Each wrong set adds refrigerant charge, a compressor or controls, none of which is on that list.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.1-b","acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0298","stem":"If an AHU inspection reveals suspect microbial contamination, what shall happen to the supply and return ducts?","options":["Inspected during that same inspection","Inspected afterward per Table 1 intervals","Inspected at the next service visit","Cleaned without further inspection"],"expl":"Per 1.9.1, if the AHU shows suspect microbial contamination, the supply and return ducts shall be inspected during that same inspection rather than on interval.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0299","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, how shall both building floor plans and mechanical plans, if available, be used?","options":["Throughout inspection and cleaning","Solely on commercial and institutional jobs","Solely after the job is done","They remain optional at the ASCS's discretion"],"expl":"The pre-inspection uses mechanical and building plans; both floor and mechanical plans, if available, shall be used during inspection, cleaning, and restoration.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.3-b","acr:1.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0300","stem":"The system component assessment must include a recommendation on the need for cleaning and what else?","options":["A defined scope and required engineering controls","A fixed price quote and the payment terms","A project schedule and the crew assignments","A refrigerant charge log and equipment service history"],"expl":"The assessment includes need-to-clean recommendation, a defined scope of work, recommended techniques, required engineering controls, and any unique requirements.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.10-c","acr:1.10-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0301","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what must the inspector understand before conducting an inspection?","options":["The impact on the occupants","The building's resale value","The owner's credit score","The duct manufacturer's warranty terms"],"expl":"The 1.2.1 risk assessment requires the inspector to understand the impact the inspection may have on the building environment and its occupants.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0302","stem":"During an inspection, what are appropriate engineering controls chiefly used to manage?","options":["The release of settled particulate","Lighting levels in the work zone","Water temperature for pressure washing","Fan speed of the air handling unit"],"expl":"Inspection can disturb settled particulate; appropriate engineering controls manage the workspace to prevent release into occupied areas.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.4-b","acr:1.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0303","stem":"If a Method 1 visual inspection is inconclusive or disputed, what is the recommended next step?","options":["Move to Method 2 or Method 3","Coat the surface and re-inspect","Declare the component clean","Document the result and stop"],"expl":"When visual inspection is inconclusive/disputed, Method 2 (Surface Comparison Test) or Method 3 (NADCA Vacuum Test) is recommended to verify cleanliness.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0304","stem":"How does the Standard treat exhaust air ducts (general, bathroom, heat recovery)?","options":["Part of the ventilation system","Excluded from the inspection scope","Solely inspected in hospitals","Left uncleaned as a matter of course"],"expl":"General, bathroom, and heat-recovery exhaust are part of the ventilation system and part of the overall inspection (1.7.5).","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.5-a","acr:1.7.5-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0305","stem":"The NADCA Surface Comparison Test or Vacuum Test may be performed specifically to do what?","options":["Determine the need to clean","Set the price for the project","Replace the work plan","Certify the technician"],"expl":"Per the 'Determining the Need' note, if there's any question on the need to clean, Method 2 or Method 3 may be performed.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-17"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0306","stem":"At minimum, what must a qualified HVAC inspector have a verifiable working knowledge of?","options":["Basic HVAC system design and the applicable industry standards","Sales and marketing practice for HVAC service work","The electrical code as it applies to HVAC equipment","Refrigerant handling and recovery in HVAC equipment"],"expl":"At minimum the inspector shall have verifiable working knowledge of basic HVAC design, fundamental engineering practices, current cleaning/restoration techniques, and applicable standards.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.2-b","acr:1.2-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0307","stem":"Information gathered from an HVAC inspection is used primarily to do what?","options":["Define the scope of work and engineering controls","Set the job price and the crew schedule for it","Confirm the system meets the current mechanical code","Certify the system for the manufacturer's warranty"],"expl":"ACR 1.10: inspection information is documented and evaluated to give the need for cleaning, the scope of work, the recommended techniques and the engineering controls. Pricing, code compliance and warranty paperwork are not what the inspection is for.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.0-b","acr:1.0-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0308","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what is the recommended AHU inspection interval for a COMMERCIAL building?","options":["1 year","2 years","3 years","6 months"],"expl":"Commercial classification recommends 1-year intervals across the AHU, supply, and return/exhaust ducts.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-f","acr:1.5.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0309","stem":"For a cleaning and restoration project, when should system inspections occur?","options":["Before the work and again after it is completed.","After the work, once the system is back in service.","Before the work, unless the customer disputes it.","During the work, while under negative pressure."],"expl":"ACR requires inspections before and after cleaning or restoration.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.1-a","acr:1.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0310","stem":"Which set gives the routine residential inspection intervals?","options":["AHU yearly; supply duct every two years; return or exhaust duct every two years.","AHU every two years; supply duct yearly; return or exhaust duct yearly.","AHU every two years; supply duct every two years; return or exhaust duct yearly.","AHU yearly; supply duct every three years; return or exhaust duct every two years."],"expl":"Residential intervals are one year for the AHU and two years for supply and return or exhaust ductwork.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0311","stem":"Which routine interval applies to commercial, industrial, and healthcare HVAC components?","options":["One year for the AHU and both duct systems.","Two years for the AHU and one year for both duct systems.","One year for the AHU and two years for both duct systems.","Two years for the AHU and for both duct systems."],"expl":"These building classifications use a one-year routine interval for all three component groups.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0312","stem":"Which group belongs in an AHU cleanliness inspection?","options":["Filters, coils, pan and drain, fans, dampers, and seals.","Coils, compressors, refrigerant piping, condensers, and meters.","Thermostats, switches, room finishes, plumbing traps, and roofing.","Filters, registers, return grilles, diffusers, sensors, and thermostats."],"expl":"An AHU inspection includes the listed internal components and overall integrity.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.1-c","acr:1.7.1-g","acr:1.7.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0313","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what should a completed system assessment produce before cleaning begins?","options":["Documented conditions, recommendations, scope, and access needs.","Register counts, room dimensions, thermostat settings, and filter dates.","A chemical list, verification results, a warranty, and photographs.","A price estimate, equipment age, the complaint, and a target date."],"expl":"The assessment should support a documented plan of action and scope.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.10-c","acr:1.10-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0314","stem":"New ductwork was exposed to construction dust. When should it be inspected?","options":["Before the HVAC system is permitted to operate.","After the first scheduled filter replacement interval.","Solely if debris later appears at occupied-space outlets.","After the HVAC system has operated for one full week."],"expl":"Construction-affected components should be inspected before system operation.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0315","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, an inspection may disturb settled particulate above an occupied area. What is required?","options":["Use engineering controls to manage the inspection workspace.","Delay controls until the inspection confirms that cleaning is needed.","Run the building fan at full speed to dilute the particulate.","Rely on personal protective equipment instead of controls."],"expl":"Inspection itself can disturb contaminants, so engineering controls may be needed.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0316","stem":"During inspection, a damaged fan guard is discovered. What is the correct action?","options":["Document the damaged or unsafe condition found.","Remove it and keep the fan running during inspection.","Call it particulate and include it in verification.","Ignore it; mechanical repair is outside the scope."],"expl":"Damaged or unsafe components observed during inspection should be documented.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.7.6-a","acr:1.7.6-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0317","stem":"Under the IICRC S520 and NADCA standards, how is 'Condition 2' mold contamination defined?","options":["Settled spores dispersed from a Condition 3 area","A normal fungal ecology matching outdoor levels","Active growth on porous structural surfaces","Heavy growth inside an air handling unit pan"],"expl":"Condition 2 is contamination primarily by settled spores or fragments dispersed from a Condition 3 area, and it may also show traces of actual growth. Condition 1 is normal fungal ecology; Condition 3 is actual growth.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 definitions; ANSI/IICRC S520","units":["acr:D-20","acr:D-21"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0318","stem":"Under ACR 2025, a routine inspection finds suspect microbial contamination in an air handling unit. What else is required?","options":["Inspect the supply and return ducts in the same visit","Clean the AHU within 30 days of the finding","Order air sampling before any other action","Keep the Table 1 interval for the ductwork"],"expl":"ACR 1.9.1: suspect microbial contamination in the AHU triggers inspection of the supply and return ducts during that same inspection, and that inspection replaces the Table 1 interval. Keeping Table 1 is the trap; this finding overrides it.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.9.1","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0319","stem":"Suspect microbial contamination is observed in an air-handling unit. What should occur during the same inspection?","options":["Inspect both the supply and return duct systems.","Inspect the outdoor-air intake and exhaust solely.","Inspect solely the upstream return duct system.","Inspect solely the downstream supply duct system."],"expl":"When suspect microbial contamination is seen in the AHU, supply and return ducts are inspected during the same inspection.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.9.1-b","acr:1.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0320","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard Table 1, what is the recommended minimum visual cleanliness inspection interval for supply ductwork in a commercial building?","options":["Every year","Every 6 months","Every 2 years","Every 3 years"],"expl":"Under ACR Table 1, commercial, industrial, and healthcare building classifications require a visual inspection of the AHU, supply ductwork, and return/exhaust ductwork at a minimum interval of 1 year.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5.1-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0321","stem":"Which findings suggest a ceiling cavity is functioning as an open air RETURN plenum?","options":["Transfer ducts, transfer grilles, and louvered doors","A condensate drain, P-trap, and pan","External duct wrap, banding, and jacketing","A VAV box, reheat coil, and controller"],"expl":"The white paper lists transfer ducts, transfer grilles, fire dampers, and louvered doors as indicators of an open air return plenum.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-return-indicators"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0322","stem":"Why must ceiling tile removal in a plenum-return building be managed carefully during inspection?","options":["Removing tiles disrupts the designed return airflow","The tiles are presumed to contain asbestos","The tiles are structurally load-bearing","The tiles are presumed to harbor mold growth"],"expl":"In a ceiling plenum with tiles, removing tiles disrupts airflow patterns; integrity of the ceiling and adjacent walls must be maintained, especially near exhausted spaces.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-ceiling-tiles"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0323","stem":"Why does particulate accumulate throughout an open air plenum over time?","options":["The air moving through it is generally unfiltered","Plenums are pressurized above 2 in. w.c.","Plenums are cleaned solely at commissioning","Plenums carry no return air pathway"],"expl":"Air moving through an open air plenum is generally unfiltered, so dust and dirt fall out of the airstream and deposit on everything inside.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-unfiltered-air"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0324","stem":"Compared with sealed ductwork, what makes open air plenums distinctive?","options":["They hold wires, pipes, and other systems that are not HVAC","They are sealed to a tighter SMACNA leakage class than ducts","They are built from sheet metal to SMACNA duct standards","They carry exhaust, relief, and outdoor air, not return air"],"expl":"Plenums are used for purposes beyond air movement and typically hold light fixtures, fire suppression, extensive data cabling, wiring, plumbing, insulation, and other materials.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-non-hvac-contents"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0325","stem":"When inspecting a multizone unit, what do leaking zone dampers most directly cause?","options":["Simultaneous heating and cooling","Condensate pan overflow","Filter bypass at the rack","Blower belt misalignment"],"expl":"Damper leakage between hot and cold decks blends air unintentionally, wasting energy and producing temperature complaints across zones.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-multizone-damper-leak"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0326","stem":"What are gaps around a filter rack that let unfiltered air pass around the media called?","options":["Filter bypass","Dust loading","Media migration","Face velocity"],"expl":"Filter bypass lets unfiltered air, and the particulate it carries, reach the coil and downstream ductwork regardless of the filter's MERV rating.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-filter-bypass","hvac:a-bypass"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0327","stem":"A client reports black particulate settling on desks from the diffusers. What should the ASCS pay closest attention to during inspection?","options":["The condition of the internal duct liner or insulation","The condition of the external duct wrap or jacket","The calibration of the zone thermostats","The condensate P-trap at the air handler"],"expl":"Black fallout at diffusers classically indicates deteriorating internal fibrous liner shedding into the airstream, so the liner condition is the first thing to assess.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-black-fallout-liner"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0328","stem":"Which drawing set is most useful for locating AHUs, duct routing, and terminal units before an inspection?","options":["Mechanical plans","Structural plans","Site plans","Fire protection plans"],"expl":"Mechanical drawings show equipment locations, duct routing, terminal units, and zoning, which is what an inspection walk-through needs.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-mechanical-plans-walkthrough"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0330","stem":"During inspection the ASCS finds visible microbial growth on porous duct liner covering roughly 40 square feet. What is the appropriate next step?","options":["Treat it as remediation, define scope, involve an IEP","Apply an antimicrobial, dwell, then continue routine cleaning","Proceed as routine cleaning, since it is under 100 sq ft","Encapsulate the liner, and note it in the ACR record"],"expl":"Porous material with active growth is removed, not treated. The project becomes remediation, with containment and an IEP protocol where the situation warrants.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-growth-remediation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0331","stem":"A visual inspection reveals standing water in the drain pan and a slimy biofilm. What is the most likely root cause to document?","options":["A clogged or untrapped condensate drain","An undersized return grille starving the blower","A filter efficiency above the design MERV","Duct static pressure above the rated class"],"expl":"Standing water almost always traces to a blocked drain line or an improperly configured P-trap; cleaning the pan without fixing the drain guarantees recurrence.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0332","stem":"Before quoting a commercial project, what is the single most important reason to physically inspect rather than estimate?","options":["Identify access, layout, and conditions","Photograph the exterior, roof, and equipment pad","Meet the facility contact and tour the site","Confirm the building's utility rate schedule"],"expl":"Access, system configuration, and existing conditions determine equipment, labor, containment, and risk. Estimating without inspection produces unworkable scopes.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-inspect-before-quote"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0333","stem":"An inspection finds the AHU interior heavily fouled but the supply ducts visually clean. What is the ACR-consistent recommendation?","options":["Base the scope on each component's documented condition","Automatically clean the entire system as one scope","Defer the cleaning until the next season","Replace the supply ductwork as found fouled"],"expl":"Scope follows documented condition, component by component, with findings communicated so the customer can make an informed decision.","domain":"Assessments","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-scope-by-component"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0334","stem":"What shall be done to equipment before it is brought onto the work site?","options":["Cleaned and inspected","Tagged with a product SDS","Sealed and wrapped for transport","Given an on-site maintenance check"],"expl":"3.1.1: cleaned and inspected so it does not introduce contaminants. On-site verification (3.1.4) is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0335","stem":"What does the Standard say about on-site maintenance verification of vacuum collection equipment before work starts?","options":["Recommended","Strictly required","Not addressed","May be omitted"],"expl":"3.1.4: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0336","stem":"During a project, servicing or changing filters on contaminated vacuum equipment shall occur ____.","options":["In a containment area or outside of the building","In the mechanical room with its door closed","In a clean corridor next to the work area","In the occupied space next to the opening"],"expl":"3.1.2.1: in an appropriate containment area or outside of the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.2.1-a","acr:3.1.2.1-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0337","stem":"What shall be done to collection devices before relocating them to another area of the building?","options":["Cleaned or sealed","HEPA vacuumed inside and out","Re-verified for airflow","Bagged and labeled"],"expl":"3.1.3: cleaned or sealed before relocating and before removal from the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0338","stem":"What HEPA filtration rating shall vacuum equipment exhausting within the building envelope have?","options":["99.97% at 0.3 micron","95% at 0.3 to 1.0 micron","99.99% at 0.3 micron","99.97% at 0.1 micron"],"expl":"3.3 and the HEPA definition: 99.97% collection efficiency at 0.3 micron.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.3-b","acr:D-27"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0339","stem":"What does the Standard require for negative pressure in the portion of the system being cleaned?","options":["Continuous and verifiable at representative points","Logged at fifteen-minute intervals on a recording manometer","Measured once at the collection device inlet at startup","Held at 0.02 inches water column relative to the corridor"],"expl":"3.4: continuous, and verifiable at representative locations during cleaning. ACR gives no numeric value.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.4-a","acr:3.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0340","stem":"In general, what does the Standard say about ambient air cleaning with HEPA scrubbers during and after work?","options":["Recommended","Not permitted","Strictly required","May be waived"],"expl":"3.6: recommended as a supplemental control. It becomes a shall inside Level 2 containment (3.10.7).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0341","stem":"What does the Standard say about having redundant equipment on site in case of negative pressure failure?","options":["Recommended","May be deferred","Strictly required","Not addressed"],"expl":"3.8: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0342","stem":"What does the Standard require for fuel-powered equipment such as generators and vacuum trucks?","options":["Positioned to keep emissions out of occupied space","Located at least 25 feet from any outside air intake","Fitted with catalytic exhaust scrubbers and monitored","Shut down whenever building occupants are present"],"expl":"3.2: positioned to prevent combustion and exhaust emissions from entering occupied space, and monitored during the project.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.2-a","acr:3.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0343","stem":"What is the minimum containment level that shall be used on every cleaning project?","options":["Level 1, with negative pressure","Level 2, with temporary barriers","Level 3, with a decon chamber","The level the IEP sets for the job"],"expl":"3.9: Level 1 is the minimum on all projects.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.9-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0344","stem":"Which is one of the three Level 1 containment requirements?","options":["Protective coverings beyond the work area","A manometer validating negative pressure","HEPA scrubbing at four air changes per hour","Temporary barriers isolating the work area"],"expl":"Level 1 = negative pressure, protective coverings, cross-contamination control. Barriers, manometer, and 4 ACH are Level 2.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3-L1","acr:3.9.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0345","stem":"Under Level 2, what does the Standard say about constructing temporary barriers to isolate the work area?","options":["Required","Recommended only","Optional","Banned"],"expl":"3.10.2: barriers shall be constructed. Their material, height, and sealing are the recommended parts.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.2-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0346","stem":"Under Level 2, what does the Standard say about building the barriers from 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene?","options":["Recommended","May be omitted","Not addressed","Strictly required"],"expl":"3.10.2.3: recommended. The floor (3.10.3) is where 6-mil FR poly is mandatory.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.2.3-a","acr:X-3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0347","stem":"Under Level 2, what shall the containment floor consist of?","options":["A two-layer floor of 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene","Two layers of 4-mil poly with taped seams","One layer of 6-mil fire retardant poly, sealed to walls","Ram board under one layer of 6-mil poly"],"expl":"3.10.3: two-layer floor of 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene or equivalent.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0348","stem":"Under Level 2, what is the minimum distance the floor material shall extend up the side walls?","options":["Six inches","Twelve inches","Eighteen inches","To the ceiling"],"expl":"3.10.3: at least 6 inches (15.2 cm) and be sealed to the walls.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0349","stem":"When a zipper is not practical, how shall a vertical cut in the containment wall be handled?","options":["Covered by two flaps, one on each side","Limited to 36 inches tall","Covered by a single weighted flap on the outside","Sealed with tape after entry, and after exit"],"expl":"3.10.4.1: the vertical cut shall be entirely covered by two flaps, one on each side.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0350","stem":"Under Level 2, what shall be used to validate negative pressurization?","options":["A manometer or airflow measuring device","A smoke pencil at the flap","A particle counter at the barrier perimeter","Visual observation of the poly bowing inward"],"expl":"3.10.6: a manometer or airflow measuring/monitoring device shall be used.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.6-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0351","stem":"Under Level 2, what minimum rate shall ambient air cleaning maintain?","options":["Four air changes per hour","Four air changes per work shift","One air change per twenty minutes","One air change per thirty minutes"],"expl":"3.10.7: HEPA-filtered scrubbing at minimum four (4) air changes per hour.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.7-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0352","stem":"Before dismantling a Level 2 containment, what shall be done to its interior surfaces?","options":["Wet-wiped and/or HEPA vacuumed","Fogged with an antimicrobial","Sampled with the NADCA Vacuum Test","Inspected by the IEP"],"expl":"3.10.8: wet-wiped and/or HEPA vacuumed before moving or dismantling.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.8-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0353","stem":"In a healthcare setting, what shall happen before a Level 2 containment comes down?","options":["A post-remediation verification of the work area shall be performed","Infection control shall sign off on the written work plan","The AHJ shall inspect the enclosure before it is removed","Airborne particulate shall return to the background level"],"expl":"3.10.8: in the healthcare environment an appropriate post-remediation verification shall be performed prior to dismantling.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.8-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0354","stem":"What does Level 3 containment add beyond Level 2?","options":["A decontamination unit with a single chamber","A decontamination unit with two chambers","Full-time oversight of the work by an IEP","A constant recording pressure monitor with an alarm"],"expl":"3.11: single chamber decon unit. Level 4 has two chambers plus the recording monitor and alarm.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11-a","acr:3-L3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0355","stem":"Under Level 3, what does the Standard say about background total particulate monitoring before containment setup?","options":["Required","Not addressed","Optional","Advised"],"expl":"3.11.3: background monitoring shall be performed prior to set-up. Monitoring during set-up is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0356","stem":"Under Level 3, what shall happen if airborne particulate exceeds background during the work?","options":["Work shall cease until levels return to background","The client shall be notified within 24 hours in writing","Scrubber capacity shall be doubled and logged","The containment shall be upgraded to Level 4"],"expl":"3.11.3: work shall cease until levels are reduced to background and the cause is corrected.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-g","acr:3.11.3-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0357","stem":"Which requirement is unique to Level 4 containment?","options":["Constant recording pressure monitor with alarm","Single flap zipper access into the decon chamber","Continuous negative pressure monitoring by instrument","Real-time total particulate monitoring"],"expl":"3.12.3: Level 4 adds a constant recording pressure monitor with an appropriate alarm. The others exist at Level 3.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.12.3-b","acr:3-L4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0358","stem":"Contaminated materials removed from the system shall be properly contained ____.","options":["Prior to removal from the building","Before the end of each shift and each day","Inside the decon chamber before disposal","Prior to off-site transport"],"expl":"3.5: properly contained prior to removal from the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0359","stem":"What shall any application of cleaning agents or chemicals follow?","options":["Manufacturer's procedures and instructions","The NADCA position paper on chemical products","The IEP's written protocol for the project site","The client's written product approval and SDS"],"expl":"3.7: in strict accordance with manufacturer's recommended procedures and product application instructions, including exhaust ventilation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.7-a","acr:3.7-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0360","stem":"At minimum, what shall all cleaning and restoration procedures achieve?","options":["Visibly clean or the contracted verification level","A passing Surface Comparison Test on porous surfaces","Condition 1 fungal ecology on airside surfaces","A passing NADCA Vacuum Test on each component"],"expl":"4.0: the minimum level of visibly clean or the specified verification level in the contract documents.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.0-a","acr:4.0-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0361","stem":"When is verifying the negative pressure differential during the project required?","options":["Under all circumstances","At Level 2 and above","When exhausting indoors","When a manometer is available"],"expl":"4.1.1: under all circumstances, you shall verify pressurization differential during the project.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0362","stem":"Negative pressure equipment without HEPA filtration shall ____.","options":["Be exhausted outdoors","Not be used on occupied projects","Discharge to the return","Be fitted with a MERV 16 final filter"],"expl":"4.1.3: all equipment used to create negative duct pressurization that lacks HEPA shall be exhausted outdoors.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1.3-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0363","stem":"Service openings in the system shall not ____.","options":["Hinder, restrict, or alter airflow","Exceed 12 inches, 300 mm, in any dimension","Be made in fibrous glass duct board or liner","Be placed within 3 feet of any damper"],"expl":"4.2.3: shall not hinder, restrict, or alter airflow. No size limit is given; fibrous glass openings are allowed with proper closure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0364","stem":"Service panels shall be mechanically fastened at minimum every ____.","options":["4 inches on center","1 foot on center","6 inches on center","10 inches on center"],"expl":"4.2.5.2: screwed or riveted at minimum every 4 inches on center.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0365","stem":"What is the minimum overlap of service panels over the ductwork on all sides?","options":["1 inch","4 inches","2 inches","1/2 inch"],"expl":"4.2.5.2: minimum 1 inch overlap on all sides.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0366","stem":"Service panels shall be of what gauge relative to the duct?","options":["Equivalent or heavier, to protect duct integrity","One gauge lighter than the duct wall","Per the pressure class table in SMACNA","Any gauge, if mechanically fastened"],"expl":"4.2.5.1: equivalent gauge or heavier so as not to compromise the duct. Pressure class governs prefab access doors (4.2.6).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a","acr:X-15"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0367","stem":"What standard shall tapes and mastics used to close service openings meet?","options":["UL 181A/B","UL 181","UL 723","ASTM E84 Class A"],"expl":"4.2.5.4: UL 181A/B. UL 181 governs surface treatments (4.15.1). UL 723 governs flame spread of door materials.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.4-a","acr:X-13"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0368","stem":"What shall the gauge of a prefabricated duct access door be based on?","options":["The duct system's pressure class","The gauge of the surrounding duct wall","The size of the opening being closed","The SMACNA seal class of the duct run"],"expl":"4.2.6: based on the pressure class of the duct system and installed per the manufacturer.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.6-a","acr:X-15"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0369","stem":"What is the maximum flame-spread rating for access door materials exposed to the airstream?","options":["Twenty-five","Fifty","Seventy-five","One hundred"],"expl":"4.2.7: flame spread not over 25, smoke developed not over 50, per UL 723.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.7-b","acr:X-14"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0370","stem":"What is the maximum smoke-developed rating for access door materials exposed to the airstream?","options":["Fifty","Seventy-five","One hundred","Two hundred"],"expl":"4.2.7: smoke-developed rating not over 50, per UL 723. Flame spread is limited to 25.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.7-c","acr:X-14"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0371","stem":"What shall be done with fibrous glass removed to create a service opening?","options":["Repaired or replaced with like material of equal thickness","Sealed with a UL 181 listed coating over the exposed cut edges","Covered by a rigid duct board patch of any available thickness","Discarded and the opening lined with sheet metal of any gauge"],"expl":"4.2.8.2: repaired or replaced with like material of the same thickness so R-value, service rating, and vapor barrier are not degraded.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.8.2-a","acr:4.2.8.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0372","stem":"What does the Standard say about service openings in flexible ductwork?","options":["Prohibited","Not addressed","Permitted at the collar only","Permitted with a listed closure"],"expl":"4.2.9: service openings shall not be made in flexible ductwork.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.9-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0373","stem":"What does the Standard say about following the manufacturer's instructions when disconnecting and reattaching flex duct?","options":["Recommended","Not permitted","Strictly required","May be waived"],"expl":"4.2.9.1: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0374","stem":"How shall HVAC systems be cleaned?","options":["Agitation to dislodge, then vacuum capture","Wet process cleaning with full waste water capture","Vacuum collection alone, at sufficient velocity","Wet cleaning followed by a UL 181 surface treatment"],"expl":"4.3: a suitable agitation device to dislodge non-adhered substances, then capture with a vacuum collection device.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.3-a","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0375","stem":"What shall cleaning agents or water never be applied to?","options":["Electrical, fibrous glass, or other porous components","Galvanized sheet metal older than twenty years","Any coil section without a condensate drain pan","Dampers, turning vanes, and air-directional devices"],"expl":"4.4: never on electrical, fibrous glass, or other porous HVAC components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.4-c","acr:4.4-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0376","stem":"During cleaning, when shall vacuum collection equipment be operated?","options":["Continuously","During agitation","Whenever occupants are present","In alternating cycles with agitation"],"expl":"4.5: operated continuously during cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0377","stem":"In obligation terms, how does the Standard classify Table 2 duct design velocities?","options":["Recommended","Not addressed","Strictly required","May be deferred"],"expl":"4.5.1: Table 2 defines recommended velocities. Sufficient velocity is the shall.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.5.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0378","stem":"What is the Table 2 velocity for average industrial dust such as grinding dust or silica flour?","options":["3,500 to 4,000 fpm","3,000 to 3,500 fpm","4,500 fpm and up","Any desired velocity"],"expl":"Average industrial dust is 3,500 to 4,000 fpm. Dry dusts and powders are 3,000 to 3,500. Heavy or moist dusts are 4,500 and up. Any desired velocity is the entry for vapors, gases, and smoke.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:T2-r5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0379","stem":"What is the Table 2 velocity for heavy or moist dusts such as quick-lime dust?","options":["4,500 fpm and up","4,000 to 4,500 fpm","2,000 to 2,500 fpm","Any desired"],"expl":"Heavy or moist dusts: 4,500 fpm and up. Heavy dusts: 4,000 to 4,500. Welding fumes: 2,000 to 2,500. Vapors, gases, and smoke: any desired.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:T2-r7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0380","stem":"Table 2 is reprinted from which source?","options":["ACGIH Industrial Ventilation manual","SMACNA HVAC Duct Construction Standards","ASHRAE Standard 62.1, Ventilation","ASHRAE Fundamentals Handbook"],"expl":"Table 2 is reprinted from ACGIH Industrial Ventilation: A Manual of Recommended Practice.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:T2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0381","stem":"What does the Standard say about wet cleaning coils, fans, condensate pans, and drains together with mechanical methods?","options":["Recommended","Strictly required","May be omitted","Not addressed"],"expl":"4.6: recommended that these non-porous AHU surfaces be wet cleaned in conjunction with mechanical methods.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0382","stem":"Waste water from wet cleaning shall be captured and disposed of according to what?","options":["The applicable local, regional, state, and federal regulations","The client's discharge permit and the site rules","The product label, SDS, site rules, and specifications","The IEP's written remediation protocol for the project"],"expl":"4.6.2: in accordance with applicable local, regional, state, and federal regulations.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6.2-a","acr:4.6.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0383","stem":"Air ducts shall be accessed through service openings large enough to do what?","options":["Allow mechanical cleaning and verification","Pass a 2.5-inch nylon brush head on a 1.5-inch hose","Admit a robotic inspection camera and its tether","Insert the 100 cm2 NADCA Vacuum Test template"],"expl":"4.7.1: large enough to accommodate mechanical cleaning procedures and allow for cleanliness verification.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.7.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0384","stem":"What shall be done with the position of dampers and air-directional devices?","options":["Marked before cleaning and restored to that mark after","Set to full open for the duration of cleaning","Verified by the test and balance contractor","Photographed and logged in the final report"],"expl":"4.8: marked prior to cleaning and restored to the marked position after.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.8-a","acr:4.8-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0385","stem":"For registers, grilles, and diffusers, ACR makes which action mandatory?","options":["Restoring them to their previous position after cleaning","Replacing any that show damage or corrosion","Cleaning them off site in a wash station","Removing them from the duct for cleaning"],"expl":"4.9: removal and cleaning is recommended if possible; restoration to previous position is shall.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.9-b","acr:4.9-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0386","stem":"What does the Standard say about cleaning activities affecting smoke or fire detection equipment?","options":["Shall not impair, alter, or damage the equipment","Shall disable the equipment with client consent","Shall bypass the equipment during agitation","Shall be scheduled, tested, and logged with the alarm company"],"expl":"4.10: cleaning shall not impair, alter, or damage smoke and fire detection equipment. Off-line handling is a work plan item (2.8).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.10-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0387","stem":"What shall all products used for odor control comply with?","options":["Local, state, and federal regulations","The client's green, low-VOC, fragrance-free policy","The IEP's approved product list for the site","The NADCA position paper on chemical products"],"expl":"4.12: comply with any local, regional, state, and federal regulations and other laws regulating such agents.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.12-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0388","stem":"When coil cleaning is performed, which sides shall be accessed?","options":["Both upstream and downstream of each section","The leaving-air side of each coil section","Whichever side is nearest the drain pan","The entering-air side of each coil section"],"expl":"4.11: both upstream and downstream sides of each coil section shall be accessed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0389","stem":"What happens if both sides of a coil cannot be accessed for cleaning?","options":["Removal and/or replacement may be required","Pressure washing from the accessible side is required","Type 2 cleaning shall be performed from one side","The coil may be left in place, with documentation"],"expl":"4.11: removal and/or replacement may be required.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0390","stem":"Before cleaning a coil, what shall a visual inspection of the coil and pan determine?","options":["Whether Type 1 or Type 2 is required","Whether fin straightening is needed","Whether the coil is Condition 1, 2, or 3","The static pressure drop baseline"],"expl":"4.11.1: to determine whether Type 1 (Dry) or Type 2 (Wet) cleaning is required.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1-b","acr:4.11.1-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0391","stem":"What does suspect microbial contamination on any portion of the coil or drain pan require?","options":["Type 2 (Wet Cleaning)","Removal and replacement of the coil","Sampling by an IEP before cleaning","Type 1 plus antimicrobial"],"expl":"4.11.1.2: Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0392","stem":"If cleaning a coil with damaged, corroded fins will cause further damage, what does the Standard say about replacement?","options":["Recommended","At the client's option","Strictly required","May be deferred"],"expl":"4.11.1.3: replacement may be necessary; if cleaning will cause further damage, replacement is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1.3-b","acr:4.11.1.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0393","stem":"During Type 1 (Dry) coil cleaning, the coil shall be ____.","options":["Isolated from the duct system to stop migration","Cleaned from the leaving-air side","Dry for at least 24 hours prior","Enclosed in a Level 2 containment"],"expl":"4.11.2: isolated from the duct system so disrupted particulate does not migrate or redeposit; negative air machines run continuously.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.2-c","acr:4.11.2-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0394","stem":"Which is listed as a Type 1 (Dry Cleaning) method?","options":["Compressed air","Coil cleaning products","Water washing at line pressure","Steam washing"],"expl":"4.11.2 lists HEPA contact vacuuming, fin brushes, compressed air, and fin straightening tools. The others are Type 2 methods.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.2-g"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0395","stem":"After Type 1 cleaning, what shall happen if debris remains or the coil is impacted?","options":["Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed on the coil","Type 1 shall be repeated and the coil re-inspected","A static pressure drop test shall be taken","The coil shall be removed and replaced"],"expl":"4.11.3: Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.3-b","acr:4.11.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0396","stem":"Which is listed as a Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) method?","options":["Pressure washing equipment","Brushes between fins","Fin straightening tools","HEPA contact vacuuming"],"expl":"4.11.4 lists coil products, water at line pressure, pressure washing equipment, hot water or steam. The others are Type 1.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0397","stem":"During Type 2 coil cleaning, what shall be done with the condensate drain pan?","options":["Inspected and drainage tested before and after","Treated with an EPA-registered pan tablet after cleaning","Replaced if biofilm or standing water is present","Drainage tested once, after coil cleaning is complete"],"expl":"4.11.4.1: pan and line cleaned and flushed; pan inspected and tested to verify drainage before and after.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0398","stem":"What does the Standard say about coil cleaning solutions that are as close to pH neutral as possible?","options":["Recommended","Required for electric coils","May be waived","Not addressed"],"expl":"4.11.4.2: recommended. Non-corrosive detergents are the shall for electric resistance coils (4.11.8).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0399","stem":"What happens if debris remains on a coil after Type 2 (Wet Cleaning)?","options":["The Type 2 process shall be repeated","The coil shall be removed and replaced","A surface treatment shall be applied","The coil is documented as adhered"],"expl":"4.11.5: the process shall be repeated. Replacement may be necessary only when Type 2 cannot remove it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.5-b","acr:4.11.5-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0400","stem":"What is ACR's recommended way to demonstrate coil cleaning effectiveness?","options":["A static pressure drop measured before and after the cleaning","The NADCA Vacuum Test performed on the coil fin face","The supply air temperature split measured before and after cleaning","A visual inspection with a light held behind the coil"],"expl":"4.11.6: visual observation can be misleading; a static pressure drop measurement before and after is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.6-b","acr:4.11.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0401","stem":"When wet cleaning a duct-mounted inline coil without a drain pan, precautions shall be taken to do what?","options":["Capture rinse water","Bypass the coil with a temporary duct","Use Type 1 only","Isolate the coil electrically"],"expl":"4.11.7: precautions shall be taken to capture rinse water when wet cleaning duct-mounted coils without drain pans.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.7-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0402","stem":"Before cleaning electric resistance coils, what shall be done with the power source?","options":["De-energized and locked out and tagged out","Switched off at the thermostat","Reduced to control voltage","Set to fan mode at the thermostat"],"expl":"4.11.8: de-energized and locked out/tagged out.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0403","stem":"If electric resistance coils are wet cleaned, what shall the detergent be?","options":["Non-corrosive","Alkaline","pH-neutral","Acid-based"],"expl":"4.11.8: only non-corrosive detergents shall be used; rinse free and dry thoroughly before re-energizing. pH neutral is the general REC.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Coils","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-b","acr:4.11.4.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0404","stem":"What shall remediating microbial contamination be performed in accordance with?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S520 and this Standard","ANSI/IICRC S500 and this Standard","ANSI/IICRC S590 alone","The EPA's mold remediation guidance"],"expl":"4.13: ANSI/IICRC S520 plus the ACR cleaning/restoration provisions.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.13-a","acr:4.13-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0405","stem":"When shall restoration procedures be performed?","options":["Only after mechanical cleaning","After cleanliness verification and client sign-off","Before mechanical cleaning to stabilize surfaces","During mechanical cleaning"],"expl":"4.14: restoration shall only be performed after mechanical cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0406","stem":"What does the Standard say about replacing components that cannot reach ACR cleanliness levels by cleaning and restoration?","options":["Recommended","Required by the Standard","Not addressed","May be deferred"],"expl":"4.14.3: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0407","stem":"What shall be done with surfaces unable to withstand proper mechanical cleaning?","options":["Documented and brought to the customer","Coated with a UL 181 treatment","Excluded from the scope of work in writing","Replaced before the project may be closed out"],"expl":"4.14.4: documented, brought to the attention of the customer, and recommended for repair or replacement.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14.4-a","acr:4.14.4-b","acr:4.14.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0408","stem":"How shall surface treatments used to restore material surfaces be applied?","options":["Only after the system is cleaned and passes verification","Only to non-porous components in the AHU","Only after cleaning, before verification begins","Only under Level 2 containment or higher"],"expl":"4.15: shall only be applied after confirming the system has been cleaned and passed the specified cleanliness verification.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.15-c","acr:4.15-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0409","stem":"Surface treatments shall meet the requirements of which standard?","options":["UL 181","UL 181A/B","UL 723","UL 94"],"expl":"4.15.1: UL 181. 181A/B is for closure tapes and mastics.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.15.1-a","acr:4.15.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0410","stem":"What does the Standard say about removing and replacing porous materials with Condition 3 growth?","options":["Recommended","Not addressed","May be omitted","Required at Condition 3"],"expl":"4.16: recommended. The mechanical surface cleaning that follows the removal is the shall.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.16-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0411","stem":"After removal of microbially contaminated porous material, what shall follow?","options":["Mechanical surface cleaning","Application of an antimicrobial","Air sampling to confirm Condition 1","A NADCA Vacuum Test on the base metal"],"expl":"4.16: this task shall be followed by surface cleaning using mechanical cleaning methods.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.16-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0412","stem":"What shall mechanical cleaning of fibrous glass liner or duct board not create?","options":["Abrasions, breaks, or tears","Changes in color or surface sheen","Stains, streaks, or uneven color","Odors released from the liner"],"expl":"4.17.1: shall not create abrasions, breaks, or tears.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.17.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0413","stem":"What does the Standard say about resurfacing or coating friable or abraded fibrous glass?","options":["May be considered","Required","Recommended","Prohibited"],"expl":"4.18: resurfacing/coating may be considered when components are friable or show abrasion or degradation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.18-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0414","stem":"Before coating fibrous glass, what shall an assessment determine?","options":["Whether the surface will provide a strong bond","Whether the R-value will be maintained after coating","Whether the coating is EPA registered for HVAC","Whether the liner has returned to Condition 1"],"expl":"4.18.1: whether the surface will provide a strong, bondable surface after proper mechanical cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.18.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0415","stem":"If fibrous glass cannot support a surfacing product or provide a long-term bond, what does the Standard say about coating?","options":["Shall not be performed","May be performed with client consent","Is not recommended","Shall be limited to two coats"],"expl":"4.18.2: resurfacing/coating shall not be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.18.2-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0416","stem":"When damage or friability means neither cleaning nor coating can restore fibrous glass, what does the Standard say about replacement?","options":["Recommended, not required","Required, within 30 days","Required","May be considered"],"expl":"4.19: replacement is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.19-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0417","stem":"Before new insulation goes in where degraded liner was removed for reasons other than mold, the metal surfaces shall ____.","options":["Be scraped clean and free of loose visible debris","Be sampled and confirmed at Condition 1 status","Pass the NADCA Vacuum Test at 0.75 mg/100 cm2","Be primed with a UL 181 listed coating before insulation"],"expl":"4.20: base surface scraped clean and free of loose, visible debris. Condition 1 applies when the removal was for mold (4.20.1).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20-a","acr:4.20-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0418","stem":"When liner was removed because of microbial contamination, what shall the base surface be cleaned to?","options":["A Condition 1 status","A sanitized surface","A Condition 2 status","A passing Method 3 result"],"expl":"4.20.1: cleaned to a Condition 1 status prior to reapplying fibrous glass.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0419","stem":"What shall installation of new thermal-acoustic insulation in the airstream comply with?","options":["SMACNA, NAIMA, and applicable codes","ASHRAE 180, IESO/RIA 6001, and IICRC S520","ANSI/IICRC S520 and NIOSH Method 0500","ANSI/IICRC S590 and NIOSH Method 0500"],"expl":"4.20.3: current SMACNA, NAIMA, and other applicable codes and standards.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0420","stem":"What does the Standard say about restoration of a non-porous surface that keeps shedding particulate after cleaning?","options":["Recommended, not required","Required, not optional","May be considered","Required before startup"],"expl":"4.21: restoration is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.21-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0421","stem":"What shall be used to categorize flood-damaged HVAC components?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S500","ANSI/IICRC S520","IESO/RIA 6001","ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180"],"expl":"4.22: industry recognized methods such as ANSI/IICRC S500. S520 is mold, ASHRAE 180 is inspection and maintenance, 6001 is fire particulate.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.22-b","acr:4.22-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0422","stem":"What shall porous surfaces be evaluated for after fire or smoke damage?","options":["Friability and odor retention","Condition 2 or Condition 3 growth","Water category per ANSI/IICRC S500","Static pressure drop across the surface"],"expl":"4.23.1: evaluated following mechanical cleaning for friability and odor retention.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0423","stem":"What shall be done with fire-damaged porous areas found friable or retaining odor?","options":["Resurfaced, or removed and replaced","Deodorized with ozone treatment","Documented and left in service","Cleaned again, then re-evaluated"],"expl":"4.23.2: shall be resurfaced or replaced.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0424","stem":"What does the Standard say about sampling to determine whether a component holds soot or smoke per IESO/RIA 6001?","options":["Recommended","Required after any fire","Not addressed","May be waived"],"expl":"4.23.4: recommended when there is a question.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0425","stem":"What shall be done with pre-existing damage found during cleaning?","options":["Documented and brought to the client's attention","Repaired before cleaning may continue in that section","Reported to the AHJ before cleaning resumes","Photographed, sealed, and noted in the work plan"],"expl":"4.24: documented and brought to the attention of the client. Repair of malfunctioning devices is outside scope (4.24.1).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.24-a","acr:4.24-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0426","stem":"Which is explicitly excluded from ACR restoration?","options":["Sealing air leaks within duct systems","Replacing damaged thermal-acoustic liner","Scraping base metal clean","Resurfacing fibrous glass"],"expl":"4.24.1: restoration does not include sealing air leaks; repair of malfunctioning mechanical devices is also out.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.24.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0427","stem":"What is cleanliness verification designed to determine?","options":["The presence of particulate","The presence of microbial contamination","Condition 1 compliance","The presence of hazardous materials"],"expl":"5.0: it determines the presence of particulate; it does not determine microbial contamination or hazardous materials.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.0-a","acr:5.0-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0428","stem":"When shall cleanliness verification be performed?","options":["Immediately after cleaning, before the system is run","Within 48 hours, following a complete operating cycle","After one full cycle of system operation","Before and after cleaning on each component"],"expl":"5.1: immediately after cleaning and prior to system operation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.1-a","acr:5.1-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0429","stem":"What does Method 1, visual inspection, apply to?","options":["Porous and non-porous components","Non-porous components but not porous ones","Porous components but not non-porous ones","Sheet metal ducts but not fibrous glass liner"],"expl":"5.2: visual inspection of porous and non-porous components shall be conducted.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5-M1","acr:5.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0430","stem":"What happens if a component is visibly clean under Method 1?","options":["No further verification is necessary","Method 3 is recommended","Documentation shall include photographs","Method 2 shall confirm it"],"expl":"5.2: if visibly clean, no further cleanliness verification methods are necessary.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0431","stem":"If Method 1 is inconclusive or disputed, what does the Standard say about using Method 2 or Method 3?","options":["Recommended","Required when disputed","Not addressed","May be deferred"],"expl":"5.2.1: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0432","stem":"What may Method 2, the Surface Comparison Test, be used on?","options":["Porous and non-porous surfaces","Porous surfaces but not non-porous ones","Non-porous surfaces but not porous ones","Coil and drain pan surfaces but not liner"],"expl":"5.3: may be used on both porous and non-porous surfaces. Method 3 is non-porous only.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3-a","acr:5-M2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0433","stem":"In Method 2, how many times shall the brush be passed over the test area?","options":["Four times","Six times, three each way","Once in each direction","Until no debris is seen"],"expl":"5.3.1: four (4) times with light to moderate pressure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-b","acr:X-5"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0434","stem":"At minimum, what shall the HEPA filtered Method 2 contact vacuum be capable of?","options":["80 inches of static lift","120 inches of static lift","60 inches of water lift","100 inches of static lift"],"expl":"5.3.1: minimum 80 inches of static lift (WC).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-e"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0435","stem":"What shall the Method 2 contact vacuum be fitted with?","options":["A 2.5-inch nylon brush on a 1.5-inch hose","A 2-inch bristle brush on a 2-inch hose","A 1.5-inch nylon brush on a 2.5-inch hose","A 3-inch round nylon brush on a 1.25-inch hose"],"expl":"5.3.1: 2.5 inch round nylon brush attached to a 1.5 inch diameter vacuum hose.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-f","acr:5.3.1-g"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0436","stem":"Under Method 2, when is a surface considered clean?","options":["No significant visible difference before and after","Four passes produce no visible dust cloud in the light","The collected debris weighs no more than 0.75 mg/100 cm2","The brush shows no visible loading after four passes"],"expl":"5.3.2: clean when there is no significant visible difference in the surface characteristics.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0437","stem":"If Method 2 is inconclusive, which obligation term does the Standard apply to using Method 3 for a final determination?","options":["May","Shall","Recommended","Prohibited"],"expl":"5.3.3: Method 3 may be used. Method 1 to 2/3 is recommended; 2 to 3 is may.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.3-a","acr:5.2.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0438","stem":"What does the NADCA Vacuum Test not apply to?","options":["Porous components such as duct board","Flexible duct inner liner","Galvanized sheet metal","Aluminum foil surfaces"],"expl":"5.3.3 and 5.4: non-porous only. Foil and polymeric flex liner are defined as non-porous.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.3-b","acr:D-13"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0439","stem":"In Method 3, the vacuum cassette ____.","options":["Shall be held clear of the tested surface","Shall contact the surface on each return pass","May contact the surface if calibrated","Shall be pressed lightly against the surface"],"expl":"5.4: at no time can the cassette contact the component surface; the template holds it above.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4-e","acr:5.4.2-l"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0440","stem":"At minimum, what shall the Method 3 air sampling pump be capable of drawing?","options":["15 liters per minute","17 liters per minute","5 liters per minute","1.5 liters per minute"],"expl":"5.4.1: capable of a minimum of 15 L/min. The flow is then adjusted to 17 ±2 during sampling.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-a","acr:X-4"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0441","stem":"In Method 3, what shall airflow be adjusted to?","options":["17 liters per minute, plus or minus 2","15 liters per minute, with no tolerance","15 liters per minute, plus or minus 2","17 liters per minute, plus or minus 5"],"expl":"5.4.2: 17 L/min ±2. The ±5 figure is the calibration device accuracy (5%).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-h","acr:X-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0442","stem":"How accurate shall the Method 3 calibration device be?","options":["±5% at 15 liters per minute","±5% at 17 liters per minute","±2 liters per minute of setpoint","±2% at 15 liters per minute"],"expl":"5.4.1: accurate to ±5% at 15 L/min.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-f","acr:X-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0443","stem":"What filter media shall be used for Method 3?","options":["37 mm MCE matched-weight filters","25 mm PVC pre-weighed filters in series","37 mm PTFE filters, 2 micrometer pore","47 mm glass fiber filters, two-piece cassette"],"expl":"5.4.1: 37 mm mixed cellulose ester matched weight filters, 0.8 or 0.45 micrometer pore, in a three-piece cassette.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0444","stem":"What sampling area does the NADCA Vacuum Test template provide?","options":["100 square centimeters","1,000 square centimeters","50 square centimeters","25 square centimeters"],"expl":"5.4.1: 100 cm2 from two 2 cm x 25 cm slots at least 2.5 cm apart.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0445","stem":"What do the standard NADCA Vacuum Test template slots measure?","options":["2 cm by 25 cm","5 cm by 5 cm","1 cm by 50 cm","2.5 cm by 20 cm"],"expl":"5.4.1.1: 2 centimeters wide by 25 centimeters long.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1.1-a","acr:5.4.1-i"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0446","stem":"The NADCA Vacuum Test template shall be how thick?","options":["15 mil","20 mil","1.5 mil","6 mil"],"expl":"5.4.1: 15 mil (0.381 mm). 6 mil is containment poly.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-g"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0447","stem":"If an alternate template shape is used, what is the maximum slot width?","options":["3.7 cm","2.0 cm","25 cm","10.0 cm"],"expl":"5.4.1.2: max width 3.7 cm so the cassette cannot touch; minimum width 2.0 cm; total area 100 cm2.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1.2-c","acr:5.4.1.2-e"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0448","stem":"What is the required state of the air handler during Method 3 sampling?","options":["Shut off for the duration of sampling","Running at the design airflow rate","Running at low speed to hold negative pressure","Running with the outside air damper shut"],"expl":"5.4.2: the air-handler shall not be running; the surface shall be dry; the template shall lay flat.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0449","stem":"In Method 3, what is the maximum speed the cassette shall move across each slot?","options":["5 cm per second","2.5 cm per second","10 cm per second","1 m per second"],"expl":"5.4.2: not greater than 5 cm/s, 5 seconds per slot each direction, each slot vacuumed twice.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-j"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0450","stem":"How shall each Method 3 template slot be vacuumed?","options":["Twice, once in each direction","Four times, alternating","Until no debris is visible","Once, end to end"],"expl":"5.4.2: twice (once in each direction).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-m","acr:X-5"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0451","stem":"What shall Method 3 analysis be based on?","options":["NIOSH Method 0500","EPA Method 5","NIOSH Method 7400","ASTM D5755 microvacuum"],"expl":"5.4.2: NIOSH Method 0500, total nuisance dust.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-q"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0452","stem":"Method 3 scale sensitivity shall be equal to or greater than what value?","options":["0.7 milligram","1 milligram","0.1 milligram","0.75 milligram"],"expl":"5.4.2: 0.7 mg. Do not confuse with the 0.75 mg/100 cm2 pass criterion.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-r","acr:5.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0453","stem":"What net debris weight shall not be exceeded to pass the NADCA Vacuum Test?","options":["0.75 mg per 100 cm2","0.075 mg per 100 cm2","0.7 mg per 100 cm2","1.00 mg per 100 cm2"],"expl":"5.5: shall not exceed 0.75 mg/100 cm2.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0454","stem":"What does the Standard say about having Method 3 samples collected by the client's designated qualified person and analyzed by an accredited lab?","options":["Recommended","May be omitted","Not addressed","Required by the Standard"],"expl":"5.4.2: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-u","acr:5.4.2-v"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0455","stem":"What does the Standard say about providing post-project documentation showing compliance?","options":["Recommended","Not addressed","Required for each project","May be waived"],"expl":"5.6: recommended. What becomes shall inside it: lab results when the vacuum test was used, and chain of custody when an outside lab was used.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0456","stem":"If the NADCA Vacuum Test was used for verification, what shall post-project documentation include?","options":["A copy of the lab results","The IEP's interpretation","The calibration certificate for the pump","Photographs of each template location"],"expl":"5.6.1: a copy of the lab results shall be included.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0457","stem":"If an outside laboratory was used, what shall post-project documentation add?","options":["Chain of custody documentation","The sampling technician's credentials","A second confirmatory sample","The lab's accreditation certificate"],"expl":"5.6.2: chain of custody documentation shall be added.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0458","stem":"What does the Standard say about including photos, HVAC plans, and submittal forms in post-project documentation?","options":["Recommended","Not addressed","May be deferred","Required at closeout"],"expl":"5.6.3: recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6.3-a","acr:5.6.3-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0459","stem":"Under Level 1, protective coverings shall ____.","options":["Extend beyond the work area where needed","Cover the immediate work area only","Be two layers of 6-mil fire retardant poly","Be removed before cleanliness verification"],"expl":"3.9.2: coverings shall extend beyond the work area to protect flooring, equipment, and furniture whenever necessary.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.9.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0460","stem":"How is zipper-type access with a single flap on a Level 2 containment described?","options":["The most common work practice","A mandatory requirement","Prohibited where a decon chamber is used","A recommended practice for healthcare"],"expl":"3.10.4: described as the most common work practice. The shall applies to the two-flap cut alternative.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.4-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0461","stem":"What does the Standard require of the Level 3 decontamination chamber?","options":["Attached and sealed directly to the containment","Vented outdoors through HEPA filtration","Located within 10 feet of the containment entrance","Built of two chambers with a recording monitor"],"expl":"3.11.2: attached and sealed directly to the containment area, separated by a zipper with single flap or two flaps.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0462","stem":"Under Level 3, what does the Standard say about particulate monitoring during containment set-up?","options":["Recommended, but not required","May be waived on site","Required, not optional","Required in healthcare"],"expl":"3.11.3: background monitoring before set-up is shall; monitoring during set-up is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-e","acr:3.11.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0463","stem":"How are ladders and staging equipment addressed in ACR?","options":["Equipment that shall be maintained in good order","Work plan safety concerns requiring others' input","Items outside the Standard's scope entirely","Recommended safety practices for field crews"],"expl":"3.1: all contractor equipment shall be maintained in good working order, including ladders and staging equipment.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1-g","acr:3.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0464","stem":"What is included among the engineering controls listed as 'may include' in 3.0?","options":["Temperature control and humidity control","A respirator program for workers","Post-remediation verification in healthcare","Chain of custody documentation for samples"],"expl":"3.0: source control, isolation barriers, pressure differentials, dust suppression, HEPA, detailed cleaning, temperature and humidity control, sanitary approach.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.0-f","acr:3.0-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0465","stem":"What does the Standard require for the location of fuel-powered equipment?","options":["Monitored and managed during the project","Approved by the fire marshal first","Recorded on the work plan","At least 50 feet from air intakes"],"expl":"3.2.1: location shall be monitored and managed during a project to prevent combustion emissions from entering occupied space.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Engineering Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.2.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0466","stem":"What shall service panels be sealed with?","options":["Gaskets, duct sealants, mastic, or tape","Screws, rivets, clips, or drive cleats","A UL 181 listed surface coating","Rivets plus a one-inch perimeter overlap"],"expl":"4.2.5.3: gaskets, duct sealants, mastic or tape. Fastening every 4 inches is a separate shall.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.3-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0467","stem":"How is the use of pressure washing equipment on coils classified?","options":["A listed Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) method","A listed Type 1 (Dry Cleaning) method","Prohibited as a damaging wet process","Permitted on inline coils that lack pans"],"expl":"4.11.4 lists pressure washing equipment under Type 2. The shall is that it not damage the coil or fins.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4-f"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0468","stem":"How is wet cleaning of inline coils described?","options":["Normally required","At the contractor's option after Type 1","Recommended","Prohibited without a condensate drain pan"],"expl":"4.11.7: wet cleaning processes using pressurized water and chemical agents are normally required for inline coils; rinse water shall be captured.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0469","stem":"What must water extraction during AHU wet cleaning be sufficient to accomplish?","options":["Collect debris and prevent water damage","Meet the local sewer discharge permit limits","Return the drain pan to Condition 1 status","Achieve a passing NADCA Vacuum Test result"],"expl":"4.6.1: sufficient to collect debris and prevent water damage to components and structure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0470","stem":"What shall service openings comply with?","options":["UL, IMC, SMACNA, NFPA, and local codes","IICRC, AIHA, IAQA, RIA, and the IEP","ANSI/IICRC S520 and the IEP's written protocol","ASHRAE 62.1 and the duct maker's instructions"],"expl":"4.2.10: applicable UL, IMC, SMACNA and NFPA standards and applicable codes.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Cleaning Procedures","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.10-a","acr:4.2.10-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0471","stem":"Any cleaning process that would damage a sound, properly installed system ____.","options":["Shall not be performed","May be performed with client consent","Is recommended against","Requires IEP approval"],"expl":"4.20.5: no cleaning process shall be performed that will damage a properly designed, installed, structurally sound system or its life expectancy.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0472","stem":"After a fire, what shall be done with a component surface that still shows heat damage after cleaning?","options":["Restored or replaced","Coated with a UL 181 surface treatment","Sampled per IESO/RIA 6001 before cleaning","Documented, left in service"],"expl":"4.23.3: restored to an acceptable condition or replaced.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0473","stem":"Replacement insulation materials shall ____.","options":["Meet or exceed the original or current code","Be approved by the IEP before installation","Match the original R-value and thickness exactly","Be NAIMA certified and foil faced"],"expl":"4.20.2: meet or exceed the specifications of the original materials or current applicable codes.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0474","stem":"What shall be done with compromised components found during restoration?","options":["Addressed to the extent possible","Excluded from the scope of work in writing","Reported to the AHJ","Replaced before cleanliness verification"],"expl":"4.14.2: addressed as part of the restoration procedure to the extent possible.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Restoration","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14.2-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0475","stem":"What shall be done with Method 3 cassettes?","options":["Sealed with shrink tape by the supplier","Pre-weighed on site by the technician","Sealed with shrink tape by the technician","Opened and visually inspected before use"],"expl":"5.4.2: cassettes shall be sealed with shrink tape by the supplier.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0476","stem":"How is the Method 3 template secured to the surface?","options":["Taping at four corners","Holding it by hand against the duct","Magnetic strips along both long edges","Adhesive backing"],"expl":"5.4.2: secured so it will not shift, i.e., taped at four corners.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0477","stem":"In what units shall Method 3 results be reported?","options":["Milligrams per 100 square centimeters","Milligrams per square meter of duct","Particles per cubic foot of air sampled","Micrograms per cassette collected"],"expl":"5.4.2: mg/100 cm2 of sampling area.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-t"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0478","stem":"Air ducts shall be cleaned so they are ____.","options":["Capable of passing NADCA cleanliness verification tests","Free of both adhered and non-adhered substances","Restored to their original as-built condition","Sampled by Method 3 at representative points"],"expl":"4.7: cleaned to remove all non-adhered substances and capable of passing NADCA cleanliness verification tests.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"Verification","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.7-b","acr:4.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0479","stem":"Return plenum is lined with fiberglass duct board. Liner is intact but heavily loaded with settled dust. No moisture, no visible growth. Owner wants it cleaned, not replaced. Correct?","options":["Clean it in place with HEPA contact vacuuming","Replace it; porous liner cannot be cleaned","Encapsulate rather than clean, to avoid fiber release","Air wash only; contact tools damage liner"],"expl":"Dry, intact porous liner may be cleaned in place by contact vacuuming. Removal is driven by moisture, growth, or damage, not by dust load.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-clean-in-place","ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0480","stem":"Same return plenum, but the liner is water damaged and delaminating. Owner still wants it cleaned. Correct?","options":["Remove and replace the damaged liner","Clean, then seal with an EPA-registered coating","Clean and apply an antimicrobial rated for porous surfaces","Dry it, then contact vacuum"],"expl":"Damaged or wet porous material is removed, not cleaned or coated. Coating over damaged liner does not restore it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers","ascs:p-liner-water-damaged"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0481","stem":"You are cleaning a supply trunk. Which finding makes removal and replacement of porous liner the ACR-recommended action?","options":["Visible mold growth on the liner","Liner age beyond fifteen years","Odor reported by occupants","Settled dust exceeding a visual threshold"],"expl":"4.16: Condition 3 growth on porous material makes replacement RECOMMENDED (not shall). Dust, odor, and age alone do not.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-removal-triggers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0482","stem":"Antimicrobial use in HVAC systems. Which matches ACR 2025 plus the NADCA position paper?","options":["Applied only after cleaning and verification, per label and regulations","Applied after cleaning on all duct surfaces, porous or not","Applied at the technician's discretion whenever an odor is present","Fogged into the ducts before cleaning to suppress airborne release"],"expl":"ACR 4.15: surface treatments only after cleaning AND passing verification; 3.7 manufacturer instructions; 4.12 regulations. Position paper: EPA-registered, label is the law.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-chem-epa-registration","ascs:p-chem-after-cleaning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0483","stem":"A product label says 'for use on non-porous HVAC surfaces.' The duct is internally lined. What may you do?","options":["Not apply it to the liner","Apply at half strength to limit absorption","Apply it if the owner signs a waiver","Apply it, since the liner faces a non-porous plenum"],"expl":"Off-label application is a violation regardless of consent. Owner permission does not override FIFRA.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-chem-label-is-law","ascs:p-chem-label-surfaces","ascs:p-epa-no-fiberglass-biocide"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0484","stem":"What is required when closing access openings cut in sheet metal duct?","options":["Duct integrity, seal and rating are restored","The opening is covered, then the system restarts","A UL 181 listed tape patch is enough to close it","The closure stays removable for future service access"],"expl":"Closure restores structural integrity, air tightness, insulation, and any fire rating. Tape alone does not.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.1-a","acr:4.2.1-b","ascs:p-fire-rated-closure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0485","stem":"You cut an access panel through a duct with a fire-resistive rating. What does closure require?","options":["A listed access door of the same rating","A sheet metal patch sealed with mastic","A screwed steel patch sealed with foil tape","Any panel, since the rating applies to the shaft"],"expl":"Penetrating a rated assembly requires a closure listed to restore that rating.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-fire-rated-closure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0486","stem":"During cleaning of an occupied office, what condition should the system be kept in?","options":["Under continuous negative pressure","Cycled on and off to loosen adhered debris","Shut down with the openings taped and sealed","Run at design airflow to carry debris out"],"expl":"Continuous negative pressure with collection exhausting outdoors or through HEPA is what keeps debris out of the occupied space.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.4-a","acr:3.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0487","stem":"Vacuum collection equipment on a job will exhaust inside the building envelope. What does ACR 2025 require of it?","options":["HEPA filtration, 99.97% at 0.3 micron","MERV 8 filtration, the 62.1 coil minimum","MERV 11 filtration, the 62.1 PM2.5 floor","MERV 16 filtration, the ASHRAE 170 OR floor"],"expl":"ACR 3.3 and 4.1.2: vacuum equipment that exhausts inside the building envelope must be HEPA filtered, 99.97% at 0.3 micron. The MERV values are real rules for other things (62.1 coil filters, 62.1 outdoor air in PM2.5 areas, ASHRAE 170 operating rooms), not collection exhaust.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §3.3, §4.1.2","units":["acr:3.3-a","acr:4.1.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0488","stem":"Correct sequence when cleaning a system with an air handler, supply, and return?","options":["Sequence is not fixed; prevent cross-contamination of cleaned components","Air handler first so that all downstream debris is captured at the coil","Return first, then supply, then the air handler, in that fixed order","Supply side first, then the return, then the air handler"],"expl":"The standard requires you prevent recontamination of cleaned areas. It does not mandate one universal order.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-no-fixed-order"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0489","stem":"A fire damper sits mid-run in a duct you must clean. Correct action?","options":["Access both sides, clean, verify operation, leave in service","Clean around it with a flex shaft without disturbing the fusible link","Remove the damper, clean through, re-test it, then reinstall it","Wire the blade open so tools pass, then restore the link afterward"],"expl":"You clean it without damage and confirm it still functions. Removing, disabling, or skipping it are all wrong.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-fire-damper-midrun"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0490","stem":"Flexible duct with a torn inner core is found during cleaning. What is correct?","options":["Replace the torn section of flexible duct","Leave it, since core tears do not affect cleaning","Clean it, then repair the core with listed tape","Clean it, then sleeve the torn core"],"expl":"A breached flex core is not restorable by cleaning or patching. Replace the run.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-flex-torn-core"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0491","stem":"What is the rule for mechanically-driven agitation devices in fibrous glass lined duct?","options":["May be used if they will not damage the liner","May be used if the owner approves in writing","May be used if the liner is coated first","Are prohibited outright in internally lined duct"],"expl":"Not banned. The limit is damage to the liner, which is a tool-selection and technique question.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-mechanical-agitation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0492","stem":"Coil cleaning on a fouled evaporator. Which is correct?","options":["Clean from the leaving air side, back through the coil","Use only dry methods to protect the fins","Chemical cleaning first, then rinse to drain","Clean from the entering side to drive debris out the back"],"expl":"Cleaning against airflow, from the leaving side, pushes debris back out the way it entered.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-coil-leaving-side"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0493","stem":"A condensate drain pan holds standing water and biofilm. What is the correct scope?","options":["Clean the pan and verify drain and trap function","Apply an EPA-registered antimicrobial and move on","Replace the pan with a stainless steel one","Clean the pan, then take no further action"],"expl":"Cleaning without correcting drainage guarantees it comes back. Verify the trap and slope.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-drain-pan-root-cause"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0494","stem":"Under ACR 2025, how is a non-ducted open air plenum used as a return handled?","options":["Outside the Standard's scope; handled per the NADCA white paper if contracted","Cleaned as part of the system, including surfaces in the airstream","Inspected at the 10 percent minimum like any return component","Cleaned only if it contains debris visible from the access point"],"expl":"ACR 2025 Scope and Definitions: non-ducted open air plenums are not part of the HVAC system. Ducted/SMACNA return plenums ARE inspected (1.7.3).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-6","acr:D-69","acr:1.7.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0495","stem":"When cleaning a multi-zone unit, what should be done with the zone dampers?","options":["Positioned so each zone gets cleaned and dampers are undamaged","Disconnected from their actuators to allow cleaning tool passage","Locked full open for the duration of the cleaning work","Left in automatic control under the building management system"],"expl":"You have to reach every zone without damaging the dampers or their actuators. Locking or disconnecting risks both.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-multizone-dampers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0496","stem":"Which statement about system component cleaning is correct?","options":["All components of the HVAC system are cleaned","Only ductwork requires cleaning; equipment is optional scope","Terminal devices are excluded if not removable","Components upstream of the filter may be excluded"],"expl":"Whole-system cleaning is the premise. Partial cleaning recontaminates what you cleaned.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-whole-system-recontamination"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0497","stem":"A customer requests supply-side cleaning only, declining the return. What should you do?","options":["Perform it and document the limited scope and its implications","Refuse the job until the owner accepts a full system cleaning","Perform it and issue a certificate of full system cleaning","Clean the return anyway, since it will recontaminate the supply"],"expl":"Limited scope is allowed. What is not allowed is representing it as a whole-system cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-limited-scope-documented"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0498","stem":"What do portable electric tools used inside a metal duct require?","options":["Double insulation or GFCI protection","Battery power exclusively, with no corded tools permitted","Low-voltage transformers stepping down at the access point","A dedicated 20-amp circuit protected at the panel"],"expl":"Conductive enclosure plus electric tools is a shock hazard. GFCI or double insulation addresses it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-power-tools-in-duct"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0499","stem":"Which is NOT an acceptable method of debris collection?","options":["Portable vacuum exhausting into the mechanical room","Truck-mounted vacuum unit exhausting to the outdoors","Portable HEPA-filtered vacuum discharging inside the space","Point-of-contact vacuuming with HEPA capture at the tool"],"expl":"Exhausting unfiltered into an interior space contaminates the building. The mechanical room is inside.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-mech-room-is-indoors"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0500","stem":"After cleaning, an existing interior liner shows minor surface fuzzing but is dry and adhered. Correct?","options":["Leave it in service","Replace the liner","Coat it to lock down fibers","Encapsulate and document"],"expl":"Cosmetic surface fuzzing on dry, adhered liner is not a failure condition. Coating adds a maintenance liability.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-fuzzing-ok"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0501","stem":"A duct interior is coated with an oily residue from a kitchen exhaust cross-connection. How should cleaning be handled?","options":["Correct the cross-connection as part of the work","Defer it until the owner elects to replace the ductwork","Switch to dry methods to avoid spreading the oily residue","Limit it to the visibly affected run and adjacent fittings"],"expl":"Cleaning without fixing the source means it reloads. The defect is the cross-connection.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-fix-contamination-source"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0502","stem":"Which is the strongest justification for replacing rather than cleaning a duct section?","options":["The section is corroded through","Cleaning cost approaches replacement cost","The section has never been cleaned","The section is over thirty years old"],"expl":"Loss of integrity is a physical failure. Age and economics are not condition criteria.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-replace-when-failed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0503","stem":"When should containment in an occupied healthcare space be established?","options":["Before any system opening is made","Only if the area is rated for infection control","After the first access is cut","Once negative pressure is confirmed"],"expl":"Containment comes first. Cutting the first opening is when release begins.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-healthcare-containment-first"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0504","stem":"You find a previously applied duct coating that is peeling into the airstream. Correct?","options":["Remove the failed coating","Encapsulate with a compatible product","Recoat over it after cleaning","Vacuum the loose material and leave the rest"],"expl":"A failed coating shedding into the airstream is a contaminant source. Recoating over failure repeats it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-failed-coating"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0505","stem":"Which best describes the required condition of the system at completion?","options":["Visibly clean and free of debris","Coated to prevent future accumulation","Sanitized to a measured microbial threshold","Returned to as-built condition"],"expl":"The deliverable is a clean system verified by the standard's methods, not a sterilized or as-new one.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.0-a","acr:5.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0506","stem":"Cleaning is complete but the filter rack has no filter installed. What should you do?","options":["Install a filter as part of restoring the system","Note it and return the system to service","Install the highest MERV that fits","Leave it; filters remain the owner's responsibility"],"expl":"Returning a system to service with no filtration undoes the work. Match the system's design, not the highest MERV available.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-filters-at-completion"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0507","stem":"In the ACR Standard's fundamental cleaning method, after dislodging non-adhered substances with an agitation device, what is the next step?","options":["Capture it with a vacuum","Allow the debris to settle out","Blow them out the registers","Seal them behind a coating"],"expl":"HVAC systems are cleaned by using a suitable agitation device to dislodge non-adhered substances, then capturing them with a vacuum collection device (4.3).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.3-b","acr:4.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0508","stem":"Prior to and throughout the cleaning process, what pressure shall the duct system be kept at?","options":["Negative relative to the space","Positive pressure relative to the space","Atmospheric pressure","Any pressure the tech prefers"],"expl":"Section 4.1 requires an appropriate negative pressure differential between the portion being cleaned and surrounding indoor occupant spaces.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1-a","acr:4.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0509","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall the ASCS do under all circumstances during the project?","options":["Verify the differential","Assume negative pressure is present","Rely on the equipment specs","Skip verification for small jobs"],"expl":"Section 4.1.1: under all circumstances you shall verify pressurization differential during the project.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0510","stem":"Vacuum collection equipment exhausting INDOORS shall ____.","options":["Be HEPA-filtered and retain the dislodged debris","Vent to the return side of the air handler","Be fitted with a standard shop vacuum filter","Skip filtration if the space is unoccupied"],"expl":"Equipment exhausting indoors shall be HEPA-filtered and capable of retaining dislodged debris (4.1.2).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0511","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, how shall equipment used to create negative duct pressurization without HEPA filtration be handled?","options":["Exhausted outdoors rather than into the building","Used indoors with a pleated pre-filter","Run at low speed to limit dust release","Wrapped in plastic sheeting inside containment"],"expl":"All negative-pressurization equipment lacking HEPA filtration shall be exhausted outdoors (4.1.3).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.1.3-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0512","stem":"To be considered clean by the NADCA Vacuum Test, what is the maximum net weight of debris allowed on the filter media?","options":["0.75 mg/100 cm²","0.85 mg/100 cm²","1.0 mg/100 cm²","0.075 mg/100 cm²"],"expl":"Section 5.5: the net weight of debris collected shall not exceed 0.75 mg per 100 cm² to pass the NADCA Vacuum Test.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0513","stem":"The NADCA Vacuum Test applies to which components?","options":["Non-porous surfaces only","Porous component surfaces only","Both porous and non-porous","Only fibrous glass"],"expl":"The NADCA Vacuum Test (Method 3) scientifically evaluates particulate on NON-porous surfaces; it does not apply to porous components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5-M3","acr:5.3.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0514","stem":"Which verification method can be used on BOTH porous and non-porous surfaces?","options":["Method 2, known as the Surface Comparison Test","Method 3, known as the NADCA Vacuum Test","A static pressure drop taken across a coil","A particle count of the occupied room air"],"expl":"The Surface Comparison Test (Method 2) may be used to determine cleanliness of both porous and non-porous component surfaces.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3-a","acr:5-M2"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0515","stem":"In the Surface Comparison Test, the brush is passed over the test area how many times?","options":["Four times, with light to moderate pressure","Two times, with firm downward pressure","Three times, with light pressure","Six times, as in routine cleaning"],"expl":"Section 5.3.1: the brush is passed over the surface test area four (4) times using light to moderate pressure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0516","stem":"What minimum static lift must the HEPA-filtered contact vacuum used in the Surface Comparison Test be capable of?","options":["80 inches WC","60 inches WC","100 inches WC","8 inches WC"],"expl":"The testing contact vacuum shall be HEPA-filtered and capable of achieving a minimum of 80 inches of static lift (WC), with a 2.5-inch round nylon brush.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0517","stem":"When cleanliness is verified by visual inspection, an interior surface is considered visibly clean when it is ____.","options":["Free of non-adhered debris","Freshly painted and sealed","Coated with an antimicrobial","Below 0.75 mg/100 cm²"],"expl":"Visibly clean means the interior surfaces are free of non-adhered substances and debris (Method 1 / definitions).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2-b","acr:D-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0518","stem":"How does the ACR describe Level 1 containment?","options":["The minimum on all projects","The maximum containment level defined","Only for microbial jobs","Optional on residential work"],"expl":"Level 1 is the minimum containment that shall be used on all HVAC system component cleaning projects (3.9).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.9-a","acr:X-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0519","stem":"What material is recommended for temporary containment barriers under Level 2?","options":["6-mil fire retardant polyethylene","4-mil standard polyethylene sheeting","Corrugated cardboard","Gypsum drywall panels"],"expl":"Section 3.10.2.3 recommends containment barriers be erected from 6-mil fire retardant polyethylene sheeting or equivalent.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.2.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0520","stem":"How far, at minimum, must a Level 2 two-layer 6-mil containment floor extend up the side walls?","options":["6 inches (15 cm)","2 inches (5 cm)","4 inches (10 cm)","12 inches (30 cm)"],"expl":"Section 3.10.3: the two-layer 6-mil floor shall extend at least 6 inches (15.2 cm) up the containment side walls, sealed airtight.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0521","stem":"What instrument does the ACR require to validate negative pressurization of a containment?","options":["Manometer","Calibrated thermometer","Steel tape measure","pH test meter"],"expl":"Section 3.10.6: a manometer or airflow measuring/monitoring device shall be used to validate negative pressurization.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0522","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, ambient air cleaning in a Level 2 containment shall maintain a minimum of how many air changes per hour?","options":["4 ACH","None","6 ACH","10 ACH"],"expl":"Section 3.10.7: ambient air cleaning with HEPA-filtered air scrubbers shall maintain at minimum four (4) air changes per hour.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.7-b","acr:X-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0523","stem":"A Level 3 containment includes a decontamination unit with how many chambers?","options":["Single chamber","Two chambers","Three chambers","No decon unit required"],"expl":"Level 3 is a containment with a single-chamber decontamination unit (3.11).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0524","stem":"What distinguishes a Level 4 containment?","options":["A two-chamber decon unit","Exempt from monitoring requirements","Positive pressure","4-mil sheeting"],"expl":"Level 4 has a two-chamber decontamination unit and adds a constant recording pressurization monitor with an appropriate alarm (3.12).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.12-a","acr:3-L4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0525","stem":"How often shall Level 3 containment areas be monitored for negative pressure?","options":["Continuously while work is under way","Once at start-up, then again at teardown","At the start of each work shift","Weekly, or on the IEP's request"],"expl":"Section 3.11.3: Level 3 areas shall be continuously monitored for negative pressure with an instrument sensitive enough to detect a loss.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0526","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall first be done with the power source when cleaning electric resistance coils?","options":["De-energized and locked out/tagged out","Left energized for airflow verification","Increased to full operating load","Disconnected from the ground"],"expl":"Section 4.11.8: when cleaning electric resistance coils, the power source shall be de-energized and locked out/tagged out.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0527","stem":"When coil cleaning is performed, which sides of the coil must be accessed?","options":["Both upstream and downstream","Upstream solely","Downstream solely","Neither; clean through the duct"],"expl":"Section 4.11: both upstream and downstream sides of each coil section shall be accessed; if not accessible, removal/replacement may be required.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0528","stem":"What does Type 1 coil cleaning refer to?","options":["Dry cleaning","Wet chemical cleaning","Chemical fogging","Steam cleaning solely"],"expl":"Type 1 (Dry Cleaning) methods remove loose dust, dirt, or debris from coil surfaces, with negative air machines running continuously.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.2-a","acr:4.11.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0529","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, if a preliminary coil inspection reveals suspect microbial contamination on the coil or drain pan, which method is required?","options":["Type 2 (Wet Cleaning)","Type 1 (Dry Cleaning)","Encapsulation solely","Compressed air blowdown"],"expl":"Section 4.11.1.2: if suspect microbial contamination is found on any portion of the coil or drain pan, Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0530","stem":"During Type 1 dry coil cleaning, why shall the coil be isolated from the duct system?","options":["To keep the disrupted particulate out of the duct system","To improve airflow through the coil face and fins","To keep the coil cleaner off the duct liner","To let the coil fins dry out more thoroughly"],"expl":"The coil is isolated during Type 1 cleaning so disrupted particulate doesn't migrate to or redeposit on unintended areas (4.11.2).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.2-d","acr:4.11.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0531","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what is recommended for coil cleaning solutions?","options":["As close to pH neutral as possible","Highly acidic brightener","Highly alkaline degreaser","Applied undiluted at full concentration"],"expl":"Section 4.11.4.2 recommends only coil cleaning solutions as close to pH neutral as possible, that don't damage/erode the coil or fins.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0532","stem":"What does the ACR recommend obtaining to measure the effectiveness of coil cleaning objectively?","options":["A static pressure drop, before and after","A refrigerant charge check, before and after","A decibel reading at the air handler","A moisture meter reading of the coil fins"],"expl":"Because visual observation can mislead, a static pressure drop measurement before and after cleaning is recommended to demonstrate effectiveness (4.11.6).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.6-b","acr:4.11.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0533","stem":"Dampers and air-directional devices have their position marked prior to cleaning. What shall then be done with them?","options":["Restored to the marked setting","Left in the fully closed position","Removed and the position documented","Locked in the open position"],"expl":"Section 4.8: dampers shall have their position marked before cleaning and be restored to that marked position afterward.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.8-b","acr:4.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0534","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, cleaning agents or water shall NEVER be applied to which components?","options":["Electrical components","Bare sheet metal duct components","Non-porous coil components","Sheet metal access door components"],"expl":"Section 4.4: cleaning agents or water shall never be applied to electrical, fibrous glass, or other porous HVAC components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0535","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, service openings shall NOT be made in which type of ductwork?","options":["Flexible ductwork","Fibrous glass duct board","Spiral ductwork","Rigid sheet metal ductwork"],"expl":"Section 4.2.9: service openings shall not be made in flexible ductwork; manufacturers' instructions are followed for disconnecting/reattaching flex.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.9-a","hvac:a-flex-no-openings"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0536","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what minimum overlap onto the ductwork surfaces shall a service panel closing a service opening have?","options":["1 inch on all four sides","2 inches on all sides","4 inches on all sides","0.5 inch on all four sides"],"expl":"Section 4.2.5.2: the panel shall overlap the ductwork surfaces by a minimum of 1 inch on all sides.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0537","stem":"Service panels used for closing openings shall be of what gauge relative to the duct?","options":["An equivalent gauge to the duct, or heavier","One gauge lighter than the duct","Matched to the duct pressure class","Any gauge, if sealed with UL 181 tape"],"expl":"Section 4.2.5.1: service panels shall be of an equivalent gauge or heavier so as not to compromise the duct's structural integrity.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0538","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall service openings installed into the system NOT do?","options":["Degrade the thermal or functional integrity of the duct","Allow a proper closure after the cleaning","Comply with local codes and UL standards","Be large enough for the cleaning equipment"],"expl":"Section 4.2.1: service openings shall not degrade the structural, thermal, or functional integrity of the system components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0539","stem":"Besides removing all non-adhered substances, what shall cleaned air ducts be capable of?","options":["Passing NADCA verification tests","Holding positive pressure","Resisting UV light","Supporting technician foot traffic"],"expl":"Section 4.7: air ducts shall be cleaned to remove all non-adhered substances and be capable of passing NADCA cleanliness verification tests.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.7-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0540","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, restoration procedures shall only be performed at what point?","options":["After the mechanical cleaning is complete","Before the mechanical cleaning begins","In place of mechanical cleaning","During the initial system assessment"],"expl":"Section 4.14: restoration and repair of mechanical systems shall only be performed after mechanical cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14-a","acr:X-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0541","stem":"What must the scope of work clearly identify?","options":["The components and controls","Solely the price","Solely the technician certifications held","The building's square footage solely"],"expl":"Section 2.2: the scope of work shall clearly identify which components are to be cleaned/restored and the required engineering controls and unique requirements.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.2-a","acr:2.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0542","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall the HVAC cleaning contractor provide before any cleaning work?","options":["A written plan","A verbal estimate","A marketing brochure","A refrigerant log"],"expl":"Section 2.0: prior to commencement of any cleaning work, the contractor shall provide a written work plan based on the inspection and assessment.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.0-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0543","stem":"For which products shall the work plan include Safety Data Sheets (SDS)?","options":["All chemical products used","Flammable products used on site","Products applied indoors only","Products the client requests"],"expl":"Section 2.7.1: the work plan shall include SDS for all chemical products to be used, and SDS shall be maintained on-site for the project duration.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0544","stem":"What does the ACR recommend when air-handling coils, fans, condensate pans and drains are cleaned?","options":["Wet cleaned with mechanical methods","Dry brushed, then cleaned by hand","Coated after being cleaned","Replaced rather than cleaned"],"expl":"Section 4.6: it's recommended that non-porous AHU surfaces (coils, fans, condensate pans, drains) be wet cleaned in conjunction with mechanical methods.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0545","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what is recommended for porous materials with microbial growth (Condition 3)?","options":["Removed and replaced","Treated with biocide, left in place","Cleaned, then encapsulated","HEPA vacuumed, then reused"],"expl":"Section 4.16: porous materials with Condition 3 microbial growth are recommended to be properly removed and replaced, followed by surface cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.16-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0546","stem":"Remediation of microbial contamination shall be performed in accordance with which document?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S520","NFPA 90A, Section 5","ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180","UL 181 listing criteria"],"expl":"Section 4.13: remediating microbial contamination shall follow ANSI/IICRC S520, alongside the ACR's cleaning/restoration provisions.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.13-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0547","stem":"Surface treatments (coatings) shall only be applied after what has occurred?","options":["Cleaning is verified as complete","The system is confirmed contaminated","The client pays in full","The coils are removed"],"expl":"Section 4.15: surface treatments shall only be applied after confirming the system has been cleaned and passed the specified level of cleanliness verification.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.15-c","acr:4.15-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0548","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall wet cleaning, power washing, and steam cleaning NOT do?","options":["Damage the components or facility equipment","Use water above normal line pressure","Be documented in the project report","Be used on non-porous metal surfaces"],"expl":"Section 4.4: wet/steam/power cleaning shall not damage or cause subsequent damage to components or equipment; never applied to electrical/porous surfaces.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.4-a","acr:4.4-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0549","stem":"When mechanically cleaning fibrous glass duct liner or duct board, what shall the selected method NOT do?","options":["Create abrasions, breaks, or tears","Remove dust, debris, or settled particulate","Use a HEPA vacuum on the surface","Follow the Section 4.7 procedures"],"expl":"Section 4.17.1: the mechanical cleaning methods for duct liner/duct board shall not create abrasions, breaks, or tears to the fibrous glass surfaces.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.17.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0550","stem":"Cleaning of fibrous glass duct liner or duct board shall be performed in accordance with which ACR section?","options":["Section 4.7","Section 3.4 (Assessment)","Section 5","Section 2.2"],"expl":"Section 4.17: cleaning of fibrous glass duct liner or duct board present in equipment or ducts shall follow Section 4.7 of the Standard.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.17-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0551","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, if fibrous glass removal was due to microbial contamination, the base surface shall be cleaned to what status before reapplying insulation?","options":["Condition 1, normal fungal ecology","Condition 2, settled spores present","Condition 3, actual growth present","Any condition, once visibly clean"],"expl":"Section 4.20.1: if removal was due to microbial contamination, the base surface shall be cleaned to a Condition 1 status prior to reapplying fibrous glass products.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0552","stem":"How should registers, grilles, and diffusers be handled during cleaning?","options":["Removed, cleaned, and reinstalled","Left installed, masked, and painted over","Discarded, replaced with new stock","Sealed shut, taped at the perimeter"],"expl":"Section 4.9: it's recommended registers, grilles, diffusers be removed if possible, properly cleaned, and restored to their previous position.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.9-a","acr:4.9-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0553","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, cleaning activities shall not impair, alter, or damage which safety equipment?","options":["Smoke and fire detection","Illuminated exit signage","Fire extinguishers","Emergency egress lighting"],"expl":"Section 4.10: cleaning activities shall not impair, alter, or damage smoke/fire detection equipment in the facility or serving the components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.10-a","hvac:s-duct-smoke-detector"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0554","stem":"During Type 2 wet cleaning, what shall be done with the condensate drain pan and drain line?","options":["Cleaned, flushed, and tested to verify drainage","Left in place and documented as found","Sealed at the joints with mastic and tape","Removed, replaced with PVC, and resloped"],"expl":"Section 4.11.4.1: the condensate drain pan and line shall be cleaned and flushed; the pan inspected and tested to verify drainage before and after cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.1-a","acr:4.11.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0555","stem":"What does the ACR indicate if debris cannot be removed from a coil using Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) methods?","options":["Replacement may be necessary","The coil may be accepted as cleaned","Type 1 may be repeated indefinitely","The debris may be encapsulated"],"expl":"ACR 4.11.5: a post-cleaning inspection follows Type 2 cleaning. If debris remains the process repeats, and when Type 2 methods cannot remove it, replacement becomes necessary. Encapsulating debris on a coil is never an ACR option.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"ACR 2025 §4.11.5","units":["acr:4.11.5-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0556","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, fuel-powered equipment (generators, vacuum trucks, compressors) shall be positioned to do what?","options":["Prevent exhaust entry","Maximize equipment noise output","Block the building entrance","Stay indoors near the work"],"expl":"Section 3.2: fuel-powered equipment shall be positioned to prevent combustion and air-exhaust emissions from entering an occupied space.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.2-a","acr:3.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0557","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, continuous negative pressure during cleaning shall be maintained and ____.","options":["Verifiable during the work","Assumed during the work from fan size","Measured once during the work","Determined during the work by the client"],"expl":"Section 3.4: continuous negative pressure shall be maintained and be verifiable at representative locations during the cleaning process.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0558","stem":"What shall be done with all contaminated materials removed from the components?","options":["Contained before removal from the building","Left on site in open bags for pickup","Exhausted outdoors by the negative air machine","Carried out loose, then bagged outdoors"],"expl":"Section 3.5: to prevent cross-contamination, all contaminated materials removed shall be properly contained prior to removal from the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.5-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0559","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, redundant equipment is recommended to be available on-site in the event of what?","options":["A negative pressure failure","A smoke detector outage","A particulate spike above background","A failed cleanliness verification"],"expl":"Section 3.8: it's recommended that redundant equipment be available on-site in the event of negative pressure failure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0560","stem":"What equipment does ambient air cleaning use as a supplemental engineering control?","options":["HEPA-filtered air scrubbers","Portable ozone generators","Positive-pressure supply fans","UV-C lamps in the air handler"],"expl":"Section 3.6: ambient air cleaning uses HEPA-filtered air scrubbers as a supplemental control for particle reduction during and after work.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.6-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0561","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, how shall opening contaminated vacuum collection equipment on-site (for servicing or filter maintenance) be done?","options":["In a containment area or outside the building","In the mechanical room, with the AHU shut down","In the occupied area, after the occupants leave","In the work area beside the service opening"],"expl":"Section 3.1.2.1: any activity opening contaminated vacuum equipment on-site shall be performed in an appropriate containment area or outside the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.2.1-a","acr:3.1.2.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0562","stem":"In the NADCA Vacuum Test, what do the two template openings measure?","options":["2 cm x 25 cm","2.5 cm x 25 cm","10 cm x 10 cm","2 cm x 5 cm"],"expl":"Section 5.4: the closed-face vacuum cassette is passed over two (2) 2 cm x 25 cm openings within the template.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4-d","acr:5.4.1-i"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0563","stem":"What is the minimum the NADCA Vacuum Test air sampling pump must draw?","options":["15 liters per minute","1.5 liters per minute","10 liters per minute","25 liters per minute"],"expl":"Section 5.4.1: an air sampling pump capable of drawing a minimum of 15 liters per minute through a closed-face cassette shall be used.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0564","stem":"During NADCA Vacuum Test sampling, what is the airflow adjusted to?","options":["17 L/min ± 2","15 L/min ± 5%","25 L/min ± 5","8 L/min ± 2"],"expl":"The sampling protocol adjusts airflow with a calibration device to 17 liters per minute ± 2 L/min.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-h","acr:X-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0565","stem":"What is the maximum rate at which the NADCA Vacuum Test cassette is moved?","options":["5 cm per second","2 cm per second","10 cm per second","1 m per second"],"expl":"The cassette is slid at a rate not greater than 5 cm per second (5 seconds per slot each direction), and each opening is vacuumed twice.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-j"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0566","stem":"What are the 37 mm matched-weight NADCA Vacuum Test filters made of?","options":["Cellulose ester","Bonded glass fiber","PTFE","Polycarbonate"],"expl":"Section 5.4.1: filter media are 37 mm mixed cellulose ester (MCE) matched-weight filters (0.8 or 0.45 micron pore size).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0567","stem":"To what accuracy shall the NADCA Vacuum Test pump be calibrated?","options":["±5% at 15 L/min","±10% at 15 L/min","±2% at 17 L/min","±2% at 5 L/min"],"expl":"Section 5.4.1: the vacuum pump shall be calibrated using a device accurate to ±5% at 15 liters per minute.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-f","acr:X-4"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0568","stem":"Analysis of the NADCA Vacuum Test sample is based on which method?","options":["NIOSH 0500","EPA Method 5 train","EPA TO-15","OSHA ID-160"],"expl":"Section 5.4: analysis is based on NIOSH Method 0500 (total nuisance dust); results reported in mg per 100 cm².","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-q","acr:R-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0569","stem":"What is the NADCA Vacuum Test template thickness specification?","options":["15 mil","5 mil stock","6 mil","25 mil"],"expl":"Section 5.4.1: the template shall be 15 mil thick (0.381 mm), providing a 100 cm² sampling area of two 2 cm x 25 cm slots at least 2.5 cm apart.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-g"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0570","stem":"How shall each NADCA Vacuum Test template opening be vacuumed?","options":["Twice, in both directions","Once, in a single direction","Three times, in one direction","Until the surface appears clean"],"expl":"Each template opening shall be vacuumed twice, once in each direction, with the cassette edges resting on the template, not touching the duct surface.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-m"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0571","stem":"What sensitivity shall the scale used to weigh NADCA Vacuum Test filters have?","options":["≥ 0.7 mg","≥ 0.1 mg","≥ 0.75 mg","≥ 1 mg"],"expl":"Section 5.4: scale sensitivity shall be equal to or greater than 0.7 milligram, calibrated per the manufacturer's recommendations.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-r","acr:X-6"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0572","stem":"If the NADCA Vacuum Test is used for verification, what shall the documentation include?","options":["The laboratory analysis results","A photo of the sampled surface","The pump calibration sticker","The technician's certification"],"expl":"Section 5.6.1: if the NADCA Vacuum Test is used, a copy of the lab results shall be included with the post-project documentation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0573","stem":"If Method 2 Surface Comparison testing is inconclusive or disputed, what can be used to make the final determination?","options":["The Method 3 NADCA Vacuum Test","A second Method 1 visual inspection","A static pressure reading","The client's written determination"],"expl":"Section 5.3.3: if Method 2 is inconclusive/disputed, Method 3 (NADCA Vacuum Test) may make the final cleanliness determination (non-porous only).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.3-a","acr:5-M4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0574","stem":"When contaminated materials are transported/relocated within the building, what shall be done with tools and vacuums?","options":["Cleaned or sealed first","Left uncovered on the cart","Rinsed with water solely","Wrapped in newspaper"],"expl":"Section 3.1.3: all collection devices, vacuums, and tools shall be cleaned or sealed before relocating to different areas and before removal from the building.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.1.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0575","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, when shall interior surfaces of a containment enclosure be wet-wiped and/or HEPA vacuumed?","options":["Before dismantling it","Solely if visibly soiled","After the client leaves","At no point in the project"],"expl":"Section 3.10.8: interior surfaces of the containment enclosure shall be wet-wiped and/or HEPA vacuumed before moving or dismantling it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0576","stem":"In a Level 3 containment, when shall background monitoring for total particulate be performed?","options":["Before set-up, as a baseline","After the job is complete, at closeout","At the midpoint, during cleaning","In healthcare occupancies, exclusively"],"expl":"Section 3.11.3: background total-particulate monitoring shall be performed prior to containment set-up to establish baseline airborne concentrations.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-c","acr:3.11.3-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0577","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall happen to work in a Level 3 containment if airborne particulate exceeds background levels?","options":["Stop until the cause is found and corrected","Continue with an added HEPA air scrubber","Switch the area to positive pressure","Move to another area and log the reading"],"expl":"Section 3.11.3: if airborne particulate exceeds background, work shall cease until levels are reduced to background and the cause is found and corrected.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.3-g","acr:3.11.3-h"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0578","stem":"What is the most common Level 2 containment access?","options":["A zipper access with a flap","An open doorway access","A revolving door access","A vertical cut access, no flap"],"expl":"Section 3.10.4: a zipper-type access with a single flap covering the zipper is the most common work practice; a two-flap vertical cut is the alternative.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0579","stem":"Under ACR 2025 1.10, what must the assessment that follows an inspection provide?","options":["Cleaning need, scope, techniques and engineering controls","Crew size, schedule, total job cost and a payment timetable","Microbial species, spore counts and occupant exposure levels","Component ratings, remaining service life and replacement costs"],"expl":"ACR 1.10: the assessment recommends whether cleaning is needed and gives a defined scope, the recommended techniques, the required environmental engineering controls and any unique requirements. Identifying microbial species is IEP sampling work, not the ACR assessment.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §1.10","units":["acr:1.10-d","acr:1.10-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0580","stem":"Post-project documentation is recommended to include organized, legible ____.","options":["Written and visual records","Solely the customer invoices","Solely the product SDS sheets","Solely a business card"],"expl":"Section 5.6: documentation showing compliance with the Standard is recommended for all work, including written and visual records.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.6-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0581","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, how must waste water generated during wet cleaning be disposed of after being captured, contained, and tested?","options":["Per local, regional, state, and federal regulations","Down floor drains, sinks, toilets, or mop sinks","Onto the roof deck, away from air intakes","Per the equipment manufacturer's instructions"],"expl":"Section 4.6.2: capture, containment, testing, and disposal of wet-cleaning waste water shall follow applicable local, regional, state, and federal regulations.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6.2-a","acr:4.6.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0582","stem":"How is 'source removal' best defined?","options":["Dislodging debris and vacuuming it out","Masking duct odors with fragrance","Coating surfaces so debris stays in place","Raising the filter MERV rating"],"expl":"Source removal is the mechanical process of physically dislodging contaminants from components and removing them with vacuum collection to prevent re-entrainment.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-38","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0583","stem":"What does the ACR indicate when both sides of a coil are NOT accessible for cleaning?","options":["Removal or replacement of the coil section","Cleaning from the one accessible side","Exclusion of the coil from the scope","Coating of the coil to lock in debris"],"expl":"Section 4.11: when both sides of a coil aren't accessible for cleaning, removal and/or replacement may be required.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0584","stem":"What is NOT a Type 1 (Dry Cleaning) physical removal method?","options":["Flooding the coil with water","HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming","Brushes penetrating between coil fins","Compressed air agitation methods"],"expl":"Type 1 methods include HEPA contact vacuuming, brushes between fins, compressed air, and fin-straightening tools, not flooding (that's Type 2 wet cleaning).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.2-a","acr:4.11.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0585","stem":"What is required when a Type 1 post-cleaning inspection finds remaining debris or an impacted coil?","options":["Performing Type 2","Signing off as clean","Repeating Type 1 cleaning","Replacing the coil section"],"expl":"Section 4.11.3: if debris remains or the coil is impacted after Type 1, Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed. Wet Cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0586","stem":"When life-safety detection equipment must be taken off-line during cleaning, what shall the work plan do?","options":["Get others' input","Ignore it","Delegate to the newest tech","Disable it permanently"],"expl":"Section 2.8: when life-safety detection (duct sensors, smoke detectors) must be off-line, the work plan shall address life-safety concerns, likely needing others' input.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.8-c","acr:2.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0587","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, service openings in fibrous glass shall be created and closed in a way that ensures what?","options":["No fibrous edges are exposed to airflow","Fibrous edges are exposed to the airstream","The R-value is intentionally reduced","The vapor barrier is removed"],"expl":"Section 4.2.8.1: openings in fibrous glass shall be created/closed so there are no exposed fibrous glass edges within the system common to the airstream.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.8.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0588","stem":"What shall any fibrous glass removed to install a service opening be repaired or replaced with?","options":["Like material of the same R-value","Any available scrap insulation stock","A mechanically fastened sheet metal patch","Foil-backed tape applied in two layers"],"expl":"Section 4.2.8.2: removed fibrous glass shall be repaired/replaced with like material of the same thickness so R-value, service rating, and vapor/air barrier aren't degraded.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.8.2-a","acr:4.2.8.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0589","stem":"All service openings shall comply with applicable UL, IMC, SMACNA, and which additional standards?","options":["NFPA and local codes","EPA regulations solely","OSHA VPP guidance","LEED credit criteria"],"expl":"Section 4.2.10: all service openings shall comply with applicable UL, IMC, SMACNA, and NFPA standards and applicable local/regional/provincial/national codes.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.10-a","acr:4.2.10-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0590","stem":"What do damaged, deteriorating, or corroded coil fins found during cleaning indicate?","options":["Replacement may be needed","Cleaning restores them to service","They should be coated and sealed","They fall outside the documentation"],"expl":"Section 4.11.1.3: when metal fins are damaged, deteriorating, or corroded, replacement may be necessary; if cleaning will cause further damage, replacement is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0591","stem":"Inline (duct-mounted) coils without drain pans require which precaution during wet cleaning?","options":["Capture the rinse water","No precaution","Positive pressure solely","Skipping wet cleaning"],"expl":"Section 4.11.7: precautions shall be taken to capture rinse water when wet cleaning duct-mounted coils without drain pans.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.7-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0592","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, non-porous material restoration is recommended when, after cleaning, the surface will continue to do what?","options":["Contribute particulate","Fail to look shiny and new","Pass cleanliness verification","Hold pressure"],"expl":"Section 4.21: if a non-porous surface after cleaning will continue to contribute particulate/odors or adversely affect air quality, restoration is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.21-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0593","stem":"HVAC components subjected to flooding shall be evaluated and categorized per documents such as what?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S500","NFPA 90A, Section 5","ANSI/UL 723","ANSI/ASHRAE 170"],"expl":"Section 4.22: flood/water-damaged components are categorized per industry methods such as ANSI/IICRC S500; the water category dictates methods and controls.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.22-a","acr:4.22-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0594","stem":"What does restoration under the ACR NOT include?","options":["Sealing duct system air leaks","Cleaning the heat-transfer coils","Replacing damaged duct insulation","Coating interior duct surfaces"],"expl":"Section 4.24.1: restoration does not include sealing air leaks within duct systems, nor repair/replacement of malfunctioning mechanical devices (outside scope).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.24.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0595","stem":"After mechanical cleaning, what shall porous surfaces subjected to fire/smoke damage be evaluated for?","options":["Friability and odor retention","Color change and staining","Weight gain and moisture content","Flame spread and smoke rating"],"expl":"Section 4.23.1: all porous surfaces subjected to fire/smoke shall be evaluated after mechanical cleaning for friability and odor retention; friable/odorous areas resurfaced or replaced.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0596","stem":"Sampling to determine whether a component is contaminated with soot or smoke from a fire can be performed per what standard?","options":["ANSI/IESO/RIA Standard 6001","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1","ANSI/UL Standard 181B","ANSI/NFPA Standard 96"],"expl":"Section 4.23.4: if there is a question about soot/smoke contamination, sampling per ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001 is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.23.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0597","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, resurfacing/coating of fibrous glass shall NOT be performed if the material ____.","options":["Cannot be bonded to","Is already clean and intact","Passed inspection","Is newly installed material"],"expl":"Section 4.18.2: if fibrous glass is beyond restoration and can't support/provide a long-term bondable surface, resurfacing/coating shall not be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.18.2-a","acr:4.18.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0598","stem":"What does the ACR recommend when there is evidence of friable or delaminating fibrous glass that cleaning or coating cannot restore?","options":["Replacing it with new material","Leaving it in place until the next cleaning","Sealing it with duct mastic","Installing new liner over it"],"expl":"Section 4.19: when damage/deterioration/friability means cleaning or resurfacing can't restore fibrous glass, replacement is recommended.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.19-a","acr:4.19-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0599","stem":"What shall be done with all metal surfaces where degraded thermal-acoustic material was removed?","options":["Scraped free of loose debris","Left as-is under the new material","Coated with mastic solely","Wrapped externally"],"expl":"Section 4.20: metal surfaces after removal of degraded thermal-acoustic material shall be scraped clean and free of loose visible debris before new insulation is installed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20-a","acr:4.20-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0600","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall new fibrous glass surfaces installed as replacement be capable of?","options":["Meeting the requirements of NADCA verification","Appearing clean to the building owner at closeout","Withstanding pressure washing without damage","Passing the NADCA Vacuum Test on the liner surface"],"expl":"Section 4.20.4: after installation of replacement materials, all new fibrous glass surfaces shall be capable of meeting NADCA cleanliness verification requirements.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.20.4-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0601","stem":"A written work plan's primary purpose is to give all parties a clear understanding of what?","options":["What tasks will be performed","The company's profit margin","The technician's schedule","The building's history"],"expl":"Section 2.1: the primary purpose of the written work plan is for all involved to have a clear understanding of what work tasks and procedures will be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.1-b","acr:2.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0602","stem":"Per ACR 2025 2.7, what must be true of the manufacturer instructions for products and coatings listed in the work plan?","options":["They are available to workers at all times","They are submitted to the client for approval","They are filed with the owner after closeout","They are posted at the AHU during the job"],"expl":"ACR 2.7: the work plan lists every general use product, chemical product and coating, and the manufacturer's use and application instructions are available to workers at all times. Documenting product submittal to the client is only a CAN (2.7.1).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"ACR 2025 §2.7","units":["acr:2.7-c","acr:2.7-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0603","stem":"How shall a single-chamber decontamination facility (Level 3) be set up?","options":["Sealed to the containment","Placed anywhere in the building","Left unsealed","Positive-pressurized"],"expl":"Section 3.11.2: the single-chamber decon facility shall be attached and sealed directly to the containment area, separated by a zipper/flap access.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.11.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0604","stem":"What are appropriate engineering controls on every HVAC cleaning project considered to be?","options":["Mandatory","Optional extras","Solely for microbial jobs","Client's responsibility"],"expl":"Section 3.13: appropriate engineering controls are mandatory on every HVAC cleaning and restoration project to protect occupants and prevent cross-contamination.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.13-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0605","stem":"Where shall clean protective coverings within the work area extend, according to the NADCA ACR Standard?","options":["Beyond the work area, to protect the flooring and furniture","Up the side walls, at least 6 inches above the floor","Up to the ceiling, sealed airtight around the work area","Into the duct interior, to shield the airside surface"],"expl":"Section 3.9.2: clean protective coverings shall extend beyond the work area to protect flooring, equipment, and furniture whenever necessary.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.9.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0606","stem":"What, at minimum, is the overall goal of cleaning and restoration procedures?","options":["Visibly clean, or as specified","A fresh, uniform interior coating","Sustained, measured positive pressure","A specific, verified IAQ number"],"expl":"Section 4.0: all procedures shall achieve the minimum level of visibly clean or the specified level of cleanliness verification defined in the contractual documents.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.0-a","acr:4.0-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0607","stem":"What does the ACR require when a coil cannot be properly cleaned by Type 1 (Dry Cleaning)?","options":["Type 2 wet cleaning of the coil","Deferring the coil cleaning to the next visit","Type 1 cleaning of the coil, repeated","Replacement of the coil, not cleaning"],"expl":"Section 4.11.1.1: if it's determined the coil can't be properly cleaned by Type 1, Type 2 (Wet Cleaning) shall be performed.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.1.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0608","stem":"When shall the condensate drain pan be inspected and tested to verify drainage?","options":["Before and after","Solely before cleaning","Solely after cleaning","At no point during cleaning"],"expl":"Section 4.11.4.1: the condensate drain pan shall be inspected and tested to verify drainage before and after cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0609","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what shall cleaning methods and products used on coils conform to?","options":["Manufacturer recommendations","UL 181 surface treatment criteria","Generic industry cleaning guidance","The technician's established habits"],"expl":"Section 4.11.4.2: cleaning methods/products shall not damage or erode the coil surface or fins and shall conform to coil manufacturer recommendations when available.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.2-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0610","stem":"Before cleaning work begins, which project document must be provided?","options":["A written work plan based on the inspection and assessment.","A final cleanliness report based on post-project verification.","A balancing report based on occupied-space airflow measurements.","A product warranty based on the selected agitation equipment."],"expl":"A written work plan is required before cleaning begins.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.0-a","acr:2.0-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0611","stem":"Which information belongs in the defined scope of work?","options":["The components, the controls, and any unique requirements.","Labor hours, travel charges, and the payment schedule.","The complaint, the equipment age, and the preferred start date.","The vacuum model, the hose length, and the agitation tool maker."],"expl":"The scope identifies included components, controls, and project-specific requirements.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.2-a","acr:2.2-b","acr:2.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0612","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, chemical products will be used on a project. What documentation must be included and available on site?","options":["Manufacturer instructions for use and the Safety Data Sheets.","Purchase receipts and the technician's product-use notes.","A signed verification form and the HVAC filter change log.","Customer consent forms and the OEM coil warranty card."],"expl":"Product instructions and SDS documents belong in the work plan and must be available.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:2.7-c","acr:2.7.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0613","stem":"What is the minimum containment level for every HVAC cleaning project?","options":["Level 1, negative pressure plus coverings","Level 2, temporary barriers plus poly floor","Level 3, a single-chamber decon unit","Level 4, a two-chamber decon unit"],"expl":"Level 1 is the minimum on every project.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.9-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0614","stem":"Which group best describes the core Level 1 controls?","options":["Negative pressure, protective coverings as needed, and cross-contamination control.","Rigid walls, a two-chamber decontamination unit, and continuous particle logs.","Wet cleaning, antimicrobial application, and post-project air sampling.","Two-layer flooring, a decontamination chamber, and alarmed pressure recording."],"expl":"Level 1 centers on negative pressure, coverings as needed, and cross-contamination control.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3-L1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0615","stem":"Which control first distinguishes Level 2 from Level 1 containment?","options":["Temporary barriers isolating the work area.","A single-chamber decontamination facility.","A two-chamber decontamination facility.","A recording pressure monitor with alarm."],"expl":"Level 2 adds temporary barriers and related controls.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.2-a","acr:X-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0616","stem":"Which feature distinguishes Level 3 containment from Level 2?","options":["A single-chamber decon facility.","A two-chamber decon facility.","A basic negative-pressure enclosure.","A four-air-change cleaning rate."],"expl":"Level 3 adds a single-chamber decontamination facility and continuous monitoring.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3-L3","acr:3.11-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0617","stem":"Which Level 2 floor construction is correct?","options":["Two layers of 6-mil poly up 6 inches on the walls.","One layer of 6-mil poly up 2 inches on the walls.","Two layers of 4-mil poly up 3 inches on the walls.","Three layers of 4-mil poly up 4 inches on the walls."],"expl":"Level 2 uses two layers of 6-mil fire-retardant poly extending at least 6 inches up the walls.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.10.3-a","acr:3.10.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0618","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what pressure relationship is required in the portion of duct being cleaned?","options":["Continuous negative pressure that is verifiable at representative locations","Steady positive pressure relative to the surrounding indoor spaces","Neutral pressure relative to the surrounding occupied indoor spaces","Intermittent negative pressure relative to the air handler cabinet"],"expl":"The cleaned portion must remain under continuous, verifiable negative pressure.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.4-a","acr:3.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0619","stem":"Negative pressure is lost while technicians are disturbing contamination. What is the best immediate action?","options":["Stop work until the required pressure has been restored.","Switch to wet cleaning, since pressure control is no longer needed.","Keep cleaning the branch while raising the supply fan speed.","Seal the nearest register and finish the branch first."],"expl":"Work that disturbs contaminants should stop until negative pressure is restored.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-pressure-lost-stop"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0620","stem":"A negative-air device used on ductwork has no HEPA filtration. Where may its exhaust be directed?","options":["Outdoors, where it cannot get back into the building.","Into an unoccupied room, with the door kept closed.","Into the containment area, if a HEPA scrubber is also running.","Into the return plenum, downstream of the filters."],"expl":"Non-HEPA exhaust must be directed outdoors and controlled against re-entry.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-exhaust-no-reentry"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0621","stem":"What is the maximum spacing for mechanical fasteners around a service panel?","options":["Four inches on center, by screws or rivets","Six inches on center, by screws or clips","Two inches on center, by tape and mastic","Eight inches on center, by clamps"],"expl":"Service panels are mechanically fastened at least every 4 inches on center.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0622","stem":"How far must a service panel overlap the duct surface on every side?","options":["At least one inch.","At least one-half inch.","At least two inches.","At least four inches."],"expl":"The minimum overlap is 1 inch on all sides.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.2-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0623","stem":"Which closure materials are specified for sealing service openings?","options":["Tapes or mastics meeting UL 181A or UL 181B.","Tapes or mastics meeting ASHRAE 62.1 or Standard 180.","Tapes or mastics meeting IICRC S520 or S500.","Tapes or mastics meeting NFPA 90A or 90B."],"expl":"Closure tapes and mastics must meet UL 181A or UL 181B as applicable.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.4-a","acr:R-8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0624","stem":"Which procedure best matches the ACR source-removal method?","options":["Agitate the material while a vacuum collection device captures it.","Mist the material while occupied registers stay open.","Raise the airflow until material moves to the filters.","Coat the material while the air handler distributes it."],"expl":"ACR source removal uses suitable agitation with vacuum capture.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.3-c","acr:4.3-a","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0625","stem":"Which component is suitable for controlled wet cleaning?","options":["A nonporous cooling coil and drain pan with water capture.","A fibrous-glass duct liner with visible surface erosion.","An energized electric resistance coil inside the air handler.","A porous sound attenuator with absorptive internal media."],"expl":"Wet cleaning is suitable for nonporous components when water is controlled and damage is prevented.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.6-a","acr:4.6.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0626","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, before a damper is cleaned, what should the technician do?","options":["Mark its existing position so it can be restored after.","Its linkage may be removed and the system rebalanced after.","It may be driven fully open and left in that position.","Lubricant may be applied before recording its position."],"expl":"Damper position is marked before cleaning and restored afterward.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.8-a","acr:4.8-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0627","stem":"During Type 2 coil cleaning, what must be done with the condensate pan and drain line?","options":["Clean and flush them, then verify drainage before and after.","Coat both, then verify drainage after the treatment cures.","Keep them dry, block the drain, and verify after operation.","Remove the pan, leave the drain line, and verify before cleaning."],"expl":"The pan and drain line are cleaned and flushed, with drainage verified before and after.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.1-a","acr:4.11.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0628","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, after wet cleaning an electric resistance coil, what must occur before energizing?","options":["Remove chemical residue and let the assembly dry thoroughly.","Energize at low voltage so heat will accelerate the drying process.","Leave a thin detergent film to protect the element from corrosion.","Operate the supply fan while the element remains damp and isolated."],"expl":"The coil must be rinsed free of chemicals and thoroughly dried before re-energizing.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-d","acr:4.11.8-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0629","stem":"How should fibrous-glass duct material be cleaned?","options":["Without creating abrasions, breaks, or tears in the surface.","With high-pressure air, held close to the substrate.","With routine wet washing, like the metal duct sections.","With aggressive wire brushing, scraping, or sanding of the surface."],"expl":"Fibrous glass must be cleaned without damaging its surface.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.17.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0630","stem":"Which surfaces may be evaluated with Method 1 visual inspection?","options":["Both porous and nonporous HVAC component surfaces.","Solely surfaces that have received wet cleaning.","Nonporous HVAC component surfaces solely.","Porous HVAC component surfaces solely."],"expl":"Method 1 applies to both porous and nonporous components.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5-M1","acr:5.2-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0631","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, Method 1 finds the component visibly clean, and the result is neither disputed nor inconclusive. What is required next?","options":["No further verification method is needed for that component.","Collect microbial samples before the system is restarted.","Perform Method 2 on each accessible component surface.","Perform Method 3 on each nonporous component surface."],"expl":"A conclusive Method 1 pass does not require another cleanliness method.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.2-c","acr:5.2.1-a"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0632","stem":"Which equipment specification belongs to Method 2?","options":["A HEPA contact vacuum capable of at least 80 inches water-column static lift.","A vacuum cassette operated at 17 liters per minute across a template.","A HEPA air scrubber capable of at least four air changes per hour.","A non-HEPA vacuum capable of at least 40 inches water-column static lift."],"expl":"Method 2 uses a HEPA contact vacuum with at least 80 inches WC static lift.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-e","acr:5.3.1-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0633","stem":"Which surface is eligible for the NADCA Vacuum Test, Method 3?","options":["A dry nonporous duct surface.","A damp porous sound-attenuator surface.","A wet nonporous coil surface.","A dry porous liner surface."],"expl":"Method 3 applies only to dry nonporous surfaces.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5-M3","acr:5.4.2-c"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0634","stem":"What is the standard sampled area for the NADCA Vacuum Test?","options":["100 square centimeters using two 2-by-25-centimeter slots.","200 square centimeters using four 2-by-25-centimeter slots.","200 square centimeters using two 4-by-25-centimeter slots.","50 square centimeters using two 1-by-25-centimeter slots."],"expl":"The standard template exposes 100 cm2 through two 2 cm by 25 cm slots.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.1-i","acr:5.4.1-h"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0635","stem":"What is the maximum cassette travel rate during the NADCA Vacuum Test?","options":["Five centimeters per second, each direction.","Ten centimeters per second, one direction.","Fifteen centimeters per second.","Twenty centimeters per second."],"expl":"The cassette travel rate must not exceed 5 cm per second.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-j"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0636","stem":"How does ACR 2025 treat removal and replacement of porous liner with Condition 3 growth?","options":["Recommended, followed by mechanical cleaning of the base surface","Required, with an antimicrobial recommended afterward","Prohibited unless an IEP has issued a written clearance","Optional, provided the liner can be coated instead"],"expl":"4.16: removal/replacement of porous material with Condition 3 growth is RECOMMENDED; the follow-on mechanical surface cleaning is the SHALL. The 4.4 SHALL is never applying water or agents to fibrous glass.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.16-a","acr:4.16-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0637","stem":"When creating access openings in ductwork to perform source removal cleaning, which of the following is strictly prohibited by NADCA standards?","options":["Cutting a service opening into flexible ductwork","Cutting access holes in rectangular metal duct","Creating openings in flat-oval spiral ducts","Cutting access panels in rigid duct board"],"expl":"Cutting access holes into flexible ductwork damages the wire reinforcement helix and inner lining, compromising structural integrity. Flexible ductwork should be disconnected at collars rather than cut.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.9-a","acr:4.2.9.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0638","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, when performing surface comparison testing (Method 2) on internal fiberglass duct lining, what minimum static lift must the HEPA contact vacuum brush have?","options":["Eighty inches of water column (WC)","Forty inches of water column (WC)","Sixty inches of water column (WC)","One hundred twenty inches of water column (WC)"],"expl":"Method 2 surface comparison testing requires a HEPA contact vacuum brush with a minimum static lift capability of 80 inches of water column (WC) passed 4 times across the test strip.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.3.1-e"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0639","stem":"What speed must the sampling cassette move across the template when conducting a Method 3 NADCA Vacuum Test?","options":["No faster than 5 cm / second","At least 2 cm / second","No faster than 10 cm / second","No faster than 20 cm / second"],"expl":"When performing the quantitative Method 3 NADCA Vacuum Test using a tared cassette and template, the cassette contact head must traverse the template surface at a maximum speed of 5 cm per second.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-j"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0640","stem":"What two essential mechanics combined define NADCA's fundamental 'Source Removal' philosophy?","options":["Mechanical agitation plus vacuum extraction","Chemical fogging plus biocide application","High-temperature steam plus ozone treatment","Duct sealing plus scheduled filter replacement"],"expl":"NADCA's core standard defines source removal as mechanical agitation (whips, brushes, air washes) to dislodge debris COMBINED WITH negative pressure vacuum extraction to collect it.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.3-c","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0641","stem":"Which feature distinguishes Level 4 containment from Level 3?","options":["A two-chamber decontamination facility.","Continuous vacuum collection during cleaning.","A single-chamber decontamination facility.","Temporary polyethylene containment barriers."],"expl":"Level 4 uses a two-chamber decontamination facility.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.12-a","acr:3-L4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0642","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, when should vacuum collection equipment operate during cleaning?","options":["Continuously while cleaning is performed.","Solely before mechanical agitation begins.","Solely after mechanical agitation ends.","Solely during the final verification."],"expl":"Vacuum collection equipment operates continuously during cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.5-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0643","stem":"What does ACR cleanliness verification determine?","options":["Whether particulate remains on the HVAC surface.","Whether the air meets an IAQ medical threshold.","Whether microbial growth is present on the surface.","Whether hazardous material is present on the surface."],"expl":"Cleanliness verification concerns particulate, not microbial or hazardous-material testing.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.0-a","acr:5.0-b"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0644","stem":"What sampling airflow is specified for the NADCA Vacuum Test?","options":["17 liters per minute, plus or minus 2 liters per minute.","7 liters per minute, plus or minus 2 liters per minute.","22 liters per minute, plus or minus 2 liters per minute.","12 liters per minute, plus or minus 1.5 liters per minute."],"expl":"The specified sampling flow is 17 L/min plus or minus 2.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:5.4.2-h","acr:X-4"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0645","stem":"Under the Open Air Plenums white paper, how are HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming and dry brushing under negative air classified?","options":["Type 1 (dry cleaning) methods","Type 2 (wet cleaning) methods","Prohibited in open air plenums","Verification methods"],"expl":"Type 1 dry methods include dry surface dusting, brushing, wiping, agitating, blasting or compressed-air washing under negative air, and HEPA-filtered contact vacuuming.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-type1-dry","ascs:p-type-method-level"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0646","stem":"How are wet wiping, water washing, pressure washing, steam cleaning, and chemical cleaning classified?","options":["Type 2 (wet cleaning) methods","Type 1 (dry cleaning) methods","Level 1 containment controls","Method 2 (surface comparison) tests"],"expl":"Type 2 wet cleaning methods include wet wiping, water washing, pressure washing, steam cleaning, and chemical cleaning.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-type2-wet","ascs:p-type-method-level"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0647","stem":"Per the NADCA Open Air Plenums white paper, what is the minimum level when a plenum is contracted for cleaning?","options":["Visibly clean, or the level agreed in scope","The NADCA Vacuum Test threshold, 0.75 mg per 100 sq cm","A Surface Comparison Test rating of 2, or better","The cleanliness of the upstream, connected ductwork"],"expl":"ACR 2025 excludes non-ducted open air plenums from its scope; the white paper governs when a plenum is contracted, and sets visibly clean or the agreed scope as the floor.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-min-cleanliness"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0648","stem":"What does the white paper recommend when plenum contaminants cannot be readily identified and the scope is unclear?","options":["Consulting an IEP to identify the contaminants and plan the work","Proceeding with Type 2 wet cleaning methods by default","Sealing off the plenum and noting it in the final report","Deferring to the building's HVAC service contractor"],"expl":"A consultation with an IEP may be necessary for identifying contaminants and developing a scope of work.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-iep-consult"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0649","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what are engineering controls such as containment in an open air plenum selected based on?","options":["The conditioned space type and the contaminants present","The contractor's available equipment inventory","The age of the building and its duct system","The local utility rate and operating schedule"],"expl":"Containment shall be used per ACR to prevent cross-contamination, and its form depends on the conditioned space type and the contaminants present.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-oap-controls-basis"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0650","stem":"What should be done with filters at the completion of an HVAC cleaning project?","options":["Replaced with filters of proper size and rating","Reinstalled after the filters are tapped clean","Upgraded two MERV ratings above design","Washed, dried, and the filters reinstalled"],"expl":"New filters of correct size and efficiency are installed at project completion; reusing loaded filters recontaminates the cleaned system.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-filters-at-completion"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0651","stem":"A negative air machine is running but the system is not holding negative pressure. What is the FIRST thing to check?","options":["Whether openings are sealed and filters loaded","Whether the exhaust hose is too short","The building's main electrical panel","The customer's thermostat setpoint"],"expl":"Loss of containment almost always comes from unsealed openings or a loaded HEPA filter restricting the AFD. Verify seals and filter loading before adding equipment.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-nam-not-holding"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0652","stem":"When cutting a service opening in a fire-rated assembly or duct with a fire damper, what must the technician do?","options":["Restore the rated assembly afterward","Cut freely; dampers are decorative","Remove the damper permanently","Seal the opening with duct tape"],"expl":"Fire-rated assemblies and damper installations must be restored to their listed condition; compromising them creates a life-safety and code violation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-fire-rated-closure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0653","stem":"What is the correct approach when cleaning a system with interior fibrous glass liner?","options":["Contact vacuuming with soft bristles","Aggressive wire brushing for more agitation","Wet washing followed by air drying in place","Encapsulating the liner before any agitation"],"expl":"Fibrous glass liner requires tools and methods that do not erode or tear the surface; NAIMA AH-122 covers acceptable cleaning of insulated duct.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-liner-soft-tools"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0654","stem":"Per the NADCA chemical products position paper, what is required of products applied inside HVAC systems?","options":["EPA-registered and applied per the label directions","Diluted to half of the EPA label strength","Fogged in before source removal begins","Chosen by the technician without label review"],"expl":"The position paper: EPA-registered, label is the law. ACR itself says manufacturer instructions (3.7), comply with regulations (4.12), and only after cleaning and verification (4.15).","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-chem-epa-registration"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0655","stem":"How is applying an antimicrobial to a surface that has not first been source-cleaned regarded?","options":["Inappropriate, since cleaning has to come before it","Standard practice, and it saves project time","Required by the ACR, before wet cleaning","Acceptable, when the antimicrobial is registered"],"expl":"Chemical application never substitutes for source removal; products are applied to already-cleaned surfaces when their use is justified and label-permitted.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-chem-after-cleaning","ascs:p-chem-not-needed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0656","stem":"Fuel-powered vacuum or compressor equipment staged for a job creates which specific hazard the 2025 ACR addresses?","options":["Carbon monoxide drawn into intakes","Uncontrolled refrigerant release nearby","Static discharge at the collection hose","Excess humidity at the equipment intakes"],"expl":"Fuel-powered equipment must be positioned so exhaust cannot be drawn into outside air intakes, openings, or occupied areas, and CO exposure must be controlled.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-fuel-equipment-co"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0657","stem":"On an occupied hospital corridor project, what is the primary reason for critical barriers and negative pressure?","options":["Prevent migration into sensitive areas","Reduce noise complaints from staff","Speed up the work schedule","Satisfy the parking requirement"],"expl":"Containment plus negative pressure keeps contaminants inside the work area; occupancy and sensitivity of the space drive the level of engineering control required.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-healthcare-barriers-purpose"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0658","stem":"A coil is cleaned with a wet method. Per the NADCA ACR Standard, what must the technician confirm before returning the system to service?","options":["The coil is dry and the drain is clear","The coil circuit refrigerant has been recovered","The filter rack has been removed","The blower belt has been loosened"],"expl":"Wet cleaning introduces water; verifying drainage and a clear condensate path prevents overflow, microbial growth, and property damage.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.4.1-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0659","stem":"Access openings cut into unlined sheet metal duct must be closed with what kind of service panel?","options":["A panel of equivalent gauge or heavier, sealed and fastened","A panel one gauge lighter, to allow thermal flexing","A foil tape patch rated UL 181A, in two layers","A hinged panel of lighter gauge, for future access"],"expl":"Service panels are equivalent gauge or heavier so the duct's structural integrity is not compromised (ACR 4.2.5.1), and they must be sealed airtight.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.2.5.1-a","acr:4.2.5.3-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0660","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what should be done with existing access doors that are found damaged or missing gaskets?","options":["Repaired or replaced so the system is returned airtight","Reused as found with the condition noted in the project record","Sealed shut with mastic for permanence","Removed and the opening panelled over"],"expl":"Leaking access doors degrade system performance and allow contaminant migration; they are repaired or replaced as part of restoring the system.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-access-doors-airtight"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0662","stem":"Which sequence correctly reflects source removal on a supply system?","options":["Set up negative pressure collection, then agitate the surfaces, then verify cleanliness","Verify cleanliness first, then agitate the surfaces, then set up negative pressure collection","Apply an antimicrobial treatment, then agitate the surfaces, then verify cleanliness","Replace the filters, then verify cleanliness, then agitate the surfaces mechanically"],"expl":"Containment and continuous negative-pressure collection are established first, agitation dislodges contaminants into the collection stream, and verification comes last.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.4-a","acr:5.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0663","stem":"If a component cannot be cleaned to the required level, what is the ACR-consistent action?","options":["Document the component, then recommend its repair or replacement","Bill the customer for the component as a cleaned item","Encapsulate the component, then recommend no further work","Skip verification and mark the component as clean"],"expl":"Components that cannot be restored are documented, and repair or replacement is recommended. Documentation protects both the customer and the contractor.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.14.4-a","acr:4.14.4-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0664","stem":"What should be done with flexible duct that has a damaged inner core discovered during cleaning?","options":["Replaced; a torn core is not restorable","Brushed with soft bristles, then reused","Sealed from outside, with UL 181B-FX tape","Encapsulated, with an airside coating"],"expl":"A torn flex core sheds material and cannot be returned to a serviceable condition by cleaning; replacement is the correct recommendation.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-flex-torn-core"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0665","stem":"Which is the correct handling of debris collected during an HVAC cleaning project?","options":["Contain and dispose of it per regulation","Blow it into the return plenum","Leave it bagged on site indefinitely","Rinse it into the condensate drain"],"expl":"Collected debris is contained and disposed of per the regulations governing that material; contaminated waste may have specific handling requirements.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.5-a","acr:3.5.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0666","stem":"According to the NADCA ACR Standard, what should project documentation at closeout include?","options":["Pre and post conditions, work performed, and verification results","The final invoice, the payment record, and the signed contract","The crew timesheets, labor hours, and vehicle mileage logs","A verbal sign-off logged by the office staff, and the final invoice"],"expl":"Documentation of conditions before and after, the work performed, and verification results is the record that demonstrates the standard of care was met.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-closeout-documentation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0667","stem":"An ASCS is asked to clean a system while the building remains fully occupied during business hours. What is the most important scope consideration?","options":["Controls and work scheduling that limit occupant exposure","Charging a premium rate for occupied-space work","Using larger agitation brushes to finish quickly","Skipping verification to avoid disturbing occupants"],"expl":"Occupied-space work drives containment, negative pressure, work sequencing, and often off-hours scheduling to keep contaminants away from occupants.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-occupied-building-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0668","stem":"Before beginning any cleaning, what condition does the ACR expect the HVAC system to be in?","options":["Shut down and locked out","Running at low speed","Set to fan-only mode","Pressurized for testing"],"expl":"Equipment is shut down and locked/tagged out where technicians will contact moving parts or energized components, per site safety requirements.","domain":"Cleaning & Restoration","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-loto-before-contact"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0669","stem":"Before entering an air handling unit to clean it, what must you do first?","options":["Lock out and tag out the unit's energy sources","Document a current respirator fit test for the entrant","Obtain a permit-required confined space entry permit","Establish negative pressure on the system being cleaned"],"expl":"LOTO first, every time. A fan that cycles on a call for cooling while you are in the cabinet will kill you.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-loto-before-contact"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0670","stem":"An AHU is large enough to enter, has limited egress, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. How is it classified?","options":["A confined space that must be evaluated for hazards","A permit-required confined space in all cases","Not a confined space, since it is equipment","Exempt from the rules while it is locked out"],"expl":"It meets the definition and must be evaluated. Whether it is permit-required depends on the hazards found.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:confined-space-def","ascs:confined-not-permit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0671","stem":"Suspect asbestos-containing pipe insulation is found in the mechanical room. Correct action?","options":["Presume ACM, do not disturb, notify the owner","Collect a bulk sample and send it out for laboratory analysis","Wear a respirator, HEPA vacuum the material, bag it","Proceed as planned; cleaning will not disturb pipe insulation"],"expl":"Presume, stop, notify. Sampling ACM is licensed inspector work, not yours.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:suspect-acm-action","ascs:acm-licensed-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0672","stem":"What drives respiratory protection selection in a contaminated system?","options":["The hazard present and an assessment","The customer's contract requirements","Company policy on minimum protection","The expected duration of the exposure"],"expl":"Hazard assessment drives selection. Duration and policy are inputs, not the basis.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:resp-hazard-selection"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0673","stem":"A building constructed in 1968 requires access holes cut through painted surfaces. Correct?","options":["Treat the paint as lead-containing unless tested","Notify the owner after the access work has been completed","Wet-cut the opening and HEPA vacuum the resulting debris","Proceed using normal dust control at the cut location"],"expl":"Pre-1978 paint is presumed lead-containing until testing says otherwise. That changes the work practice before you cut.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:lead-presume"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0674","stem":"What is the required safety procedure before servicing electric resistance coils?","options":["De-energizing the power source and applying a lockout/tagout","Ground fault testing of the energized circuit","Wearing insulated gloves while the coil is live","Posting a warning sign at the disconnect switch"],"expl":"Electrical energy must be controlled: de-energize and lockout/tagout the power source before working on electric resistance coils (4.11.8; NADCA Safety Manual pg. 278).","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0675","stem":"What is the primary concern with a confined space in HVAC work?","options":["A restricted exit and a hazardous atmosphere","Limited lighting and poor visibility","Small clearances and awkward access for tools","Elevated noise and sustained vibration"],"expl":"Confined spaces (Safety Manual pg. 49) pose hazards from restricted entry/exit and potentially hazardous atmospheres; entry procedures and testing are required.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:confined-space-def"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0676","stem":"Is respiratory protection required on HVAC jobs, and if so, when?","options":["Contaminants may exceed limits","No; dust masks may be sufficient","Healthcare work may require it","Outdoor work may require it"],"expl":"Respiratory protection (Safety Manual pg. 332) is used when airborne contaminants (dust, spores, chemicals) may exceed safe exposure limits; selection depends on the hazard.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:engineering-first","ascs:resp-hazard-selection"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0677","stem":"What does a Safety Data Sheet (SDS) provide workers with?","options":["Hazard, PPE, and first-aid data","Pricing, distributor, and stocking data","Warranty, service, and interval data","Sizing, selection, and capacity data"],"expl":"An SDS communicates a chemical's hazards, safe handling, required PPE, and first-aid measures; SDS shall be maintained on-site for all project chemicals.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:sds-content"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0678","stem":"Which agency's regulations govern worker safety on U.S. HVAC cleaning job sites?","options":["The Occupational Safety and Health Administration","The Environmental Protection Agency","The Federal Communications Commission","The Consumer Product Safety Commission"],"expl":"OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration) sets and enforces worker-safety regulations; the candidate guide references OSHA's mold-in-the-workplace guide.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:osha-governs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0679","stem":"What does the ACR recommend when there is potential for exposure to hazardous materials during inspection?","options":["Consult a qualified IEP","Proceed without an IEP consultation","Wear sunglasses instead of PPE","Notify the client, not an IEP"],"expl":"Section 1.8: if the inspector or others may be exposed to hazardous materials, consultation with a qualified IEP or health/safety professional is recommended.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.8-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0680","stem":"What should be used to protect portable electric tools on the job site against shock?","options":["GFCI protection","An OSHA-listed extension cord","UL-listed duct tape","A higher-wattage AC circuit"],"expl":"Ground fault protection devices are part of equipment maintenance/safety (3.1; Safety Manual pg. 205, Portable Electric Tools) to protect against electric shock.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:gfci-tools"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0681","stem":"How shall materials deemed hazardous by governmental agencies be handled?","options":["Per applicable codes, laws, and regulations","As the building owner directs, in writing","As the IEP directs in the written assessment","Like ordinary construction debris, bagged"],"expl":"Section 3.5.1: materials deemed hazardous by governmental agencies shall be handled in strict accordance with applicable codes, laws, and regulations.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.5.1-a"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0682","stem":"What does protecting the job site during cleaning primarily aim to do?","options":["Prevent cross-contamination of the building","Maintain negative pressure inside the ducts","Verify cleanliness before the system restarts","Document pre-existing damage for the owner"],"expl":"Protecting the job site means engineering controls and coverings that keep dislodged contaminants out of occupied areas and protect occupants and property. Negative duct pressure is the cleaning method, verification is the post-cleaning check, and pre-existing damage is documented separately.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.0-a","acr:3.13-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0683","stem":"Understanding 'exposure effects' as part of health and safety means knowing that contaminants can do what?","options":["Harm workers and occupants","Solely affect equipment","Improve air quality","Be ignored if invisible"],"expl":"Exposure effects: microbial and particulate contaminants can produce adverse health effects, so worker/occupant exposure must be controlled.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:exposure-effects"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0684","stem":"What does risk management on an HVAC project begin with?","options":["Identifying the hazards","Purchasing liability insurance solely","Completing the job first","Ignoring low-probability risks"],"expl":"Understanding risk management means identifying job-site hazards up front and applying appropriate controls (engineering controls, PPE, procedures) throughout the project.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:risk-starts-hazards"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0685","stem":"When wet-process cleaning electric resistance coils, what shall be done with the coil after cleaning?","options":["Rinsed and fully dried","Re-energized while still wet","Left with detergent","Immediately painted and sealed"],"expl":"Section 4.11.8: use only non-corrosive detergents, and the coil shall be rinsed free of chemicals and thoroughly dried prior to being re-energized.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:4.11.8-c","acr:4.11.8-d"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0686","stem":"Combustion emissions from fuel-powered equipment are a safety hazard because they can do what?","options":["Enter occupied spaces","Improve IAQ","Cool the building","Clean the ducts"],"expl":"Section 3.2: fuel-powered equipment must be positioned so combustion and exhaust emissions don't enter occupied spaces, protecting occupant health.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:3.2-a","ascs:p-fuel-equipment-co"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0687","stem":"What does the OSHA reference in the candidate guide specifically address?","options":["Mold in the workplace","Refrigerant recovery","Ladder ANSI duty ratings","Fire sprinkler design"],"expl":"The candidate guide's OSHA reference is 'A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace,' supporting the health & safety domain.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:osha-mold-guide"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0688","stem":"Who is the authorized employee in a lockout or tagout procedure?","options":["The worker who locks or tags out equipment to service or maintain it.","The supervisor who approves the work order but stays out of the work area.","The worker who normally operates the equipment but performs no energy isolation.","The building occupant who reports that the equipment is not operating correctly."],"expl":"The authorized employee performs the energy-control steps.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:loto-authorized"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0689","stem":"Which sequence best represents basic lockout or tagout control?","options":["Identify energy, shut down, isolate, lock or tag, release stored energy and verify isolation.","Notify occupants, lower the thermostat, apply a warning sign, and check fan operation.","Shut down, begin service, identify energy, release stored energy, then lock or tag it.","Disconnect controls, start the fan, release stored energy, and isolate the equipment last."],"expl":"Energy sources are identified, isolated, locked or tagged, stored energy is controlled, and isolation is verified before work.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:loto-sequence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0690","stem":"According to OSHA, at what oxygen concentration is an atmosphere oxygen deficient?","options":["Below 19.5 percent by volume.","Below 20.5 percent by volume.","Below 20.95 percent by volume.","Below 20 percent by volume."],"expl":"OSHA defines oxygen deficiency as less than 19.5 percent by volume.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:o2-deficient"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0691","stem":"Before required use of a tight-fitting respirator, which set of actions is necessary?","options":["Hazard-based selection, medical evaluation, training, and fit testing under a written program.","Air sampling, equipment cleaning, facial-hair trimming, and post-use medical testing solely.","Employee purchase, supervisor approval, voluntary use, and annual filter replacement.","Visual inspection, customer consent, odor testing, and a signed liability waiver."],"expl":"Required respirator use involves a written program, medical evaluation, training, fit testing, and hazard-based selection.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:resp-program"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0692","stem":"What is the primary engineering control used during HVAC cleaning to prevent contaminated dust and bioaerosols from escaping into occupied building spaces?","options":["Establishing negative pressure with a HEPA-filtered AFD","Opening exterior windows to dilute the workspace air","Applying an odor counteractant while cleaning","Using a shop vacuum with a non-HEPA paper bag filter"],"expl":"Establishing negative pressure in the duct section being cleaned using a dedicated HEPA Air Filtration Device (AFD) ensures all loosened contaminants are drawn directly into HEPA filters without contaminating indoor air.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-31","acr:D-94"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0693","stem":"Prior to performing maintenance or entering an air handler blower compartment, what OSHA safety procedure MUST be executed?","options":["Lockout / Tagout","Hazard Communication","Fall protection","Hearing conservation"],"expl":"Lockout/Tagout (LOTO) is required by OSHA before working inside AHU blower cabinets to prevent accidental energization or unexpected start-up of fans or moving components. LOTO) of power sources.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:p-loto-before-contact","ascs:osha-1910-147"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0695","stem":"Who is an affected employee under lockout or tagout?","options":["A worker who operates the equipment or works in the area.","A worker who supplies parts but stays out of the servicing area.","A worker who designed the equipment but is absent during service.","A worker who applies the device and verifies isolation first."],"expl":"An affected employee operates or uses the equipment or works where servicing occurs but does not perform the lockout.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:loto-affected"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0696","stem":"At what oxygen concentration is an atmosphere oxygen enriched?","options":["Above 23.5 percent by volume.","Above 22.5 percent by volume.","Above 20.95 percent by volume.","Above 23 percent by volume."],"expl":"More than 23.5 percent by volume is oxygen enriched.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:o2-enriched"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0697","stem":"Which condition makes a required tight-fitting respirator unacceptable?","options":["Facial hair crosses the sealing surface of the facepiece.","The user has completed the required medical evaluation.","The user performs a seal check each time the respirator is donned.","The user wears corrective lenses that do not disturb the seal."],"expl":"Facial hair cannot interfere with the sealing surface of a required tight-fitting respirator.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:facial-hair"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0698","stem":"Which hazardous materials are specifically flagged as possible in older-building open air plenums?","options":["Asbestos and other hazardous materials","Refrigerant and glycol residue","Chlorofluorocarbon blowing agents","Compressed gases, such as nitrogen"],"expl":"In older buildings, open air plenums could contain asbestos and other hazardous materials, which is why assessment precedes entry.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:plenum-hazmat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0699","stem":"What does NADCA recommend developing and following before working in an open air plenum?","options":["Risk Management Plan","Refrigerant recovery log","Duct leakage test report","Commissioning schedule"],"expl":"Technicians shall follow risk-reduction protocols; an RMP or JHA is recommended because plenums hold essential electrical, plumbing, gas, fire protection, sewer, and telecom systems. RMP) or Job Hazard Analysis (JHA.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:plenum-rmp"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0700","stem":"What is NOT something an open air plenum Risk Management Plan should communicate?","options":["The client's payment terms and change-order pricing","Each risk item associated with essential building systems","Likelihood and severity assessment of a risk event","Risk-reduction and emergency-response actions plus an emergency contact"],"expl":"The RMP communicates risk items, likelihood/severity, risk-reduction actions, emergency-response actions, and the emergency contact. Commercial terms are not part of it.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:rmp-contents"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0701","stem":"Which additional training may be required before entering an open air plenum?","options":["Confined space, fall protection, rescue","Refrigerant transition, recovery, reclaim","Forklift, aerial lift, scissor lift","Backflow prevention, cross-connection control"],"expl":"The white paper lists confined space, air monitoring, rig to rescue/rope training, ladder safety, fall protection, and combustible dust as training considerations.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:plenum-training"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0702","stem":"Which condition alone makes a confined space a PERMIT-required confined space?","options":["It contains or may contain a hazardous atmosphere","It may be entered more than once per shift","It may measure more than six feet in height","It may require two technicians to service"],"expl":"A permit space contains or has the potential to contain a hazardous atmosphere, contains engulfment material, has an entrapping internal configuration, or contains any other recognized serious hazard.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:permit-space-triggers"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0703","stem":"An internal configuration with inwardly converging walls or a floor sloping to a smaller cross-section makes a space permit-required because it can do what?","options":["Trap or asphyxiate an entrant","Reduce the system's airflow","Raise humidity in the space","Void the equipment warranty"],"expl":"Inwardly converging walls or a downward-sloping, tapering floor can trap or asphyxiate an entrant, which is one of the four permit-space triggers.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:entrapment-config"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0704","stem":"What is the correct ORDER for atmospheric testing before confined space entry?","options":["Oxygen, then flammable, then toxic","Toxic, then oxygen, then combustible","Combustible, then toxic, then oxygen","Any order, provided each one is tested"],"expl":"Test oxygen first (meters need oxygen to read correctly), then combustible gases and vapors, then toxic contaminants.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:test-order"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0705","stem":"Below what level is an atmosphere oxygen deficient, and above what level is it oxygen enriched, respectively?","options":["19.5% and 23.5%","16% and 21%","20.95% and 25.0%","18.0% and 22.0%"],"expl":"Below 19.5% oxygen is deficient; above 23.5% is enriched and creates a fire/combustion hazard.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:o2-deficient","ascs:o2-enriched"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0706","stem":"A flammable atmosphere in a confined space is defined as a concentration exceeding what?","options":["10 percent of its lower flammable limit (LFL)","1 percent of the lower flammable limit","5 percent of the lower flammable limit","Its permissible exposure limit (PEL)"],"expl":"Flammable gas or vapor in excess of 10% of its lower flammable limit (LFL, also called the lower explosive limit) constitutes a hazardous atmosphere. Exceeding a PEL is the separate test for a toxic atmosphere.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:flammable-10-lfl"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0707","stem":"During a permit-required entry, what is the attendant's primary duty?","options":["Remain outside, monitor the entrants, and order an evacuation","Enter alongside the technician, assist with agitation, and bag debris","Perform the verification inside, once cleaning is complete","Operate the negative air machine, staged at the portal"],"expl":"The attendant remains outside the space for the duration of entry, monitors conditions and entrants, and orders evacuation. Untrained entry rescue is a leading cause of multiple fatalities.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:attendant-duty"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0708","stem":"A technician collapses inside a permit space. What is the correct first action by the attendant?","options":["Summon rescue services and attempt non-entry rescue with the retrieval system","Enter immediately and drag the entrant clear of the hazard zone","Shut down the building electrical service at the main disconnect","Begin ventilating the space and wait for the entrant to recover"],"expl":"Attendants must not enter. Summon the rescue service and use non-entry retrieval; untrained would-be rescuers are the most common additional fatality.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:attendant-rescue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0709","stem":"What must portable electric tools used on a job site be protected by?","options":["A GFCI or assured grounding conductor program","A UL-listed surge protector at the tool","A driven ground rod bonded at the panel","A dedicated 20A branch circuit breaker"],"expl":"Ground-fault circuit interrupter protection, or an assured equipment grounding conductor program, is required for portable electric tools and cord sets.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:gfci-tools"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0710","stem":"What may a portable tool marked 'double insulated' legitimately do?","options":["Be used without a grounding prong","Be used in damp or wet locations","Run without its factory guards","Skip the daily cord inspection"],"expl":"Double-insulated tools have two independent insulation layers and therefore do not require a grounding conductor. All other safe-use rules still apply.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:double-insulated"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0711","stem":"Which practice with portable electric tools is prohibited?","options":["Carrying the tool by its cord and yanking the plug","Inspecting cords before each use","Keeping the factory guards in place","Using cords rated for hard usage"],"expl":"Tools shall never be carried or lowered by the cord, and cords must be disconnected by the plug body, not by pulling the cord.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:cord-handling"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0712","stem":"An extension cord on a duct cleaning job site is found with cracked outer insulation exposing conductors. What is the correct action?","options":["Remove it from service immediately, per OSHA","Wrap it in electrical tape, then continue","Keep it for the low-amperage vacuum","Ground the exposed conductor to the duct"],"expl":"Damaged cords are removed from service. Field repairs with tape do not restore the rated insulation.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:damaged-cord"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0713","stem":"What requirement applies to extension cords used on job sites?","options":["Three-wire type rated for hard or extra-hard usage","Two-wire type rated for general household duty","Any listed cord in lengths under 25 feet","Field-spliced with insulated wire connectors"],"expl":"Job-site extension cords shall be three-wire type and designed for hard or extra-hard usage.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:extension-cord-type"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0714","stem":"After applying a personal lockout device, who is permitted to remove it?","options":["The employee who applied it","Any crew member on site","The building staff engineer","The project manager on record"],"expl":"Each lock is removed only by the employee who applied it; removal by anyone else requires a documented procedure with verification the employee is not on site.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:loto-removal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0715","stem":"After locking and tagging the AHU disconnect, what is the required verification step?","options":["Attempt a start to confirm no operation","Assume zero energy and begin work","Measure system static pressure","Photograph the applied tag"],"expl":"Verify the isolation by attempting a normal start-up, confirming no operation, then returning the operating control to the neutral/off position.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:loto-verify"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0716","stem":"What is the correct sequence for a required tight-fitting respirator program?","options":["Medical evaluation, then fit test, then user seal check","Fit test, then medical evaluation, then seal check","User seal check, then medical evaluation, then fit test","Refresher training, then fit test, then medical evaluation"],"expl":"Medical evaluation comes first, fit testing follows and is repeated at least annually, and a user seal check is performed every time the respirator is donned.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:resp-order"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0717","stem":"What is the OSHA assigned protection factor for a half-mask or filtering facepiece respirator?","options":["10","1000","25","50"],"expl":"Half-mask air-purifying respirators, including N95 filtering facepieces, carry an assigned protection factor of 10.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:apf-half-mask"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0718","stem":"A technician opening a plenum in a 1968 building finds friable pipe wrap suspected of containing asbestos. What is the correct action?","options":["Stop work and notify the client","Bag it and dispose of it as construction debris","Wet it down and continue cleaning","Encapsulate it in duct mastic"],"expl":"Suspect asbestos-containing material triggers stop-work. It must not be disturbed, and abatement or assessment is performed by qualified, licensed personnel.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:suspect-acm-action","ascs:acm-licensed-only"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0719","stem":"Buildings constructed before which approximate era are most likely to contain asbestos-containing HVAC materials?","options":["Before about 1980","Before the 2010s","Before the mid-2000s","None; it was not used in HVAC"],"expl":"Asbestos was widely used in duct insulation, tape, and pipe lagging in buildings constructed before about 1980.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:pacm-1980"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0720","stem":"Disturbing painted surfaces in a pre-1978 residence during HVAC access work may trigger requirements under which document?","options":["The EPA Renovation, Repair and Painting rule","NFPA 90A, installation of air conditioning systems","ASHRAE 62.1, ventilation for indoor air quality","IICRC S500, water damage restoration practice"],"expl":"Pre-1978 housing may contain lead-based paint; disturbing it during renovation, repair, or painting activity brings EPA RRP requirements into play.","domain":"Health & Safety","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:rrp-scope"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0721","stem":"According to ACR 2025, which item is explicitly outside the Standard's scope?","options":["Non-ducted open air plenums","Fan-powered terminal boxes","Heat recovery exhaust ducts","Return plenums built to SMACNA"],"expl":"Scope excludes non-ducted open air plenums, electrical/pneumatic repair, pressure vessels, gas/oil controls, and OEM PM. The other three are inspected components.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-6","acr:1.7.3-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0722","stem":"Under ACR 2025, how is having an ASCS or equivalent responsible for project oversight treated?","options":["Recommended","Mandatory (SHALL)","Permitted (MAY)","Not addressed"],"expl":"Qualifications: personnel shall be trained and qualified; ASCS oversight is recommended, not required.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-12","acr:X-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0723","stem":"What does the Standard say when a local regulation on microbial remediation contradicts ACR?","options":["The regulation supersedes ACR","The IEP resolves the conflict","ACR overrides the regulation","The stricter of the two applies"],"expl":"Disclaimer: codes, laws, and regulations on microbial assessment/remediation supersede contradictory ACR content.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-15"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0724","stem":"ACR 2025 describes itself as which combination?","options":["Performance standard with minimum procedures","Prescriptive standard with performance targets","Guideline with no mandatory content","Code adopted by reference in the IMC"],"expl":"Foreword: a performance standard that also provides minimum procedural requirements.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-3"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0725","stem":"Which trigger for cleaning is on the ACR need-to-clean list?","options":["Microbial contamination at Condition 2 or 3","Condition 1 fungal ecology confirmed by an IEP","System age exceeding ten years of service","A change in building ownership or occupancy"],"expl":"The list includes Condition 2 or Condition 3. Condition 1 is normal fungal ecology, not a trigger. Age and ownership are not listed.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0726","stem":"If there is a question on the need to clean, what does ACR say about Method 2 or Method 3?","options":["May be performed to decide","Is recommended before the job is quoted","Is required on commercial systems","Shall be performed before cleaning"],"expl":"Need-to-clean note: Method 2 or Method 3 may be performed. Permissive, not mandatory.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-17"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0727","stem":"Which is listed by ACR as a reason to recommend cleaning?","options":["Deterioration of the fibrous glass duct liner","A MERV upgrade at the filter rack","A seasonal changeover from cooling to heating mode","Replacement of the blower motor assembly"],"expl":"Deterioration of fibrous glass duct liner, duct board, or other porous components is on the list.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-10"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0728","stem":"ACR names which maintenance standard as a basis for routine cleaning and inspection programs?","options":["ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180","ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 62.1 on ventilation","NFPA 90B on warm air heating systems","ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 on healthcare"],"expl":"Standard 180 is the inspection and maintenance practice for commercial HVAC. 62.1 is ventilation, 170 is healthcare ventilation, 90B is warm-air installation.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Scope & General","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:N-11","acr:1.5-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0729","stem":"Per ACR definitions, what does 'SHALL' denote?","options":["A mandatory requirement with no deviation","A practice advised but not required by the Standard","A possibility open to the user","Permission expressed within the Standard's limits"],"expl":"SHALL is mandatory. RECOMMEND is advised. MAY is permission. CAN is possibility.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-19"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0730","stem":"Per ACR definitions, what does 'MAY' signify?","options":["Permission that the document expresses, not a requirement","An ability or possibility open to the user","Advice or a suggestion, but not a requirement","A mandatory requirement permitting no deviation"],"expl":"MAY = permission expressed by the document but not a requirement. CAN = possibility. RECOMMEND = advised.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-21","acr:G-24"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0731","stem":"Per ACR, what is an 'adhered substance'?","options":["Material not removed by Section 4 procedures","Debris that an agitation device can remove","Particulate that a contact vacuum nozzle can remove","Growth that is dormant, hidden, or inactive"],"expl":"Adhered substance: not removed by Section 4 cleaning and restoration procedures, e.g. paint, mastic, sealant, stains.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-7"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0732","stem":"How does ACR define 'cleaning'?","options":["The removal of non-adhered substances","Restoration to original condition","The removal of all contaminants","Source removal plus antimicrobial treatment"],"expl":"Cleaning: the removal of non-adhered substances. Restoration is bringing back to a former state.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0733","stem":"Per the ACR definition, what must a 'collection device' exhausting inside the building be?","options":["HEPA-filtered to 99.97% at 0.3 micron","Fan driven with a non-HEPA filter","Ducted to the return air plenum","Fitted with a MERV 13 final filter"],"expl":"Collection device: a HEPA machine discharging indoors, or a fan-driven non-HEPA machine discharging outside the envelope.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-33","acr:D-34"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0734","stem":"Per ACR, Condition 2 is an environment primarily contaminated with what?","options":["Settled spores dispersed from a Condition 3 area","Hidden growth behind finishes and within cavities","Spores reflective of normal fungal ecology only","Actual growth, active or dormant, visible or hidden"],"expl":"Condition 2: settled spores or fragments dispersed directly or indirectly from a Condition 3 area, possibly with traces of growth.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-20","acr:D-21"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0735","stem":"Per ACR, Condition 3 includes growth that is ____.","options":["Active or dormant, visible or hidden","Present on porous materials such as liner only","Visible and active only","Confirmed by culture, PCR, or microscopy"],"expl":"Condition 3: actual growth, active or dormant, visible or hidden, with its spores and fragments.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-23","acr:D-22"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0736","stem":"Per ACR, what may Condition 1 include?","options":["Settled spores reflective of a normal ecology","Traces of growth dispersed from a Condition 3 area","No spores or fungal fragments of any kind","Dormant growth confined to non-porous surfaces"],"expl":"Condition 1: may have settled spores, fragments, or traces of growth whose identity, location, and quantity reflect normal fungal ecology.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-19","acr:D-18"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0737","stem":"Which task is part of the ACR definition of an IEP?","options":["Verifying return of fungal ecology to Condition 1","Certifying ASCS candidates for NADCA","Performing Type 2 wet cleaning of coils and pans","Running the NADCA Vacuum Test on every project"],"expl":"IEP: assess fungal ecology, create sampling strategy, sample, interpret lab data, determine Conditions, verify return to Condition 1.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-84"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0738","stem":"Per ACR, what does 'permanent' mean?","options":["The life of the system","Mechanically fastened rather than taped","Until the next scheduled cleaning","Ten years or more"],"expl":"Permanent: the life of the system.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-54"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0739","stem":"Per ACR, what is 'surface treatment (non-antimicrobial)'?","options":["A coating that repairs or modifies a surface","A mastic that seals gaps at duct joints","An agent that kills or suppresses microbial growth","A sealant that closes a service opening airtight"],"expl":"Surface Treatment (non-antimicrobial): coating or treatment designed to repair surface defects or modify surface characteristics.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-104"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0740","stem":"Per ACR, what is 'source removal'?","options":["Mechanical dislodging and vacuum removal at origin","Chemical neutralization of contaminants left in place","Encapsulation of adhered substances with a coating","Replacement of contaminated system components"],"expl":"Source Removal: contaminants physically dislodged and removed with vacuum collection, eliminating them at their point of origin.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-38","acr:4.3-b"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0741","stem":"Per ACR, what does 'standard of care' mean?","options":["Practices common to prudent, recognized trade members","The manufacturer's published maintenance practice","The scope of work agreed in the written work plan","The minimum, code-adopted requirements"],"expl":"Standard of Care: practices common to reasonably prudent members of the trade recognized as qualified and competent.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-88"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0742","stem":"Per ACR, what does 'chain of custody documentation' record?","options":["Seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition","Calibration, sampling, labeling, shipping, weighing, and reporting","The pump model, template size, and flow rate recorded for each sample","Client approval of each sampling location, sample, and lab result"],"expl":"Chain of Custody: the chronological paper trail of seizure, custody, control, transfer, analysis, and disposition of evidence.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-90"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0743","stem":"Per ACR, what is 'ambient air cleaning'?","options":["Removing particulate from indoor air outside of the HVAC system","Filtering return air through a MERV 13 or better filter","Cleaning the airside surfaces inside the air-handling unit cabinet","Holding the work area at negative pressure to the occupied space"],"expl":"Ambient Air Cleaning: removing particulate from indoor air outside of the HVAC system.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-98"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0744","stem":"Per ACR, what is 'appropriate negative pressure'?","options":["Enough to keep debris out of occupied space","A 10 percent differential to the adjacent corridor","Verified by a recording manometer","At least 0.02 inches of water column to the corridor"],"expl":"Appropriate Negative Pressure: enough to prevent debris from entering the occupied space or leaving the contained area. No number.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-94","acr:D-96"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0745","stem":"Which document governs professional water damage restoration?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S500","ANSI/IICRC S520","ANSI/IKECA C10","ANSI/IESO/RIA 6001"],"expl":"S500 water, S520 mold, IESO/RIA 6001 fire particulate on HVAC surfaces, IKECA C10 commercial kitchen exhaust cleaning.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:R-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0746","stem":"Which document is for assessing HVAC systems after a water, fire, or mold event?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S590","ANSI/IICRC S520","ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA 180","ANSI/IKECA C10"],"expl":"S590: Standard for Assessing HVAC Systems Following a Water, Fire, or Mold Damage Event.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:R-3"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0747","stem":"Which document covers the methodology for cleaning commercial kitchen exhaust?","options":["ANSI/IKECA C10","ASHRAE 62.1","NFPA 90A installation standard","NFPA 96 commercial cooking"],"expl":"IKECA C10 is the cleaning methodology. NFPA 96 is ventilation control and fire protection of commercial cooking.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-ikeca"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0748","stem":"What does NFPA 96 cover?","options":["Ventilation control and fire protection of commercial cooking","Standard test method for surface burning of building materials","Installation of air-conditioning and ventilating systems in buildings","Installation of warm air heating and air conditioning"],"expl":"NFPA 96 = commercial cooking. 90A = A/C and ventilating installation. 90B = warm air heating. 255 = surface burning.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nfpa-90b","ascs:nfpa-96","ascs:surface-burning-tests","hvac:a-nfpa96-scope"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0749","stem":"What does UL 181 cover?","options":["Factory-made air ducts and air connectors","Closure systems for flexible air ducts","Closure systems for rigid air ducts","Surface burning characteristics of materials"],"expl":"UL 181 factory-made ducts (and ACR surface treatments). 181A rigid closures. 181B flexible closures. 723 surface burning.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ul-181","hvac:a-ul181-family"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0750","stem":"UL 181B covers closure systems for ____.","options":["Flexible air ducts and air connectors","Rigid air ducts and air connectors","Rigid fibrous glass duct board","Grease exhaust ducts under NFPA 96"],"expl":"181B is flexible; 181A is rigid, which includes fibrous glass duct board. NFPA 96 covers commercial kitchen grease exhaust.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ul-181a-181b","hvac:a-ul181-family"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0751","stem":"What does ASHRAE/ASHE Standard 170 cover?","options":["Ventilation of health care facilities","Ventilation and IAQ in commercial buildings","Inspection and maintenance of commercial HVAC","Ventilation and IAQ in residences"],"expl":"170 healthcare ventilation. 62.1 commercial IAQ. 62.2 residential IAQ. 180 inspection and maintenance.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ashrae-170"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0752","stem":"What does NAIMA AH 122 cover?","options":["Cleaning fibrous glass insulated air duct systems","Fiberglass HVAC insulation and indoor environmental quality","Fibrous glass duct liner specification","Fibrous glass duct construction standards"],"expl":"AH 122 cleaning. AH 116 construction. AH 110 IEQ. ASTM C1071 is the liner specification.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:naima-ah116","ascs:astm-c1071","ascs:naima-ah122","ascs:naima-ah110"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0753","stem":"What does NADCA Standard 05 cover?","options":["Requirements for installing service openings in HVAC systems","Guidance on using chemical products in HVAC systems","Performance testing of vacuum collection equipment","Inspecting and cleaning open air plenums in HVAC systems"],"expl":"Standard 05 service openings. Standard 03 vacuum equipment. Plenums and chemicals are position papers.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nadca-std-05"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0754","stem":"What does NADCA Standard 03 cover?","options":["Requirements for the testing of vacuum collection equipment","Requirements for installing service openings in HVAC systems","General specifications for cleaning commercial HVAC systems","Worker safety practices in the NADCA Safety Manual"],"expl":"Standard 03: Requirements for Testing Vacuum Collection Equipment.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nadca-std-03"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0755","stem":"Which document is a NADCA position paper listed in ACR 2025?","options":["Using Chemical Products in HVAC Systems","Evaluation of HVAC Surfaces for Fire-Related Particulate","Cleaning Fibrous Glass Insulated Air Duct Systems","Installation of Service Openings"],"expl":"Position papers: open air plenums, and chemical products. The others are NADCA Standard 05, NAIMA AH 122, and IESO/RIA 6001.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nadca-position-paper"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0756","stem":"ASTM E84 is a test method for what?","options":["Surface burning characteristics of materials","Air leakage testing of duct systems under pressure","Fibrous glass duct lining insulation specification","Total nuisance dust sampled by gravimetric analysis"],"expl":"E84 surface burning (like UL 723 and NFPA 255). C1071 is the liner spec. NIOSH 0500 is nuisance dust.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:surface-burning-tests","ascs:astm-c1071"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0757","stem":"Which NIOSH publication is referenced for Method 3 analysis?","options":["Manual of Analytical Methods, Fifth Edition","Criteria Document for Particulates Not Otherwise Regulated","Industrial Ventilation, A Manual of Recommended Practice","Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards"],"expl":"NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods, 5th edition, contains Method 0500. The Industrial Ventilation manual is ACGIH.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nmam-5th"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0758","stem":"ASHRAE Standard 62.2 covers ventilation and acceptable IAQ in ____.","options":["Residential buildings","Health care facilities","Commercial buildings","Industrial facilities"],"expl":"62.2 residential. 62.1 commercial. 170 healthcare.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ashrae-62-1","ascs:ashrae-62-2"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0759","stem":"The ACR references list mold guidance from a city health department. Where is it from?","options":["New York City","City of Houston","Chicago","Los Angeles County"],"expl":"NYC Department of Health and Mental Hygiene Guidelines on Assessment and Remediation of Fungi in Indoor Environments.","domain":"Standards","sub":"References","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nyc-mold-guidelines"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0760","stem":"How does ACR 2025 describe itself?","options":["The standard of care for the industry","A guideline with no mandatory requirements","A code adopted by reference in the IMC","An OSHA-recognized best practice"],"expl":"Purpose: supersedes all previous editions and is considered the standard of care for the HVAC cleaning and restoration industry.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0761","stem":"Per ACR, what does 'friable' mean?","options":["Easily crumbled or pulverized","Softened by water intrusion","Contaminated with Condition 3 growth","Delaminated from its substrate"],"expl":"Definition: Friable, easily crumbled or pulverized.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-101"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0762","stem":"Where does the term 'preliminary determination' come from?","options":["ANSI/IICRC S520","ASHRAE 180","ANSI/IICRC S500 water damage standard","NADCA Standard 03 for vacuum equipment"],"expl":"Definition cites S520: a conclusion from an initial inspection about moisture, growth, and the need for other experts.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:prelim-determination-s520"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0763","stem":"What may the HVAC system 'also include'?","options":["Dedicated exhaust, ventilation, and make-up air","Interstitial cavities used as return paths","Open air plenums, chases, and shafts","Building structure exposed in the airstream"],"expl":"HVAC System definition: may also include dedicated exhaust and ventilation components and make-up air systems.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-68"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0764","stem":"'Damp wiping' is a process that ____.","options":["May be used on restoration projects","Should follow each Type 2 coil cleaning","Can replace the Method 2 comparison test","Is banned on non-porous surfaces"],"expl":"Definition: a process of wiping HVAC component surfaces that may be used on restoration projects.","domain":"Standards","sub":"Definitions","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:D-39"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0765","stem":"How is ACR best described?","options":["A consensus industry standard for HVAC cleaning","An OSHA safety code adopted by reference","A federal regulation that EPA enforces","A membership requirement document for NADCA firms"],"expl":"Consensus standard. It becomes binding when specified in a contract or adopted by an authority.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:acr-consensus"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0766","stem":"Relationship between ACR and local mechanical code?","options":["The adopted code governs; the standard never overrides it","ACR applies only where no adopted local code exists","Where ACR is stricter, ACR supersedes the local code","The two documents never overlap in scope or subject"],"expl":"A standard never overrides an adopted code. Where they conflict, code wins.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:code-over-standard"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0767","stem":"EPA's role relative to duct cleaning is to ____.","options":["Register antimicrobial products and publish duct cleaning guidance","Enforce ACR compliance through regional duct inspections","Set required duct cleaning intervals for each system type","Certify and license duct cleaning contractors through regional offices"],"expl":"EPA registers pesticides under FIFRA and publishes guidance. It does not certify contractors or enforce ACR.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:epa-role"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0768","stem":"A project specification and ACR conflict on a cleaning method. What should you do?","options":["Resolve the conflict with the specifier before proceeding","Follow ACR, since it is the recognized industry standard","Follow whichever of the two is the more stringent requirement","Follow the written specification exactly and without comment"],"expl":"You raise it. Silently picking one exposes you either way.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:spec-conflict"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0769","stem":"Who or what is ASCS certification issued to?","options":["The individual","The project","The member company","The company location"],"expl":"Individuals are certified. That is why a company change requires a transfer.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:certifies-individuals"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0770","stem":"What does NADCA membership require a company to do?","options":["Employ a full-time certified ASCS at each location","Certify each field technician as an ASCS","Maintain at least one CVI-certified inspector on staff","Complete annual third-party audits of completed projects"],"expl":"One full-time ASCS per location. Not every tech, and CVI is not a membership condition.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:membership-ascs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0771","stem":"A HEPA filter used in HVAC cleaning work must remove what fraction of particles at the most penetrating particle size?","options":["99.97% of particles at 0.3 micron","99.999% of particles at 0.1 micron","95% of particles at 0.3 micron","99.97% of particles at 1.0 micron"],"expl":"HEPA is defined by performance, not brand: 99.97% removal efficiency at 0.3 micron, the most penetrating particle size. Filters marketed as 'HEPA-type' or 'HEPA-like' do not meet this and are not acceptable where the ACR requires HEPA.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:hepa-type"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0772","stem":"According to SMACNA duct cleanliness for new construction guidelines, how many general levels of cleanliness are recommended for typical duct installations?","options":["Three levels","A single level","Two levels","Four levels"],"expl":"SMACNA recommends three general levels of cleanliness for typical duct installations. (NADCA official practice question.)","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0773","stem":"Which organization publishes the S520 Standard for Professional Mold Remediation?","options":["IICRC","EPA","CDC","ISO"],"expl":"ANSI/IICRC S520 is the Standard for Professional Mold Remediation, referenced throughout the ACR for microbial work.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:R-2"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0774","stem":"NFPA 90A is the standard for what?","options":["Installation of HVAC systems","ANSI mold remediation practice","IICRC water damage restoration","NAIMA fibrous glass fabrication"],"expl":"NFPA 90A is the Standard for the Installation of Air-Conditioning and Ventilating Systems.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:R-6"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0775","stem":"What does NAIMA's AH-122 document cover?","options":["Cleaning fibrous glass duct systems","Lockout and tagout procedures","Respiratory protection programs","Kitchen exhaust system cleaning"],"expl":"NAIMA AH-122 is 'Cleaning Fibrous Glass Insulated Air Duct Systems,' referenced for fibrous glass work.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:naima-ah122"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0776","stem":"What does SMACNA stand for?","options":["Sheet Metal and Air Conditioning Contractors' National Association","Sheet Metal and Cooling Contractors National Alliance","System Maintenance and Certification National Authority Board","Sealed Metal and Air Conditioning Network Alliance Council"],"expl":"SMACNA is the Sheet Metal and Air Conditioning Contractors' National Association, publisher of HVAC Duct Construction Standards.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:smacna-org"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0777","stem":"What does ASHRAE Standard 180 (ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA) address?","options":["Commercial HVAC maintenance","ANSI mold remediation procedures","SMACNA duct construction gauges","NFPA fire damper test intervals"],"expl":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 is the Standard Practice for Inspection and Maintenance of Commercial Building HVAC Systems.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:1.5-c"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0778","stem":"What does the word SHALL denote in the ACR Standard?","options":["A mandatory requirement","A recommendation","An optional practice","A possibility"],"expl":"SHALL denotes a mandatory requirement; conformance requires no deviation when SHALL is used.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-19"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0779","stem":"When the ACR uses RECOMMEND(ED), it means the practice is ____.","options":["Advised, but not required","Mandatory, not advisory","Prohibited by the standard","Permissible at the ASCS's option"],"expl":"RECOMMEND(ED) means the practice/procedure is advised or suggested but is not a requirement of the Standard.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-20"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0780","stem":"What does the ACR term MAY signify?","options":["Permissible, but not required","A mandatory, required action","A prohibition, disallowed entirely","A recommendation, advised practice"],"expl":"MAY signifies permission expressed by the document: permissible within the limits of the document but not a requirement of the standard of care.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-21"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0781","stem":"What does the ACR term CAN signify?","options":["An ability","A strict requirement","A prohibition","A recommendation"],"expl":"CAN signifies an ability or possibility open to the user, capable of application but not a requirement of the standard of care. Not a requirement.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-22"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0782","stem":"What do UL 181A and UL 181B pertain to?","options":["Closure systems for air ducts","Purity of coil cleaning chemicals","Respirator fit testing","Mold sampling in air ducts"],"expl":"UL 181A covers closure systems for rigid air ducts/connectors; UL 181B covers closure systems for flexible air ducts/connectors.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ul-181a-181b","hvac:a-ul181-family"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0783","stem":"UL 723 is the test standard for what?","options":["Surface burning characteristics of building materials","Motor efficiency of portable vacuum collectors","Purity of refrigerant reclaimed from equipment","Air leakage of installed metal ductwork"],"expl":"UL 723 is the Test for Surface Burning Characteristics of Building Materials (flame-spread and smoke-developed ratings).","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:surface-burning-tests","acr:R-9"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0784","stem":"Which organization is ACCA?","options":["Air Conditioning Contractors of America","American Cleaning Certification Association","Air Cleaning Compliance Authority","Associated Contractors of Canada and America"],"expl":"ACCA is the Air Conditioning Contractors of America, a co-sponsor of ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:acca-org"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0785","stem":"What does the 2025 edition of ACR supersede?","options":["All previous ACR editions","Only the 2021 edition","No prior editions","Only non-ANSI versions"],"expl":"The purpose statement: ACR 2025 supersedes all previous editions and is considered the standard of care for the industry.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-2"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0786","stem":"In the language of the NADCA ACR Standard, what does SHALL indicate?","options":["A mandatory requirement for conformance.","An optional method selected by the customer.","A recommended practice that may be omitted.","An example provided solely for residential work."],"expl":"SHALL denotes a mandatory requirement.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-19"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0787","stem":"Which organization is most directly associated with guidance for cleaning fibrous-glass insulated air ducts?","options":["NAIMA","EPA","UL","ICC"],"expl":"NAIMA publishes fibrous-glass insulation and duct-cleaning guidance, including AH-122.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:naima-ah122"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0788","stem":"Which organization is most directly associated with sheet-metal duct construction guidance?","options":["SMACNA.","AIHA.","IICRC.","NAIMA."],"expl":"SMACNA publishes sheet-metal and air-conditioning construction guidance.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:smacna-org"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0789","stem":"Which organization publishes fire and life-safety standards such as NFPA 90A?","options":["NFPA.","ASTM International.","NAIMA.","EPA."],"expl":"NFPA publishes NFPA 90A and related fire and life-safety standards.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nfpa-org"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0790","stem":"A chemical label does not list ductwork as an approved use, but the client signs consent. What is correct?","options":["Do not use it in ductwork","Use it once cleaning is done","Use it if disclosed as off label","Use it if the space is vacant"],"expl":"Customer consent does not permit use inconsistent with product labeling or applicable law.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:chemical-governing-set"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0791","stem":"Which set should govern a chemical application in an HVAC system?","options":["The product label, manufacturer instructions, law, and SDS controls.","The cleaning invoice, final photos, room temperature, and filter date.","The customer request, technician preference, schedule, and OEM warranty.","The odor strength, duct age, fan speed, and a signed liability release."],"expl":"Chemical use must be lawful, label-consistent, documented, and controlled for exposure.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:chemical-governing-set"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0792","stem":"Which NFPA standard governs the installation of air conditioning and ventilating systems in commercial buildings?","options":["NFPA 90A","NFPA 2001","NFPA 70","NFPA 101"],"expl":"NFPA 90A is the Standard for the Installation of Air-Conditioning and Ventilating Systems in commercial and institutional structures.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety","ascs:nfpa-90a-90b-split"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0793","stem":"Which organizations are associated with Standard 180 for HVAC inspection and maintenance?","options":["ASHRAE and ACCA.","OSHA and NIOSH.","IICRC and NAIMA.","NADCA and SMACNA."],"expl":"ANSI/ASHRAE/ACCA Standard 180 addresses HVAC inspection and maintenance.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":0,"ref":"","units":["ascs:acca-org"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0794","stem":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 is the standard for what?","options":["HVAC duct construction, metal and flexible","Verification of cleanliness, by vacuum test","Assessment of mold, and its remediation","Respiratory protection, and program elements"],"expl":"ANSI/SMACNA 006 is the HVAC Duct Construction Standards, Metal and Flexible. Duct cleanliness for new work is covered by a separate SMACNA guideline.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:smacna-006","hvac:a-smacna-pressure-class"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0795","stem":"What are the three levels named in SMACNA's Duct Cleanliness for New Construction Guidelines?","options":["Basic, intermediate, and advanced","Light, medium, and heavy","Class I, II, III","Bronze, silver, and gold"],"expl":"The three SMACNA cleanliness levels for new construction are basic, intermediate, and advanced, each with its own criteria.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:smacna-cleanliness-levels"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0796","stem":"Which standard governs fire damper and smoke detection requirements in air distribution systems?","options":["NFPA 90A","IICRC S520","ASHRAE 52.2","NAIMA AH-122"],"expl":"NFPA 90A, the Standard for the Installation of Air-Conditioning and Ventilating Systems, drives fire damper placement, smoke detection, and material flame/smoke ratings in air-handling spaces.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:nfpa-90a-life-safety"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0797","stem":"IICRC S520 is the standard and reference guide for what?","options":["Professional mold remediation","Water damage restoration","Carpet cleaning","Fire and smoke restoration"],"expl":"S520 is the Standard and Reference Guide for Professional Mold Remediation and is on the ASCS additional reference list.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:R-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0798","stem":"What is the current edition of ACR, The NADCA Standard?","options":["2025 edition","The 2021 edition","2013 edition","ACR 2016"],"expl":"NADCA released the 2025 edition of ACR in September 2025; it supersedes the 2021 edition and is the edition candidates should study.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["acr:G-2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0799","stem":"Which organization publishes 'A Brief Guide to Mold in the Workplace,' listed on the ASCS reference list?","options":["OSHA","EPA","NIOSH","AIHA"],"expl":"OSHA publishes the workplace mold guide referenced by the ASCS Candidate Guide; EPA publishes the schools and commercial buildings mold remediation guide.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:osha-mold-guide","ascs:epa-mold-guide"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0800","stem":"On the ASCS acronym list, what does IEP stand for?","options":["Indoor Environmental Professional","Independent Equipment Provider","Internal Exhaust Plenum","Integrated Energy Program"],"expl":"IEP is Indoor Environmental Professional, the qualified party who assesses contamination and writes remediation protocols.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:iep-acronym"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0801","stem":"On the ASCS acronym list, what does MUA refer to?","options":["Make Up Air","Multi-Unit Assembly","Maximum Airflow","Mold Assessment"],"expl":"MUA is Make Up Air, the outdoor air introduced to replace air exhausted from a space, and appears on NADCA's approved acronym list.","domain":"Standards","sub":"","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:mua-acronym"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0802","stem":"What does a pressure-independent VAV terminal read to hold its airflow setpoint?","options":["an averaging velocity-pressure sensor at its own inlet collar","a static-pressure sensor two-thirds down the main duct","a discharge-air temperature sensor past the reheat coil","a position feedback pot on the damper actuator shaft"],"expl":"Pressure-independent boxes carry an inlet flow sensor (flow cross or ring) so the controller can hold CFM no matter what the inlet static does. The duct static sensor controls the fan, not the box.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-pressure-independent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0803","stem":"A pressure-dependent VAV terminal differs from a pressure-independent one in that its airflow ____.","options":["varies with inlet pressure at a fixed damper angle","holds steady whatever the inlet pressure or fan does","is set by a reheat valve position instead of a damper","is measured downstream and sent to the supply fan VFD"],"expl":"No flow sensor, so damper position is the only control. If main duct pressure rises, the zone gets more air at the same damper angle.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-pressure-dependent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0804","stem":"What is the cross-shaped or ring-shaped tubing at the inlet collar of a VAV box?","options":["a pickup that averages inlet velocity pressure","a fusible link that lets the fire damper drop shut","a sound baffle that keeps fan noise out of the zone","a turning-vane set that straightens flow at the damper"],"expl":"That is the flow sensor. It averages velocity pressure across the inlet and feeds the box controller. Bend it, coat it, or plug its ports and the box misreads its own airflow.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-pressure-independent","hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0805","stem":"A cleaning crew coats the ports of a VAV inlet flow sensor with encapsulant. What is the most direct consequence?","options":["the box controller misreads the airflow and mispositions its damper","the reheat coil runs continuously to make up the difference","the supply fan VFD trips out on high duct static pressure","the smoke damper downstream of the terminal fails closed"],"expl":"The box controls CFM from that sensor. A false low reading drives the damper open and overfeeds the zone; a false high does the reverse. Protect flow sensors during cleaning.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-flow-sensor-fouling"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0806","stem":"Why are VAV terminals set to a minimum airflow instead of closing fully?","options":["the zone still gets ventilation air when cooling load is gone","the supply fan cannot ride into its surge region at low load","the duct static sensor stays inside its calibrated span","the return plenum never goes positive relative to the space"],"expl":"Minimum position (often 20-30% of max) keeps outdoor air reaching the space and keeps air moving. Reheat then handles overcooling at that minimum.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-minimum"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0807","stem":"In a parallel fan-powered VAV box, what does the fan do?","options":["sits beside the primary path and runs only on a heating call","sits in the primary path and runs whenever the AHU runs","modulates with the primary damper to hold discharge static","reverses to push surplus primary air back into the plenum"],"expl":"Parallel = fan beside the primary path, cycling on to pull warm plenum air when the zone needs heat. Series = fan in the path, running continuously.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-parallel-fan"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0808","stem":"Compared with a parallel fan-powered box, what does a series fan-powered box deliver?","options":["constant zone airflow while primary air varies","discharge airflow that tracks primary one to one","warmer primary air from its fan ahead of the cooling coil","less plenum air from a smaller induction opening"],"expl":"Series fan runs continuously and mixes primary with induced plenum air, so the zone always sees the same total CFM; only the primary/plenum ratio changes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-series-constant"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0809","stem":"Where does the 'free' heat a fan-powered VAV box recovers in heating mode come from?","options":["plenum return air warmed by the lights and roof","hot water held in the reheat coil between calls","primary air leaving the central air handling unit","condenser heat piped down from the rooftop unit"],"expl":"The induction port pulls plenum air, already warmed by lighting and roof gain, before any reheat is needed.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-plenum-free-heat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0810","stem":"As the zone load falls, what is the standard single-duct VAV reheat sequence?","options":["the damper closes to its minimum, then the reheat coil energizes","reheat energizes first, then the damper closes to minimum","damper and reheat modulate together over the full range","the damper closes fully, then reheat warms the plenum air"],"expl":"Cooling is reduced by throttling primary air to minimum. Only when the zone keeps falling does reheat come on at that minimum flow.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0811","stem":"What does a bypass VAV system use its bypass damper to do?","options":["dump surplus supply air to the return so fan flow is constant","route hot-deck air around the cooling coil to every terminal unit","relieve building pressure to the outdoors as the terminals close down","feed plenum air into the boxes when heating is called"],"expl":"Bypass VAV pairs a constant-volume fan with VAV boxes. The bypass damper keeps fan airflow steady while the zones throttle, common on small systems with no VFD.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-bypass-vav"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0812","stem":"What makes a dual-duct VAV terminal recognizable?","options":["two inlet collars fed by the hot deck and the cold deck","an internal fan that induces plenum air on every single heating call","a bypass port that returns unused primary air to the ceiling","a single inlet plus an electric reheat coil at the discharge"],"expl":"Dual duct: hot and cold decks run to each box, which mixes them and varies volume. Fan-powered and reheat boxes have one inlet.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dual-duct-terminal"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0813","stem":"What does a single-duct VAV terminal typically NOT contain?","options":["an induction opening drawing air from the return plenum","an inlet damper driven by a 24 V modulating actuator","a velocity-pressure flow sensor at the inlet collar","a DDC controller wired to the zone temperature sensor"],"expl":"Induction ports belong to fan-powered boxes. A plain single-duct box is a damper, actuator, flow sensor, and controller, sometimes with a reheat coil.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-box"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0814","stem":"Why do VAV systems cut fan energy relative to CAV?","options":["fan power falls with the cube of airflow as boxes throttle","supply air can be delivered warmer all year","the return fan and drive are eliminated in most designs","terminal reheat is no longer needed at part load"],"expl":"Fan affinity law: half the airflow needs about one-eighth the power. The VFD slows the fan as the boxes close and duct static rises.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-half-speed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0815","stem":"Static pressure reset in a VAV system lowers the fan setpoint until what happens?","options":["the most-demanding terminal damper is nearly wide open","the supply air temperature reaches its upper reset limit","the return fan speed matches the supply fan speed","the outdoor air damper drops to its minimum position"],"expl":"Trim-and-respond: keep dropping duct static until one box is almost fully open, which means the fan is doing no more work than the worst zone needs.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-static-reset"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0816","stem":"Supply air temperature reset raises the cooling setpoint in mild weather to ____.","options":["cut reheat and cooling energy at the cost of fan energy","cut fan energy at the cost of more reheat energy","hold every zone at a constant relative humidity setpoint","keep the economizer locked out until the heating season"],"expl":"Warmer supply means boxes need more CFM (fan energy up) but less overcooling to reheat and less chiller work. Designers balance the two.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-sat-reset"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0817","stem":"When is a VAV zone 'satisfied'?","options":["it hits setpoint and its damper drops to minimum","its reheat coil reaches the discharge-air setpoint","its box is passing the maximum design airflow","the duct static sensor reads the fan setpoint"],"expl":"Satisfied = no cooling call. The damper sits at minimum until the zone warms and asks for more.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-vav-reheat-sequence"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0818","stem":"Why can a cooling-only VAV box at minimum flow still overcool the zone?","options":["the primary air is still about 55°F at minimum volume","the box fan keeps pulling in cold plenum air at minimum flow","the supply fan speeds up as the boxes throttle to minimum","the flow sensor drives the damper open on low static"],"expl":"Volume drops but temperature does not. That is why interior-zone boxes get reheat or a fan to temper the air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-cooling-only-overcool"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0819","stem":"Before cleaning ductwork downstream of a VAV terminal, how should the box damper be commanded?","options":["open through the BAS or the box controller so air and tools can pass","closed to isolate the zone from the rest of the duct system during cleaning work","to minimum position to limit dust migration into the occupied zone","to its actuator fail position with the controller left unpowered"],"expl":"A box parked at minimum blocks agitation and negative-pressure airflow. Override it open, note the original state, and restore it afterward.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-open-box-cleaning"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0820","stem":"Why is energy wasted in a CAV system with terminal reheat?","options":["air cooled for the peak-load zone is reheated for the rest","the supply fan slows below its efficient range at part load","each zone damper throttles supply air at part load","return air bypasses the cooling coil and is then reheated"],"expl":"Supply air is cooled for the zone with the biggest cooling load, and every other zone reheats that air to stay comfortable: simultaneous heating and cooling. Slowing fans and throttling dampers describe VAV, which cuts airflow instead.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"DOE Minimizing Reheat Guide","units":["hvac:s-cav-reheat"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0822","stem":"What does 'primary air' mean at a terminal unit?","options":["air from the central AHU through the main duct","plenum air induced by the box fan","outdoor air delivered straight from a dedicated OA unit","room air recirculated across the box reheat coil"],"expl":"Primary = what the AHU sent. Secondary or induced air is what a fan-powered box pulls from the plenum.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-primary-air"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0823","stem":"What is a cleaning-relevant feature of fan-powered VAV boxes?","options":["a filter on the plenum induction inlet that loads with dust","a fusible link that must be replaced after any access","a coil that cannot be cleaned without pulling the box out","a sealed cabinet with no access panel to the fan section"],"expl":"The induction inlet pulls unfiltered plenum air through a small filter. It clogs, the fan starves, and the zone runs cold or noisy.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-induction-filter"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0824","stem":"Why does a multizone air handler waste energy?","options":["it mixes hot-deck and cold-deck air for each zone's setpoint","it cannot run an economizer during the shoulder seasons","each zone duct needs its own reheat coil and a separate filter","its supply fan slows below its efficient speed at part load"],"expl":"Heating and cooling at the same time, at the unit. Codes now restrict new multizone and dual-duct designs for that reason.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"VAV & terminals","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-multizone-waste"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0825","stem":"What does ASHRAE 90.1 address?","options":["energy efficiency in commercial buildings","ventilation rates for acceptable indoor air quality","thermal comfort conditions for building occupants","filter test methods and MERV rating procedures"],"expl":"90.1 is the commercial energy standard: envelope, lighting, HVAC efficiency, economizers, VAV minimums, simultaneous heating and cooling limits. 62.1, 55, and 52.2 are the other three.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["cvi:ashrae-901"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0826","stem":"What does SEER express?","options":["seasonal cooling output per watt-hour of the electrical input","cooling output at a single 95°F outdoor rating point","seasonal heating output per watt-hour, for heat pumps","fuel converted to useful heat over a year, for furnaces"],"expl":"Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio, BTU per watt-hour across a modeled cooling season. EER is the single-point number; HSPF is heating; AFUE is fuel.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-seer2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0827","stem":"EER differs from SEER in that EER is ____.","options":["a single-point rating at 95°F outdoor and 80/67°F indoor","a seasonal average weighted across a modeled cooling season","a heating-mode rating used for heat pump equipment","a fuel-based rating used for gas-fired equipment"],"expl":"EER = steady-state BTU per watt at 95°F outdoor, 80°F/67°F indoor. Useful for peak-load and commercial equipment; SEER models a whole season.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-eer2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0828","stem":"What does HSPF rate?","options":["a heat pump's seasonal heating output per watt-hour","a furnace's share of fuel turned into useful heat over a year","a chiller's kilowatts consumed per ton of cooling","an air conditioner's cooling output at peak conditions"],"expl":"Heating Seasonal Performance Factor. Higher is better; it is the heating-side twin of SEER.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-hspf2"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0829","stem":"What does AFUE rate?","options":["a furnace's fuel energy delivered as heat over a year","an air conditioner's seasonal cooling efficiency over a modeled year","an electric heat pump's seasonal heating efficiency","a blower's airflow delivered per watt of motor power"],"expl":"Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency. 80% is a standard non-condensing furnace; 90%+ requires a condensing secondary heat exchanger.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-afue"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0830","stem":"What is coefficient of performance (COP)?","options":["heat moved divided by work input with no units","BTU per watt-hour, over a whole modeled cooling season","kilowatts per ton for a chiller plant","BTU per hour per square foot of coil"],"expl":"COP is a dimensionless ratio. Electric resistance heat is COP 1.0; a heat pump moving 3 units of heat per unit of electricity is COP 3.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-cop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0835","stem":"A constant-airflow ECM blower responds to a loading filter by ____.","options":["ramping up to hold its CFM until it hits a power limit","slowing down so that the static pressure stays at the design value","shutting off on the high-limit until the filter is replaced","holding a fixed RPM so airflow drops as static rises"],"expl":"Constant-airflow ECMs chase a CFM target and draw more watts as static climbs. A PSC or constant-torque motor simply loses airflow. Either way, a dirty system costs energy.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-ecm"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0840","stem":"A differential enthalpy economizer is preferred over dry-bulb in humid climates because it ____.","options":["compares the total heat of outdoor and return air including moisture","opens the outdoor damper whenever outdoor dry-bulb is lower than return","holds the outdoor damper at minimum until the chiller shuts off","uses one fixed outdoor changeover setpoint through the year"],"expl":"Cool but muggy outdoor air can carry more total heat than return air. Enthalpy control catches that; dry-bulb control would bring it in and load the coil.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-econ-drybulb-enthalpy"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0841","stem":"What does an 'integrated' economizer mean?","options":["free cooling from outdoor air runs alongside mechanical cooling","the economizer damper is built into the return fan housing","the economizer locks out whenever the compressor runs","the economizer shares one actuator with the smoke damper"],"expl":"Integrated = first stage of cooling is outdoor air; if that is not enough, the compressor or chiller adds the rest while the damper stays open.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-econ-integrated"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0842","stem":"Demand control ventilation reduces energy by ____.","options":["trimming outdoor air on CO2 when occupancy is low","raising fan speed to dilute contaminants faster","closing the relief damper to hold pressure","switching the economizer to enthalpy control"],"expl":"CO2 tracks people. Fewer people, less outdoor air to condition, subject to code minimums. Common in conference rooms, gyms, and assembly spaces.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dcv","hvac:a-dcv"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0843","stem":"What does an enthalpy (energy recovery) wheel transfer between exhaust and outdoor air?","options":["sensible heat and moisture via a desiccant-coated rotor","sensible heat alone across sealed aluminum plates","moisture alone through a permeable membrane core","sensible heat through a pumped glycol runaround coil loop"],"expl":"The rotor's desiccant carries moisture as well as heat. Downside: some cross-leakage, handled with a purge section. Plate exchangers and runaround loops are sensible-only.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0844","stem":"Which energy recovery device keeps the two airstreams completely separate, even at a distance?","options":["a runaround loop of two coils joined by pumped fluid","an enthalpy wheel fitted with a purge section","a rotary sensible-heat wheel with brush seals and a purge","a total-energy desiccant wheel with labyrinth seals"],"expl":"Runaround coils can sit in separate air handlers; nothing but glycol moves between them. Wheels always pass some air across.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-runaround"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0845","stem":"Why does a DOAS paired with chilled beams or radiant panels save fan energy?","options":["only ventilation air is ducted while the sensible load rides on water","the beams induce enough room air to eliminate the outdoor air unit entirely","the DOAS runs at very high velocity in small ducts","the radiant panels replace the chiller in cooling season"],"expl":"Water moves heat far cheaper than air. DOAS handles outdoor air and dehumidification; the water side handles the space sensible load.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-doas"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0846","stem":"An active chilled beam differs from a passive one in that the active beam ____.","options":["uses ducted primary air to induce room air over its coil","relies only on natural convection with no duct connection at all","is fed with refrigerant rather than with chilled water","is mounted below the floor for displacement ventilation"],"expl":"Active beams get primary air from the DOAS through nozzles; the jet induces room air over the coil. Passive beams are just convective coils in the ceiling.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-active-beam"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0847","stem":"What is the 'balance point' of a heat pump?","options":["the outdoor temperature at which output equals building heat loss","the indoor setpoint where the reversing valve changes over","the refrigerant charge at which superheat equals subcooling","the supply airflow at which sensible and latent capacity are equal"],"expl":"Below the balance point the heat pump alone cannot keep up and supplemental heat (strips or a furnace in dual-fuel) is staged on.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-balance-point"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0848","stem":"Which system types inherently heat and cool at the same time?","options":["constant-volume reheat, as well as multizone and dual-duct systems","constant-volume single-zone rooftops with an airside economizer","single-zone heat pumps, which switch modes at a reversing valve","single-zone VAV rooftops that modulate fan speed with the load"],"expl":"Each cools the air first and then reheats or remixes it. Energy codes limit this by requiring VAV minimums, deadbands, and reset strategies.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-simultaneous-heat-cool","hvac:s-multizone-waste"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0849","stem":"Per the fan affinity laws, cutting fan speed in half reduces fan power to about what?","options":["one-eighth of the original","one-quarter of the original","one-half of the original","three-quarters of the original"],"expl":"Power varies with the cube of speed. Airflow varies directly, static pressure with the square. That cube is why VAV and VFDs pay off.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-fan-half-speed"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0850","stem":"A heat pump beats electric resistance heat because it ____.","options":["moves more heat energy into the building than the electricity it consumes","converts electricity to heat with far less transmission line loss","runs its compressor off-peak, when electricity rates are lower","produces hotter supply air than electric resistance strips do"],"expl":"Resistance is COP 1.0. A heat pump at COP 3 delivers three units of heat per unit of electricity, until the outdoor temperature drops toward its balance point.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-balance-point","hvac:a-cop"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0851","stem":"What does supply duct leakage into an attic in cooling season most directly cause?","options":["lost conditioned air and a depressurized house pulling in attic air","a pressurized house pushing cooled air out through the windows and doors","a warmer evaporator coil that short-cycles the compressor","a return plenum filling with condensate carried off the coil"],"expl":"Every CFM lost from supply must be replaced by infiltration through the envelope, pulling in hot attic and outdoor air. Sealed ducts cut load and keep the house at neutral pressure.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-supply-vs-return-leaks"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0852","stem":"Roughly how much air should a 3-ton residential split system move?","options":["1,200 CFM across the indoor coil","600 CFM across the indoor coil","2,400 CFM across the indoor coil","12,000 CFM across the indoor coil"],"expl":"Rule of thumb 400 CFM per ton (350-450 depending on humidity design). 3 × 400 = 1,200. Low airflow freezes coils; high airflow hurts dehumidification.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-cfm-per-ton","hvac:a-cfm-per-ton","hvac:a-tons-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0853","stem":"What formula estimates the sensible heat carried by an airstream?","options":["BTU/h = 1.08 × CFM × temperature difference","BTU/h = 4.5 × CFM × temperature difference","BTU/h = 0.68 × CFM × temperature difference","BTU/h = 500 × GPM × water temperature difference"],"expl":"1.08 is the constant for standard air. 4.5 × CFM × Δh gives total heat, 0.68 × CFM × Δgrains gives latent, and 500 × GPM × ΔT is the water-side formula.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-sensible-108","hvac:a-heat-constants"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0854","stem":"An oversized air conditioner hurts humidity control because it ____.","options":["satisfies the thermostat before the coil has removed much moisture","runs the coil so cold that condensate freezes onto the fins","moves too little air per ton across the evaporator coil","raises supply air temperature above the room dew point"],"expl":"Short cycles cool the air but barely dehumidify. Right-sizing (Manual J then Manual S) and longer run times fix the clammy-house complaint.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-oversized-ac"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0856","stem":"What does ASHRAE Standard 55 cover?","options":["the thermal conditions a majority of occupants find acceptable","the minimum energy efficiency for commercial buildings","the ventilation rates for acceptable indoor air quality","the test methods for rating air filters by particle size"],"expl":"55 = comfort (temperature, humidity, air speed, radiant, clothing, activity). 90.1 = energy. 62.1 = ventilation. 52.2 = filters.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["cvi:ashrae-55"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0857","stem":"What does ASHRAE 62.2 apply to?","options":["ventilation and IAQ in low-rise residential buildings","ventilation and IAQ in commercial, institutional, and high-rise buildings","ventilation and pressurization in healthcare facilities","HVAC inspection and maintenance program requirements"],"expl":"62.1 is commercial, 62.2 is residential, 170 is healthcare, 180 is maintenance.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["ascs:ashrae-62-2","ascs:ashrae-62-1"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0858","stem":"What does the equal friction duct sizing method do?","options":["sizes every section to the same loss per 100 ft","sizes each section so the static pressure stays constant at every outlet","assigns velocities that step down from the fan to the outlets","sizes the trunk from the sum of branch equivalent lengths"],"expl":"Pick a friction rate (often 0.08-0.10 in. w.g. per 100 ft for low-pressure), read the size off a ductulator for each section's CFM. Simple, common, needs balancing dampers.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-equal-friction"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0859","stem":"What does the static regain duct sizing method do?","options":["sizes each section so that the static it regains offsets its own friction","sizes each section to one fixed friction rate per 100 ft of run","sizes each section to drop velocity by a fixed step at each branch takeoff","sizes each section to hold one velocity from fan to last outlet"],"expl":"As velocity drops at each takeoff, velocity pressure converts to static pressure. Sized right, every outlet sees about the same static. Used on high-velocity and long commercial systems.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-static-regain"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0860","stem":"Round duct is preferred over rectangular for the same airflow because it has ____.","options":["the least perimeter per unit of cross-sectional area","a lower velocity at the same CFM and duct area","no longitudinal seam that needs sealing","a higher aspect ratio that resists collapse"],"expl":"A circle encloses the most area with the least wall. Less surface drag, less material, stiffer under pressure, easier to seal.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-round-vs-rect"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0861","stem":"Rectangular duct aspect ratio should generally be kept at or below what?","options":["4 to 1","1 to 1","8 to 1","12 to 1"],"expl":"1:1 is ideal; up to 4:1 is the usual limit. Flat, wide ducts add friction, metal, and flex, and are harder to clean and seal.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-aspect-ratio"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0862","stem":"What is the 'equivalent length' of a duct fitting?","options":["the length of straight duct that would produce the same pressure loss","the centerline length of the fitting along its throat radius","the fitting's pressure loss expressed in inches of water","the length of straight duct required downstream of the fitting"],"expl":"Manual D tables give equivalent lengths so fittings can be added to straight runs to find total effective length, which sets the friction rate.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-equivalent-length"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0863","stem":"What does ACCA Manual D cover?","options":["residential duct system design","residential heating and cooling load calculation","residential equipment selection","register and grille selection"],"expl":"J = loads, S = equipment, D = ducts, T = terminals (registers and grilles). Do them in that order.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-acca-manuals"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0864","stem":"What is ACCA Manual J used to do?","options":["calculate a home's heating and cooling loads","select equipment capacity from manufacturer data","size the supply trunk and branch ducts","pick registers and grilles by throw and noise"],"expl":"Manual J load first. Then Manual S picks the unit, Manual D sizes the ducts, Manual T picks the outlets.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-acca-manuals"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0865","stem":"Flexible duct installed with slack and sag ____.","options":["adds friction and cuts airflow well below the chart value","behaves the same as rigid metal duct of the same diameter and length","reduces noise enough to skip a sound attenuator","increases static regain at each downstream outlet"],"expl":"Compressed flex can more than double friction versus fully extended flex, which already has more loss than metal. Pull it tight, support it, minimize length.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-flex-friction"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0866","stem":"The ADC flexible duct installation standard limits support spacing and sag to ____.","options":["5 ft, with sag no more than ½ in. per foot of spacing","5 ft, with sag no more than 1 in. per foot of spacing","4 ft, with no measurable sag permitted between supports","8 ft, with sag no more than 2 in. between supports"],"expl":"ADC: support per the manufacturer or local code, but never more than 5 ft apart, with sag no more than ½ in. per foot between supports and hanger material at least 1½ in. wide. The 1 in. per foot option doubles the allowed sag. Local codes can be tighter.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"ADC Flexible Duct Performance & Installation Standards","units":["hvac:a-flex-support"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0867","stem":"What does raising the velocity in a given duct primarily increase?","options":["friction loss, which rises with velocity squared, and duct noise","the duct's leakage class and the seal class it requires","the equivalent length, in feet, of each downstream fitting","the air temperature rise across the furnace heat exchanger"],"expl":"Pressure loss tracks velocity pressure, which goes with V². Double the velocity in the same duct and friction roughly quadruples.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-velocity-squared"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0868","stem":"What formula gives velocity pressure for standard air?","options":["VP = (V ÷ 4,005)² in inches of water, V in FPM","VP = V ÷ 4,005 in inches of water, V in FPM","VP = 1.08 × V ÷ 1,000 in inches of water, V in FPM","VP = V² ÷ 1,000 in inches of water, V in FPM"],"expl":"4,005 FPM equals 1 in. w.g. of velocity pressure at standard air. Pitot tube reads VP; solve for V.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-v-4005"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0869","stem":"What is total external static pressure (TESP) on a furnace or air handler?","options":["supply static plus return static measured at the unit","the pressure difference across the blower wheel, measured alone","the pressure drop across filter and coil only","the static at the farthest supply register"],"expl":"Everything outside the blower cabinet. Read supply static after the unit and return static before it, add the absolute values. Many furnaces are rated for 0.5 in. w.g. max; higher means the ducts are choking the blower.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-tesp","hvac:a-tesp-example"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0870","stem":"What does a lower SMACNA duct leakage class number (CL) mean?","options":["a tighter duct with less leakage at test pressure","a duct built for a lower static pressure class","a duct that needs fewer sealed seams and joints","a duct with a larger allowable aspect ratio"],"expl":"Leakage class (CL) is the allowed leakage in cfm per 100 sq ft of duct surface at 1 in. w.g., so a lower number means a tighter duct. Pressure class and seal class are separate ratings. Recommended CL values vary by SMACNA and ASHRAE edition, so learn the direction, not one number.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"SMACNA HVAC Air Duct Leakage Test Manual","units":["hvac:a-leakage-class"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0871","stem":"What minimum R-value does the IECC set for supply and return ducts 3 inches and larger in an attic?","options":["R-8","R-4","R-6","R-11"],"expl":"R-8 for ducts in attics (3 in. and larger), R-6 in other unconditioned spaces. Ducts inside conditioned space need no insulation.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-attic-duct-r8"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0872","stem":"What does an undersized return duct show up as?","options":["high external static and low airflow across the coil","low external static and high airflow across the coil","excess supply air that overpressurizes the living space","low external static with high blower amp draw"],"expl":"Returns are the usual choke point. Bigger, lower-velocity returns also cut noise. Check TESP before blaming the blower.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-undersized-return"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0873","stem":"What is a ductulator used to do?","options":["read duct size from CFM and friction rate","measure airflow at a supply diffuser face","measure static pressure drop across a coil","calibrate a pitot tube before a traverse"],"expl":"Slide rule for duct sizing. Set CFM against friction rate, read round diameter and equivalent rectangular sizes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-equal-friction"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0874","stem":"Compared with a straight tap, a 45° or conical branch takeoff ____.","options":["lowers turbulence and pressure loss at the branch","raises velocity in the trunk downstream","requires a balancing damper by mechanical code at the takeoff","is permitted only on return ductwork"],"expl":"Smooth entry into the branch. Straight taps create eddies and a higher equivalent length.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-takeoffs"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0875","stem":"Compared with a radius elbow, a mitered elbow without turning vanes ____.","options":["has a much higher pressure loss","has a much lower pressure loss","is quieter at the same velocity","is required in fire-rated ductwork"],"expl":"Sharp corners separate flow. Radius elbows or turning vanes in a mitered elbow bring the loss back down.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-turning-vanes"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0876","stem":"Duct leakage is tested by ____.","options":["pressurizing the sealed system and metering the CFM needed to hold it","running the system fan and reading total pressure at each and every outlet","closing every register and timing how long the blower runs","measuring temperature drop from the supply plenum to the farthest register"],"expl":"Duct blaster or similar calibrated fan. Leakage CFM at test pressure compared with the allowed class.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-leak-test"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0877","stem":"What is the 'throw' of a supply outlet?","options":["how far the jet travels before slowing to a stated terminal velocity","how many degrees the jet spreads out from the outlet centerline","how far the jet drops vertically at the end of its travel","how loud the outlet is at design airflow, as an NC rating"],"expl":"Throw ends where the jet slows to a stated terminal velocity; catalogs list it at 150, 100 and 50 fpm. Spread, drop and NC rating are separate outlet measures that together decide whether a room mixes without drafts.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-throw"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0878","stem":"The Coanda effect matters to ceiling diffusers because it ____.","options":["keeps the jet on the ceiling so it mixes before dropping","forces the jet straight down onto the occupants below the diffuser","reduces the noise criteria rating of the diffuser at design flow","pulls return air into the diffuser neck at low flow"],"expl":"Air hugging the ceiling travels farther and entrains room air. At very low VAV turndown the jet detaches and dumps cold air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-coanda"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0879","stem":"Closing a bedroom door in a house with one central return, and no transfer path, causes ____.","options":["the closed room to pressurize and the rest of the house to depressurize","the room to depressurize and draw attic air in through the ceiling","the supply register in that room to deliver more airflow","the air handler to move more total airflow through the coil"],"expl":"Supply keeps pushing air in, nothing lets it out. Fix with jumper ducts, transfer grilles, or undercut doors so pressure equalizes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-transfer-path"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0880","stem":"Doubling the airflow through an existing duct raises its friction loss by roughly how much?","options":["four times","two times","eight times","one and a half times"],"expl":"Friction goes with velocity squared. That is why boosting CFM through undersized ductwork spikes static so fast.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-velocity-squared"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0881","stem":"What sets the fan static pressure for a duct system?","options":["the most restrictive run or critical path","the average pressure loss of all the runs, added and divided","the shortest run nearest the fan discharge","the sum of every run's loss added together"],"expl":"The fan must overcome the worst-case path; other runs get balancing dampers to throttle down to design.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-critical-path"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0882","stem":"Fibrous glass duct board systems are typically limited to what pressure class?","options":["2 in. w.g.","½ in. w.g.","6 in. w.g.","10 in. w.g."],"expl":"Duct board is a low-pressure product, UL 181 Class 1 air duct, rated for 2 in. w.g. positive and lower negative. Metal takes the higher classes.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Duct design","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-board-pressure"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0884","stem":"In the vapor-compression cycle, the evaporator is where refrigerant ____.","options":["boils, absorbing heat from the air over the coil","condenses, giving up heat to the air over the coil","is compressed into a hot, high-pressure vapor","expands through an orifice into a cold mist"],"expl":"Indoor coil in cooling mode. Low-pressure liquid boils at about 40°F, pulling heat and moisture out of the supply air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-evap-cond"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0885","stem":"What does the condenser in a split system do?","options":["rejects heat and turns hot vapor back into liquid","absorbs heat and turns the liquid into a low-pressure vapor","meters liquid refrigerant into the evaporator coil","stores liquid refrigerant between run cycles"],"expl":"Outdoor coil in cooling mode. Fan-blown air carries away the heat the evaporator picked up plus the compressor's work.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-evap-cond"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0895","stem":"When does a furnace high-limit switch open?","options":["the heat exchanger overheats from low airflow","the inducer fails to prove draft at the pressure switch","flames roll out of the burner box toward the vestibule","the flue gas in the vent cools below its dew point"],"expl":"Dirty filter, closed registers, dead blower. The limit kills the gas valve; the blower keeps running to cool the exchanger.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-high-limit"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0898","stem":"What does a dirty blower wheel most directly cause?","options":["reduced airflow because the cupped blades lose their capacity","higher airflow because the heavy wheel stores momentum","a tripped pressure switch caused by excessive draft","refrigerant overcharge showing up on the indoor coil"],"expl":"Squirrel-cage blades filled with dust move less air at the same RPM. Cleaning the wheel is often the biggest airflow win in a duct cleaning job.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-dirty-wheel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0899","stem":"On a belt-driven blower, fan speed is changed by ____.","options":["adjusting or swapping out the motor sheave (pulley)","resetting the blade pitch on the blower wheel","tightening or loosening the belt tension idler","rewiring the motor leads for a different supply voltage"],"expl":"Adjustable motor sheaves open or close to change effective diameter and drive ratio. Belt tension affects slip and wear, not the design ratio.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-belt-drive"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0900","stem":"Cooling coil face velocity is normally kept around 500 FPM because higher velocity does what?","options":["blows condensate off the fins into the duct","keeps the refrigerant from boiling off completely","raises the coil's leaving air temperature","backs up the drain pan until it overflows"],"expl":"Moisture carryover wets the duct and liner past the drain pan. Coils are sized for roughly 400-550 FPM face velocity.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-face-velocity","hvac:a-face-velocity"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0901","stem":"What does adding rows or fins per inch to a coil do?","options":["raises capacity and air-side pressure drop and makes cleaning harder","lowers capacity and also lowers the air-side pressure drop","raises face velocity through the coil at the same airflow","removes the need for a trap on the condensate drain line"],"expl":"More surface, more heat transfer, more resistance, more places for dirt to bridge. Deep coils are cleaned from both faces.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-coil-rows"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0906","stem":"Face-and-bypass dampers control coil output by ____.","options":["routing part of the air around the coil","throttling the hot water or steam flow through the coil control valve","varying the fan speed upstream of the coil face","cycling the coil control valve fully open and shut"],"expl":"Common on preheat and steam coils. Constant water flow, variable air split.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-face-bypass"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0907","stem":"Why is an evaporative (wetted-media) humidifier in an air handler a cleaning concern?","options":["its wet pad and sump can grow organisms into the airstream","it raises supply air temperature above the high-limit setting","it deposits refrigerant oil on the downstream coil","it requires steam that scalds the downstream liner"],"expl":"Any standing water in the airstream is a growth site. Steam humidifiers avoid that but leave mineral dust.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-humidifier-types"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0908","stem":"What must a technician do before entering an air handler fitted with UVGI lamps at the coil?","options":["confirm that the UV-C lamp circuit is de-energized and locked out","leave the lamps running to keep the coil surface sterile","cover the lamps with foil to reflect UV onto the coil","raise the fan speed to dilute any ozone the lamps produce"],"expl":"UV-C damages eyes and skin in seconds. LOTO the lamp circuit, and know that the lamps degrade nearby plastics and some liners.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-uvc-loto"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0909","stem":"What does an outdoor air intake located near a cooling tower or exhaust discharge risk?","options":["pulling contaminated or moist air into the supply","freezing the preheat coil during winter operation","over-pressurizing the mixing box at full outdoor air","tripping the freeze stat on warm, humid days"],"expl":"Re-entrainment of exhaust, tower drift (Legionella risk), loading dock fumes. ASHRAE 62.1 sets separation distances.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-oa-intake","hvac:a-intake-separation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0910","stem":"A mixing box brings 20% outdoor air at 30°F together with 80% return air at 70°F. What is the approximate mixed air temperature?","options":["62°F","50°F","58°F","66°F"],"expl":"Weighted average: 0.2 × 30 + 0.8 × 70 = 6 + 56 = 62°F.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-oa-fraction","hvac:a-mixed-air-temp"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0911","stem":"What does a freeze stat (low-limit thermostat) across a coil face do?","options":["stops the fan and closes the OA damper when the coil nears freezing","opens the OA damper wide to purge stagnant cold air off the coil","starts the heating coil pump after the coil face has already frozen solid","alarms when the averaged coil face temperature drops below freezing"],"expl":"Capillary element trips at about 35-38°F anywhere along its length. Protects water coils from bursting when cold OA stratifies.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-freeze-stat"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0913","stem":"What is a 'sequence of operation'?","options":["the written description of how the controls run","the order in which a duct system's sections are cleaned","the start-up checklist for a new compressor","the firing order of a furnace's burners"],"expl":"Ask for it before touching a BAS. It tells you what the dampers, fans, and valves do in each mode.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-sequence-of-operation"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0914","stem":"In unoccupied mode, what does a typical commercial AHU sequence do?","options":["closes the OA damper and lets the space float to setback","opens the OA damper fully to flush the building","runs the supply fan at maximum to hold duct static","holds the occupied setpoint using terminal reheat only"],"expl":"No people, no ventilation air needed. Some sequences add a pre-occupancy purge or optimal start before the day begins.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Controls","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-unoccupied"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0915","stem":"Compared with a return fan, a relief fan ____.","options":["runs only to exhaust surplus air in economizer mode","runs continuously to pull air from every zone back to the AHU","is installed upstream of the cooling coil","is required whenever the AHU has a mixing box"],"expl":"Return fan = always moving return air. Relief fan = dumps building pressure during economizer mode only, less energy.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-return-relief-fans"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0916","stem":"Where are laminar-flow diffusers used?","options":["operating rooms and clean spaces needing a non-mixing air curtain","large gyms and warehouses to throw air across the whole room floor","VAV offices to hold the Coanda effect at low turndown","commercial kitchens to capture grease-laden cooking vapor"],"expl":"Laminar arrays push a non-mixing air curtain straight down over the table at low velocity. They are HEPA-filtered and must not be cleaned like a normal diffuser.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Components","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:d-laminar-or"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0917","stem":"What does an enthalpy wheel's 'purge section' do?","options":["blows a slice of outdoor air back through the rotor","dries the desiccant with a heating coil every single night","reverses the rotor direction to shake off dust","bypasses the wheel entirely in economizer mode"],"expl":"Clears exhaust carryover and cuts cross-contamination to a fraction of a percent. Still not acceptable for exhaust streams like toilets in some codes; check 62.1 class of air.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Systems & efficiency","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:s-enthalpy-wheel"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0918","stem":"What does cooling an air sample without changing its moisture content cause relative humidity to do?","options":["rise until it reaches 100% at the dew point","fall as its capacity for moisture grows","hold steady because grains per pound stay put","drop to zero once the coil is below dew "],"expl":"Same moisture, less capacity, higher RH. At the dew point the air is saturated and water condenses on the coil.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-rh-rises"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0919","stem":"When does wet-bulb temperature equal dry-bulb temperature?","options":["the air is fully saturated at 100% RH","the air is completely dry at 0% relative humidity","the air is at 29.92 in. Hg sea-level pressure","the dew point of the air drops below 32°F"],"expl":"No evaporation off the wick means no cooling. The spread between wet and dry bulb is how a sling psychrometer reads RH.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-dry-wet-bulb"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0920","stem":"What is enthalpy of moist air?","options":["the total heat (sensible plus latent) per pound of dry air","the sensible heat only, read from a dry-bulb thermomete","the latent heat only expressed in grains of moisture","the heat needed to raise one pound of water one degree"],"expl":"Read on a psychrometric chart along the wet-bulb lines. Economizers and ERVs are all about enthalpy differences.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-enthalpy"],"reserved":true},{"id":"ascs-0921","stem":"Moisture content of air is given in grains per pound. How many grains equal one pound of water?","options":["7,000 grains","1,000 grains","12,000 grains","144 grains"],"expl":"7,000 grains per pound. Latent heat formula: 0.68 × CFM × Δgrains.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-grains"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0922","stem":"What is the sensible heat ratio (SHR) of a cooling load?","options":["sensible load divided by total load","latent load over sensible load","supply air temperature over return air temperature","evaporator capacity over condenser capacity"],"expl":"Total = sensible plus latent. SHR 0.75 means 25% of the coil's work is removing moisture. Humid climates need equipment with a lower SHR.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-sensible-latent"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0923","stem":"Where does latent load in an occupied space come from?","options":["people and cooking and showers and humid infiltration","lights and computers and solar gain through the window glass","supply fan motor heat and conduction through duct walls","the compressor discharge line inside the air handler"],"expl":"Latent = moisture. Sensible = temperature. The lighting and solar items are sensible.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":1,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-latent-sources"],"reserved":false},{"id":"ascs-0924","stem":"A hot-gas or electric reheat dehumidification mode works by ____.","options":["overcooling the air below dew point and then reheating it","raising the evaporator temperature to keep more moisture in the air","bypassing return air around the coil to cut its sensible load","slowing the condenser fan to raise the head pressure"],"expl":"Overcool to condense moisture, add heat back so it leaves drier at a comfortable temperature. Hot-gas reheat uses free compressor heat; electric reheat pays for it.","domain":"Mechanical","sub":"Air properties","tier":2,"ref":"","units":["hvac:a-reheat-dehumidify"],"reserved":false}],"built":"2026-09-21"});
